window.OPUS_CATALOG = [{"id":"001","slug":"001-aurora-glass","title":"Aurora Glass — Stillwater","file":"001-aurora-glass.html","textFile":"001-aurora-glass.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/001-aurora-glass.jpg","prompt":"Design a premium landing page for \"Stillwater\", a fictional breathing and meditation app, in refined glassmorphism. Background: deep midnight (#070b1f) with three slow-drifting aurora ribbons rendered on a canvas (layered, sine-deformed gradient bands in teal #2de2c4, violet #7b5cff and rose #ff6fa8, additive blending, very soft) over a faint twinkling star field. Foreground: frosted-glass panels (backdrop-filter blur + saturate, 1px inner highlight border, subtle grain) — a hero with a large, airy, light-weight sans headline; a centred \"breathing orb\" that expands and contracts on a 4-7-8 rhythm with a caption that cross-fades Inhale / Hold / Exhale; a row of three feature cards; a testimonial strip; and a pricing section whose monthly/yearly toggle animates a sliding glass pill. Delight: cards tilt in 3D toward the pointer with a moving specular glare; the aurora bends gently toward the cursor. Interaction: hover/tilt cards; click the orb to start/stop a guided one-minute session with a progress ring; pricing toggle. Mobile: cards stack, the orb stays hero-centred.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A landing page for \"Stillwater\", a fictional breathing and meditation app, built as refined glassmorphism over a living night sky. A full-viewport canvas paints a faint twinkling star field and three slow aurora curtains (teal #2de2c4, violet #7b5cff, rose #ff6fa8) on deep midnight #070b1f; everything else floats above it on frosted glass with grain and a hairline inner highlight. The hero is an editorial composition rather than a centred template: a huge, ultra-light two-line headline staggered left/right, a breathing orb centred beneath it on a 4-7-8 dial, the lede and calls to action anchored bottom-left, and a glass legend card top-right that lights up the current phase. Below: three tilting feature cards with small animated SVG vignettes, a glass testimonial strip with a drifting marquee, a pricing section with a sliding glass billing pill, and a footer that states plainly that the product and people are fictional.","techniques":"- Aurora rendered on a quarter-resolution offscreen canvas: each ribbon is hundreds of 1px columns drawn from a pre-rendered vertical gradient sprite, their edge deformed by layered sines, their alpha modulated into curtain \"rays\", composited additively (\"lighter\") and upscaled with smoothing plus a second, wider pass for a soft glow\n- Aurora bends toward the pointer via a Gaussian falloff around the smoothed cursor position, brightening where it is pulled; it also lifts slightly as the page scrolls\n- Star field from one pre-rendered radial sprite with per-star twinkle phases; density scales with viewport area\n- Frosted glass: backdrop-filter blur + saturate, a 1px gradient border made with a masked pseudo-element (mask-composite exclude), inset highlight shadows, and a runtime-generated noise tile used as overlay grain\n- Breathing orb: stacked radial gradients with a slowly rotating conic \"caustic\" layer in soft-light, a specular highlight, a scaling halo and an expanding ripple at every inhale\n- SVG 4-7-8 dial: three phase arcs sized 4/19, 7/19 and 8/19 of the circumference (stroke-dasharray), 19 second ticks with majors at phase boundaries, and a travelling marker dot\n- Session progress ring built from a conic gradient masked into a ring and swept by a second conic mask, with a glowing head whose colour interpolates teal to violet to rose\n- Gradient-clipped accent words, staggered intro animations, IntersectionObserver reveals, CSS-only marquee with edge fade masks\n- 3D card tilt with a moving radial specular glare plus an angled sheen, both driven by CSS custom properties from one rAF loop","originalInteraction":"- The orb breathes continuously on the 4-7-8 rhythm; the caption cross-fades Inhale / Hold / Exhale with a per-second countdown and the matching legend row and dial arc light up\n- Click, tap, Enter or Space on the orb starts or stops a guided one-minute session: the progress ring sweeps, the status line counts down, and completed minutes are tallied in localStorage (guarded with try/catch)\n- Optional \"Chime\" toggle (off by default) creates a Web Audio context on first use and rings a soft synthesized bell at each phase change during a session\n- Feature and pricing cards tilt toward the pointer with a moving glare (reduced for touch, disabled for reduced motion)\n- Monthly / Yearly billing toggle slides a glass pill with a spring, rolls the prices numerically and cross-fades the billing notes; arrow keys switch periods\n- The aurora follows the cursor; the testimonial marquee pauses on hover or focus\n- prefers-reduced-motion slows the sky, stops twinkle, marquee and ornament animation and disables tilt; the canvas loop pauses while the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"34122401a6d001a5fecee6b303e9778f3c9c832ea7499c77cf4a48b762cfdeb2","name":"极光与呼吸","category":"交互网页","description":"午夜天空中的极光缓缓流动，磨砂玻璃浮在星光之上。呼吸圆环、卡片倾斜与价格切换共同组成一页冥想产品概念。","interaction":"点击呼吸圆环开始或停止引导；移动鼠标观察玻璃反光，向下滚动体验价格切换。"},{"id":"002","slug":"002-neon-rain","title":"Neon Rain","file":"002-neon-rain.html","textFile":"002-neon-rain.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/002-neon-rain.jpg","prompt":"Create an immersive cyberpunk street at night titled \"NEON RAIN — District 9 Night Market\" (fictional). A full-viewport canvas scene with four parallax layers of skyline silhouettes (#05010f → #1a0433) and randomly lit windows; holographic vertical signs with katakana-like glyphs drawn in canvas that flicker with believable neon failure (brief dropouts, buzz); a wet street reflecting the signs (mirrored, blurred, rippled); dense diagonal rain with splash particles on the ground. Palette: hot magenta #ff2a6d, cyan #05d9e8 and acid-yellow #f9f002 accents on ink black. Overlay a thin HUD: time, temperature, \"SIGNAL\" bars and a scrolling ticker of fictional news in monospace. Typography: condensed uppercase sans with chromatic-aberration text-shadow. Interaction: the pointer moves the parallax camera; a click triggers a lightning flash that briefly lights the whole skyline plus a thunder rumble (Web Audio noise, after a gesture, with a mute toggle); a toggle switches rain intensity. Must stay fluid at 60fps using pooled particles.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A full-viewport, live-rendered cyberpunk street at night in the fictional District 9 night market. Four parallax skylines step back from ink black (#05010f) to deep violet (#1a0433) against a smog-lit sky, with hundreds of windows that switch on and off over time. Hand-traced katakana-like neon signs hang off the near towers in hot magenta, cyan and acid yellow. They fail the way real neon fails: stutters, dropouts, brownouts and one dead glyph, and a HOTEL sign whose \"L\" stays dark, a joke the news ticker picks up. A wireframe holographic koi swims above Tower 61 inside a projector beam. Market stalls glow under striped awnings, steam rises from the stalls and pedestrians with glowing umbrella shafts walk the kerb. Everything is mirrored into a rain-rippled, blurred wet street, and dense diagonal rain splashes and rings across it. A thin monospace HUD, a condensed chromatic-aberration title and a scrolling fictional newswire complete the frame.","techniques":"- Four pre-rendered skyline layer canvases (normal + half-resolution \"lightning facade\" version) composited with per-layer parallax ranges and violet fog bands between depths\n- Procedural buildings (setbacks, spires with blinking aviation lights, water tanks, slanted and domed roofs), neon rim lighting on the near layers, and live window toggling painted directly into the layer canvases\n- Neon signs built from hand-traced polyline glyphs stroked as glass tubes (wide glow pass, second glow, bright core), cached in on / broken-glyph / unlit variants, plus light spill sprites and a sweeping holographic scan band\n- Per-sign failure state machine (stutter, dropout, brownout, dead-glyph sputter) with buzz jitter; the dying sign drives a synthesized 120 Hz neon buzz\n- Wet-street reflection: the lower scene is captured each frame into a low-resolution flipped buffer, then redrawn row by row with layered sine offsets (mirrored, blurred by upscaling, rippled), under an asphalt overlay with soft puddle cut-outs, kerb and lane paint\n- Pooled typed-array particle systems: up to 1,700 depth-sorted rain streaks batched into three stroke passes, 700 splash droplets with gravity, 260 flattened ripple rings, 44 steam puffs\n- Holographic koi: an analytic spine sampled along a figure-eight path, a blunt-headed body profile, wave undulation, fins, wireframe ribs, clipped kohaku patches, a chromatic magenta ghost, destination-out scanlines, a glow pass and occasional slice glitches, projected by a light-cone beam\n- Lightning: midpoint-displacement bolt with branches, drawn in three additive passes; multi-pulse flash envelope brightening sky, clouds and every skyline facade\n- CSS: condensed uppercase title with chromatic-aberration text-shadow and clip-path slice glitches, scanline/vignette overlay, corner-bracket HUD, CSS-transform news marquee","originalInteraction":"- Pointer or touch movement steers the parallax camera; after four idle seconds the camera drifts on its own; arrow keys nudge it\n- Click or tap anywhere on the street (or press Space / L, or the STRIKE button) to call a lightning strike near the pointer: the skyline lights up, the HUD glitches, SIGNAL bars drop and a thunder rumble follows after a distance delay\n- Sound (Web Audio, started only by a gesture): filtered-noise rain bed, panned brown-noise thunder with crack and rumble modulation, and a neon buzz synced to the failing sign; the SOUND button or M key mutes it\n- RAIN button or R key cycles Drizzle / Downpour / Monsoon, which changes drop density, ripple strength, the precip readout and the rain audio level\n- HUD shows live local time, drifting temperature, precipitation and animated signal bars; hover the ticker to pause it\n- prefers-reduced-motion: less and slower rain, steady neon, no ambient flashes, one soft flash per strike, slower hologram and a near-still ticker; rendering pauses and audio suspends while the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"44ab361d1c9f709d445c0a069d022ab760fe34a149ccc3b8a493ed4724dea10f","name":"霓虹雨夜","category":"生成艺术","description":"四层城市天际线、闪烁招牌、雨幕与湿地倒影构成一座虚构夜市。光线和视差让整条街道保持流动。","interaction":"移动指针改变视角，点击街道召唤闪电；可切换雨量与声音。"},{"id":"003","slug":"003-swiss-poster-machine","title":"Raster — Swiss Poster Machine","file":"003-swiss-poster-machine.html","textFile":"003-swiss-poster-machine.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/003-swiss-poster-machine.jpg","prompt":"Build \"RASTER — Swiss Poster Machine\", an homage to the International Typographic Style. The page is a single poster on an off-white (#f2efe8) sheet set on a strict 12-column modular grid (revealable with the G key or a toggle that shows hairline cyan grid lines). Palette strictly signal red #e30613, black #111 and white. A heavy grotesque sans stack (\"Helvetica Neue\", Helvetica, Arial, \"Nimbus Sans\", sans-serif) used with extreme scale contrast: a giant numeral or word, small flush-left body text, rotated captions. Geometric elements (circles, bars, quarter-arcs) occupy grid cells. Every click (or Space) recomposes the poster from a new seed — elements slide and rotate to new cells with crisp eased FLIP-style transitions, never overlapping text illegibly. Show the seed as an edition mark (\"No. 0417\"). A small control strip: previous/next composition, grid toggle, invert (black ground). It must look like a museum-quality, Müller-Brockmann-inspired print at any viewport, reflowing the grid for portrait phones.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"RASTER is a poster machine that sets one Weltformat-style sheet on off-white paper (#f2efe8) using only signal red #e30613, black #111 and white. The sheet is built on a live modular grid: 12 columns in landscape and 6 columns on portrait phones, with rows derived so that modules stay nearly square. Every composition comes from a four-digit edition number that doubles as the seed. It picks a zone split, where the display type sits beside or above a column of small type, and one of seven motifs: Zahl (a numeral as tall as the zone with an overprinted red sun), Drei Sprachen (one word stacked in German, French and Italian), Bogen (concentric quarter arcs in the manner of the Beethoven posters), Kreis (disc, moon and a word across the base), Takt (a time signature above bars), Senkrecht (a word climbing a strip beside a halftone field of dots) and Diagonale (a band of rotated bars). Small type is set flush left with its cap height hung from the grid line. It includes a bilingual headline, a red date (sometimes knocked out of a red panel), ragged body columns, an edition mark (\"No. 0417\") and a rotated imprint caption that says the programme is fictional.","techniques":"- Grid geometry is computed at runtime (margin, gutter, column and row modules), and every element is placed in module units. The layout reflows from 12 to 6 columns for portrait phones.\n- Giant type is fitted and aligned by its real ink bounds, measured with canvas actualBoundingBox and fontBoundingBox metrics. A numeral sits exactly on the grid line even when it is rotated −90°.\n- Shapes and display type use mix-blend-mode: multiply, like overprinting. Red under black stays black, and red over red deepens into a darker red ring. Invert mode switches to screen blending on a black ground.\n- The geometry is pure CSS. Discs and rings use border-radius. Quarter discs and quarter rings use a single rounded corner with only the top and left borders. Bars are plain divs rotated about their centre.\n- A hand-written tween engine (requestAnimationFrame and transform-only writes) animates a pool of about 95 persistent elements. Shapes slide, rescale and turn by the shortest angle to their new cells. Text whose content changes leaves a fading ghost behind and wipes in with an animated clip-path polygon. Interrupted animations resume from their current visual state.\n- The seeded PRNG is mulberry-style. Dates are real calendar dates in 2027, with the weekday computed, so every sheet is internally consistent.\n- The construction grid overlay uses cyan hairline column bands and row bands that draw in with staggered scale transitions. It also shows column numbers and row letters, crop marks, a spec line (modules, gutter, margin) and a live crosshair that reads out \"COL 7 · ROW C\".\n- A paper tooth is a noise tile generated once and repeated across a DPR-aware canvas with a multiply blend.\n- The edition counter in the control strip is a four-digit mechanical odometer with rolling digit columns.","originalInteraction":"- Clicking or tapping the sheet, or pressing Space, Enter or →, moves to the next edition. ← or Shift+Space goes back, and R jumps to a random edition. Each change recomposes with eased slide, turn and wipe transitions.\n- The G key or the Grid button toggles the cyan construction grid. While it is shown, the pointer drives a crosshair with a column and row readout.\n- The I key or the Invert button switches to a black ground. Colours cross-fade and the blend modes flip.\n- The control strip holds previous and next buttons, the odometer edition number and the toggle buttons, with aria-pressed state. Focus-visible rings are red.\n- The edition is written to the URL hash (#0417) so a sheet can be revisited. The last edition and the grid and invert preferences are kept in guarded localStorage.\n- Resizing re-lays the grid instantly, including phone portrait reflow. Reduced-motion users get instant recomposition. A polite live region announces each new edition, its motif and its word. The print stylesheet prints the bare poster.","sourceHash":"35167a4db4d6cf102a7229b07bc41f2256a55fec7e0ebea92ee0a0db39087c29","name":"瑞士海报机器","category":"排版实验","description":"红、黑、白与模块网格构成不断重组的瑞士风格海报。每个版本编号都是种子，决定文字、图形和留白的位置。","interaction":"点击画面或按空格重排；左右键切换版本，G 显示网格，I 反转底色。"},{"id":"004","slug":"004-brass-orrery","title":"The Clockmaker's Orrery","file":"004-brass-orrery.html","textFile":"004-brass-orrery.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/004-brass-orrery.jpg","prompt":"Create \"The Clockmaker's Orrery\", an interactive mechanical model of the solar system in the style of an 18th-century brass instrument. Parchment background (#efe3c6 with a subtle radial vignette and paper fibres from layered gradients), deep ink lines (#2b1d0e), and brass/gold metallic gradients (#b8860b → #f3d27a → #7a5410) for arms, rings and gears. Centre: a glowing sun; eight planets on brass arms orbiting at correct relative orbital periods (Mercury 0.241 y … Neptune 164.8 y) with log-compressed distances; an engraved zodiac ring with degree ticks around the edge; interlocking gears in a corner that rotate in sync with simulated time. Typography: engraved-looking serif small caps (\"Iowan Old Style\", \"Palatino Linotype\", Palatino, \"P052\", Georgia, serif) with generous letter-spacing. UI: a brass-styled readout of the simulated date, a speed dial (pause, 1 day/s, 1 month/s, 1 year/s), and click-a-planet to open an ornate cartouche with its name, period and a short engraved description. Delight: optional tiny ticking sound (Web Audio, after a gesture) and hover glints on the brass. SVG for the instrument, requestAnimationFrame for motion.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"The Clockmaker's Orrery\" is an imagined 1774 brass instrument laid out as a museum plate on aged parchment: an engraved title and gear train on the left, the orrery itself filling the centre, and a column of screwed-down brass plates on the right. Eight painted globes ride on tapered brass arms around a glowing, slowly turning Sun. Each arm stands at its planet's real heliocentric longitude for the simulated date, reckoned from J2000 mean orbital elements plus the equation of the centre, and each moves at its true orbital period. Distances are log-compressed. Around the planets runs a silvered kalendar ring, with day ticks and month names placed where the Earth stands on each date, and a brass zodiac ring engraved with Latin sign names, degree ticks and numerals. A dotted index hand from the Earth arm points at today's date on that ring. The Earth carries a tiny Moon on its own sub-arm. In the corner, a hand-cranked annual gear train (48/16/32/24 teeth, phase-correct meshing) and an oscillating balance wheel turn with simulated time.","techniques":"- Parchment built only from layered gradients: a radial vignette, crossed hairline \"fibre\" repeats, faint foxing spots and a warm central bloom\n- Zodiac ring in CSS conic-gradient brass, masked to an annulus with a radial mask. Its sheen angle follows the pointer (hover glint), and inset and outer box-shadows lift it off the plate\n- Engraving drawn twice in SVG, once as a pale offset highlight and once in ink, so the lines read as cut grooves: 360 degree ticks, sign boundaries, text on textPath for sign and month names, rotated numerals, lozenge flourishes and a curved Latin motto\n- Kalendar ring computed from the Earth's longitude on every day of the current year, so each month label sits exactly where the Earth arm points on that date\n- Globes use radial gradients with a sun-facing highlight plus a terminator overlay in each arm's rotating frame, so the lit side always faces the Sun. Top-down details include Jupiter's concentric belts and red spot, polar caps on Earth and Mars, and Saturn's multi-band ring counter-rotated to stay fixed in space\n- A separate shadow layer, offset in screen space and deeper for lower arms, gives a stacked 3D feel to the arm heights\n- Procedural gear teeth, spoke windows and axle holes built as one even-odd path per wheel. Wheels are filled with a repeating radial \"engine-turned\" brass gradient that does not change as they rotate. Meshing phases are derived from pitch angles so the teeth genuinely interlock\n- A balance wheel with a hairspring and a jewelled cock swings on a decaying amplitude that dies away when time is halted\n- Four-point star glints spawned via the Web Animations API wherever the pointer crosses brass. Brass HTML plates get a sweeping specular band and slotted screws drawn with gradients\n- An ornate cartouche dialog with engraved corner scrollwork, a crest, a rubricated drop cap and a painted portrait globe","originalInteraction":"- The simulation runs at one day per second from the real current date, opening with a 1.3 s \"setting\" sweep that fans the arms out from 0° to their true places\n- The Rate of the Heavens dial is a knurled brass knob with four detents (Halt, Day, Month, Year). Click the knob to cycle, click a label, or use the arrow keys in the radiogroup. The rate eases toward its target so the machine spins up and down with inertia\n- Click, tap or focus a planet (or its row in the Index of Wanderers) and press Enter to open its cartouche. It shows period, distance, diameter, day length, a live heliocentric place and an accurate short history. The orbit is highlighted. Close with the ✕, Esc, or a click on the plate\n- The index lists each planet's live zodiacal place and period. The date plate shows the date, Roman-numeral year, weekday, Julian Day and the Sun's sign\n- Drag the ebony crank of the great wheel to wind time forward or back: one turn is one year. With the crank focused, arrow keys step a week, Page Up and Page Down step a year, and Home returns to now\n- \"Today\" winds the instrument back to the present with an eased sweep. Clicking the Sun sets off a solar flare pulse (and a soft chime when sound is on)\n- The optional escapement tick is synthesized with Web Audio (a band-passed noise click plus a sine body) and synced to the balance wheel. It is off until you press the Ticking toggle\n- prefers-reduced-motion starts the instrument halted and skips the intro sweep, glints, sheen motion and ray rotation. Planet labels hide on small screens except for the selected planet","sourceHash":"1817d9b1cce724106e12d171700f302a29fa2ed23480cd66b9a18e4e2e2726aa","name":"钟表匠的太阳系","category":"科学与模拟","description":"黄铜行星仪如同一件陈列于旧羊皮纸上的机械藏品。行星沿金属臂运行，刻度与齿轮共同呈现天体运动。","interaction":"切换时间速度，点选行星查看信息，观察不同轨道周期。"},{"id":"005","slug":"005-sumi-ink-in-water","title":"Sumi — Ink in Water","file":"005-sumi-ink-in-water.html","textFile":"005-sumi-ink-in-water.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/005-sumi-ink-in-water.jpg","prompt":"Make \"Sumi — Ink in Water\", a meditative fluid simulation. A full-screen canvas showing warm washi paper (#f4efe6 with procedurally generated fibre noise). Clicking or tapping drops ink that blooms, curls and diffuses like sumi ink in water — implement a small grid-based stable-fluids solver (advection, diffusion, pressure projection) at reduced resolution with smooth upscaling, or an equivalent dye-advection approach; dragging the pointer stirs the water. Default ink is deep sumi black-indigo (#1b1f2a); a minimal vertical toolbar on the right, set in a quiet serif, switches to indigo (#2c3e75), vermillion (#d23c26) or pale gold (#b89b5e). A red hanko seal in the corner (SVG, carved-looking characters approximated with geometric strokes) signs the piece; a \"Still the water\" button slowly fades the ink away. Typography: an elegant vertical title using writing-mode: vertical-rl. Delight: an occasional falling ink droplet when idle; ink leaves faint tidelines at its edges. Must run smoothly on laptops and lower its resolution on mobile.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Sumi — Ink in Water is a quiet, full-screen ink painting that you make by letting drops fall into water over handmade washi. A small stable-fluids solver runs underneath. Each drop blooms as a feathered source whose ink thins as it empties, which leaves dark rims and paler centres. Its advancing front sheds irregular vortex pairs that curl into mushroom caps and smoke-like filaments. Dragging stirs the water, and a very slow divergence-free current keeps everything drifting gently. The ink is rendered as Beer–Lambert absorbance, multiplied over procedurally generated paper that has formation clouds, kozo fibres, bark flecks, grain and a deckle shadow, so overlapping colours mix the way pigments do. Thin tidelines form where a front thins out, and the paper keeps a faint stain of where the ink lingered. The page is framed like a hanging scroll. An upright vertical title \"SUMI\" sits over a sideways italic subtitle and a whispered verse. A carved red hanko reading 水月 (suigetsu, \"moon in water\") signs the lower-left corner beside a small colophon. A minimal serif toolbar on the right offers the four inks, \"Still the water\" and a sound toggle.","techniques":"- Grid-based stable fluids (Stam): semi-Lagrangian self-advection, implicit Gauss–Seidel viscosity, pressure projection with a warm-started Poisson solve, vorticity confinement and a breathing stream-function current; it runs at reduced resolution (about 144×90 on a laptop, about 49×105 on a phone)\n- Dye carried on a 2× finer grid with MacCormack advection plus a min/max limiter, so thin filaments stay sharp; slow explicit diffusion feathers the pigment\n- Ink drops modelled as spreading sources: an irregular angular \"finger\" profile, a drifting and pulsing core, and an irregular vortex-shedding ring at the front, plus stray eddies from the impact\n- Three-channel absorbance dye (Beer–Lambert, derived from each ink hex against the paper #f4efe6), upsampled in absorbance space before a lookup-table exp, then composited onto the paper with mix-blend-mode: multiply\n- Tidelines drawn as a gradient-weighted iso-line near the thin edge of each stain; a persistent stain buffer records where fronts lingered; static paper-sizing noise modulates how strongly each spot takes the ink\n- Procedural washi on its own canvas: two octaves of soft formation clouds, a fine grain tile, about 1,500 translucent kozo fibres as quadratic curves, bark flecks and a vignette\n- Overlay canvas for water ripples with meniscus highlights, a falling droplet that shrinks with a sharpening shadow, and a brush-tip cursor ring in the current ink colour\n- SVG hanko in haku-bun style: strokes cut from a mask, then roughened and worn by feTurbulence/feDisplacementMap and a noise alpha mask\n- Vertical typography with writing-mode: vertical-rl: an upright title, a sideways italic subtitle and a letter-spaced verse column","originalInteraction":"- Click or tap to drop the current ink, with a ripple and a soft synthesized plink; hold still to keep pouring, so the bloom grows; drag to stir the water\n- Toolbar: Sumi, Indigo (ai), Vermillion (shu) and Pale gold (kin), with aria-pressed state; \"Still the water\" fades the ink, the stains and the currents away over a few seconds\n- When idle, a droplet occasionally falls on its own, with its shadow converging before the splash\n- Keyboard: Space/Enter on the focused water drops ink in a random spot; 1–4 choose the ink; S stills the water; M toggles sound\n- Sound is Web Audio only, created on the first gesture: a rising sine plink with stereo pan and a low exhale, through a filtered feedback echo. It has a visible toggle, and the preference is stored in guarded localStorage\n- Adaptive quality lowers the solver grid if frames run heavy; resizing resamples the painting so it survives; the loop pauses while the tab is hidden; reduced motion slows the flow and turns off idle drops, ripples and the opening overture","sourceHash":"54c63a9a0d8c057bcc6199edc9e6be8507689e2032c14a93f68279187d130312","name":"水中墨迹","category":"生成艺术","description":"墨滴在和纸上的水层里扩散，边缘卷成细密涡流。浓淡、沉积与水流由流体模拟共同塑造。","interaction":"点击滴墨，按住持续注入，拖动搅动水流；切换墨色或让水面归于平静。"},{"id":"006","slug":"006-brutalist-zine","title":"Loud House Records — Issue 07","file":"006-brutalist-zine.html","textFile":"006-brutalist-zine.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/006-brutalist-zine.jpg","prompt":"Design \"LOUD HOUSE RECORDS — Issue 07\", a neo-brutalist web zine for a fictional independent record label. Raw, confident and loud: flat saturated colours (cobalt #1d3cff, electric yellow #ffe600, black #000, off-white #f7f5f0, one hit of hot pink #ff3ea5), 3–4px black borders everywhere, hard offset shadows (8px 8px 0 #000) with no blur, oversized uppercase grotesk headlines, monospace metadata, and a deliberately collaged layout on a visible grid. Sections: a massive masthead with a marquee strip of release names; a \"NEW RELEASES\" grid of album cards whose covers are generated geometric CSS/SVG art, unique per card; a tracklist table; a tour-dates list with rotated SOLD OUT stamps; and a sign-up box. Interaction: cards physically \"press down\" on hover/click (shadow collapses, element translates); a sticker you can drag anywhere; a \"SHUFFLE COVERS\" button that re-rolls the cover art; and a \"preview\" button that plays a short synthesized drum loop (Web Audio, after a gesture). Must remain highly legible and responsive.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A neo-brutalist web zine for the fictional label Loud House Records, laid out like a photocopied fanzine pasted onto a visible 32px grid with a numbered 12-column ruler. The masthead collages a giant black \"LOUD\" (with a pink stroked O that jumps on every kick drum), a tilted cobalt \"HOUSE\" slab and a yellow \"RECORDS\" block, all with 4px black borders and hard 8px offset shadows, beside a spinning \"Issue 07\" roundel, an editor's note and a contents index. Two crossing marquee strips carry the release names; below come six record cards with procedurally generated geometric sleeves, a sticky tracklist with per-track tempos, a yellow tour spread with rubber-stamped SOLD OUT marks, a pink/black sign-up spread and a black back cover. The palette is strictly cobalt #1d3cff, yellow #ffe600, black, off-white #f7f5f0 and one hit of pink #ff3ea5; type is an oversized heavy grotesk stack against uppercase monospace metadata.","techniques":"- CSS background grid (two 1px linear-gradients) plus a 12-column ruler strip and registration-style collage elements: halftone dot panels (radial-gradient), masking-tape strips, slight per-card rotations driven by a --r custom property\n- Ten seeded SVG cover generators (bullseye, rotated bands, Bauhaus quarter-circle tiles, checkerboard + circle, chevron zigzags, halftone field, giant catalogue numeral, staircase, sine waves, sunburst) drawing only from the five-colour palette with contrast rules; each sleeve carries a catalogue label and a vertical \"SLEEVE STYLE · 0xSEED\" readout\n- A CSS vinyl record (repeating-radial-gradient grooves, conic sheen, coloured label) hidden behind each sleeve that slides out and spins on hover or while previewing\n- Hard, blur-free box-shadows that collapse when elements are pressed (translate + shadow to 0) for cards, buttons, picker thumbnails and chips\n- Rubber-stamp SOLD OUT marks: double border via border + outline, rotated, multiply-blended and eroded by an inline SVG filter (feTurbulence thresholded into ink holes + slight displacement), slammed in with an overshoot keyframe when scrolled into view\n- Stroked outline display words (-webkit-text-stroke with a solid fallback), a rotating SVG textPath roundel, a CSS-only dual marquee (duplicated track, translateX -50%)\n- A draggable SVG starburst sticker living in a clipped full-page layer so it can never cause horizontal scroll\n- Web Audio drum machine: synthesized kick (pitch-swept sine + click), snare (noise + triangle body), 3-burst clap, closed/open hats (high-passed noise), rim and a filtered saw bassline, scheduled with a look-ahead clock and per-release 16-step patterns with swing","originalInteraction":"- Cards physically press down: hover shifts them 3px and shrinks the shadow, pointer-down drives them flush to the page; clicking a card (or Enter/Space when focused) loads its tracklist\n- \"Shuffle covers\" (or the S key) re-rolls every sleeve with new seeds and styles, flipping each sleeve in 3D with a stagger and bumping the \"Pressing #\" counter; the tracklist art, picker thumbnails and player update to match\n- \"Preview\" plays an 8-bar synthesized drum loop in that release's tempo and pattern; the floating player shows the sleeve, a 16-step LED sequencer lit in time with the audio clock, a progress bar, mute and stop; the playing card's sleeve, the Issue 07 roundel and the pink O thump on every kick\n- Tracklist: six sleeve thumbnails switch releases; every row has its own ▶ that plays the loop at that track's BPM; totals and runtime are computed from the track lengths\n- The PLAY IT LOUD! sticker drags anywhere with mouse or touch (it leans with the drag velocity and slaps down with a sound); arrow keys move it, Enter/double-click re-angles it; its position persists in localStorage (guarded)\n- Sign-up form validates the email, shakes on error, and on success slams a YOU'RE IN! stamp (nothing is sent; only a flag is stored); genre chips are real checkboxes\n- Global SOUND ON/OFF toggle in the top bar (persisted), Esc stops a preview, audio stops when the tab is hidden; ticket buttons answer with a toast; reduced-motion removes marquees, spins and stamp animations","sourceHash":"3d6597ee4b099f706042689cfaea492f09837ac97f30440950a864ae19e94cb2","name":"独立唱片小报","category":"交互网页","description":"粗黑边框、硬阴影、拼贴标题与滚动条组成一份虚构唱片厂牌小报，带着复印纸和地下音乐刊物的气质。","interaction":"点选唱片查看曲目；使用 Shuffle covers 重新生成封面。"},{"id":"007","slug":"007-neumorphic-synth","title":"Soft Machine","file":"007-neumorphic-synth.html","textFile":"007-neumorphic-synth.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/007-neumorphic-synth.jpg","prompt":"Create \"SOFT MACHINE\", a playable neumorphic synthesizer. Everything is sculpted from one soft clay surface (#e4e8ee) with paired light/dark shadows (#ffffff / #b8bec7), raised and pressed states, and a single accent — warm coral #ff6b5a — for active LEDs and the waveform. Layout: a device centred on the page with an OLED-like inset screen showing a live oscilloscope (AnalyserNode drawn on canvas); four rotary knobs (cutoff, resonance, attack, release) dragged vertically; a waveform selector (sine/triangle/saw/square) as neumorphic segmented buttons; a 16-step sequencer row of soft pads; and a two-octave keyboard of soft keys. Typography: a rounded sans stack (\"SF Pro Rounded\", Nunito, \"Varela Round\", \"Segoe UI\", system-ui, sans-serif) with small-caps labels in muted slate. Interaction: click/touch keys or use computer keys A–K to play (Web Audio oscillator → filter → envelope); knobs update the sound in real time with smooth value tooltips; the sequencer plays/stops with adjustable tempo. Audio starts only after the first gesture. Responsive: the device scales down and the keyboard becomes compact on phones without page overflow.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"SOFT MACHINE (model SM-16, a fictional instrument) is a fully playable polyphonic synthesizer sculpted as a single slab of soft clay (#e4e8ee) floating on a matching clay page. Every control is raised out of or pressed into that one surface with paired #ffffff / #b8bec7 shadows, and the only colour anywhere is warm coral #ff6b5a, used for LEDs, knob value arcs, the tempo fill and the glowing oscilloscope trace. An OLED-like inset screen dominates the upper left: before any sound it shows a live, band-limited preview of the chosen waveform as it would leave the filter (Fourier series times a two-pole low-pass response, so resonance produces real ringing), then switches to the actual AnalyserNode signal once you play. Four rotary knobs (cutoff, resonance, attack, release), a four-way waveform selector, a play/tempo transport, a 16-step sequencer in four grouped trays and a two-octave soft keyboard complete the device. Engraved small-caps labels in muted slate, screws in the corners, a speaker grille and a power LED that flickers with the output level give it the feel of a real object.","techniques":"- Neumorphism from one palette only: raised states use paired outer shadows, pressed states use paired inset shadows, and every control (knobs, segmented buttons, pads, keys, chips, slider) animates between the two\n- Oscilloscope canvas sized to the container x devicePixelRatio (max 2) with a pre-rendered dot grid, a zero-crossing trigger for a stable live trace, and a three-pass coral glow stroke instead of expensive shadowBlur\n- Idle \"preview\" trace computed additively from 48 harmonics of the selected waveform shaped by the current cutoff/Q, so turning the knobs visibly changes the wave even before audio starts\n- Knobs built from inline SVG: an engraved groove, a highlight ring, eleven tick marks and a coral value arc driven by stroke-dasharray on a pathLength-normalised circle; the cap is a concave dish rotated through 270 degrees\n- Floating clay tooltips that rise and fade on hover, drag and keyboard focus, showing formatted values (Hz/kHz, Q, ms/s)\n- Engraved wordmark via light/dark text-shadow, CSS-only speaker grille, screw heads with rotated slots, and a procedural film-grain canvas behind the whole page\n- Sequencer playhead lit sample-accurately from a ring buffer of scheduled audio times, with a pulse ring for the current step and a dashed \"listening\" ring for an armed step\n- Phone layout: screen stacks above a single row of four knobs, the sequencer folds into 8 x 2 and the keyboard rebuilds itself as one compact octave","originalInteraction":"- Play the keyboard with mouse or touch (glissando by sliding across keys) or with the computer keys A W S E D F T G Y H U J K (O L P ; extend upward); Z / X or the - / + chips shift the octave\n- Sound path per voice: oscillator -> resonant low-pass (with a short filter pluck) -> attack/release amplitude envelope -> darkened echo -> compressor -> analyser -> master; polyphonic, voices cleaned up when they end\n- Knobs: drag vertically (Shift for fine control), mouse wheel, arrow/Page/Home/End keys when focused, double-click to reset; changes are applied to sounding voices in real time\n- Waveform selector is a radio group (click or arrow keys) and retunes playing voices instantly\n- Sequencer: play/stop button or Space; tempo slider 60-180 BPM; tap a pad to light it and it \"listens\", then play any key to give that step its note; Shuffle writes a new A-minor-pentatonic phrase, Clear empties the pattern\n- Audio context is created only on the first gesture; the speaker button mutes/unmutes with a short fade while the scope keeps drawing\n- Settings, pattern, tempo and octave persist in localStorage (guarded with try/catch); the sequencer stops and held notes release when the tab is hidden; reduced-motion users get a still preview trace and no pulsing","sourceHash":"9a6980a53ebd51892de4f392d6004cb38fffd804b1c8d91ef4c9ebb1387fd22b","name":"柔软合成器","category":"音乐与游戏","description":"一台像从软陶里雕出的复音合成器。旋钮、琴键、示波器与音序器嵌在同一块浅灰表面中。","interaction":"点击琴键或使用 A W S E 等键演奏；调整旋钮、八度与节拍。"},{"id":"008","slug":"008-lighthouse-longform","title":"The Last Keepers","file":"008-lighthouse-longform.html","textFile":"008-lighthouse-longform.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/008-lighthouse-longform.jpg","prompt":"Design a long-read editorial feature: \"The Last Keepers — Life at the Edge of the Light\", a fictional magazine story about lighthouse keepers. Write original, evocative literary prose (~900 words), impressionistic rather than presenting invented facts as real history. Typography-led: a classic book serif (\"Iowan Old Style\", \"Palatino Linotype\", Palatino, \"P052\", Georgia, serif) for body text at a comfortable measure, a large Didone-feeling headline, an ornamental drop cap, small-caps bylines, pull quotes that break the column, and marginal notes on wide screens. Palette: cream paper #f6f1e7, ink navy #14213d, signal red #c0392b, fog grey. Hero: a full-bleed SVG illustration of a lighthouse on rocks at dusk with a rotating light beam that sweeps across the headline and a gently animated sea. Scroll storytelling: sections fade and rise in; a reading-progress bar styled as a lighthouse beam; a section where the background shifts from dusk to night as you scroll; and an illustrated \"anatomy of a lens\" figure (Fresnel lens rings) that assembles on scroll. Footnotes pop up on click. Must read beautifully on phones.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"The Last Keepers\" is a long-read feature from the fictional Tidewater Quarterly. It is typography-led: Iowan Old Style / Palatino body text at a ~62-character measure on cream paper (#f6f1e7), ink-navy (#14213d) type, signal-red (#c0392b) accents and fog-grey rules. The first frame is a full-bleed SVG dusk seascape with a red-and-white banded lighthouse on basalt, a cottage with woodsmoke, a steamer on the horizon, gulls, drifting fog and a live sea. Its rotating lamp throws two beams, and the light really lands on the headline: a lit copy of the Didone headline is clipped to the beam's shape every frame, so the letters glow wherever the beam crosses them. The original ~900-word impressionistic story then runs through four chapters: an ornamental boxed drop cap, small-caps bylines and run-ins, pull quotes that break out of the column, and marginal notes that float into the right margin on wide screens. \"The Watch\" is a sticky scene that turns from dusk to night as you scroll, with a keeper's clock and log. \"The Lens\" is an engraved-style plate of a Fresnel lens, in section and elevation, that assembles itself as you scroll. The page closes with endnotes, a note that the story is fiction, and a navy colophon.","techniques":"- Hand-built inline SVG dusk scene (sky and sea gradients, sun glow, banded tower with directional shading, lantern halo, rocks with rim light, cottage, steamer, gulls, foam), re-framed in JS for every aspect ratio. On portrait screens the camera pulls back into an extended sky so the tower sits small at the right and clears the headline.\n- The beam is modelled as a projected rotation: each of the two opposite beams has a length of |cos θ|, gets brighter as it turns toward the viewer, and a flare plus a full-hero light wash fire when a beam points straight at you.\n- The beam lights the headline through two duplicate headline layers (core and penumbra), each with a per-frame CSS clip-path polygon that matches the beam cone in the headline's own coordinates.\n- The sea is seven rAF-animated wave-crest paths (sums of sines with perspective-scaled amplitude and wavelength) with moving dash patterns for glinting crests, plus a foreground swell, shimmering sun-glitter bars and pulsing foam.\n- The reading-progress bar is a lighthouse beam: a small SVG lighthouse in the navy masthead throws a widening clip-path cone, with a glowing tip and a red hairline that grows with progress. The current chapter name appears at the right.\n- Dusk to night: a sticky 100vh sky whose three gradient stops, sea colour and cloud tint are interpolated from scroll progress. Stars fade in, a moon rises with glitter on the water, a distant lighthouse turns on the horizon, and a Didone clock runs from 19:40 to 04:40.\n- The Fresnel plate is two SVGs (section and elevation). Prisms, rings, frame and lamp fly in with back-eased translate, rotate and scale tied to scroll position. Red rays draw in with pathLength dash offsets, then lettered callouts and a \"level beam\" wash fade in.\n- Editorial details: a boxed engraved drop cap with conic-gradient light rays and a red corner, Roman chapter numerals in italic Didone, small-caps run-ins, hanging red quotation marks, a diamond ornament on pull-quote rules, wave-edged transitions between paper and sky, and a canvas-painted paper grain.\n- Sections fade and rise in via IntersectionObserver. All motion pauses offscreen or when the tab is hidden. Reduced motion freezes the beam and sea and turns the reveals into plain fades.","originalInteraction":"- Scroll drives everything: the beam progress bar, the chapter label in the masthead, reveal-on-scroll, the dusk-to-night sky, clock and log, and the lens assembly (which reverses when you scroll back).\n- Footnote markers are buttons that open a small dark note card anchored under the reference (a bottom sheet on phones). It closes with the × button, a click outside or Escape, which returns focus to the marker. The endnotes list has back-links to each reference.\n- Each pull quote has a copy button that uses the clipboard with a textarea fallback (both guarded) and shows a small toast.\n- \"Begin reading\" scrolls to the standfirst. \"Return to the light\" in the colophon scrolls back to the top. The masthead brand links to the top.\n- The keyboard works throughout, with red focus-visible rings. Glyphs are aria-hidden and the prose is marked up as article, sections, asides and figures.","sourceHash":"9e684ea4ba659964fceb80e0208b49952ec1d6db06cfeb37b7778e638eb5e128","name":"最后的守塔人","category":"交互网页","description":"虚构杂志中的灯塔长篇特写。海浪、雾气和灯束铺开开场，滚动逐步带出章节、注释与灯塔结构。","interaction":"向下滚动阅读；点击脚注打开补充说明，观察天空和灯光随阅读进度变化。"},{"id":"009","slug":"009-constellation-mapper","title":"Asterism — Draw Your Own Sky","file":"009-constellation-mapper.html","textFile":"009-constellation-mapper.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/009-constellation-mapper.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Asterism — Draw Your Own Sky\", an interactive star chart where you invent constellations. A deep indigo night gradient (#060818 → #141a3d) with a procedurally placed star field (varying magnitude, subtle colour temperature from blue-white to amber, twinkle), a faint Milky Way band made of clustered noise particles, and fine gold line work (#e8c872) in the style of antique celestial atlases. Click one star and then another to draw gold connecting lines; when you close a shape (or press \"Name it\"), the constellation receives a generated mythic name (combined from curated word lists, e.g. \"The Weeping Heron\") and an engraved label with a faint illustrated halo appears. A side panel (glass, collapsible on mobile) lists your constellations; hovering highlights them. Also: a slowly rotating sky (drag to rotate), a toggle for a coordinate grid of curved RA/Dec-style lines, \"Clear sky\" and \"Save as PNG\". Constellations persist in localStorage. Typography: elegant serif capitals with wide tracking and italic Latin-flavoured subtitles.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Asterism is an antique celestial atlas that you engrave yourself. A full-bleed canvas shows a deep indigo night (#060818 to #141a3d) under an aquatint grain, with about 4,300 seeded stars on a real celestial sphere: a logarithmic magnitude distribution, colour temperatures from blue-white to amber, gentle twinkle and diffraction glints on the brightest. A Milky Way of clustered noise particles follows the true galactic plane, with a dark rift toward the galactic centre. A stereographic projection lets the sky turn slowly about the pole, and you can drag it anywhere. Click one star, then another, and fine gold (#e8c872) line work is drawn between them. Each line stops just short of its stars, as the old plates did. Closing a figure (or pressing Name it) gives it a mythic English name and a true Latin participle subtitle (\"The Weeping Heron / Ardea Lacrimans\"). The figure then inks itself in edge by edge, gains an engraved cartouche label in widely tracked serif capitals, and is wrapped in a rounded-hull halo of dotted outline and breathing burin hatching. A thin gold double frame with corner ornaments and a degree-tick ruler holds the page. A glass catalogue panel lists every figure with Roman numerals.","techniques":"- Real spherical geometry: stars are unit vectors with a stereographic projection about a moving view centre (right ascension and declination), so rotation turns the sky about the celestial pole and grid lines curve correctly\n- Seeded PRNG (mulberry32), so the same sky comes back on every visit and constellations persist as star indices\n- Magnitude drawn from a logarithmic distribution; colour temperature interpolated across five stops; pre-rendered tinted glow sprites for bright stars; cross glints on stars brighter than mag 1.2\n- Milky Way: about 2,300 additive ('lighter') soft blobs plus 3,600 dust points, sampled along the real J2000 galactic plane, thicker toward the galactic centre, clumped by a trigonometric noise field and cut by a Great-Rift-style dark lane\n- Coordinate grid (toggle): true RA meridians every hour and declination parallels every 15°, an emphasised celestial equator, a dashed ecliptic inclined 23.44°, hour and degree labels, and pole markers\n- Constellation halo: the convex hull of the figure (monotone chain), grown by a radius into a rounded \"cloud\", filled with a faint radial gold wash, edged with a dotted engraving and arc-length-spaced hatching whose stroke lengths ripple slowly\n- Line work in two passes (soft glow and a crisp 1.15px stroke) with gaps at each star and small gold rings on member stars; new figures ink in edge by edge\n- Engraved labels: per-character letter-spaced serif capitals, diamond-tipped flourish rules, an italic Latin subtitle, and greedy collision avoidance that tries positions below and above the halo while staying clear of the toolbar and panel\n- Pre-rendered background canvas (gradient, vignette, aquatint grain); occasional shooting star; a live AR/Decl. readout of the chart centre\n- Glass panel with backdrop blur, a double inner rule, and a CSS repeating-gradient tick ruler on the frame","originalInteraction":"- Click or tap a star to start a figure, then click more stars to draw gold lines; a dashed rubber-band line follows the pointer and snaps to the hovered star\n- Selecting an already linked star branches the figure; returning to the first star (after 2+ lines) closes the shape and names it automatically; \"Name it\" (or Enter) names an open figure\n- Hovering a star shows a rotating reticle and its fictional catalogue number and magnitude; hovering a named figure on the sky lights it and its catalogue entry\n- Drag (mouse or touch) to rotate the sky, with inertia; the wheel zooms; arrow keys turn the chart; the Rotate toggle pauses the slow westward drift\n- Catalogue panel: hovering or focusing an entry lights that constellation and dims the others; selecting it flies the sky to centre that figure; × removes it\n- Undo (Backspace or Ctrl/Cmd+Z) removes the last line, or the last named figure when no figure is open; Esc sets an open figure aside\n- Grid (button or G) shows the RA/Dec grid, the ecliptic and the poles; Clear sky asks you to confirm with a second press\n- Save PNG exports the chart with an engraved title cartouche\n- Figures and the grid preference persist in localStorage (guarded); a first visit is seeded with three example figures\n- The panel collapses into a \"Catalogue\" pill on phones; reduced motion stops the drift, twinkle mostly settles and reveals appear at once","sourceHash":"94579f1913a700f7ce88cc353f2030f5991dfb26bd8baed14749f39fa5d7758c","name":"亲手连起星空","category":"生成艺术","description":"深靛蓝星图上散布着数千颗星星，银河与细密颗粒营造古老天文图册的观感。你可以亲手绘制星座。","interaction":"依次点击星星连线，回到起点闭合图形；使用命名功能为星座起名。"},{"id":"010","slug":"010-liquid-mercury","title":"Mercury","file":"010-liquid-mercury.html","textFile":"010-liquid-mercury.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/010-liquid-mercury.jpg","prompt":"Create \"MERCURY\", a hero page for a fictional generative-audio studio whose centrepiece is a liquid chrome blob. On near-black (#050506), render a metaball cluster (canvas or SVG gooey filter) shaded as polished liquid metal — environment-map-like banding in silvers (#e8ecf1, #9aa3ad) and deep blacks with a thin iridescent rim (cyan → magenta → gold). The blobs orbit, merge and split organically; the cursor acts as a magnet, pulling a droplet that stretches and then snaps back with viscous easing. A giant wordmark \"MERCURY\" in a wide, heavy sans with a chrome gradient fill and a slow specular sweep; the blob passes behind and in front of letters (layering). Minimal navigation, a one-line manifesto, three glass \"capability\" chips, and a CTA button whose hover makes a small droplet drip from it. Interaction: pointer magnetism, click to split a droplet off, scrolling gently shrinks the blob into the corner. Tasteful, luxurious, silky 60fps; reduced motion shows a static sculpted frame.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A luxurious hero page for MERCURY, a fictional generative-audio studio. On a near-black (#050506) stage, a live WebGL fragment shader renders a cluster of liquid-chrome droplets: a heavy core lobe with two inner lobes, six orbiting satellites on tilted 3D rings, a pointer-driven tendril and click-spawned droplets. The giant wide, heavy \"MERCURY\" wordmark is drawn inside the same shader as polished chrome with a bevel, a hard horizon line and a slow specular sweep, so the metal can genuinely pass behind and in front of the letters: satellites cross in front on one side of their orbit and behind on the other, while the core drifts through the letter plane over time. Around it, a quiet editorial frame: minimal mono navigation with a sound toggle, a serif-italic one-line manifesto, three frosted-glass capability chips, a chrome CTA that drips a droplet on hover, and accurate mercury facts in the corners (Hg, 80, 200.592 u, liquid from −38.83 °C to 356.73 °C). Below the hero, a studio statement, a list of fictional \"selected pours\" and a contact block; as you scroll, the sculpture shrinks into the bottom-right corner and stays with you.","techniques":"- Single full-screen WebGL fragment shader (one triangle), sized to the viewport × devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) with a pixel budget and a conservative quality governor.\n- Droplets as a smooth union (log-sum-exp) of C1 sphere-cap height profiles: every droplet is a perfect mirror sphere, and necks/fillets form only where droplets touch, with no seams between merged lobes.\n- Analytic normals from the union's gradient, reflected into a procedural studio environment: silver sky (#e8ecf1 / #9aa3ad), a wobbling hard horizon, black floor, overhead rig band, three softboxes and dark pillars, rotated by pointer position.\n- A thin iridescent rim (cyan → magenta → gold) on the silhouette plus a 2 px outer rim glow and soft halo; crease occlusion where lobes meet; a pointer-steered specular highlight.\n- Wordmark rasterised once into a texture with three channels (crisp coverage, bevel blur, wide blur via the shadowBlur trick and additive compositing); the shader derives bevel normals, a chrome horizon gradient, iridescent bevel tint and a moving specular sweep from it.\n- Per-pixel depth compositing: each droplet carries a z value from its 3D orbit; the metal is layered over or under the letters accordingly, with cast shadows and a dark contact line so the letters stay legible over the metal.\n- Gooey SVG filter (blur + alpha threshold, composited atop) on the chrome CTA pill so its hover droplet necks off and falls.\n- Glass chips with backdrop-filter, inset highlights and an iridescent masked border on hover; an iridescent gradient accent in the manifesto; dithering to prevent banding.","originalInteraction":"- Pointer magnetism: near the sculpture, a five-bead tendril springs out toward the cursor (stiffer at the root, lazier at the tip), stretches into a neck and snaps back with underdamped, viscous spring easing; the whole cluster also leans toward the pointer.\n- Click (or tap) anywhere that isn't a control to split a droplet off: it flies toward the click, coasts with drag, is pulled back and re-absorbed. The \".\" key does the same from the keyboard.\n- Scrolling eases the sculpture down to the bottom-right corner at about a quarter of its size; clicking the corner droplet returns to the top.\n- Sound toggle (nav) and \"Hear it breathe\" button start a synthesized Web Audio drone (open fifths through a breathing low-pass with echo) whose filter follows pull and pointer speed; droplets \"plip\" when they split and \"gloop\" when absorbed. Silent until you opt in; mute anytime.\n- \"Commission a pour\" scrolls to contact; \"Copy address\" copies a non-URL address with a clipboard fallback and a toast.\n- Live readout of droplet count and pull; rendering pauses when the tab is hidden; prefers-reduced-motion shows a single composed still frame with no animation.","sourceHash":"38102404393cf1230e537181b2adc929d2db3c0cb5c886e916bc997a8e6092f6","name":"液态水银","category":"交互网页","description":"液态金属在黑色舞台中聚合、分裂，巨大银色字标与水滴共享同一光照空间。这是一家虚构声音工作室的概念首页。","interaction":"靠近金属移动指针，观察黏性牵引；点击分裂水滴，主动开启声音后体验合成音景。"},{"id":"011","slug":"011-victorian-herbarium","title":"Herbarium Imaginarium","file":"011-victorian-herbarium.html","textFile":"011-victorian-herbarium.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/011-victorian-herbarium.jpg","prompt":"Design \"Herbarium Imaginarium\", a Victorian botanical specimen cabinet of procedurally grown plants that do not exist. Aged paper (#efe6d2 with foxing spots and fibre texture from layered radial gradients), sepia ink (#4a3622), faded botanical greens (#6b7f4e, #9aa77a) and madder-red accents for seeds and berries. Each specimen card shows a plant grown by an L-system/recursive branching algorithm and drawn in SVG with ink-like stroke variation, leaves, buds and occasional flowers; a paper tape strip \"holding\" the stem; a handwritten-feeling label in an italic serif with an invented Latin binomial (generated from syllable lists), a collection date and a specimen number. A grid of six specimens on desktop, one or two on mobile. Interaction: \"Collect a new specimen\" grows a plant stroke by stroke (animated stroke-dashoffset); clicking a card opens it large in a lightbox with a magnifying-loupe hover; a \"Press\" button toggles a flattened pressed-flower look (desaturated, slightly translucent). Typography: \"Iowan Old Style\", Palatino, \"P052\", Georgia, serif, with italic labels and ornamental rules.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Herbarium Imaginarium is laid out as the index card and open drawer of a Victorian specimen cabinet. A sticky left panel carries a brass drawer-label holder, the title in Palatino-style roman and italic, an ornamental rule with a madder lozenge, a drop-capped introduction, the two actions, a dotted-leader ledger and an index of the drawer. To its right, six herbarium sheets sit in a 3 × 2 grid, sized so that the whole drawer fits one desktop screen. Each sheet is aged paper (#f6efdd with a procedural fibre tile and random foxing) carrying a plant that exists nowhere else. The plant is grown by a small parametric L-system with six growth habits (spike, much-branched shrub, umbellifer, fern, climbing vine, rosette with scapes) and inked in SVG in sepia and faded botanical greens, with madder berries and seeds. Gummed paper tape straps the stems, and every sheet has a printed-and-handwritten label with a generated Latin binomial, an accession stamp, a centimetre scale bar and sometimes a seed packet or a determination slip. Everything is clearly fictional: the plants, collectors, localities and names are all invented.","techniques":"- Parametric L-system (production rules with parameters, stochastic variants and terminal rules) followed by a turtle interpreter with tropism (upward growth, or gravity for arching fern fronds and vines), angle jitter, width taper and slightly curved (quadratic) internodes\n- Six habits with distinct grammars: racemes of nodding bells or star flowers, a fractal forking shrub with berry clusters, compound umbels of florets, bipinnate fern fronds with sori and coiled croziers, a tendrilled climber with drooping berry clusters, and a basal rosette with daisy heads or dandelion-like seed clocks\n- Leaf blades built from shape-profile functions (lanceolate, ovate, cordate, lobed, pinnatifid, orbicular, spathulate, linear, oblong pinnules), with sawtooth serration, curl, asymmetry, a petiole, a watercolour half-wash, a midrib and lateral veins\n- Ink stroke variation: tapering stems drawn segment by segment with round caps, a lighter offset highlight line on thick stems, and an SVG drop-shadow that lifts the fresh plant off the page\n- Stroke-by-stroke growth using pathLength=1 with stroke-dashoffset animations; each segment's delay comes from its distance from the root, so the ink travels at one steady speed; leaves unfurl and flowers bloom through CSS scale and rotate on nested SVG groups\n- Roots in three characters (fibrous, taproot, scaly rhizome) with clinging soil crumbs; they grow down while the shoot grows up\n- Paper: a seamless fibre texture drawn on a canvas at runtime, layered radial-gradient foxing both on the viewport and on each sheet, and inset edge toning\n- Mounting furniture drawn in SVG: zigzag-cut translucent tape with a shadow offset, a double-ruled label with dotted handwriting lines, an imperfect madder accession stamp that \"thumps\" down, a scale bar, a folded seed packet and an annotated determination slip\n- The \"pressed\" state interpolates a matching filter list (drop-shadow, saturate, sepia, contrast, brightness) plus opacity, so the plants fade into a flat, translucent, dried look while the tape and labels stay crisp\n- A FLIP reflow when a new sheet is collected: the new sheet is laid down, the others slide one place along, and the oldest is \"filed away\" as a fading ghost\n- A brass loupe (conic-gradient rim, wooden handle, glass highlight) that shows a 2.6× live clone of the vector sheet, so it stays sharp at any zoom","originalInteraction":"- \"Collect a new specimen\" (or N) grows a new random-habit plant in the first slot over about four seconds: roots and stems ink in, then leaves unfurl, flowers bloom, tape is applied and the label and stamp appear\n- Clicking a sheet, or its entry in the drawer index, opens a lightbox with the enlarged sheet and collector's notes: habit, leaves, flowers, height read from the scale bar, date, locality, collector and a whimsical field note\n- In the lightbox, hovering the sheet shows the magnifying loupe. On touch screens, pressing and dragging shows the loupe floated above the finger\n- The Previous and Next buttons or ← → turn through the sheets. Esc, the × button or a click on the backdrop closes the lightbox. Focus is trapped inside the dialog and returned when it closes\n- \"Press the sheets\" (or P, also in the lightbox) toggles the pressed look with a brief squash of every sheet, and the state is remembered in localStorage (guarded with try/catch)\n- Hovering a card straightens and lifts it. Focus rings are visible, and prefers-reduced-motion skips the growth, reflow and squash animations","sourceHash":"8e8012f38cec595f12de0cb400282b47b0aacfa6851c55d5f1ff449df93dc99b","name":"想象植物标本室","category":"生成艺术","description":"黄铜铭牌、旧纸、胶带与压制植物组成一只维多利亚式标本抽屉。程序生成的植物从根系开始逐步生长。","interaction":"点击生成新标本，观察枝叶与花朵展开；点开任意标本放大查看。"},{"id":"012","slug":"012-nimbus-weather","title":"Nimbus — Weather for Port Aurelia","file":"012-nimbus-weather.html","textFile":"012-nimbus-weather.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/012-nimbus-weather.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Nimbus\", an elegant weather dashboard for the fictional coastal city of Port Aurelia (clearly fictional sample data). The hero is an animated sky that changes with the selected condition: clear day (drifting sun glare, soft clouds), rain (parallax raindrops, darker gradient), snow (flakes with sway), thunderstorm (lightning flashes) and clear night (stars, moon). The palette adapts per condition but stays sophisticated (e.g. day #7cc4f5 → #e8f6ff, night #0b1026 → #2b2f63). Foreground glass cards: current temperature as a huge thin numeral, feels-like, humidity and UV; an animated wind compass; a sunrise/sunset arc with the sun's current position; a 24-hour temperature line chart (SVG smooth curve with gradient fill and hover tooltip); and a 7-day forecast strip with custom SVG weather icons that animate on hover. Interaction: clicking a forecast day switches the whole scene and its data with a smooth cross-fade; a °C/°F toggle. Typography: a clean sans with tabular numerals.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Nimbus is a calm, editorial weather dashboard for the fictional harbour city of Port Aurelia during an invented early-December week. A full-viewport canvas sky sits behind everything and is the hero: it renders five distinct weathers (clear day, rain, thunderstorm, snow, clear night) and cross-fades between them, while frosted-glass cards float above it. Text colour, glass tint, accent and glow are scene variables, so the whole interface re-inks itself: navy ink on the bright day and snow skies, pale ink on rain, storm and night. The current temperature is a huge hairline numeral drawn as single-stroke SVG paths that write themselves in, so it stays thin on every platform. Around it sit a conditions card (feels-like from the wind-chill formula, humidity with a Magnus dew point, UV on a WHO-coloured scale, precipitation chance), an animated wind compass, a sun-and-sea card with the sunrise/sunset arc, tide and sea temperature, a 24-hour chart, and a seven-day strip. Every figure is invented sample data and the page says so.","techniques":"- Canvas sky engine with cached offscreen sprites (procedural cumulus in six tints, sun glow, rotating ray fans, snow crystals, a moon with maria, craters and a soft gibbous terminator, a Milky Way band); each scene is drawn with its own weight during a 1.25 s eased cross-fade, and the three-stop sky gradient is interpolated per frame\n- Clear day: breathing sun glare with two counter-rotating ray layers, drifting clouds and faint hexagonal lens-flare ghosts that line up with the pointer\n- Rain and storm: three-depth parallax rain batched into one stroke per layer; storm lightning uses branched midpoint-displacement bolts with glow/core passes, a multi-pulse flicker envelope, sky flash and cloud underlighting\n- Snow: soft bokeh flakes and rotating six-armed crystals swaying on individual sine paths; night: twinkling star field, glinting bright stars, shooting stars\n- Hairline temperature numerals: hand-designed stroke glyphs (0–9, minus, degree) animated with pathLength and stroke-dashoffset\n- Scene-driven design tokens (ink, glass, accent, glow) with colour transitions; backdrop-filter glass cards with an inset highlight\n- SVG chart: Catmull-Rom to Bezier curve, a temperature-coded stroke gradient, accent area fill, precipitation bars, min/max labels and a \"now\" marker; it morphs between days\n- Wind compass with 72 generated ticks, a spring-damped needle with gust wobble, and flow streaks clipped to the dial that move downwind at a speed tied to the wind\n- Sun arc drawn along the true ellipse up to the sun's position, with a filled progress area; after sunset it shows a moon\n- Hand-built SVG weather icons (sun, rain, storm, snow, moon) that animate with CSS: spinning rays, falling drops, flickering bolt, drifting flakes, twinkling stars","originalInteraction":"- Click or tap any of the seven days to switch the whole scene: the sky cross-fades, cards fade and blur through the change, the numeral redraws, the chart morphs and the needle swings to the new wind\n- Arrow keys, Home and End move between days; a URL hash such as #wed opens a given day\n- °C/°F segmented toggle converts every temperature and switches wind and visibility to mph and miles; the choice is saved in localStorage (guarded with try/catch)\n- Hover, drag or touch the hourly chart for a tooltip with temperature and precipitation chance; it is keyboard-focusable and the arrow keys step through the hours\n- Pointer movement adds gentle parallax to clouds, rain, stars and the sun or moon\n- Animation pauses while the tab is hidden; reduced-motion mode slows the particles, softens the lightning, and turns off parallax, the numeral stroke animation and the compass streaks","sourceHash":"fd510a16f5c9c15fd2843db2d42e464fdf61a89041837bd113513ec28e4c0f91","name":"港口天气","category":"数据与工具","description":"虚构港口一周的天气被表现为一整片会变化的天空。玻璃面板、数字与图表随晴、雨、雪和夜色共同换色。","interaction":"选择日期切换天气场景，切换摄氏与华氏；也可用方向键浏览日期。"},{"id":"013","slug":"013-knitted-life","title":"Woolgathering","file":"013-knitted-life.html","textFile":"013-knitted-life.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/013-knitted-life.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Woolgathering\", Conway's Game of Life rendered as a living piece of knitting. Each cell is drawn on a canvas as a knit stitch (a small V-shaped pair of loops with shading); live cells are coloured yarn and dead cells are background yarn, so the evolving pattern reads as Fair Isle knitwear. Palette of cosy wools: oatmeal #e9dcc5 background, rust #b5502b, mustard #d9a441, forest #2f5d50, cream #f6efe2 — plus a colourway selector with four palettes. Add a subtle fuzz/halo on stitches and gentle drape shading across the fabric. Frame the canvas as if it hangs from knitting needles (SVG at the top) with yarn-ball illustrations. Warm rounded controls: play/pause, step, speed, randomise, clear, and preset patterns (glider, pulsar, Gosper glider gun, a snowflake motif). Draw cells with pointer or touch. Show the generation count as a knitted row counter. Typography: a friendly serif (\"Cooper\", Georgia, \"URW Bookman\", serif).\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Woolgathering\" presents Conway's Game of Life as a piece of Fair Isle knitting hanging from a wooden needle on an oatmeal linen wall. Every cell is a hand-shaded stockinette stitch: two twisted, slightly fuzzy yarn loops forming a V, drawn in cream ground yarn when dead and in rust, mustard or forest when alive. Rows are banded like a Fair Isle yoke, mirrored about the middle row with a second oatmeal ground behind the wide bands, and the page opens by knitting the cloth down from the needle before an eightfold snowflake motif starts to bloom symmetrically. Beside the cloth, a dark \"ball band\" label with a knitted row counter holds warm, stitched-edge controls, four colourways and the preset motifs. Three balls of wool, two crossed needles and a working strand that tugs with every row complete the still life.","techniques":"- Canvas stitch sprites pre-rendered per colour and per variant: each V is two rotated ellipses with a cross-strand gradient, tucked-end shading, diagonal ply-twist lines, a soft drop shadow and random loose fibres for the fuzzy halo\n- Shading darkens toward each palette's own warm shadow colour (not black), so cream stays warm and navy stays deep\n- Stitches are drawn bottom row first so each V overlaps the one below, as real stockinette does; small per-stitch jitter and tone variation keep it handmade\n- Mohair halo: a one-pixel-per-cell colour map, scaled with bilinear smoothing, is painted beneath the stitches (so colour shows in the gaps) and faintly above them\n- Drape: a pre-computed fold field lights one side of each vertical fold and shades the other, deepens toward the hem, shades the top under the needle and curls the selvedges; the same fold function makes the hem wave and casts a soft shadow of the whole cloth on the wall\n- Mirrored Fair Isle band layout (rust / mustard peerie / forest) around the centre row, with a second ground shade behind the forest bands\n- SVG rig laid out from the canvas geometry: a birch needle with a turned knob and taper, a loop-front pattern whose tile width equals one stitch so every loop sits on the needle, and a working-yarn Bezier that tugs taut on each generation while the big ball's wraps turn\n- Hand-drawn SVG yarn balls with clipped wrap ellipses and a spherical shading gradient, recoloured live through CSS custom properties\n- Knitted row counter: the generation number is knitted in a 3x5 stitch font between two forest border rows on its own canvas\n- Cast-on intro that knits the cloth row by row from the needle, eased births with a small overshoot \"pop\", and fading deaths\n- Stitched UI language: dashed inner \"stitching\" on buttons and on the card, a twisted-yarn range track with a yarn-ball thumb, and colourway swatches as wool balls","originalInteraction":"- Play/pause (big rust button or Space), \"Knit one row\" step (or N), pace slider from 1 to 30 rows a second\n- Draw on the fabric with mouse or touch: the first stitch decides whether the stroke knits or unpicks, and strokes are filled cell by cell along the line; hovering shows a dashed ring around the stitch\n- Keyboard drawing: focus the fabric, move a cursor with the arrow keys, toggle stitches with Enter\n- Randomise scatters a four-way mirrored motif (R); Clear (C) empties the needle\n- Preset motifs with live pictograms (keys 1 to 4): a flock of gliders knitted in the round, a period-3 pulsar, Gosper's glider gun (turned on its diagonal on narrow phones), and an eightfold snowflake\n- Four colourways (Hebridean Hearth, Shetland Sea, Heather & Peat, Midwinter Night) re-dye the stitches, yarn balls, strand, title and page; the choice is remembered in localStorage (guarded)\n- \"Knit in the round\" toggles toroidal wrapping; flat mode uses a hidden margin with an absorbing edge so gliders leave the cloth cleanly and the gun keeps firing\n- Colour by Fair Isle bands or by age (fresh mustard, established rust, old forest)\n- Optional synthesized needle clicks (Web Audio, off by default, toggle with visible state)\n- Status line reports stitches in yarn and detects still lifes and repeating periods; if left untouched after it settles, the page \"woolgathers\" and casts on a new mirrored motif\n- Honours prefers-reduced-motion (no intro, no pop, instant changes, slower default pace); animation loop idles while the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"ab560d1b0a8619343f453739c7c75ad392909eca9405a37bcdb255e7281de009","name":"编织生命游戏","category":"科学与模拟","description":"康威生命游戏被织成一块费尔岛针织布。每个细胞都是一枚有体积与毛边的针脚，图案随代际不断变化。","interaction":"播放、暂停或单步推进；直接在织物上绘制或拆除针脚，调整演化速度。"},{"id":"014","slug":"014-kinetic-manifesto","title":"MOVE — A Manifesto in Motion","file":"014-kinetic-manifesto.html","textFile":"014-kinetic-manifesto.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/014-kinetic-manifesto.jpg","prompt":"Design \"MOVE — A Manifesto in Motion\", a scroll-driven kinetic typography experience. Black background (#0a0a0a), off-white type (#f1f1ec) and one acid accent (#c6ff00). Seven original manifesto statements about motion, attention and design each get a full-screen scene with a distinct typographic motion driven by scroll progress: letters assembling from scattered positions; words stretching horizontally with scaleX; a line splitting in half to reveal an accent line; characters cascading like dominoes; a word rotating in 3D; a sentence typed out with a caret; and a giant word zooming until its counter becomes the next scene's background. Use sticky sections and custom scroll-progress mapping, with CSS transforms only for performance. Typography: a heavy grotesque/condensed sans stack for display and monospace for small annotations such as scene numbers (\"SC. 03 / 07\"). A progress rail on the side. Reduced motion: statements appear statically with simple fades.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"MOVE is a scroll-played kinetic typography manifesto in three colours only: black #0a0a0a, off-white #f1f1ec and acid #c6ff00. It opens on a film-slate title card where a hand-drawn SVG \"MOVE\" rises letter by letter, its O counter already filled with acid, above an acid ticker. Seven sticky full-screen scenes follow, each with its own original statement and its own motion driven by scroll progress: \"Nothing arrives whole.\" assembles from scattered, rotated glyphs; \"Time is material.\" stretches each word with scaleX until the three lines form a justified block (with live ×n readouts); \"Transitions are the content.\" is sliced by an acid blade and its two halves part to reveal an acid insert band; \"Every move sets off the next.\" rises letter by letter like dominoes standing up in perspective, with a knock of overshoot; \"Look again. Then closer.\" turns on a solid four-faced 3D prism like a trivision billboard; \"If it moves, it must mean something.\" is typed with a caret, including a typed-and-struck-through \"DECORATE\" that is backspaced; and the finale zooms into the counter of the O in \"MOVE\" until the acid counter becomes the background of the closing \"YOUR MOVE.\" page. Monospace annotations (SC. 03 / 07, t = 0.600, a running timecode) frame everything like an editor's timeline.","techniques":"- Sticky 100vh stages inside tall sections; per-scene progress computed from getBoundingClientRect and lightly smoothed toward the scroll target, all rendering done with transform/opacity only\n- Custom heavy condensed M, O, V, E glyphs drawn as SVG paths once in a defs library and reused with <use>, so the title card and the finale share the same letterforms and acid counter\n- Scroll-mapped zoom with a computed maximum scale and origin at the counter of the O, with the counter translated to the viewport centre so the zoom lands exactly on the next scene's acid background\n- Seeded random scatter (translate, rotate, scale) per glyph with staggered quartic easing for the assembly scene\n- scaleX stretch with measured natural widths so each word ends exactly at the measure, plus live scale readouts\n- Two clip-path polygon halves of the same word, cut at the measured visual centre of the capitals (canvas font metrics), with a drawn acid blade and a scaleY insert band\n- Per-letter rotateX from the baseline with its own perspective and a damped overshoot for the domino rise, with acid landing ticks\n- A preserve-3d prism with four solid faces, stepped hold-and-flip easing, face shading from the relative angle and a pointer-driven tilt\n- Typing state machine (type, mistake, pause, strike-through, backspace, retype) with a blinking acid caret and keystroke counters\n- Outlined ghost scene numerals with parallax, a hairline grid on the intro, a CSS marquee ticker and a timecode that treats the scroll as a playhead\n- Display type fitted to the viewport in JS, so layouts hold across different system condensed fonts","originalInteraction":"- Scroll is the playhead: every scene plays forward and backward with the wheel, trackpad or touch\n- Side progress rail (a bottom bar on phones) shows each scene's progress; click a segment to jump to that scene\n- Keys 1–7 jump to scenes, 0 returns to the top, M toggles motion; native keyboard scrolling works throughout\n- Pointer velocity skews the intro \"MOVE\" and springs back; pointer position tilts the 3D prism in scene 5\n- \"Motion on/off\" toggle (remembered with guarded localStorage) switches to a static reading mode; prefers-reduced-motion starts in that mode, with statements shown in their final state and simple fades\n- The closing page lists all seven statements as buttons that jump back to their scenes, plus a \"Replay from the top\" button","sourceHash":"88b9b7f535cdce0bb4945e31646b4df72f1b2f59c035e6c77a1917b40580fad6","name":"运动宣言","category":"排版实验","description":"七个滚动驱动的字体场景，让文字经历聚拢、拉伸、分裂与旋转。页面把时间和阅读进度变成排版材料。","interaction":"滚动控制每一幕的进退；数字键 1–7 跳转场景，M 切换动效。"},{"id":"015","slug":"015-art-deco-hotel","title":"The Aurelian","file":"015-art-deco-hotel.html","textFile":"015-art-deco-hotel.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/015-art-deco-hotel.jpg","prompt":"Create a landing page for \"The Aurelian\", a fictional 1928 Art Deco grand hotel. Black lacquer (#0b0b0c) and deep emerald (#0f3d33) grounds with gold (#d4af37, grading to #f5e3a1) geometric ornament — sunbursts, stepped ziggurat frames, chevrons and fan motifs, all inline SVG. Typography: tall, thin, high-contrast display capitals (stack: Didot, \"Bodoni 72\", \"Bodoni MT\", \"C059\", Georgia, serif) with wide letter-spacing, plus refined small caps. Sections: a hero with an animated sunburst radiating behind the hotel name and a symmetrical Deco frame that draws itself in gold; \"The Suites\" as three arched cards with gilded hover states; a dining section with a menu styled as an engraved card; an elevator-dial floor selector that scrolls to sections while its needle rotates; and a reservation form with Deco-bordered inputs. Delight: a gold-foil shimmer sweeping across headings and fan patterns that open on scroll. Elegant and symmetrical on desktop, gracefully stacked on phones.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A symmetrical, jazz-age landing page for The Aurelian, a fictional 1928 grand hotel, built as a tower you ride through: the hero is the lobby, and each section below is a floor reached by a brass elevator dial. Black lacquer and deep emerald grounds carry gold ornament that grades from #d4af37 to #f5e3a1: a slowly turning sunburst radiates behind a tall, vertically stretched Didot-stack wordmark, while a stepped ziggurat frame draws itself in gold from the bottom centre outwards on both sides at once, ending in a keystone fan that cradles the diamond monogram. Below come three arched suite cards with painted SVG window vignettes, an emerald Salon with a frieze of Deco fans that unfold as they scroll into view, a supper club whose menu is an engraved ivory card with a guilloche rosette, and a reservation desk with double-bracketed, stepped-corner inputs. It is elegant and mirrored on desktop and stacks into a single column on phones.","techniques":"- Hero sunburst: 72 procedurally built SVG ray wedges filled with a user-space radial gold gradient, counter-rotating dotted rings and three staggered \"radiating\" pulse rings, all animated with CSS transforms\n- Self-drawing Deco frame: JavaScript computes a mirrored stepped-ziggurat outline, inner offset line, pilasters, corner quarter-sunbursts, a keystone fan and foot chevrons to the hero's exact size; every path uses pathLength=\"1\" and a staggered stroke-dashoffset animation, and is rebuilt on resize\n- A shared SVG gold gradient whose highlight stop is animated with SMIL, so a light glint travels along all gold linework\n- Gold-foil shimmer: a multi-stop foil gradient clipped to text (background-clip:text) sweeping across the wordmark and every section heading on a timed loop\n- Tall display capitals: the Didot/Bodoni/C059 stack with wide tracking and a scaleY stretch for the Deco \"tall and thin\" proportion, plus all-small-caps labels in a Baskerville/Palatino text stack\n- Arched suite cards made with oversized border radii (a perfect semicircle at any width), a double gilt keyline, a skewed sheen sweep on hover and hand-drawn SVG vignettes (moonlit skyline, rising sun with rays, a searchlit spire) whose rays rotate on hover\n- Fans that open on scroll: blades generated per fan, pivoting from a closed stick to their spread angles with centre-out staggered transitions, triggered by IntersectionObserver\n- Engraved menu card: layered ivory radial gradients, triple gold keylines via box-shadow rings and a double border, stepped corner ornaments, a procedural guilloche rosette of rotated ellipses and letterpress text-shadows\n- Seigaiha-style fan-scale background for the Salon built purely from repeating radial gradients\n- Deco-bordered inputs: sixteen gradient layers on one pseudo-element draw double stepped brackets at each corner that spread and brighten on focus; custom chevron select arrows","originalInteraction":"- Fixed elevator floor indicator: numerals L and 2 to 5 sit on a brass arc; clicking one smooth-scrolls to that floor, and the needle, driven by a damped spring in requestAnimationFrame, tracks scroll continuously, dwelling on each floor and swinging with a slight overshoot between them\n- Up and down call buttons step one floor at a time; the plaque under the dial names the current floor (announced politely to screen readers)\n- Optional lift chime: a synthesized Web Audio bell (sine partials with exponential decay), created only after the user presses the visible Chime toggle, rings on each floor arrival and on a confirmed booking\n- On phones the dial collapses into a compact medallion that expands on tap; tapping outside or pressing Escape closes it again\n- Suite cards lift and gild on hover and focus; each \"Select\" button pre-fills the reservation form's residence, scrolls to the desk and focuses the name field\n- Reservation form: dates default to three weeks ahead, the departure date follows the arrival date, a live estimate spells out the nights and total, inline validation messages appear, and on submit a fictional key-card confirmation with a generated code is shown (nothing is sent; the guest name is remembered in guarded localStorage)\n- Keyboard: skip link, native buttons and links throughout, visible gold focus rings; prefers-reduced-motion freezes the sunburst, foil and needle spring and shows the frame fully drawn with fans open","sourceHash":"a9b3651ec4793e126b7f00af2b46c2d4c25da080c532fb18f5ce064768101b52","name":"爵士时代酒店","category":"交互网页","description":"黑漆、翡翠绿与金色几何构成虚构酒店的 Art Deco 大堂。页面被组织为楼层，电梯指针引导整段浏览。","interaction":"点击楼层编号或上下呼叫按钮切换区域，观察金色指针与页面滚动联动。"},{"id":"016","slug":"016-vaporwave-mall","title":"Plaza Aeterna","file":"016-vaporwave-mall.html","textFile":"016-vaporwave-mall.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/016-vaporwave-mall.jpg","prompt":"Build \"PLAZA AETERNA — Mall of Tomorrow (Est. 1994)\", a vaporwave dreamscape. Background: a sunset gradient (#ff71ce → #b967ff → #01cdfe) with a striped retro sun, a 3D-perspective neon grid floor scrolling toward the viewer, palm silhouettes, and a slowly rotating marble bust built from layered SVG shapes with procedurally generated marble veining. Overlay draggable Windows-95-style windows (bevelled grey #c0c0c0, navy title bars) containing: a \"Store Directory\" with pastel icons, a mock MIDI player that plays a synthesized chord progression (Web Audio, after a gesture), and a \"Welcome\" message in full-width styling (Ａ Ｅ Ｓ Ｔ Ｈ Ｅ Ｔ Ｉ Ｃ). Typography: a mixture of serif italics, pixel-feeling monospace and widely spaced letters. Interaction: drag windows by their title bars, minimise them to a taskbar with a Start button and clock, click desktop icons to open windows, and a VHS tracking glitch on hover. Responsive: windows become stacked cards on phones.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Plaza Aeterna is a fictional 1994 shopping mall shown as a Windows-95-style desktop floating in a vaporwave sunset. The sky runs from cyan through violet to hot pink, with a striped yellow-to-magenta sun, wireframe mountain ranges and a neon grid floor rushing toward the viewer. Two procedurally drawn palms frame a marble bust of Helios on a plinth, which turns slowly on its axis while pink and cyan light rake across it. Bevelled grey windows with navy title bars float over the scene: a Welcome.txt note with full-width ＡＥＳＴＨＥＴＩＣ lettering and a chrome serif-italic logotype, a tabbed Store Directory with pastel shop icons and a live floor plan, and a Jukebox MIDI player that synthesises lo-fi mall muzak. A taskbar, Start menu, VHS on-screen display and CRT scanlines complete the illusion. On phones the windows become a column of stacked cards under a full-height hero of the sun and bust.","techniques":"- Pseudo-3D marble bust in layered SVG: the head is a rotational solid whose silhouette is recomputed every frame from per-height elliptical cross-sections, so the jaw and chin shift correctly as it turns\n- Facial features (eyes with incised irises, lids, brow shadow, a two-plane shaded nose, lips, nasolabial folds, chin) defined as 3D curves on the head surface and projected each frame, fading out as they turn away from the viewer\n- About 120 hair curls placed on a spherical shell, depth-sorted and swapped between behind-skull and in-front-of-face groups as they rotate; ears foreshorten from edge-on to full view\n- Procedural marble veining: seeded random walks on the head surface (re-projected and back-face culled every frame) plus clipped static veins on the torso\n- Torso with a himation drape and fibula brooch, narrowed by a projected-width scale as it turns; front anatomy and drape cross-fade into back detail (spine, shoulder blades) when seen from behind\n- Canvas neon grid floor with true perspective spacing (y = horizon + D/z), a double-stroke glow pass, horizon haze, a sun reflection and low-poly wireframe mountain ranges\n- Striped sun built from an SVG mask whose black bands scroll and thicken toward the horizon\n- Palm fronds generated procedurally as spines with drooping leaflet triangles in a shared SVG symbol, swaying gently with CSS\n- Authentic Win95 chrome from stacked inset box-shadows (raised and sunken bevels, tabs, trackbar, checkboxes, dithered pressed task buttons)\n- Chrome serif-italic logotype using a background-clip gradient with a hard cyan offset shadow; full-width and katakana type in a CJK serif; mono labels with wide letter-spacing\n- VHS layer: scanlines and vignette, a periodic tracking band sweeping down the screen, a PLAY ▶ SP on-screen display with a date stamp and live tape counter, and a per-window hover glitch (RGB-split drop-shadows, horizontal jitter, a noisy band that sweeps down the window)\n- Jukebox LCD with a scrolling marquee, glowing time and chord readouts, an analyser-driven spectrum canvas and a 4-octave keyboard that lights the sounding notes","originalInteraction":"- Drag windows by their title bars (pointer capture works for mouse and touch); double-click a title bar or use the maximise button to toggle maximise; arrow keys move a focused title bar (Shift moves faster)\n- Minimise a window and it shrinks into its taskbar button; clicking a taskbar button restores the window, focuses it, or minimises it again; close buttons remove the window and its taskbar entry\n- Desktop icons (Welcome.txt, Directory, Jukebox.mid, Fountain.exe, Recycle Bin) open or refocus their windows on click; the Start menu (keyboard-navigable, Esc closes) opens every program, toggles the VHS filter and offers a \"Shut Down…\" screen\n- Store Directory: Level 1–3 tabs (arrow-key navigable); hovering or focusing a store lights its unit on the floor plan and shows its hours in the status bar\n- Jukebox: play/pause/stop/previous/next, three synthesised tracks with real chord progressions (FM electric piano, detuned saw pad, bass, bells, soft drums, tape-wow LFO, convolution reverb), a volume slider, \"Slowed + reverb\" tape mode and Mute; sound starts only after a click, and the tray speaker is a global mute\n- Wishing Fountain: toss a penny for a coin arc, a splash ripple, a chime and a random wish; the penny count is kept in localStorage (guarded with try/catch)\n- Recycle Bin: \"Empty\" crumples the files away, and Mall Management then restores them\n- Drag the bust sideways to spin it with momentum; hovering windows and icons with a mouse triggers the VHS glitch\n- prefers-reduced-motion stops the grid, the sun bands, the bust rotation, the glitches and the marquees; on short desktops (under 830 px tall, e.g. 1366×768 and 1280×720) the windows switch to compact versions (tighter paper, smaller map, playlist hidden, with tracks changed via previous/next) and a layout pass stacks the Directory and Jukebox so no windows collide; on phones the windows stack as full-width cards, minimising collapses a card to its title bar, and the taskbar shows icon-only buttons","sourceHash":"f9ca97173c3edb3ccc26da2b4213a40fac5ba4b6ed5858a0da836efb044cca21","name":"永恒商场 1994","category":"交互网页","description":"蒸汽波夕阳、霓虹网格、棕榈树与大理石头像包围着一套 Windows 95 式桌面，重构虚构购物中心的数字记忆。","interaction":"拖动窗口，双击标题栏放大；最小化后从任务栏恢复窗口。"},{"id":"017","slug":"017-pendulum-wave","title":"Harmonia — Pendulum Wave","file":"017-pendulum-wave.html","textFile":"017-pendulum-wave.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/017-pendulum-wave.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Harmonia — Pendulum Wave\", a physically accurate pendulum-wave simulation as minimalist kinetic art. Charcoal background (#16171b); 18 pendulums whose lengths are chosen so they complete 51, 52, 53… oscillations in 60 seconds (the classic pendulum-wave set-up), rendered from a slightly elevated 3D perspective with fine white strings and glowing spheres in a gradient spectrum from #ffd6a5 to #9bf6ff, plus optional light trails that paint Lissajous-like ribbons. A thin brushed-metal mounting bar at the top. Beneath, an elegant monospace readout: elapsed time, cycle phase, and a timeline scrubber showing where you are in the 60-second pattern (snake, split, chaos, realignment). Controls: play/pause, speed (0.25×–4×), trails toggle, view toggle (side / top-down), and \"Release\", which pulls every pendulum back and lets them drop. Optional soft chime whenever a pendulum crosses centre (Web Audio pentatonic, after a gesture, muted by default). Typography: thin sans plus mono.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Harmonia presents the classic pendulum-wave apparatus as a quiet piece of kinetic art on a charcoal (#16171b) stage. Eighteen bifilar pendulums (each bob hangs from two fine white strings in a V, so it swings in a single plane) hang from a brushed-metal bar seen in a slightly elevated three-quarter perspective. Their lengths come from L = g(T/2π)² so that they complete exactly 51, 52 … 68 oscillations in 60 seconds (34.4 cm down to 19.3 cm). Every bob starts from the same 5.5 cm throw and follows the exact small-angle solution, so the snake, the two-, three- and four-row splits, the chaos and the realignment at 60 s all come out of the physics rather than from choreography. The glowing spheres run through a spectrum from peach #ffd6a5 to ice #9bf6ff, and optional light trails sweep out translucent silk ribbons as the wave line moves through space. Beneath the stage, a monospace instrument console shows elapsed time, cycle phase, the named pattern, the neighbour phase offset, an orrery-like phase dial, and a scrubbable 60-second timeline marked with the pattern regions and the ¼ ⅓ ½ ⅔ ¾ split points.","techniques":"- Hand-rolled 3D camera (yaw/pitch/perspective) projecting world-space metres onto a DPR-aware canvas, with an auto-fit step that frames the apparatus for any viewport and moves the masthead clear of it\n- Closed-form small-angle solution θ(t) = θ₀·cos(ωt) with lengths from L = g(T/2π)², so the 60 s realignment is exact and every past instant can be evaluated directly\n- Light-trail ribbons: the wave curve through all 18 bobs is re-evaluated at 58 past instants and stroked additively with a single peach→mint→ice gradient, fading quadratically with age, so the trails appear instantly even after scrubbing\n- Brushed-metal bar built from back-face-culled box faces; the long faces use an offscreen streak texture mapped onto each projected face with an affine pattern transform, plus shading gradients, a machined edge highlight and an anisotropic sheen that slides as the camera orbits\n- Pre-rendered sphere and glow sprites per pendulum (additive glow whose intensity follows each bob's speed |sin ωt|), depth-sorted every frame\n- Soft light pools projected onto an implied floor plane beneath each bob, and ceiling cables that fade away with height and with camera pitch\n- Phase dial: 18 concentric orbits with one dot per pendulum at phase ωt, joined by a hairline so the fan, spiral and split patterns are legible at a glance\n- Timeline built from CSS bands (gradient snake, solid split, hatched chaos, white realignment) with a masked tick ruler and a glowing playhead\n- Smooth camera easing between the elevated side view and a true top-down view; in portrait the top view turns the bar vertical to fit the phone","originalInteraction":"- Play/Pause, speed selector (0.25×–4× in eight steps), Trails toggle, View toggle (Side / Top) and Chime toggle, all as labelled buttons with pressed states and visible focus rings\n- Release: a translucent release board sweeps in and gathers every bob to the same 5.5 cm throw with a slight stagger, holds, then drops them all together at t = 0\n- Timeline scrubber (pointer drag or keyboard: arrows ±0.5 s, Page Up/Down ±5 s, Home/End) jumps anywhere in the 60 s pattern, trails included\n- Drag the stage to orbit the camera (horizontal drag only on touch, so the page still scrolls); double-click resets the angle\n- Hover (or tap) a sphere to highlight it and read its swing count, period, length and live angle in a tooltip\n- Optional chime: after a user gesture, each pendulum plays its own major-pentatonic bell tone (sine plus an inharmonic partial, panned by position, through a soft delay) when it crosses centre; muted by default and rate-limited\n- Keyboard shortcuts: Space play/pause, R release, T trails, V view, C chime, ←/→ scrub, [ ] speed\n- prefers-reduced-motion starts at half speed with trails off and camera moves become instant; animation stops while the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"17185353064c06133630150a6838d8b4dc63910df929abde92d1fbb7b1d251f1","name":"摆的合奏","category":"科学与模拟","description":"十八枚金属摆锤由不同长度的双线悬挂，从一致的起点出发，逐渐分离成波纹，再重新聚拢。","interaction":"切换播放速度、轨迹和视角；使用 Release 重新释放摆锤，可主动开启钟音。"},{"id":"018","slug":"018-paper-cut-diorama","title":"Foxglove Hollow","file":"018-paper-cut-diorama.html","textFile":"018-paper-cut-diorama.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/018-paper-cut-diorama.jpg","prompt":"Design \"Foxglove Hollow\", a layered paper-cut forest diorama. Seven to nine stacked layers of hand-cut-looking paper (SVG paths with slightly irregular edges) — distant hills, pines, a winding river, mushrooms, ferns, a small fox and foreground grasses — each casting a soft drop shadow onto the layer behind it so the scene has real depth. Day palette: sage #9bb89b, moss #5f7f5b, deep teal #274e52, ochre #d9a45b, burnt orange #c8642d, cream sky #f3e8d2. Night palette: indigo layers, tiny glowing windows, fireflies and a paper moon. Subtle paper grain from procedural noise. Interaction: pointer movement creates parallax between layers (touch-drag on mobile); a day/night toggle recolours every layer with a staggered transition; clicking the fox makes it hop to a new spot; paper leaves drift gently. The title sits on a small paper tag hanging from a string, in a warm serif. The composition must recompute nicely for portrait screens.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Foxglove Hollow is a full-screen paper theatre: nine sheets of scissor-cut paper stacked inside a dark teal board frame with a deckle-edged cream mat, each sheet throwing a soft shadow onto the one behind it. From back to front: a cream sky, pale far hills, a rolling hill with tiny pines and two cottages, a sage pine ridge, a meadow crossed by a winding river, moss pines on two banks, towering deep-teal pines that frame the hollow, the ochre forest floor (burrow, mushroom clusters, ferns, pebbles, fallen leaves and the fox) and a foreground fringe of grass and burnt-orange foxgloves. The sun, clouds, moon and stars hang from threads like a little fly-system, and the title is written on a luggage tag swinging from a string. Every shape is generated at runtime from a seeded random cut, so the composition recomputes from scratch for portrait or landscape screens rather than just scaling.","techniques":"- Runtime SVG geometry: hills, banks, pines, mushrooms, ferns, grass blades and foxgloves are built as polygons whose edges are subdivided and nudged sideways, so every outline looks hand-cut; all polygons are winding-normalised so overlapping pieces fuse into one sheet\n- One seeded PRNG drives the whole cut, so a resize re-lays out the same forest for the new aspect ratio, and \"Recut\" draws a new one\n- Per-sheet depth from CSS drop-shadow filters, with a 1px light top edge standing in for the cut paper rim; shadow colour switches from warm brown to deep indigo at night\n- Procedural paper grain: a noise-and-fibre canvas tile becomes an SVG pattern overlaid on each sheet, so the grain moves with its sheet\n- Day/night recolour through CSS fill transitions with a per-sheet transition-delay, so night spreads from the back of the box to the front; the sky crossfades, windows glow through a radial gradient, river glints twinkle and mushroom spots turn bioluminescent\n- Hanging sun, clouds, moon and paper stars ride up and down on strings with an anticipating, overshooting cubic-bezier and swing on staggered CSS pendulums; pinprick stars twinkle in the night sky\n- Canvas fireflies drawn from a pre-rendered glow sprite (pollen motes by day), pooled DOM paper leaves with 3D flutter, and a damped-pendulum title tag\n- The fox is a hand-drawn multi-piece SVG with SMIL tail wag and blink; hops use squash, stretch, an arc and a burst of paper dust on landing","originalInteraction":"- Mouse movement parallaxes the nine sheets (touch-drag on phones); when idle the view drifts gently on its own\n- Click or tap the fox (or focus it and press Enter/Space, or press F) to make it hop to a new spot along the ground\n- The Day/Night paper switch (or N) re-colours every sheet with a staggered transition, raises the sun and clouds, and lowers the moon and stars; the choice is remembered in localStorage (guarded)\n- Clicking anywhere else shakes loose a small burst of paper leaves; pointer speed swings the title tag\n- \"Recut\" generates a new seeded forest and replays the layers sliding into the box\n- Arrow keys look around; a polite live region announces hops and day/night changes; prefers-reduced-motion removes the idle drift, intro, swinging and most drifting motion; the animation loop pauses while the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"956d336f62abd6ec55de60053daa12a6e9392873b0ffc531d7a49fd296bf780f","name":"纸雕森林剧场","category":"生成艺术","description":"九层剪纸在深青色框内叠成山丘、溪流、松林与狐狸的栖息地，每一层都向后投下一道柔软阴影。","interaction":"移动鼠标或拖动屏幕体验层间视差；点击狐狸让它跳跃，切换白天与夜晚。"},{"id":"019","slug":"019-phosphor-terminal","title":"HELIOS/OS","file":"019-phosphor-terminal.html","textFile":"019-phosphor-terminal.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/019-phosphor-terminal.jpg","prompt":"Build \"HELIOS/OS v2.3\", a retro CRT terminal operating system simulation. Full-screen phosphor green (#33ff66 on #031a07) with themes for amber (#ffb000 on #1a0f00) and ice (#9fe7ff). CRT effects in CSS/canvas: scanlines, subtle screen curvature (vignette, rounded corners, inner shadow), phosphor glow/bloom text-shadow, gentle flicker and a rolling refresh bar. On load: a believable BIOS boot sequence (memory check counting up, device list) followed by a login banner. A working shell with an input line and blinking block caret supporting: help, ls, cat <file> (several short fictional files including a poem and a mission log), clear, date, whoami, theme <green|amber|ice>, matrix (glyph rain for a few seconds), weather (ASCII art), snake (a small playable ASCII snake game inside the terminal), command history with ↑/↓, and tab completion. Typography: a monospace stack. Keyboard-first, with tap-to-focus on phones so the soft keyboard appears. Optional typing sounds (Web Audio clicks, off by default).\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"HELIOS/OS v2.3 \"Aphelion\" is a fictional 1987 operating system running on a fictional Heliograph HX-14 monochrome terminal aboard a relay station. The page is the whole monitor: a graphite bezel with a power LED, an engraved badge and a row of physical keys frames a curved, glowing phosphor tube. The tube powers on with a collapsing-beam animation, runs a POST screen (a vendor sun badge, a counting memory test, device detection, a kernel progress bar and service checks), auto-types the operator login, then prints a neofetch-style welcome: a box-drawing HELIOS logo, a block-shaded sun emblem beside a system card, and a short message of the day. From there it is a working shell with a small fictional filesystem and a light storyline (a mission log, a decoded signal, a poem someone left on tty3). Three phosphors are available: P1 green (#33ff66 on #031a07), P3 amber (#ffb000 on #1a0f00) and ice (#9fe7ff).","techniques":"- CRT tube built from stacked layers: a radial phosphor gradient, 3 px scanlines, a faint RGB aperture-grille stripe, animated film grain on a low-resolution canvas, a rolling refresh band, an irregular stepped flicker, a vignette with deep inset shadows for the curved glass, and a glass reflection highlight.\n- Three-layer text-shadow bloom on all text, brighter \"hot\" glow for headings, inverse-video tags and a status line, with every colour driven by per-theme CSS custom properties (including an RGB triplet for rgba glows).\n- The power-on and power-off animations (a horizontal beam expanding into the raster, then collapsing to a dot) use CSS keyframes on the tube plus a separate glowing beam element.\n- Theme changes play a \"degauss\" wobble (skew, scale and hue-rotate keyframes); occasional random sync glitches jolt the raster.\n- Phosphor persistence: clearing the screen clones the old text into a ghost layer that blurs and fades out.\n- Output streams at a simulated line speed (19200 baud by default, adjustable with `baud`), appending characters to text nodes on each animation frame.\n- Glyph rain draws on canvas: mirrored katakana, Greek and digit glyphs, glowing heads, frame-rate-independent translucent trails, random shimmer, and a fade-out that brings the text back.\n- The snake game is drawn as HTML inside the terminal: a box-drawing border, a dotted field, a blinking <> pellet, and a body that fades through the shade characters █ ▓ ▒ ░ toward the tail.\n- ASCII weather: five hand-drawn condition icons, a day-seeded forecast, and a three-day strip.\n- The banner re-renders to fit the terminal's width in character columns, measured with a probe, so the logo and system card stay intact from phones to wide screens.","originalInteraction":"- Keyboard-first shell: type commands at a blinking block caret. The caret goes solid while you type and hollow when the input is unfocused. Any key or click fast-forwards the boot.\n- Commands: help, ls [-a] [-l] [dir], cd, pwd, cat <file>, clear, date, whoami, theme <green|amber|ice>, matrix, weather, snake, history, echo, uname [-a], uptime, fortune, sound <on|off>, baud <rate|max> and reboot. There are hidden extras (sudo, exit/logout, answer) and a hidden .plan file.\n- ↑/↓ walk through the command history, which persists in localStorage. Tab completes commands, file paths (directory-aware, hidden files only when requested) and command arguments, and lists candidates when the match is ambiguous. Ctrl+C cancels the line, Ctrl+L clears the screen, Ctrl+U kills to the start of the line, and Enter/Esc/Ctrl+C flush long streaming output.\n- Bezel keys auto-type common commands (Help, Ls, Poem, Log, Weather, Snake, Matrix, Clear). The P1/P3/Ice keys and the in-screen [P1:GRN] status item switch phosphor. The Sound key or [SND:OFF] toggles Web Audio typing clicks, the Enter thock, error beeps and a degauss thunk. Sound is off by default and the AudioContext is created only on a gesture.\n- Snake: arrows/WASD steer, P or Space pauses, Q or Esc quits. On touch, swipe on the screen, or use the on-screen D-pad that replaces the command keys during play. The best score is saved.\n- Matrix: any key or click stops the rain early.\n- Phones: tap anywhere on the tube to focus the real, invisible input so the soft keyboard appears. The layout follows visualViewport height, and the key row scrolls horizontally.\n- Reduced motion turns off flicker, the roll band, grain animation, beam and degauss effects, and streaming delays, so the boot completes instantly.","sourceHash":"59e3c6649b0e41591e608c4d4b3aea27bf4dc052e4a0e968652f6a808fe7d6be","name":"荧光终端","category":"数据与工具","description":"一台虚构的 1987 年终端从开机自检开始，逐步进入可操作的命令行。弧面屏、余辉与实体按键还原旧显示器质感。","interaction":"输入 help 查看命令；尝试 ls、cat、weather 或 snake，任意键可跳过开机过程。"},{"id":"020","slug":"020-rivers-of-the-world","title":"Long Water — The World's Great Rivers","file":"020-rivers-of-the-world.html","textFile":"020-rivers-of-the-world.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/020-rivers-of-the-world.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Long Water — The World's Great Rivers\", an elegant data-art visualization comparing the ten longest rivers. Use these commonly cited approximate lengths (label them \"approx.\" and note that sources vary): Nile 6,650 km; Amazon 6,400; Yangtze 6,300; Mississippi–Missouri 6,275; Yenisei 5,539; Yellow River 5,464; Ob–Irtysh 5,410; Paraná 4,880; Congo 4,700; Amur 4,444. Each river is a flowing, meandering SVG ribbon whose length is proportional to its real length, flowing left to right with particles drifting along it like current. Palette: deep river blues and teals on pale limestone (#f3f1ec) — a sequential scale from #0b3d5c to #7fc8c2 — with thin ink labels. Typography: refined serif titles, small-caps continent labels, tabular numerals. Interaction: hover a river to highlight it and show a tooltip (length, continent, where it flows out); sort toggle (by length / by continent); unit toggle (km / miles); a \"race\" button where droplets travel every river at the same speed so the differences in length can be felt. Include an elegant legend and a short intro paragraph. Mobile: ribbons become vertical flows.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"An editorial data-art plate printed on pale limestone paper. A large italic serif masthead, \"Long Water\", floats over faint contour lines, next to a short essay on why river lengths are slippery and three headline figures (combined length, times around the Equator, continents). Below, the ten longest rivers run from a shared \"source\" line on the left to their seas on the right. Each is a meandering ribbon whose horizontal extent is drawn to one km scale. It starts as a pale thread and swells into a deep-blue mouth that fans into a small delta. The ribbon's final width is scaled to the square root of approximate mean discharge, so the Amazon's flood and the Nile's thread tell a second story beside length. Shade follows a sequential scale from #0b3d5c (longest) to #7fc8c2 (shortest). Thin ink labels give rank, name, continent in small caps, \"≈\" tabular lengths and the receiving sea. A legend, a scale bar and a footnote on measurement uncertainty close the page. On phones the ribbons turn into ten vertical flows that pour down the page under rotated labels.","techniques":"- SVG ribbons built at runtime from an along/cross coordinate system: a three-harmonic meander centreline, analytic normals and a tapering width profile form each filled polygon. The same geometry maps to horizontal rows (desktop) or vertical columns (phone).\n- Per-river userSpaceOnUse linear gradients (pale source to full-colour mouth), a Gaussian-blurred \"floodplain\" halo under each ribbon, a faint glint line on wide rivers, and quadratic-curve delta distributaries at every mouth.\n- A canvas overlay (DPR-aware, max 2) draws pooled current particles as short streaks in two weights. They are sampled more densely where the river is wide and follow the ribbon's centreline and normals, with no per-frame allocation.\n- A staggered intro reveal uses per-river clipPaths, so rivers pour out of their sources one after another. A gentle stroke-dash animation draws the three-line wave ornament.\n- A procedural paper grain and vignette are painted once onto a fixed background canvas from a noise tile. Parallel contour lines sit behind the title via SVG <use>.\n- Editorial typography: a Baskerville/Hoefler serif stack, lower-cased small-caps labels with wide tracking, tabular lining numerals throughout, and an italic drop cap.\n- The race layer draws glowing droplets with fading trails, a dashed \"front line\" marking the distance travelled, and expanding ripples at each mouth on arrival.","originalInteraction":"- Hover (mouse) or tap (touch) a river: the other rivers fade, and a tooltip card shows rank, continent, length in both units, where the river rises, where it flows out, approximate mean discharge and a short fact. Tapping empty space dismisses it.\n- Keyboard: every river is focusable. Focus shows its card, arrow keys move between rivers in the current order, Enter/Space toggles the card and Escape closes it.\n- Sort toggle (Length / Continent): rows (or columns on phones) glide to new positions. Continent mode adds small-caps section dividers for Africa, Asia, North America and South America.\n- Units toggle (km / miles) rewrites every label, the axis ticks, the headline total, the scale bar, the tooltip and the screen-reader table.\n- \"Race the droplets\": a droplet sets off down every river at the same speed while an odometer counts distance. Each arrival triggers a ripple and a live message, and a closing line reports how far behind the shortest river the Nile arrives. Pressing again stops the race.\n- Resizing or rotating rebuilds the geometry for the new width, switching between horizontal rows and vertical flows. With reduced motion the reveal is instant and the current barely drifts. Animation pauses while the tab is hidden.","sourceHash":"6fc034926d2b9b0645964cad875e886514612e5ea3e999cdd82871dfe5df124e","name":"世界长河","category":"数据与工具","description":"十条大河从共同源线出发，以同一长度比例蜿蜒至海。淡色地形线、蓝色水带和衬线文字组成一张数据艺术图版。","interaction":"悬停、点击或用键盘选择河流，查看长度、源头、流向与相关数据。"},{"id":"021","slug":"021-bauhaus-composer","title":"Formlehre — Bauhaus Composer","file":"021-bauhaus-composer.html","textFile":"021-bauhaus-composer.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/021-bauhaus-composer.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Formlehre — Bauhaus Composer\", a tactile composition toy honouring Bauhaus design. A board on warm cream (#f1ead8) with a tray of primary shapes — circle, square, triangle, semicircle, quarter-circle, bar — in the Bauhaus triad red #d62d20, yellow #f4c20d and blue #1d4e9e, plus black #111. Drag shapes onto the board; they snap to a hidden 8×8 grid with a satisfying spring; rotate with the wheel or R key, scale with handles, recolour by clicking a swatch, bring to front. A \"Kandinsky\" button auto-composes a balanced composition using Kandinsky's colour–form associations (yellow triangle, red square, blue circle) with a short explanatory caption. Typography: a geometric sans stack (Futura, \"Century Gothic\", \"Avenir Next\", \"URW Gothic\", sans-serif) with lowercase-only headings in the Bayer \"universal\" spirit and large numeral section markers. The toolbar is made of coloured geometric buttons. Export the composition as PNG. Keyboard: Delete removes, arrows nudge. Works with touch.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"formlehre — bauhaus composer\" is a tactile composition toy laid out like a printed Bauhaus sheet: a deep-cream paper page with a heavy black rule under an oversized lowercase masthead, and five-plus numbered sections whose giant numerals each sit on a coloured primary form (1 tray, 2 board, 3 colour, 4 actions, 5 notes, 6 balance). The centre of the page is a warm cream board (#f1ead8) with a hard black offset shadow and chess-style a–h / 1–8 edge labels; forms in red #d62d20, yellow #f4c20d, blue #1d4e9e and black #111 are dragged onto it from a tray, caught by a hidden 8×8 grid with a springy settle, and edited with handles, swatches and geometric toolbar buttons. A \"kandinsky\" button composes a balanced arrangement from his colour–form pairings, and a live balance map shows where the composition's visual weight sits. All headings and UI copy are lowercase in the spirit of Herbert Bayer's universal alphabet, set in a geometric sans stack (Futura, Century Gothic, Avenir Next, URW Gothic).","techniques":"- Board rendered as an 800-unit SVG; every form is a single path (circle, square, equilateral triangle, semicircle, quarter circle, bar) placed with translate / rotate / scale, so position, angle and size can all be sprung independently\n- Damped-spring physics (semi-implicit Euler, 120 Hz substeps) for position, rotation and size: drops overshoot and settle, recolours and size changes \"pulse\", deleted forms shrink away, Kandinsky compositions pop in with a staggered cascade\n- Grid snapping by bounding-box parity (odd-sized forms centre on cells, even-sized on grid lines, bars ride the lines) with rotated-bbox handling; the hidden grid and a dashed snap preview fade in only while dragging\n- Selection frame with non-scaling dashed stroke, four resize handles and a yellow rotate knob, all kept a constant on-screen size via a units-per-pixel factor\n- Printed-paper feel: a runtime-generated noise tile multiplied over the page and over the board (so the flat colours read as ink), a soft top-left paper sheen, hard 11px black offset shadows, flat drop-shadows on lifted forms\n- Numeral section markers built from CSS pseudo-elements clipped into triangle, square, circle, semicircle and quarter-circle shapes behind heavy digits; toolbar and swatches drawn as coloured geometric SVG buttons\n- Balance map: a miniature ghost of the board with a crosshair at the centre of visual weight (area × colour heaviness) that springs into place, plus a stacked colour-weight bar\n- PNG export drawn to a 1600px canvas with Path2D from the same path data, with a black rule and lowercase caption band","originalInteraction":"- Drag a form from the tray (mouse or touch) onto the board; a ghost follows the pointer at true board scale and lands where you drop it, then springs to the snapped cell. Tap or press Enter on a tray tile to place it in the emptiest spot\n- Drag forms on the board to move them; drop one outside the board to remove it\n- Rotate with the mouse wheel (15° steps), the R key (45°, Shift+R back), the \"turn\" button, or by dragging the yellow knob (15° snaps); resize with the corner handles, + / − keys or buttons\n- Click a swatch to recolour the selected form; with nothing selected the swatch recolours the tray (click again to return to Kandinsky's pairings). Keys 1–4 recolour too\n- Toolbar: turn, larger, smaller, front, copy, remove, undo, clear, plus \"kandinsky\" (also the K key) and \"png\" export\n- Keyboard: Tab to a form to select it, arrows nudge by one cell, Delete/Backspace removes, F brings to front, D duplicates, Escape deselects, Ctrl/Cmd+Z undoes (80-step history)\n- The composition and composition number persist in localStorage (guarded with try/catch); first visit opens on a seeded Kandinsky composition\n- The live readout names the selected form in German and English with its grid span, angle and size; prefers-reduced-motion switches the springs to critically damped and removes the stagger","sourceHash":"1ee0cc5be945a75ec68a906492a6d718a78973f0c487ed0b5b2aad93fad9783f","name":"包豪斯构图台","category":"生成艺术","description":"圆、方、三角与三原色落在一块带坐标的纸面上。形式、色块和重心成为可以亲手调整的构图材料。","interaction":"从托盘拖入图形，在画板上移动和组合；将图形拖出画板即可移除。"},{"id":"022","slug":"022-deep-sea-descent","title":"Descent — Eleven Thousand Metres Down","file":"022-deep-sea-descent.html","textFile":"022-deep-sea-descent.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/022-deep-sea-descent.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Descent — Eleven Thousand Metres Down\", a scroll-driven journey from the ocean surface to the bottom of the Mariana Trench (~10,935 m). As you scroll, the background transitions through the ocean zones — sunlight (#1e90c8), twilight (#0b3a66), midnight (#04142b), abyssal (#020812) and hadal (#000204) — while a fixed depth gauge counts metres and shows the zone name, pressure (≈ 1 additional atmosphere per 10 m) and temperature notes. Illustrated SVG/canvas creatures appear at approximately correct depths: dolphins and sunlit fish near the top, jellyfish, a sperm whale (~1,000 m and deeper), an anglerfish with a glowing lure, a vampire squid, a gulper eel, amphipods, and finally the trench floor. Deeper creatures are bioluminescent, with pulsing blue/green light; marine snow drifts through the water; light rays from the surface fade out gradually. Short, accurate captions (keep claims general and well established). Interaction: scroll; in the dark zones the pointer is a faint torch revealing nearby creatures; clicking a creature opens a caption card. It ends with an elegant \"Return to surface\" button that smooth-scrolls up.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A scroll-driven descent from a bright split-level ocean surface down to the floor of the Challenger Deep. The first frame shows the waterline cutting through the italic serif title \"Descent\": the upper half is navy on a pale morning sky with a sun, drifting clouds and a distant ship, and the lower half is refracted, pale and swaying underwater, with dolphins and sunlight shafts below. As you scroll, a fixed background gradient sinks through the five zone colours (#1e90c8, #0b3a66, #04142b, #020812, #000204). A glass depth gauge counts true metres and shows zone name, pressure (1 atm + 1 per 10 m), water temperature and light notes, with a proportional 0 to 10,935 m track. Sixteen hand-drawn SVG subjects appear at typical depths: dolphins, sardines, moon jelly, lanternfish, hatchetfish, vampire squid, sperm whale, Atolla, anglerfish, gulper eel, comb jelly, tripod fish and sea pigs on an abyssal ledge, dumbo octopus, snailfish between trench walls, the 1960 bathyscaphe Trieste, and amphipods at a food fall on the floor. It ends on \"10,935 m\", a short historical note and a \"Return to surface\" button.","techniques":"- Piecewise depth-to-scroll mapping (sunlit zone stretched, hadal compressed), with every creature, zone heading, ruler tick, trench and floor placed in page coordinates by JS\n- Fixed background whose two-stop gradient is recomputed each frame from the depth at the top and bottom of the viewport, interpolating the zone palette\n- Full-viewport canvas layer: swaying additive god rays clipped below the moving waterline, fading out with depth; pooled marine snow with scroll parallax and torch-proximity brightening; bubbles; blue/green bioluminescent sparks and ambient blinks of unseen animals in the dark zones\n- Creatures as layered inline SVG. The body group's opacity follows a per-creature \"lit\" value (ambient light at its depth, raised by the torch or by hover and focus), while separate light-organ groups use shared radial gradients and stay visible in darkness with pulsing CSS animation\n- Signature details: Atolla's chasing ring of blue lights, a CSS-animated comb jelly with rainbow comb rows, flapping dumbo fins, porpoising dolphins, a shimmering sardine school built from one symbol, sperm-whale echolocation arcs, the anglerfish's glowing esca, the gulper's tail light and the Trieste's warm floodlight\n- Split-level title: two stacked copies of the word, clip-path cut at the waterline, the lower copy skewed and swaying like refraction; animated SVG wave layers, horizon glints and caustic bands\n- Trench walls as stretched SVG with non-scaling strokes; an abyssal ledge with nodules and brittle stars fading into the dark\n- Glass gauge panel with tabular monospace digits, pressure bar and a depth track with a glowing marker; on phones it collapses into a compact top bar","originalInteraction":"- Scroll (wheel, touch, keyboard) to sink; the gauge, background, rays, snow and lighting all respond live\n- In the twilight zone and below, the pointer is a faint torch: a soft light pool that reveals nearby creatures' bodies and snow and leaves glowing sparks as it moves; on touch it follows the last tap and otherwise rests near the centre\n- Click, tap or press Enter on any creature to open a caption card with name, Latin name, typical depth and a short caption, plus a lit copy of the illustration; close it with the × button, Escape, or a click elsewhere, and focus returns to the creature\n- \"Return to surface\" runs an eased ascent with a stream of bubbles while the gauge counts back up; any wheel, touch or key input cancels it; reduced motion jumps straight to the top\n- A skip link jumps to the trench floor; creatures are real buttons with visible focus rings; prefers-reduced-motion stops CSS animation and calms the canvas; drawing pauses while the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"ad4b26599e916f5c5b58a5176f416030e5691a6414348af2264cfca0c9b84d5e","name":"下潜一万一千米","category":"交互网页","description":"从明亮海面一路下潜到海沟，水色、光束与生物随深度变化。标题被水线折射，阅读本身就是潜航过程。","interaction":"滚动控制下潜；深水中的指针像一束微弱手电，照亮附近海域。"},{"id":"023","slug":"023-symmetry-painter","title":"Rosette — Symmetry Painter","file":"023-symmetry-painter.html","textFile":"023-symmetry-painter.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/023-symmetry-painter.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Rosette — Symmetry Painter\", a radial-symmetry drawing instrument. A black canvas (#070707) where every stroke is mirrored across N rotational segments (selectable 3–24, plus mirror on/off) around the centre, producing mandala-like jewel artwork. Brushes: glowing neon line (additive blending), ink line with velocity-based width, dotted stardust, and ribbon. Palette presets: \"Jewels\" (#ff006e #fb5607 #ffbe0b #8338ec #3a86ff), \"Byzantine\" (golds and deep blues), \"Porcelain\" (cobalt on white — switches the background to white), plus a hue-cycling rainbow mode. A faint guide shows the segment lines while drawing and fades out afterwards. UI: a floating, elegant control dock (glass on dark) with segment slider, brush picker, palette swatches, undo, clear (with a dissolve animation) and PNG download. Delight: an idle \"autodraw\" that paints slowly on its own until the user touches the canvas, and a gentle rotation mode that spins the artwork. Touch-friendly with pointer capture.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Rosette is a radial-symmetry drawing instrument on a near-black #070707 canvas. Every stroke is copied across 3 to 24 rotational folds, and each fold can also be mirrored, so the copies form a dihedral rosette. With mirror on, the mirrored copies take the next colour in the palette, which interleaves two colours into a jewel-like pattern. The page opens on a finished mandala that the instrument painted itself and keeps painting slowly until someone touches the canvas. The title \"Rosette\" is set in an italic old-style serif with a slowly turning ✺. A frosted glass dock floats at the bottom and holds the controls. Four palettes change the mood. Jewels uses #ff006e, #fb5607, #ffbe0b, #8338ec and #3a86ff. Byzantine uses golds with lapis and deep blues. Porcelain turns the ground to white and paints in cobalt glaze. Rainbow cycles the hue along the stroke.","techniques":"- Strokes are stored in unit space (radius = 1) with per-point distance, velocity-derived width, smoothed normal and twist phase. The artwork therefore rescales with the viewport and can be replayed deterministically for undo and resize.\n- The artwork lives on an offscreen square canvas big enough to cover the rotated viewport, capped at 2600 px, at DPR up to 2. The view canvas composites it each frame with a rotation for spin mode.\n- The symmetry engine draws every copy with a canvas transform: rotate(k·2π/N), then scale(1,−1) for the mirrored copy.\n- Neon is one quadratic-smoothed Path2D stroked in four passes (wide halo, glow, body, white-hot core) with additive 'lighter' blending. While drawing it is re-stroked each frame on a live layer so the joints never bead. On Porcelain it uses multiply instead.\n- Ink draws tapered quads plus round joints, with width driven by pen velocity: faster strokes are thinner.\n- Stardust is a seeded scatter of glints with soft halos and occasional four-point sparkles, so replays match exactly.\n- Ribbon is a twisting band of silk. Its half-width follows sin(phase) along the stroke, and translucent face shading plus hairline edges show which side is turned toward the viewer.\n- Colour interpolates smoothly through the palette along the stroke's length. Rainbow uses computed HSL.\n- Segment guides (solid fold lines, dashed mirror axes and the unit circle) fade in while drawing and ease out afterwards.\n- On mouse devices, symmetric ghost cursors show where every copy will land.\n- The clear dissolve samples the artwork's pixels into about 2,600 coloured particles that spiral outward, drift and fade. Meanwhile the old layer swells slightly and fades.\n- The dock uses glass (backdrop blur and saturation, hairline border, inset highlight) and switches to a light theme on Porcelain. The range sliders use gradient-filled tracks. Palette swatches are conic gradients and the brush icons are inline SVG.","originalInteraction":"- Draw with mouse, pen or touch. Pointer capture and coalesced events give smooth strokes, and the drawing continues even while the rosette is spinning.\n- Dock controls: the Folds slider (3–24), the mirror toggle, four brushes (neon, ink, stardust, ribbon), four palette swatches, a brush size slider, undo, clear (with the particle dissolve), spin, autodraw and PNG download.\n- Autodraw runs on load and yields as soon as the canvas is touched. It resumes after 30 s of idleness while enabled, and the Auto button toggles it. Undo after a clear restores the whole drawing.\n- Keyboard: 1–4 choose a brush, ↑/↓ change folds, [ ] change size, M mirror, R spin, A autodraw, Z or Ctrl/Cmd+Z undo, C clear, S save, P cycles the palette.\n- Save composes the background and the rotated artwork into a PNG with toBlob, falling back to a data URL.\n- Settings persist in guarded localStorage. On phones the dock wraps into three rows and can be collapsed with the chevron, which re-centres and enlarges the canvas. A status toast confirms actions, and reduced motion disables spin and the decorative animation.","sourceHash":"5686cb62168b43da9a4d5d0b946d73027d4530dd001c6de8bbf5e7695712db60","name":"对称花绘","category":"生成艺术","description":"一笔绘画被实时复制到多重旋转与镜像中，逐渐形成发光的花形。暗色画布让线条和色彩成为唯一焦点。","interaction":"直接绘制，调整对称份数与镜像；画面旋转时也可以继续落笔。"},{"id":"024","slug":"024-luxury-watch","title":"Maison Varenne — Calibre 01","file":"024-luxury-watch.html","textFile":"024-luxury-watch.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/024-luxury-watch.jpg","prompt":"Design a product page for \"Maison Varenne — Calibre 01\", a fictional minimalist luxury watch. Palette: ivory #f5f1ea, champagne #d8c3a5, charcoal #1c1c1c and a hint of midnight blue #1b2a41 for a dial variant. The hero is a large, photoreal-feeling SVG watch built entirely from gradients: a brushed-steel case with conical highlights, a domed sapphire reflection, applied indices with tiny shadows, dauphine hands showing the REAL current local time, and a smoothly sweeping seconds hand (8 beats per second). Typography: a refined high-contrast serif for headings with wide-tracked small sans captions. Sections with generous whitespace: an exploded view of the movement whose layers separate on scroll; a dial variant selector (ivory / midnight / salmon) that recolours the watch with a smooth transition; a specification table; and a \"Reserve\" CTA. Interaction: hovering the watch tilts it slightly and moves the sapphire reflection; a \"Night\" toggle shows lume glowing on the hands and indices. Quiet, precise animation — nothing gaudy.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A quiet, editorial product page for \"Maison Varenne, Calibre 01\", a fictional 39 mm automatic watch. The palette is ivory (#f5f1ea), champagne (#d8c3a5) and charcoal (#1c1c1c), with a midnight-blue (#1b2a41) dial variant. The hero splits into a high-contrast serif headline (\"Time, rendered quietly.\") on the left and a large gradient-built SVG watch on the right. The watch shows the viewer's real local time with faceted dauphine hands and a seconds hand that beats eight times a second (4 Hz). It opens in the classic 10:10:35 catalogue pose, then glides to the true time. Below the hero, a sticky scroll scene opens the watch into eight layers in 3D. Then comes a technical-drawing specification sheet, and finally a charcoal reservation desk. A Night toggle turns the page dark so that the luminescent hands and indices glow.","techniques":"- Brushed steel built procedurally as conical \"fans\" of 180 to 320 thin annular SVG sectors. Their brightness follows two-lobe cosine highlights plus seeded per-sector noise, which reads as radial brushing, and blurred specular arcs sit on top\n- Domed sapphire: a rim-weighted radial gradient plus a crescent glare path that slides opposite the 3D tilt. The floor shadow moves with it\n- Dial built from a CSS-variable radial gradient, a clipped 180-wedge sunray whose opacity is set per variant, and a rehaut inner shadow. Ivory, midnight and salmon recolour smoothly through transitions on stop-color and fill\n- Applied indices, each built from two facet rectangles whose light and dark overlays are computed from the index angle against a fixed top-left light. A blurred shadow copy is offset in screen space, and each index carries a lume pip\n- Dauphine hands are split into lit and shaded halves. Blurred shadow silhouettes sit at increasing offsets for hour, minute and seconds, to suggest the height of each hand\n- Night mode: CSS filter brightness on the case, dial and hands, plus a separate lume layer run through a two-radius Gaussian glow filter that fades in\n- Leather strap with a gradient, a grain pattern and contrast dashed stitching, faded into the page with SVG masks. The crown has knurled flutes and slides out when pulled\n- Exploded movement uses CSS 3D (preserve-3d, rotateX 62°). Eight hand-drawn SVG layers separate along the scroll: crystal, hands, dial, a perlage mainplate with cut-outs, the going train, bridges with a Côtes de Genève stripe pattern and ruby jewels, a swinging gold rotor with text on a path, and the caseback. Leader-line labels are placed by projecting each layer's depth through the perspective\n- The technical drawing combines plan and profile views with champagne dimension lines over a faint drafting grid","originalInteraction":"- Hover over the watch to tilt it in 3D (up to about 9°). The sapphire reflection and floor shadow move with the tilt and ease back when the pointer leaves\n- The dial radiogroup (three swatches, with arrow-key support) recolours the watch and the exploded dial and updates the name and note. The reservation form and localStorage stay in sync with the choice\n- Clicking the crown, or pressing Enter or Space on it, pulls it out and \"hacks\" the seconds hand to a stop, like a real watch. Pushing it back resynchronises to the live time\n- The Night toggle darkens the page and brings out the glowing lume. The label switches between Night and Day\n- Scrolling through \"The Movement\" opens the eight layers with an eased progress meter and slowly rotates the stack. Labels fade in one after another. The balance wheel beats at 4 Hz and the rotor swings\n- The Reserve form (dial, strap, boutique) shows an on-page confirmation with a fictional reference and stores it locally (guarded by try/catch). Nothing is transmitted\n- The nav turns frosted after scrolling, and anchor links scroll smoothly. prefers-reduced-motion removes the tilt, the intro glide and the animations, and shows the movement fully opened","sourceHash":"79cbd7176c925bde059366af8a0b9c9f67a021d2da70f93cd50c5612936cdabb","name":"静奢腕表","category":"交互网页","description":"象牙白、香槟色和炭黑托起一枚虚构自动腕表。表盘、蓝宝石反射与落地阴影随指针微微倾转。","interaction":"移动鼠标观察表体和反射；点选表盘色样，继续向下浏览产品细节。"},{"id":"025","slug":"025-isometric-city","title":"Pocket Metropolis","file":"025-isometric-city.html","textFile":"025-isometric-city.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/025-isometric-city.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Pocket Metropolis\", an isometric tiny-city builder toy. A canvas-rendered isometric grid (12×12 on desktop, fitted on mobile) in soft pastels: grass #a8d5a2, water #7ec8e3, roads #d6d0c4, and buildings in peach #ffb5a7, blush #fcd5ce, mint #b8e0d2 and lavender #cdb4db, with gentle ambient-occlusion shading on each block face. A bottom toolbar places houses, towers, trees, parks, roads and water; buildings pop in with a squash-and-stretch bounce. Little cars travel along connected road tiles and tiny people stroll in parks. A day/night cycle slowly shifts the lighting (sky gradient behind, windows lighting up at night, glowing streetlamps). A stats bubble shows population and happiness (parks near homes increase it). Interaction: click/tap tiles to place; right-click/long-press to bulldoze with a puff of dust; drag to pan; pinch/scroll to zoom; \"Random town\" seeds a charming city. Persist to localStorage. Typography: a rounded, friendly sans.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Pocket Metropolis is a small pastel city-builder that fits in your pocket. A 12×12 isometric island floats in a soft sky, with grassy tiles on a layered soil base and a blurred shadow underneath. Every object is drawn procedurally on a single canvas: gabled houses in peach, blush, mint and lavender with chimneys and doors, and pastel towers with window grids, pilasters and rooftop tanks, gardens or blinking antennas. There are round-canopy trees that sway, parks with winding paths, flowerbeds, fountains, benches and strollers, and roads that auto-join their markings, sidewalks and zebra crossings. Water tiles have sandy banks and drifting glints. Little cars keep to the right-hand lane of connected roads. A slow day/night cycle moves the sun and moon along an arc, tints every surface through an ambient-light model, flips side-lighting and shadow direction between morning and evening, and at night lights up windows, streetlamps, antennas and headlights. The HUD is set in a rounded, friendly sans and rendered as cream cards: a title card with a sun/moon dial clock, a speech-bubble stats card whose drawn face smiles or frowns with happiness, and a raised pastel toolbar.","techniques":"- Hand-rolled isometric projection on canvas with a camera transform (pan and zoom around the cursor), back-to-front painter's sorting of buildings, trees, lamps, cars and walkers by depth, and ground/shadow passes drawn first\n- Box primitive with per-face lighting: side faces take sun-dependent factors and vertical ambient-occlusion gradients toward the ground, plus a soft edge highlight; contact AO under building bases\n- Directional cast shadows projected on the ground; their length and direction follow the sun's elevation and azimuth\n- Keyframed day/night model: ambient RGB multiplies every material; sky gradients, twinkling stars, a sun with bloom, a crescent moon and drifting clouds\n- Emissive night layer: lit windows (hash-seeded on/off pattern), lamp light pools and halos, antenna beacons and car headlights, composited with 'lighter'\n- Auto-tiling roads from a neighbour bitmask: dashed centre lines, sidewalks and curb corners where the road ends, zebra crossings at junctions, and a roundabout on a lone tile\n- Squash-and-stretch pop-in (volume-preserving scale around the tile base), with a cascade from the centre on \"Random town\", expanding ground rings, and dust-puff particles when bulldozing\n- Procedurally generated towns: a meandering river with a bridge, two avenues and a cross street, towers near the centre, houses along roads, parks next to homes, and trees along riverbanks\n- Inline SVG isometric tool icons, a rotating sun/moon dial, and an animated smile path in the stats bubble","originalInteraction":"- Click or tap a tile to build with the selected tool (House, Tower, Tree, Park, Road, Water); building on the same type again re-rolls its look\n- Right-click or long-press (touch, with a haptic tick where available) to bulldoze with a puff of dust; the Bulldoze tool also works with a plain click\n- Drag to pan, mouse wheel or pinch to zoom around the pointer; hovering shows a dashed tile cursor and a translucent ghost of the building\n- Stats bubble: population comes from houses and towers; happiness is weighted by residents and rises with nearby parks, trees and water and road access, and drops for houses crowded by towers\n- Clock controls: pause/resume and 1×/6×/24× speed; \"Random town\" seeds a new city; the sound toggle controls synthesized pops and dust noise (Web Audio, created on the first gesture)\n- Keyboard: 1–7 select tools, arrows move a tile cursor on the focused map, Enter/Space builds, Delete/Backspace bulldozes, +/− zoom, R for a random town\n- The town, the day count and the time of day persist in guarded localStorage; animation pauses when the tab is hidden; reduced motion slows traffic and time and skips the bounce and glints","sourceHash":"024782d72eb94c6601ef72354212ba57ed6be4e449c891f06a40025998bd6b09","name":"口袋城市","category":"音乐与游戏","description":"一座粉彩等距小岛由 12×12 地块组成，住宅、高楼、树木、公园与道路可以逐格建起。","interaction":"选择工具后点击地块建设；重复放置同类物体会改变外观，右键或长按进行移除。"},{"id":"026","slug":"026-pulse-visualizer","title":"PULSE — Generative Audio Visualizer","file":"026-pulse-visualizer.html","textFile":"026-pulse-visualizer.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/026-pulse-visualizer.jpg","prompt":"Build \"PULSE — Generative Audio Visualizer\". On first click, a built-in generative electronic track starts, synthesized entirely with Web Audio (kick, hi-hat from filtered noise, a bassline and an arpeggiated pad in a minor key at ~110 BPM, evolving every 8 bars). An AnalyserNode drives the centrepiece: a radial frequency bloom — 128 bars around a circle with mirrored symmetry, a pulsating core that swells on each kick, orbiting particles that accelerate with high frequencies, and a smoky trail (canvas feedback with a slight zoom/rotation each frame). Before audio starts, an idle breathing version of the visual must already look beautiful. Palette: sunset-to-violet gradient (#ff9a3c → #ff3c78 → #7b2ff7 → #16d9e3) on deep plum-black #0c0612. Typography: wide, spaced uppercase sans for the title and a small monospace readout of BPM, bar count and track section. Controls: play/pause; mode switch between \"Bloom\", \"Tunnel\" (concentric rings rushing toward the viewer) and \"Horizon\" (mirrored waveform landscape); volume. Optional \"Use microphone\" button with a graceful fallback if permission is denied.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"PULSE is a full-screen generative music instrument and visualizer on deep plum-black #0c0612. A single canvas fills the viewport; before any sound it already breathes as a symmetric flower of 128 bars that fades orange #ff9a3c at the top through hot pink #ff3c78 and violet #7b2ff7 to cyan #16d9e3 at the bottom, with orbiting sparks smeared into smoky spiral trails. The first click starts an original track synthesized entirely in the browser (A minor, 110 BPM) that moves through a new section every 8 bars (Intro, Groove, Lift, Drop, Breakdown, Drop II, Drift, then cycles with variations), and the visuals switch from breathing to listening. Typography is a wide, widely tracked uppercase geometric sans with a sunset-gradient \"P U L S E\" wordmark, set against small monospace readouts; the controls live in a single frosted pill dock.","techniques":"- Canvas feedback loop: every frame the previous frame is copied to an offscreen buffer and redrawn slightly zoomed, rotated and faded (per-mode zoom/rotation/fade), which turns bars and particles into smoke, spirals and warp streaks; the ring's interior is damped each frame so the core stays a glowing core\n- Bloom: 128 bars around a circle using 64 log-spaced frequency bands mirrored left/right, drawn crisp over the smoke with a 256-step gradient lookup table, faint inward reflections, glow sprites on hot bar tips, a pre-rendered radial core sprite that swells on every kick, and 340 orbiting sprite particles whose angular speed scales with high-frequency energy\n- Tunnel: 28 spectrum-modulated rings on a perspective z-axis (radius = base / z) rushing toward the viewer with depth-based width, fade and hue cycling, plus radial speed streaks and a strong-zoom feedback for warp trails\n- Horizon: a 34-row scrolling history of the spectrum rendered as a perspective landscape (mountains highest at the bass centre), each row occlusion-filled then stroked, mirrored above the horizon as a faint reflection, a striped synthwave sun sprite, perspective guide lines and the live time-domain waveform drawn along the horizon\n- Idle mode synthesizes a breathing pseudo-spectrum and a slow heartbeat so the first frame is fully alive; live/idle crossfade smoothly on play/pause\n- Pre-rendered tinted glow sprites, typed arrays and pooled particles/rings (no per-frame allocation in the hot loops); canvas sized to viewport x devicePixelRatio (capped at 2 and a pixel budget)\n- Frosted-glass UI panels, CSS vignette and animated grain overlay, gradient-clipped title, conic-gradient play button, sliding gradient pill in the mode switch, live LO/MID/HI meters and a 7-segment section timeline with in-section progress","originalInteraction":"- First click/tap anywhere (or the play button) starts the Web Audio track: sine-sweep kick with sidechain ducking of the pad, band-passed noise hi-hats and open hats, clap and rim, saw+sub bassline, detuned-saw pad through a section-automated low-pass, and a square/triangle arpeggio through a dotted-eighth feedback delay and a generated-impulse convolution reverb; patterns, progressions (Am-F-C-G, Am-Dm-F-Em, F-G-Am) and arp shapes change every 8 bars\n- Readout shows BPM, bar count, current section and bar within it, and the audio source; kicks are taken from the scheduler's own event queue so the core pulses exactly on the beat\n- Play/pause (Space), mode switch Bloom/Tunnel/Horizon (radio group, arrow keys, or 1/2/3), volume slider (Arrow Up/Down), mute toggle (M), fullscreen; mode, volume and mute persist in guarded localStorage\n- \"Use microphone\" analyses live input with onset detection for kicks; if permission is denied or unavailable, a toast explains and the built-in track keeps playing\n- Clicking the canvas while playing fires a coloured shockwave ring from the pointer; audio suspends when the tab is hidden; prefers-reduced-motion lowers particle count and removes rotation/zoom drift","sourceHash":"b81f2a1a1245af43e98b8aea6ab848f3be6f48a355a305a2408ba66ea627920f","name":"脉冲可视化","category":"音乐与游戏","description":"橙色与紫色的频谱像花瓣一样呼吸，电子节奏、粒子和几何图形在同一全屏舞台里响应。","interaction":"点击播放启动生成音乐，观察视觉随节奏变化，再尝试场景中的控制项。"},{"id":"027","slug":"027-origami-crane","title":"Senbazuru — Fold a Paper Crane","file":"027-origami-crane.html","textFile":"027-origami-crane.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/027-origami-crane.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Senbazuru — Fold a Paper Crane\", an animated, step-by-step origami crane made with CSS 3D transforms or canvas 3D projection. A square of patterned washi paper (generated seigaiha waves or asanoha pattern in indigo #264653 on cream, with a coral #e76f51 reverse side) folds through about eight key stages — square base, bird base, narrowing folds, head fold, wings — with smooth hinge rotations, correct front/back faces and soft directional shading on each facet that changes with its angle. A clean Japanese-minimal layout: off-white #f7f3ec, lots of space, the step number as a large thin numeral, a one-line instruction for each step, and a progress rail of eight dots. Interaction: next/previous buttons, arrow keys and an auto-play toggle; drag to orbit the model; a picker for three washi patterns. Finale: the finished crane flaps gently and floats above a soft shadow while a counter reads \"1 of 1000\". Typography: an elegant serif plus tiny wide-tracked sans labels.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Senbazuru — Fold a Paper Crane\" folds a real, physically consistent paper crane in the browser. A square of washi (indigo #264653 pattern on cream, coral #e76f51 reverse) is simulated as rigid facets hinged along true crease lines. It passes through eight stages (square, triangle, square base by reverse-folding the corners, front petal fold, back petal fold / bird base, narrowing, inside-reverse-folded neck and tail, then a reverse-folded head and opening wings) and each fold animates as hinge rotations that keep the paper attached. Front and back faces are always correct, so the coral reverse appears exactly where real paper would show it: the lifted petals and the wings. The layout is Japanese-minimal on off-white #f7f3ec with plenty of space: a huge hairline-outline step numeral, the step name in italic serif, one line of instruction, quiet circular controls, three washi swatches, and an eight-dot progress rail under the model. A pale coral sun sits behind the paper, and a soft ground shadow sits beneath it. At the finale the crane flaps gently, floats and slowly turns, and a counter reads \"1 of 1000\" (it keeps counting cranes folded in this browser).","techniques":"- A small rigid-origami engine: each facet is a paper-space polygon with a 3x4 rigid transform; every fold splits the facets it crosses and rotates the moving side by pi about the crease line (60 facets by the end)\n- Folds are recorded as timed hinge rotations and composed in order during playback (kite folds then petal lift, back then front, neck then tail), so adjacent facets stay joined throughout the motion rather than being morphed\n- Layer order is tracked explicitly (reverse folds tuck flaps between layers) and converted to tiny per-layer offsets along the paper normal so stacked paper sorts correctly\n- Rendered with CSS 3D: every facet is two backface-hidden elements (washi front, coral back) with a matrix3d transform, a clip-path polygon grown ~0.6px to hide seams, and the browser's own 3D depth sorting\n- Procedural washi painted onto canvases: seigaiha waves, asanoha hemp-leaf star lattice and shippo interlocking circles, all set at 45 degrees to the diamond, plus paper fibres and a faint edge line so stacked layers read clearly; each facet samples its own region, so patterns stay continuous across folds\n- Per-facet directional lighting: the facet normal is taken through the camera rotation and dotted with a key light; the result drives a dark or warm-light overlay, so each facet's shade changes live with its angle\n- Camera direction per stage (it swings round behind the model for the back petal fold, then returns), auto-fitted to each stage's bounding box, with gentle idle sway\n- Finale: wings flap about their true hinge line, the crane bobs above a shadow that shrinks and fades as it rises, and it turns slowly like a hanging mobile\n- Outline numeral via -webkit-text-stroke, wide-tracked micro labels, a hand-drawn SVG hanko seal with a crane silhouette","originalInteraction":"- Auto-play starts shortly after load (not with reduced motion) and loops: fold, pause on each stage, fly for a while, then unfold and begin again; Pause/Auto-play toggle and the P or Space key control it\n- Previous / next buttons, Left/Right arrow keys, Home (unfold to the square) and End (jump to the crane); clicking a rail dot animates quickly through the intermediate folds to that step\n- Drag (mouse or touch) on the stage to orbit with inertia; double-click resets the view; with the stage focused, W A S D orbit and 0 resets\n- Washi picker (radio group, arrow keys supported) repaints the paper's textures instantly, mid-fold if you like; the choice persists in localStorage\n- The \"of 1000\" crane counter persists in localStorage (all storage access guarded with try/catch)\n- prefers-reduced-motion: no auto-play, shorter folds, no sway, turning, bobbing or flapping; animation pauses while the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"0eaf48ea9943e6668bbe5f96c6ef31acbac763abc010eaaf3bee4a4467d96c60","name":"折一只千纸鹤","category":"科学与模拟","description":"一张双面和纸沿真实折痕逐步变为纸鹤。刚性纸面与折叠铰链保留了纸张结构，而非简单图形变形。","interaction":"观看自动折叠，暂停观察中间步骤；使用阶段控制探索展开与成形过程。"},{"id":"028","slug":"028-periodic-table","title":"Elementa","file":"028-periodic-table.html","textFile":"028-periodic-table.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/028-periodic-table.jpg","prompt":"Design \"Elementa\", an interactive periodic table of all 118 elements with a premium dark look (#0d1117). Each tile shows atomic number, symbol and name, coloured by category with a harmonious palette (alkali metals #ff6b6b, alkaline earth #ffa94d, transition metals #ffd43b, post-transition #69db7c, metalloids #38d9a9, nonmetals #4dabf7, halogens #748ffc, noble gases #da77f2, lanthanides #f783ac, actinides #e599f7) at soft opacity with glowing borders. Use standard atomic weights, rounded sensibly. The layout follows the standard 18-column table with the f-block below; on narrow phones the table pans horizontally inside its own scroll container (the page itself must not overflow). Interaction: hovering or focusing a tile fills a large detail panel with an animated Bohr-model shell diagram (electrons orbiting on shells with correct per-shell counts), category, atomic mass and state at room temperature; the category legend buttons filter and highlight; a search box jumps to an element. Keyboard navigable with arrow keys.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Elementa is a premium dark (#0d1117) periodic table of all 118 elements. It follows the standard 18-column layout with the lanthanide and actinide rows set below, dashed \"57–71\" and \"89–103\" placeholders in group 3, group numbers across the top and period numbers down the side. Each tile is a softly tinted glass square in its category colour (the ten brief hexes at low opacity with glowing borders). It shows the atomic number in mono, the symbol in a geometric sans with a colour glow, and the name. On desktop the large detail panel sits inside the table's natural gap above the transition metals. The panel pairs an animated Bohr diagram with the element's identity, standard atomic weight, state at about 20 °C, period and group, block, full electron configuration and a K–Q shell ladder. The page's ambient glow, the panel wash and the group/period highlights all take on the colour of the element you are looking at. On phones and small tablets the panel moves above the table, which pans horizontally inside its own scroller.","techniques":"- All 118 elements come from a compact data string (symbol, name, IUPAC standard atomic weight rounded, category, room-temperature state). Grid positions, period, group and block are derived from the atomic number.\n- Electron shells are computed at runtime: Madelung (aufbau) filling plus a hard-coded table of the 20 known exceptions (Cr, Cu, Nb, Mo, Ru, Rh, Pd, Ag, La, Ce, Gd, Pt, Au, Ac, Th, Pa, U, Np, Cm, Lr). This gives correct per-shell counts (for example Au 2·8·18·32·18·1, Lr 2·8·18·32·32·8·3) and noble-gas-core configurations with superscripts. The results were checked against reference values for all 118 elements.\n- The Bohr diagram is SVG: faint dashed ghost orbits for all seven shells, real orbits in the category colour, electrons with soft halos spaced evenly per shell, and a golden-angle nucleon cluster. Shells spring in one after another, then counter-rotate at speeds that slow with distance via requestAnimationFrame. Rotation pauses when the tab is hidden or reduced motion is on.\n- Tiles use CSS custom properties (--rgb per category) for gradient fills, borders, symbol glow and hover. They cascade in diagonally on load. Hover lifts a tile with a bloom and reveals its atomic mass. The pinned tile carries a glowing dot, and search jumps trigger a flash ring.\n- The detail panel is placed in the table gap as a size-contained grid item (contain: strict) so it never distorts the rows. It has a category-tinted radial wash, a hairline top highlight, state icons (solid, liquid, gas, unknown) and animated shell-occupancy bars.\n- Category filtering dims and desaturates every non-member while members pick up a glowing outline. Legend chips preview a category on mouse hover.\n- A faint blueprint grid and a colour-reactive top glow sit behind the page. On narrow screens the horizontal scroller fades at its edge until you reach the end.","originalInteraction":"- Hover (mouse) previews an element in the detail panel. Leaving the table returns to the pinned element. Click, tap or keyboard focus pins an element.\n- Keyboard: the table uses a roving tabindex. The arrow keys move to the nearest element in that direction, jumping gaps and moving into and out of the f-block. Home and End go to the ends of a row, and Enter or Space pins. Pinning announces the element's key facts through a polite live region.\n- Legend chips are toggle buttons (aria-pressed) that filter by category. \"All elements\" resets, and Esc clears the filter. The group-3 placeholders toggle the lanthanide or actinide filter.\n- Search is an accessible combobox. It matches atomic number, symbol or name (exact first, then prefix, then substring) and lists up to seven results with the arrow keys. Enter or a click jumps to the element: the page scrolls it into view in the pan container, focuses and pins it, and flashes it. The \"/\" key focuses search from anywhere.\n- Group and period labels light up in the category colour for the element on show.","sourceHash":"67d29e5159ade388a6d922522fe99e8a70f120f561b5f9a6c101d4b399204882","name":"元素图谱","category":"数据与工具","description":"118 种元素组成完整周期表，暗色界面中以分类色和细致数字突出结构。详情面板随选择更新。","interaction":"悬停预览元素，点击固定详情；使用键盘在表格中移动选择。"},{"id":"029","slug":"029-typewriter-letters","title":"Carriage Return — A Typewriter for Letters","file":"029-typewriter-letters.html","textFile":"029-typewriter-letters.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/029-typewriter-letters.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Carriage Return — A Typewriter for Letters\", an interactive typewriter writing experience featuring a fictional machine, the \"Marlowe 44\". A three-quarter illustrated typewriter (SVG; mint-green enamel #9fc9b6 with cream keys and chrome details) sits at the bottom, and a sheet of textured paper (#faf6ec) feeds out above it. Typing on the physical keyboard (or tapping on-screen keys) stamps characters onto the paper with realistic imperfections: slight baseline jitter, varied ink density, an occasional faint double strike, ribbon texture. The carriage visibly shifts left as you type, a bell dings near the right margin, and Enter triggers a carriage return with the lever animation and a line feed. Synthesized sounds (Web Audio clicks and bell) after the first gesture, with a mute toggle. Typography: a typewriter monospace stack (\"Courier Prime\", \"American Typewriter\", \"Courier New\", \"Nimbus Mono PS\", monospace). Extras: a red/black ribbon toggle, \"New sheet\" pulls the paper out with an animation, and \"Save letter\" downloads a PNG of the page. A warm desk background with wood-grain gradients and soft lamplight.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Carriage Return puts a fictional mint-green portable, the Marlowe 44, on a lamplit walnut desk. It is drawn in hand-written SVG in a slight three-quarter view: an enamel shell (#9fc9b6) with a darker side cheek, chrome-rimmed cream keys on visible stems, ribbon spools in chrome cups, a fan of typebars in the basket and a script badge. A sheet of #faf6ec paper stands in the machine the way paper really does. The unfed rear part leans back behind the platen in perspective, while the typed front part rises from the platen and grows a line at a time. Every keystroke stamps a character onto a 2× paper canvas with deliberate imperfections, and the carriage (platen, knobs, bail, margin scale and paper) steps left under a fixed printing point. The bell rings six spaces from the margin; Return swings the chrome lever and glides the carriage home with a line feed. The desk is procedural walnut planks with knots and a dried coffee ring, lit by a warm lamp and drifting dust motes. Beside the machine sit a ruled operator's index card, a pencil and a shipping tag that tracks the sheet, line, column and keystrokes. The first sheet arrives already dated, with a short salutation.","techniques":"- Layered machine: rear sheet, platen, front sheet, bail/scale/knobs/lever, body and printing point are separate absolutely positioned layers that move together, so the paper really sits between platen and bail\n- Rear sheet in CSS perspective (rotateX) whose height shrinks as lines feed; the front sheet is a clipped canvas that translates up one line per Return, with a curl shadow where it wraps the platen\n- Character stamping: each glyph is drawn onto a small offscreen plate with ink bleed (shadow blur), a slight random rotation and baseline jitter, then a fabric-ribbon weave is knocked out with destination-out; some strikes print lighter on one half; the plate is multiplied onto the paper at varied density, with occasional faint double strikes\n- Deterministic per-stroke seeds, so a restored letter re-stamps identically\n- Procedural paper: cloudy formation, about 900 curved fibres, speckle grain, edge toning and a faint fictional \"Marlowe Bond\" watermark\n- Carriage motion by transform with an escapement-snap easing, and a slower overshooting glide on return; on narrow phones the travel is split between the carriage and the printing point so the sheet stays on screen\n- A margin scale built at runtime so its ticks line up exactly with paper columns, a bell marker, and a red column pointer\n- SVG gradients for chrome, enamel, the rubber platen and cream key caps; a typebar strike flash and a spark at the printing point; a \"ding\" flourish that floats up at the bell\n- Procedural walnut desk canvas (planks, sine-warped grain, knots, seams), lamplight radial gradients, and a rAF dust-mote layer that pauses when the tab is hidden","originalInteraction":"- Type on a physical keyboard: printable keys stamp characters, Space advances, Enter is a carriage return plus line feed, Backspace steps back without erasing (overstrike like a real machine), Tab jumps to the next five-space stop\n- Tap or click the illustrated keys (with Shift and Shift Lock), the space bar, or the carriage-return lever itself\n- On phones, tap the sheet or \"Keys\" to type with the native keyboard through a hidden textarea\n- The margin locks at column 48: further keys pile up at the margin until you return; the bell sounds once per line near the end\n- Ribbon switch: Black or Red changes the ink of later keystrokes and raises the ribbon vibrator\n- Sound: Web Audio key clacks (filtered noise plus a pitch-dropping thump), space thud, a multi-partial bell, a ratchet-and-slide carriage return and a paper swoosh; it starts on the first gesture, and the mute state is remembered\n- New sheet pulls the paper up and out, then feeds a fresh numbered sheet; Save letter downloads the paper canvas as a PNG\n- The letter, carriage position, ribbon and sheet number persist in localStorage (guarded); reduced motion shortens every transition and animation","sourceHash":"c2bb9a5a6f3dac34cdc5623a47761d6b03de081542ddecbcb9c16980449e08de","name":"打字机来信","category":"排版实验","description":"薄荷绿便携打字机放在灯下木桌上，金属键帽、纸张、字锤和回车动作共同还原机械书写。","interaction":"直接使用键盘输入；Enter 回车换行，Backspace 后退但不擦除，可叠印字符。"},{"id":"030","slug":"030-gravity-sandbox","title":"Orbital — N-Body Gravity Playground","file":"030-gravity-sandbox.html","textFile":"030-gravity-sandbox.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/030-gravity-sandbox.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Orbital — N-Body Gravity Playground\". A full-screen canvas of deep space (#03040a) with a subtle nebula (layered noise gradients in #1b1464 and #5f0f40) and parallax stars. Click and drag to fling a new body: the drag vector sets its velocity, shown as a dotted aiming line with a predicted trajectory that accounts for existing masses. Bodies attract with softened Newtonian gravity, leave glowing fading trails, and merge on collision (conserving momentum and mass, mixing colours) with a flash. Colour and glow reflect mass (small icy blue → large hot orange/white). Presets: \"Binary star\", \"Solar system\", \"Figure-eight three-body\", \"Galaxy collision\" (hundreds of light particles around two cores). A minimal monospace HUD shows body count, total energy and simulation speed. Controls: mass selector for new bodies, pause, slow motion, trail length, clear, and \"follow\" (camera tracks the heaviest body). Scroll/pinch to zoom, right-drag or two-finger drag to pan. Use a fixed-timestep velocity-Verlet integrator.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Orbital is a full-screen n-body gravity playground set in deep space (#03040a). A procedurally generated nebula of domain-warped value noise in indigo (#1b1464) and plum (#5f0f40), crossed by dark dust lanes, sits behind three parallax star layers that twinkle and drift as the camera moves. Bodies attract with softened Newtonian gravity, integrated by a fixed-timestep velocity-Verlet scheme; they leave glowing trails that fade along their length and merge on contact, conserving mass and momentum, blending their colours and releasing a flash and shock ring. Colour and glow follow mass on a logarithmic scale, from icy blue pebbles through white to hot orange giants with soft diffraction spikes. The page opens on a live galaxy collision: two cores, each wrapped in a disk of 420 massless dust particles on circular orbits, fall together and throw out tidal debris. A quiet mission-control HUD in monospace and a single floating dock hold every control.","techniques":"- Canvas 2D at viewport × devicePixelRatio (capped at 2), with a world-space camera (pan, zoom, follow) applied through setTransform for trails and manual projection for sprites.\n- Offscreen nebula generated once from 5-octave fbm with domain warping and a dust-lane mask, drawn with slow parallax; three star layers with different parallax factors, tints, twinkle and cross-flares on the brightest.\n- Additive ('lighter') compositing for radial-gradient glows sized by mass, trail polylines drawn in 12 fading chunks from ring buffers, dust particles as motion streaks built from their velocity, and merge flashes.\n- Solid body cores with an offset radial gradient: a lit limb and dark terminator for cool bodies, a white-hot centre for stars; a one-time pulse ring on freshly placed bodies.\n- Aiming overlay: dotted drag vector with arrowhead and speed label, a ghost of the new body, and a fading dotted predicted trajectory. The prediction integrates the new body together with every existing mass (a fixed field above 40 bodies) and ends in an impact marker if it would collide.\n- HUD with body and dust counts, total energy (kinetic plus softened potential), speed, zoom, sim time, relative energy drift and a drift sparkline showing the integrator's bounded error.\n- Geometric wide-tracked wordmark, hairline panels, mass swatches whose dot size and colour match the simulation's mass palette.","originalInteraction":"- Drag on empty space to fling a body: it spawns at the press point and its velocity follows the drag vector. A tap without dragging drops it into a circular orbit around the strongest local attractor, in the same direction as that attractor's companions.\n- Mass selector (radio group, arrow-key navigable): 5, 40, 250, 1.2k and 5k.\n- Presets: Binary star with circumbinary planets and a disrupted dust ring; Solar system with six planets, a moon around the giant and an asteroid belt; the Chenciner–Montgomery figure-eight three-body orbit; Galaxy collision.\n- Pause/play (Space), slow motion ×0.25 (S), follow the heaviest body (F), clear (C), trail-length slider ([ and ]), presets 1–4, zoom with + and −, pan with the arrow keys, and optional synthesized sound (M) for launches and merger thumps, off by default.\n- Mouse wheel zooms around the cursor; right-drag, middle-drag or Shift-drag pans. On touch, pinch zooms and a two-finger drag pans, cancelling any aim in progress.\n- Rendering stops while the tab is hidden; prefers-reduced-motion slows the simulation and removes twinkle, shock rings, birth pulses and camera easing.","sourceHash":"883b2d9921874317b17aaed789bb23e20b75848b5703c636c2823aaaa7785d36","name":"引力沙盒","category":"科学与模拟","description":"星云与深空中运行着一个多体引力系统。新天体的质量、初速度与已有轨道共同影响接下来的运动。","interaction":"在空白处拖动发射天体；轻点可让新天体进入附近主天体的环绕轨道。"},{"id":"031","slug":"031-twenty-four-seasons","title":"Nijūshi Sekki — The Twenty-Four Seasons","file":"031-twenty-four-seasons.html","textFile":"031-twenty-four-seasons.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/031-twenty-four-seasons.jpg","prompt":"Design \"Nijūshi Sekki — The Twenty-Four Seasons\", a contemplative calendar of the traditional East Asian 24 solar terms. A large circular SVG dial divided into 24 arcs at 15° intervals of the sun's ecliptic longitude, each filled with a traditional Japanese colour suited to that part of the year (e.g. sakura-iro #fef4f4, moegi #aacf53, ruri #1e50a2, kuchiba #e0c38c, kakishibu). The current date's term is marked by a small sun computed from today's date (approximate dates are fine; say so). Selecting a term shows its romanized name, kanji and English meaning (e.g. Risshun 立春 \"Beginning of Spring\"), its approximate date range, a short poetic description, and a softly animated seasonal generative motif behind the text (petals, rain, fireflies, falling leaves, snow). Typography: vertical Japanese text using writing-mode for the kanji and an elegant serif for English. Palette: washi white #f8f5ee and sumi #2b2b2b, tinted by the selected term's colour. Interaction: click arcs, arrow keys to step through, a \"Today\" button. Responsive: the dial scales and the detail panel moves below it on phones.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Nijūshi Sekki is a contemplative calendar built around a large SVG volvelle of the twenty-four solar terms. The wheel is laid out in the traditional East Asian way, with spring (east) on the left, summer (south) at the top, autumn on the right and winter at the bottom. It is divided into 24 arcs, one for every 15° of the sun's ecliptic longitude, and each arc is painted in a traditional Japanese colour suited to its time of year (kōbai-iro plum pink, sakura-iro, moegi, fuji-iro, ruri-iro, himawari-iro, kikyō-iro, akane-iro, kakishibu-iro, kon-iro and more), with the term's kanji standing upright in it. Around the wheel run 72 fine ticks for the microseasons (kō), a month ring placed at the sun's true longitude on the first of each month, and today's sun: a vermilion disc computed from the date, with a dashed hand to the centre. At the heart of the wheel, a brush-drawn ensō holds the season's kanji. The whole washi page (#f8f5ee with kozo fibres, sumi #2b2b2b ink) is tinted by the selected term's colour and sky, and a quiet generative motif drifts behind everything. To the right (below on phones), the selected term appears as large vertical kanji with a vermilion seal, its romanized name with macrons, the English meaning, its approximate date range and longitude span, an original short description, one of its three traditional microseasons and its colour swatch. A footnote says plainly that the dates are approximate.","techniques":"- A procedural SVG dial: annular-sector arcs from polar maths, 72 microseason ticks, month labels positioned with a low-precision solar-longitude formula, and seasonal kanji placed on the equinox and solstice axes\n- The wheel rotates like a volvelle so that the selected arc sits under a fixed vermilion index (at the right on desktop, at the bottom on phones). It is animated by attribute in one requestAnimationFrame loop, and every label is counter-rotated so the kanji stay upright throughout\n- The selected arc springs outward on an underdamped spring, hovered arcs lift slightly, and on load the arcs unfold in sequence while the wheel turns a quarter-circle round to today\n- An astronomy-based \"today\": the sun's ecliptic longitude uses the Astronomical Almanac approximation, and each term's start and end come from solving for the moment the sun crosses its 15° boundary (Newton-style iteration), shown in Japan Standard Time\n- A generated ensō: a closed brush-stroke outline with a varying width profile, a thick loaded start and a tapered, dry-brush tail of dashed arcs\n- Page tinting through registered @property colour custom properties (--tint, --sky, --accent, --term), so the whole washi page and sky gradient cross-fade to the new term's colour\n- A kozo-fibre washi texture drawn once to a canvas tile and multiplied over the page\n- A full-page canvas particle engine with cross-fading layers and 15 motif kinds: plum and cherry petals (with and without a notch) that flip in 3D, maple, ginkgo, bamboo and round leaves, soft sprite snow, slanted rain with ripples, drifting mist, glowing fireflies at dusk, rising motes, fluttering butterflies, swallows, a V of wild geese, red dragonflies that dart and hover, twinkling dew, wind streaks, winter stars, and dandelion or cotton fluff\n- Vertical Japanese typography with writing-mode: vertical-rl in a Mincho stack, with a clip-path \"brush\" reveal character by character and a seal that stamps in","originalInteraction":"- Click or tap any arc to select its term. The wheel turns it under the index, the page re-tints and the motif cross-fades\n- Drag the ring to spin the wheel freely by hand. On release it snaps to the term under the index\n- ← → (or ↑ ↓) step through the terms, Home and End jump to the first and last, T returns to today. Arcs are focusable (roving tabindex), and Enter or Space selects\n- The Previous and Next buttons and the \"Today\" button (shown as pressed while today's term is selected) sit beneath the dial\n- A polite live region announces each new term. Focus rings are visible\n- Reduced motion turns the wheel instantly, skips the unfold and brush reveals, and slows and thins the particles","sourceHash":"a559ced7884093d3da1c72c1a4aa8353ecfa0bbe1b70d552c351b49ea4b3d15d","name":"二十四节气轮","category":"数据与工具","description":"二十四节气被排成一只可转动的日历圆盘，季节色彩与象征图案随选择缓慢过渡。","interaction":"点击节气或拖动圆环；松手后圆盘吸附到相应节气，页面随之换色。"},{"id":"032","slug":"032-holographic-card","title":"Mythic — Holographic Card","file":"032-holographic-card.html","textFile":"032-holographic-card.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/032-holographic-card.jpg","prompt":"Create \"MYTHIC — Holographic Card\", a premium collectible trading card with realistic holographic foil. One large card centred on a dark velvet background (#0e0b16 with a soft spotlight). The card art depicts an original creature, \"Lumidrake, the Prism Wyrm\", composed entirely of SVG shapes: a stylised crystalline dragon silhouette over a starburst. The foil: layered conic/linear rainbow gradients with color-dodge/overlay blend modes, a procedural glitter texture, and a diagonal glare band — all driven by pointer position so the rainbow shifts and the glare slides as the card tilts in 3D (rotateX/Y with spring easing and perspective). An embossed frame, a stat block (ATK/DEF, rarity stars), italic-serif flavour text and a set number \"077/250\". Interaction: pointer tilt (touch-drag on mobile); click to flip the card to its patterned back; a \"Pack opening\" button plays a short sequence in which a foil pack tears open and the card slides out with a flash. Foil palette: iridescent pastels over deep indigo art.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"MYTHIC is a fictional trading-card game. The page presents a single Holo Rare, Lumidrake, the Prism Wyrm (No. 077/250 from the invented Prism Tide set), like a museum piece under a soft purple spotlight on dark velvet (#0e0b16). The card is a real 3D object: it sways gently on its own, tilts toward the pointer on a damped spring, lifts as it flips to a guilloche-patterned back, and every foil layer reads its live rotation. The art is entirely SVG and mostly procedural. A faceted crystal dragon (a Catmull-Rom spine extruded into lit triangle facets, with shard wings, back spikes, horns and claws) sits over a forty-ray starburst, with a white beam entering its chest and leaving as a seven-colour spectrum, all on deep indigo. Around it is an embossed silver-pastel frame, engraved name and type plates, a pearl text box with an ability and an italic serif flavour line, ATK/DEF plaques, five rarity stars and the set number. Side plaques in a spaced serif and a floating pill toolbar complete the gallery setting.","techniques":"- CSS 3D card: perspective, preserve-3d, two backface-hidden faces; rotateX/rotateY, flip and lift all driven by spring physics in requestAnimationFrame\n- The live rotation is converted into CSS custom properties (--px, --py, --hyp, --bp) on each face (mirrored on the back), so every foil layer moves with the tilt\n- Frame foil: a silver metallic gradient multiplied by a travelling pastel rainbow, plus an overlay cross-hatch \"etched\" texture\n- Art holo: repeating rainbow bands hard-light blended with diagonal etch stripes and applied with mix-blend-mode color-dodge; its strength follows how far the card is tilted\n- Glitter: two procedurally generated sparkle sheets (canvas to data URI) with parallax offsets so they twinkle against each other, radially masked around the light point\n- Diagonal glare band (soft-light) that slides across the card, plus an overlay radial glare hotspot\n- Foil lettering: the name is a clipped gradient that shifts with the tilt; the plates are embossed with layered inset and outset shadows\n- Procedural SVG: faceted wyrm with hue running from cyan to pink along the body, wings and horns, starburst, star field and spectrum fan; the back uses parametric rosette (guilloche) curves and a sine-wave border\n- Velvet room: a canvas-made nap texture in overlay blend, vignette, a blurred spotlight cone, dust motes that brighten inside the beam, a blurred conic rainbow halo behind the card and a contact shadow that shifts with the tilt\n- Booster pack in the same foil language, with crimped edges and one shared jagged tear line (clip-path polygons); the Web Animations API runs the enter, shake, tear, rise, flash and settle\n- Sparkle burst particles (pooled) on the flash, and a few sparkles on each flip","originalInteraction":"- Mouse: move over the card to tilt it; the foil, glitter, glare and halo all respond. After a short pause the card returns to a slow idle sway\n- Touch: drag on the card to tilt (touch-action none), tap to flip\n- Click or tap the card, the Flip button, or press F / Enter / Space (card focused) to flip to the patterned back\n- Arrow keys tilt the focused card\n- \"Open a pack\" button or P: the card fades away, a foil booster springs in, crinkles and shakes, tears along its jagged seam, the card slides out, a white flash and sparkle burst follow, the wrapper drops away and the card settles back to centre\n- Synthesized Web Audio effects (foil crinkle, tear, flip swish, bell chime) start only after a gesture; a speaker toggle mutes them and the setting is saved in localStorage (guarded with try/catch)\n- Announcements through an aria-live region; animation pauses when the tab is hidden; reduced-motion mode stops the idle sway and motes, stiffens the springs and shortens the pack sequence to simple fades","sourceHash":"0980c8aa895e8f583983e6bae887251e1aa262d2aa190728052108c558598a14","name":"全息龙卡","category":"生成艺术","description":"虚构卡牌游戏中的稀有龙卡被陈列在紫色聚光灯下，虹彩薄膜、闪粉与表面光泽随角度改变。","interaction":"移动鼠标或触摸拖动卡片，观察倾角对全息反射与光晕的影响。"},{"id":"033","slug":"033-stone-labyrinth","title":"Labyrinthos","file":"033-stone-labyrinth.html","textFile":"033-stone-labyrinth.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/033-stone-labyrinth.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Labyrinthos\", a maze game styled as an ancient stone labyrinth seen from above. Generate a new perfect maze each round (recursive backtracker or Wilson's algorithm), rendered on canvas as carved terracotta/sandstone walls (#c4773b, #e2b07a, with highlight and shadow edges for relief) on a darker dusty floor (#6b4a2b), with subtle noise texture and a Greek-key meander border. The player is a small flickering flame that lights only a radius around it — the rest of the maze is dark (fog of war that dimly remembers visited corridors). Controls: arrow keys/WASD, swipes on touch, and an on-screen d-pad on mobile. The exit is a glowing golden thread spool. Features: the maze visibly carves itself at the start of each round; a \"Show Ariadne's thread\" hint animates the solution path (BFS) as a red thread; a timer; three sizes; and a victory screen with a laurel illustration. Typography: an inscriptional Roman-capitals feeling serif with wide spacing.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Labyrinthos\" is a torch-lit maze game built as a carved stone tablet seen from above, set in a dark umber chamber between two pedimented steles. Every round starts in full light: a sandstone slab, scored with the mason's setting-out lines, is cut into a perfect maze before your eyes, with a glowing chisel spark and flying stone chips following the recursive backtracker as it winds through the grid. Then darkness falls. You are a small flickering flame at the entrance whose light reveals only a warm circle of terracotta walls and dusty floor, while the corridors you have already passed stay dimly remembered and a thin golden thread traces the path you have walked. In the far corner a golden thread spool glows like a beacon. The frame is a recessed channel carrying a relief Greek-key meander with rosettes at the corners. Inscriptional Roman capitals with wide spacing, Latin labels (TEMPVS, GRADVS, OPTIMVM, EXITVS INVENTVS) and a gilded laurel wreath on the victory screen complete the antique mood.","techniques":"- Procedural textures built once per round: multi-octave mottling made by upscaling tiny random canvases, per-pixel grain, pebbles and hairline cracks for the floor (#6b4a2b); a sandstone gradient (#e2b07a to #c4773b) with bedding strata for the walls\n- Relief walls: a single Path2D of every wall tile clips the sandstone texture; a blurred cast shadow falls to the lower right; per-edge highlight and shadow bevels, block joints and chisel scratches; walls are narrower than corridors for a carved look\n- Greek-key meander drawn along all four sides by rotating one strip routine, stroked three times (shadow, highlight, face) for relief, set in a recessed channel with carved rosette corner blocks\n- Carving animation: the finished maze is revealed tile by tile over an uncut slab in the exact order the backtracker carved it, led by a radial chisel spark and pooled chip particles\n- Fog of war: a half-resolution fog canvas is filled with darkness, then \"destination-out\" erases a flickering radial light around the flame, a softer halo around the spool and a smoothed per-tile memory map, so visited corridors stay dimly lit\n- Warm torchlight tint over the lit circle; flame drawn from layered Bezier teardrops with swaying tip, blue base and rising embers\n- Ariadne's thread: BFS path through chamber centres with a deterministic wobble, drawn with rounded quadratic joins, a glow shadow and a highlight strand, unspooling progressively to the spool\n- The player's own trail is a pale gold thread beneath the fog, visible only where there is light or memory\n- Golden spool drawn in canvas with wooden flanges, animated thread wraps, a loose end and a pulsing glow; gold particle burst on victory\n- HTML steles with a clipped triangular pediment, incised text shadows, gradient-clipped title lettering and an SVG meander keyline; SVG laurel wreath built from one leaf shape reused along an arc and mirrored","originalInteraction":"- Arrow keys or WASD walk one chamber per press and keep walking while held; Shift plus a direction runs along a corridor, following bends until a junction\n- Touch: swipe on the maze to run a corridor, tap to step toward the tapped side; an on-screen stone d-pad appears on phones and touch devices (hold to keep walking)\n- Mouse: click on the maze to step toward the click\n- Three sizes (Small 10x10, Medium 16x16, Large 24x24, with Latin names and Roman numerals); each change or \"New labyrinth\" (N) generates and carves a fresh perfect maze\n- \"Show Ariadne's thread\" (T, or the thread button on phones) animates the BFS solution from your current position and keeps it updated as you move; using it is noted on the victory screen and excludes the run from best times\n- Timer starts on the first step; steps are counted; the best unaided time per size is kept in localStorage (guarded with try/catch); the round number is shown in Roman numerals\n- Victory dialog with laurel wreath, time, steps, how the exit was found, a new-best note and a button to enter another labyrinth\n- Optional Web Audio sound (off by default, visible toggle): stone footsteps, a dull thud on walls, grinding while the maze is carved, and a plucked lyre arpeggio on victory\n- Walking into a wall gives a small bump animation; screen-reader announcements for new rounds, the lit torch, the thread and the victory\n- prefers-reduced-motion: instant carving, steady flame, no particles or bump, thread shown at once","sourceHash":"6f5a0ae46b385dc73b39141e37bf98e36b50558925ba45af4774bd1235ffe609","name":"石刻迷宫","category":"音乐与游戏","description":"迷宫被雕成一块置于暗室中的砂岩石板。火光和有限的可见范围让路线探索带有考古般的氛围。","interaction":"用方向键或 WASD 行走；Shift 加方向键沿走廊快跑，手机可用滑动操作。"},{"id":"034","slug":"034-flow-field-studio","title":"Currents — Flow Field Studio","file":"034-flow-field-studio.html","textFile":"034-flow-field-studio.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/034-flow-field-studio.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Currents — Flow Field Studio\", generative art made of thousands of particles following a Perlin/simplex noise vector field (implement the noise inline). Particles leave long, fine, semi-transparent strokes that accumulate into silky flowing textures like wind maps or marbled paper. Five curated palettes, each with its own background: \"Kyoto Dusk\" (#1a1423; #e8a87c #d45d79 #6e5773 #ea9085), \"Glacier\" (#f4f7f6; #264653 #2a9d8f #8ab17d), \"Ember\" (#0d0d0d; #ff4800 #ff7b00 #ffb700), \"Riso\" (paper #f2eee3; fluorescent pink #ff48b0 and blue #0078bf with multiply blending) and \"Mono Ink\". A slim, elegant side panel holds noise scale, particle count, stroke length, curl/turbulence, palette, a seed field, \"Regenerate\" and \"Download PNG\". Clicking the canvas plants an attractor/vortex that bends the field locally; the piece slowly evolves over time (the noise's z-axis). Typography: understated monospace labels and a serif title. Must stay performant (typed arrays, batched paths).\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Currents is a quiet generative studio: the artwork fills the window edge to edge and a slim, typographically calm side panel sits to its right (a pull-up sheet on phones). Thousands of particles ride a seeded 3D simplex-noise vector field and lay down long, hair-fine, semi-transparent strokes that pile up into silky ribbons wherever the currents converge, like wind maps or marbled paper. The field's z-axis drifts slowly, so the piece keeps evolving, and a very slow, periodic fade keeps it from silting up. Five curated palettes each re-theme the whole room: Kyoto Dusk (dusky violet ground, peach, rose, mauve, salmon), Glacier (pale ice ground, slate, teal, moss), Ember (near-black ground with additive orange light), Riso (cream paper, fluorescent pink and blue multiplied like a risograph print, with paper grain) and Mono Ink (sumi-black ink on warm paper). The title is set in a book serif with an italic accent, controls are labelled in understated monospace, and a museum-style caption names each piece (\"Currents, No. 0860\") from its seed.","techniques":"- Inline, seeded 3D simplex noise (Gustavson-style) drives the flow angle, with a curl multiplier and an optional finer turbulence octave blended in\n- Typed-array particle state (Float32Array positions, Int16Array lifetimes, Uint8Array colour) and one batched path per pigment per frame: particles are grouped into contiguous colour ranges so each colour is a single stroke() call with thousands of polyline segments\n- Colour regions from a second, slower noise field with quantile thresholds, so pigments flow in territories like marbling instead of averaging into one tone\n- Palette-specific compositing: source-over for Kyoto Dusk and Glacier, additive \"lighter\" for Ember, multiply for Riso and Mono Ink\n- Periodic low-alpha background fades (every 24 frames) that keep trails long but avoid 8-bit ghosting\n- Procedural paper-grain canvas layered with a CSS blend mode on light palettes and composited into the exported PNG\n- Vortices as local tangential plus inward forces with a quadratic falloff; they are drawn on a separate overlay canvas with rotating dashed rings and spin arrows\n- A time-boxed pre-roll and a warm-up phase with extra substeps, so the first frame already reads as a finished textile\n- A cover-fit repaint on resize that keeps the painting rather than wiping it\n- DPR-aware canvases (capped at 2); the whole UI re-themes through CSS custom properties with eased colour transitions","originalInteraction":"- Click or tap the canvas to plant a vortex that bends the field locally; new vortices alternate spin, Shift reverses one, clicking an existing vortex lifts it (up to 12)\n- Live sliders for noise scale, curl/turbulence, particle count (500–8,000) and stroke length, with accent-filled tracks and monospace readouts\n- Five palette swatches (keys 1–5); a seed field (Enter to apply) plus a dice button for a new named seed; \"Regenerate\" re-renders the current seed reproducibly\n- \"Download PNG\" exports the canvas plus grain via toBlob; a toast confirms the file name\n- Pause/Flowing toggle, \"Clear vortices\", and keys R, N, Space, C and S\n- On phones the panel is a bottom sheet with a Controls toggle; the choice of palette and seed is remembered with guarded localStorage; the loop stops while the tab is hidden, and reduced-motion slows the drift and substeps","sourceHash":"59857631b0d2cfbebe0a67d4739910b8f37b3f8e19506d022f6f49a7db801405","name":"流场工作室","category":"生成艺术","description":"数千粒子沿噪声向量场游走，线束在整幅画布中交织。纤细控制栏提供对场、色彩与轨迹的调整。","interaction":"点击画布放置涡旋，调整滑杆改变流场；再次点击已有涡旋将其移除。"},{"id":"035","slug":"035-coffee-roasters","title":"Ember & Oak Coffee Roasters","file":"035-coffee-roasters.html","textFile":"035-coffee-roasters.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/035-coffee-roasters.jpg","prompt":"Design a landing page for \"Ember & Oak Coffee Roasters\" (fictional). Warm palette: espresso #2b1a12, roasted brown #5c3a21, crema #c8a27a, oat #efe5d6, copper #b87333. Hero: a large SVG coffee cup at a three-quarter angle with animated curling steam (turbulence-displaced SVG paths) and a latte-art rosetta in the crema that redraws on hover. Sections: \"Our Beans\" — a horizontal scroll-snap carousel of origin cards (fictional estates in real coffee-growing countries such as Ethiopia, Colombia and Guatemala), each with a custom SVG illustration and altitude/process notes; an interactive tasting-notes flavour wheel (SVG donut with sub-segments — fruity, floral, sweet, nutty, spices, roasted) where hovering expands segments and selecting one shows matching beans; a \"Roast level\" slider that shifts a bean illustration from green to dark roast with crackle particles at first crack; brew-guide steps with timers; and a subscription CTA. Typography: characterful serif headings (Cooper, Rockwell, Georgia, serif) with a clean sans for body text. Tactile hover states throughout.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A warm, tactile landing page for Ember & Oak, a fictional small-batch roastery, built in the brief's espresso, roasted brown, crema, oat and copper palette with a faint paper grain over everything. The hero pairs a chunky Cooper-style serif headline (\"Roasted slow, over ember & oak\", with a hand-inked copper underline) with a large SVG stoneware cup seen at a three-quarter angle: a two-tone drip glaze, a saucer, steam that curls through an animated turbulence displacement, a rosetta that pours itself into the crema, and a slowly turning seal of ring text. Below, an espresso ticker lists the season's regions, then come a drag-and-snap carousel of origin cards with hand-drawn landscapes, a flavour wheel on a warm oat panel, a dark roasting section where a bean darkens from green to glossy French roast, a brew bar with step timers, and a copper subscription block with a kraft coffee bag.","techniques":"- Hero cup drawn entirely in inline SVG: layered linear and radial gradients for the ceramic body, the drip glaze with a wavy edge, the rim and crema, speckles in the clay, a saucer with its own shadow, and a handle drawn as a closed loop\n- Steam: four thick strokes passed through an feTurbulence + feDisplacementMap + blur filter whose baseFrequency is animated with SMIL, while each plume rises and fades on a staggered CSS loop\n- Latte-art rosetta: seven stacked leaf strokes, a heart and a pull-through line drawn in flat space, squashed with scale(1, .3) to sit in the perspective of the crema, and drawn one after another with pathLength and stroke-dashoffset\n- A rotating seal: textPath around the backdrop disc, fitted exactly with textLength\n- Seven hand-drawn SVG landscape vignettes (Ethiopian highlands with a cherry branch, Colombian slopes with wax palms, a Guatemalan volcano, Kenyan terraces, a Brazilian drying patio, Sumatran lake mist, Costa Rican cloud forest)\n- Flavour wheel generated procedurally: annular sector paths for six families and eighteen sub-notes, tinted per family, with tangential and radial labels that flip to stay upright\n- Roast bean: an SVG bean with a fractal-noise surface texture, a shading overlay, a crease that widens, a body colour interpolated across nine roast stops, swelling scale and an oil sheen that appears after second crack; a canvas overlay throws pooled chaff flakes and sparks at first and second crack\n- An illustrative bean-temperature curve with a marker that rides the path via getPointAtLength\n- Tactile UI: buttons with a solid bottom \"edge\" shadow that press down, pill tabs, radio chips, and cards that tilt in 3D toward the pointer\n- A full-page feTurbulence grain overlay blended with multiply for a printed-paper feel","originalInteraction":"- Hovering the cup with a mouse, or tapping it, pours the rosetta again; the cup also drifts slightly toward the pointer\n- Origin carousel: native scroll-snap for touch and trackpads, click-and-drag with the mouse (snapping to the nearest card on release), previous/next buttons, arrow keys when focused, and a progress bar showing position\n- Flavour wheel: hovering or focusing a family slides its segment outward; clicking or pressing Enter/Space selects it, dims the rest, updates the centre count and lists the matching beans; each match scrolls the carousel to that card and flashes it\n- Roast slider: dragging updates the stage name, approximate bean temperature, tasting description, the bean's colour, size and sheen, and the marker on the roast curve; crossing first crack (and second crack) bursts chaff and sparks and shakes the bean\n- Brew guide: accessible tabs (arrow-key navigation) switch between pour-over, French press and moka pot recipes; the timer starts, pauses, resumes and resets, highlights the active step, fills each step's progress bar and counts down the time left in the step\n- Optional sound (off by default, toggled in the header): synthesized crackle bursts at first and second crack, soft tones on step changes, the finish and wheel picks\n- Subscription: bag size and frequency chips update the per-bag and monthly price live; submitting validates the email and shows a fictional confirmation; the choice is remembered in guarded localStorage; the bag's label shows the next Tuesday or Friday roast day\n- prefers-reduced-motion stops the ticker, steam loop, tilt and parallax and shows the rosetta already poured","sourceHash":"ae1b12e1011aa9267ebdd7349e34040fe4c23442086b4a807606f4691efceda4","name":"余烬与橡木咖啡","category":"交互网页","description":"烘焙棕、燕麦色与铜色组成虚构咖啡品牌首页。拉花、纸感与产地内容共同塑造温暖的实体质感。","interaction":"悬停或点击咖啡杯重新绘制拉花，横向浏览产地卡片。"},{"id":"036","slug":"036-transit-map","title":"Meridian Metro","file":"036-transit-map.html","textFile":"036-transit-map.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/036-transit-map.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Meridian Metro\", an animated transit map for a fictional city in the tradition of Beck and Vignelli diagrams. White ground (#fbfbf9); six coloured lines (#e4002b red, #0072ce blue, #00a651 green, #ffb81c amber, #9b26b6 purple, #00b2a9 teal) drawn as thick SVG paths with only 45° and 90° bends; interchange stations as white capsules with black outlines, regular stations as tick marks; a river as a pale blue band and a park area. Station names in a clean humanist sans with careful placement. Trains (small rounded rectangles in the line colour) run along the lines on simulated schedules, pausing at stations. Interaction: hover a line to spotlight it (others fade); hover or click a station for a card with next departures whose countdowns tick live; a route planner — pick origin and destination to highlight the shortest path (Dijkstra on the station graph) with transfer count and estimated time; zoom/pan on mobile. The line legend doubles as toggles. Include a subtle \"service status\" panel (\"Good service\", \"Minor delays\"). It should look like a crisp printed map.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Meridian Metro is a live network diagram for an invented city, drawn in the tradition of Beck and Vignelli. It looks like a printed poster: a double-ruled sheet on warm white paper, with a title band, a legend strip along the foot and a sidebar of passenger information. Six lines in the brief's colours run only at 90° and 45° with rounded bends: a west–east Crown Line, a north–south Harbour Line, two diagonals (Parkway and Sunrise) that meet at the eight-spoke Meridian Central hub, an octagonal Meridian Circle and a Riverside Line that follows the pale-blue River Vell. There are 40 stations: interchanges are white capsules with black outlines, ordinary stops are coloured ticks and termini are cross-bars. Two dotted parks and a north arrow complete the sheet. Timetabled trains glide along both sides of every track and pause at each station. The sidebar holds a Dijkstra journey planner, a service-status board (one line has minor delays, which the simulation actually applies) and a map key.","techniques":"- Hand-designed network geometry on a 45°/90° grid; line paths are built from vertex lists with quadratic rounded corners, and the same sampled polylines drive both the drawing and the train motion\n- Station positions are projected onto each line to get arc lengths, which feed a per-line timetable model (run time at a fixed speed, dwell times, a terminus turnaround sized so the headway divides the cycle exactly)\n- Trains are small outlined rounded rectangles in the line colour with a dashed \"window\" stripe, offset to the left or right of the centreline by direction (double track), rotated to the track tangent, with eased acceleration and braking between stops\n- Printed-map finish: a double-rule frame, paper-coloured halos on labels (paint-order stroke), capsule interchanges oriented to the track, tick marks placed automatically on the label side, a large black-and-white hub roundel, dotted park fills, and an italic river label with wide letter-spacing\n- Staggered stroke-dashoffset draw-in of the six lines on load, then fade-in of stations, labels and trains\n- Humanist sans stack (Frutiger, Seravek, Segoe UI, Myriad, Calibri, Gill Sans, Cantarell…) with tabular figures for clocks and countdowns\n- The route overlay has a paper casing, full-colour leg segments, hollow stop dots, and A/B pins with pulsing SVG rings; the rest of the network dims beneath it\n- Labels are counter-scaled when zoomed out on small screens so station names stay readable","originalInteraction":"- Hover a line (or its legend entry) to spotlight it; other lines, trains and stations fade back\n- Legend entries toggle their line's visibility (aria-pressed); stations served only by hidden lines disappear with them\n- Hover, focus or click a station for a departure card: line chips, a fictional fare zone and the next two departures in each direction, counting down live against the simulated clock (\"due\", \"N min\", or m:ss at 1× speed); clicking pins the card, and Esc or × closes it\n- The journey planner takes From/To selects (or \"Route from here\" / \"Route to here\" on a station card) and runs Dijkstra on a (station, line) graph with a 4-minute penalty per change. It shows the total time, the number of changes and stops, each leg with its direction and duration, and the next departure from the origin, updated live\n- Swap and Clear buttons; the planned route is drawn over the dimmed map\n- Pan by dragging, zoom with the mouse wheel, pinch on touch screens, the +/−/reset buttons, or arrow keys and +/−/0 while the map has focus\n- Simulation speed control (1×, 20×, 60×) runs the network clock, which starts at your local time of day; everything pauses while the tab is hidden, and reduced motion defaults to real-time speed with no draw-in","sourceHash":"05acdd85fc98152fad7f668dcfd18f7b5b5d92a73b9a9c43380e99df98cb1b0c","name":"城市交通图","category":"数据与工具","description":"一张虚构城市的地铁网络图以海报方式呈现。列车在线路中持续运行，站点与图例提供清晰入口。","interaction":"悬停线路突出显示，点击图例切换线路可见性；选择站点继续探索。"},{"id":"037","slug":"037-frost-garden","title":"Frost Garden — Diffusion-Limited Aggregation","file":"037-frost-garden.html","textFile":"037-frost-garden.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/037-frost-garden.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Frost Garden — Diffusion-Limited Aggregation\", a crystal-growth simulation that produces delicate branching ice and coral structures. A canvas with a deep blue-black gradient (#020617 → #0b1d3a). Random walkers diffuse and stick when they touch the growing cluster; stuck particles are coloured by arrival time (pale ice #e0f7ff through cyan #67e8f9 to violet #a78bfa) with a soft glow, so the structure looks like frost lit from within. Use an efficient approach (spawn walkers near the cluster radius, grid occupancy, many steps per frame) so growth is visibly lively. Modes: \"Point seed\" (radial frost), \"Line seed\" (frost growing up from the bottom like on a window) and \"Snow\" (6-fold symmetric growth). Interaction: click to add seed points; a stickiness slider (branch density); speed; palette (Ice / Coral / Gold filigree); pause; download. A subtle particle count and elapsed time in elegant monospace. Title in thin, widely spaced capitals.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Frost Garden treats diffusion-limited aggregation as a pane of night glass where ice grows in real time. Against a deep blue-black field (#020617 into #0b1d3a, with a faint frosted-glass grain) hundreds of random walkers drift as faint motes, and each one that touches the crystal freezes onto it with a brief flash. Every particle is coloured by when it arrived, from pale ice #e0f7ff through cyan #67e8f9 to violet #a78bfa, and a soft additive glow layer makes the dense heart of the structure look lit from within. Three growth modes give three different pictures: a radial point-seed frost, window frost rising in a forest of fronds from a glowing sill, and six-armed stellar snowflakes with 60° side branches. The title is set in thin, widely spaced capitals, and a slim frosted instrument panel with crystalline corner marks holds the monospace readout and the controls.","techniques":"- Point and line modes: off-lattice random walkers (continuous random directions, so the square grid leaves no four-armed bias) moving over a grid occupancy map with a \"near cluster\" flag layer\n- Hierarchical safe jumps: 8-cell and 32-cell block maps let a walker far from any ice leap to a uniform point on the rim of its clear disc, the exact Brownian first-passage distribution, so growth stays lively\n- Walkers spawn just outside each seed's current radius and are recycled past a kill radius; the line mode spawns just above the tallest frond with horizontal wrap-around\n- Snow mode is a separate engine: DLA on a hexagonal lattice with noise reduction (a perimeter site freezes only after several walker hits) grown from a tiny hexagonal plate; each frozen site is copied through all 12 symmetries of the hexagon in cube coordinates, so the flake is exactly six-fold with side branches at 60°\n- Arrival-time colouring uses each seed's own growth clock, through a 64-step palette LUT of pre-rendered dot and glow sprites (Ice, Coral, Gold filigree); switching palette re-colours the whole structure from the stored particle list\n- Layered canvases: a static background (radial gradient plus noise grain), a quarter-resolution glow layer accumulated additively and composited with mix-blend-mode: screen, an incrementally drawn crisp frost layer, and a live layer for walkers, freeze flashes, twinkling cross-glints and seed ripples\n- Only new particles are drawn each frame; the full structure is redrawn only on palette change or resize, where the ice is re-centred rather than lost (a large change such as a phone rotation starts fresh)","originalInteraction":"- Seed modes Point / Line / Snow (buttons or keys 1 2 3); each restarts the chamber with a small head start so the first frame already shows frost\n- Click or tap the glass to plant another seed; in Snow mode a click starts another snowflake (up to ten)\n- Stickiness slider (0.05–1.00): lower gives dense coral-like growth, higher gives lacy ice; in Snow mode it sets how many hits a site needs, so lower values give smoother, more needle-like arms\n- Speed slider (1–10) sets how many freezing events happen per frame, within a per-frame time budget\n- Palette buttons Ice / Coral / Gold (or P) re-colour everything instantly\n- Pause/Grow (Space), Clear (C), and PNG download (S), which composites background, glow and frost into one image with a small caption\n- Live readout: particle count, elapsed growth time, active walkers, percentage grown, and an arrival-time colour key; a toast announces when the pane is fully frosted\n- prefers-reduced-motion halves the growth rate and hides drifting walkers and glints; animation stops while the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"24e3c820917dbf257218a18955535d633859fd6e43fe76e0b0047719dc892bf8","name":"霜之花园","category":"科学与模拟","description":"随机游走粒子在冰晶边缘凝结，扩散限制聚集算法在夜色玻璃上长出细密霜枝。","interaction":"切换点、线或雪模式重新生长；点击玻璃增加新的凝结种子。"},{"id":"038","slug":"038-neon-breakout","title":"HYPERBRICK","file":"038-neon-breakout.html","textFile":"038-neon-breakout.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/038-neon-breakout.jpg","prompt":"Create \"HYPERBRICK\", a polished, playable Breakout-style arcade game with a synthwave aesthetic. A canvas playfield framed by an arcade bezel; background dark purple (#12002b) with a perspective grid horizon and a glowing sun. Bricks in glowing neon rows (#ff2e97, #ff9e00, #fff200, #00f5d4, #00bbf9, #9b5de5); a sleek neon capsule paddle; a ball that leaves a motion trail. Juicy feedback: bricks shatter into particles, screen shake on impact, a combo counter with pop text, paddle squash, flashes, and synthesized sound effects (Web Audio blips whose pitch rises with the combo; mute toggle). Power-ups drop from some bricks: multi-ball, wide paddle, laser, slow-mo. Five hand-designed levels (e.g. heart, invader, spiral) plus an endless mode. Controls: mouse/touch drag and arrow keys; Space to launch/pause. Screens: a title screen with an attract-mode demo, pause, and game over with a high score saved in localStorage. Typography: bold italic sans with neon glow for the HUD. Scales to phone screens with a portrait playfield.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"HYPERBRICK is a complete, playable Breakout game built as a synthwave arcade cabinet. A backlit marquee carries the chrome-and-neon logo; below it a screwed-down bezel with pink and cyan neon rims frames a portrait CRT playfield (scanlines, vignette, glass glare), flanked on desktop by illuminated side panels for score, hi-score, stage, lives, a combo meter and a live power-up legend. The whole cabinet stands in a synthwave landscape: a huge striped sun, stars, and a CSS perspective grid rushing toward the viewer. Inside the screen, a painted sky, striped sun and neon-rimmed mountains sit behind glowing glass bricks, and the floor is an animated perspective grid whose speed rises with your combo. On phones the side panels fold into a compact HUD strip and the logical playfield grows taller, so the game stays a true portrait field.","techniques":"- Canvas 2D at devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) with a fixed 600-unit-wide logical field whose height adapts (800 on desktop, up to 1000 on tall phones)\n- Neon bricks, the ball glow and the sky/sun/mountain backdrop are pre-rendered to offscreen canvases (glow baked in with shadowBlur once per resize) so each frame is mostly drawImage calls\n- Striped sun made with destination-out compositing; perspective floor grid redrawn every frame with hyperbolic line spacing\n- Pooled particle system (520 slots): rotating shards on additive blending, shock-ring ellipses, paddle sparks and a stage-clear firework\n- Juice: screen shake, hit-stop on big combos, white brick flashes, cracked two-hit bricks, paddle squash, tapered two-pass ball trail that shifts cyan to orange to pink as the combo grows, floating score pop text, red flash on a lost ball and a violet tint during slow-mo\n- DOM overlays with CSS container-query type: skewed neon menu buttons, a sliding stage banner, a popping combo counter, a chrome gradient logo with a cyan offset outline\n- Page backdrop: CSS-masked striped sun, a rotated repeating-gradient grid plane with a looping animation, and a glowing horizon","originalInteraction":"- Title screen runs an attract-mode demo, with a predictive AI paddle playing a random stage behind the menu; choose Arcade (five hand-built stages: Love Circuit, Invader, Spiral, Palm Drive, Hyper Sun) or Endless (seeded, symmetric generated waves that get denser and tougher)\n- Mouse moves the paddle directly; on touch devices you drag anywhere (relative drag with a little gain) and tap to launch; Arrow keys or A/D also move it\n- Space (or click / tap) launches; Space, P or Esc pauses; the Pause button and the pause overlay offer Resume or Quit\n- Power-up capsules drop from about 15% of bricks: Multi-ball, Wide paddle (14 s), Twin laser with auto-fire (10 s) and Slow-mo (8 s), each with a countdown bar on the playfield and in the side legend\n- Combos count bricks broken between paddle touches and multiply the points; the Web Audio synth pitches brick blips up a pentatonic scale as the combo rises, with separate sounds for walls, paddle, armour, lasers, power-ups, a lost life, stage clear and game over\n- Sound starts only after a user gesture; the Sound button or M toggles mute (remembered). The hi-score is saved in localStorage (guarded), and a finished arcade run can carry its score and lives into endless mode\n- The game pauses automatically when the tab is hidden; a polite live region announces stages, lives and results; reduced motion removes shake, hit-stop and big flashes and slows the backgrounds","sourceHash":"04036321081059aa5de4c328a3511366f47ab9526f7dd4740a4e10dd480315eb","name":"霓虹打砖块","category":"音乐与游戏","description":"一台合成波风格街机拥有发光灯牌、CRT 屏幕和霓虹边框，完整承载可玩的打砖块关卡。","interaction":"从标题菜单选择模式并开始游戏，移动挡板接球，尝试不同关卡。"},{"id":"039","slug":"039-virtual-gallery","title":"Galerie Nocturne","file":"039-virtual-gallery.html","textFile":"039-virtual-gallery.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/039-virtual-gallery.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Galerie Nocturne\", a walk-through virtual museum room in CSS 3D. A corridor with a polished marble floor (CSS gradients plus reflection), warm walls (#e9e2d6), soft ceiling spotlights casting pools of light, and eight framed paintings hung on the side walls. Each painting is an original generative artwork rendered on canvas and seeded so it is stable: a colour-field piece, a primary-colour grid, a geometric abstraction, a pointillist sunset, a flow field, an op-art piece, an ink landscape and a gold mosaic. Gilded or slim black frames with museum placards (title, fictional artist, year, medium). Navigation: arrow keys/WASD or on-screen buttons move the camera along the corridor with smooth easing; clicking a painting walks up to it and shows it frontally with a detail card; Escape steps back. A small floor-plan minimap shows your position. Typography: a museum-grade sans for placards and a classic serif for the title. On phones, fall back to a swipeable wall of the same framed works.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Galerie Nocturne is a small museum corridor after closing time, built entirely in CSS 3D. Warm plaster walls (#e9e2d6 where the light falls) stand in darkness, lit only by ceiling track spots. Each spot throws a scallop of light down the wall and a pale beam through the air. A polished black-and-white marble floor reflects the room. Eight original generative paintings hang on alternating walls, each drawn on canvas from a fixed seed so it is identical on every visit: a pointillist harbour sunset, a primary-colour grid, a soft colour-field painting, a bulging op-art checkerboard, an ink-wash mountain hanging scroll, a Bauhaus-style geometric abstraction, a plotter-style flow field, and a gold mosaic sun. Works sit in gilded frames with bead mouldings or in slim black frames with silk or white mats, and each has a crisp museum placard. The titles are set in a Didot/Bodoni-class serif, and the placards and interface in a Helvetica-class sans. All artists, dates and works are fictional.","techniques":"- A true CSS 3D room: walls, floor, ceiling and end wall are planes in one preserve-3d world. A single camera transform (translateZ(perspective) · pitch · yaw · translate) makes it walkable.\n- Floor reflections come from real mirrored geometry. A second copy of the walls and paintings sits under a scaleY(-1) group reflected about the floor plane, and a semi-transparent procedural marble texture lets it show through.\n- Lighting is layered gradients: per-painting elliptical wall scallops, floor light pools, ceiling fixtures with halos, clip-path light-beam planes that fade out in close-up, a camera-relative depth fog, and a vignette.\n- Procedural textures: canvas fBm Perlin marble with veins and grout lines, and a plaster grain tile. Both are exported as data URLs and layered with CSS gradients.\n- Eight generative painters on canvas using a seeded PRNG and Perlin noise:\n  - divisionist dot sampling with complementary accents\n  - recursive grid subdivision with brush texture\n  - feathered, layered colour fields\n  - supersampled per-pixel lens-warped checkerboard\n  - per-column ink-wash gradients with dry-brush texture, pines and a seal\n  - multiply-blended geometric shapes\n  - a 1,400-line flow field that swirls around a hidden disc\n  - gold, lapis and red tesserae laid in concentric rings\n- Gilded frames use layered inset shadows and a masked bead ring. A sheen band slides across the gold frames and mosaic as the camera moves.\n- Placards and the end-wall title are built at 4× and scaled down inside the 3D context so their text stays sharp up close.\n- The camera uses exponential easing with speed caps, subtle head-bob, idle sway and pointer parallax.","originalInteraction":"- ↑/W and ↓/S walk the corridor; hold for continuous walking. ←/A and →/D turn to look at either wall. The mouse wheel and a vertical drag also walk, and a horizontal drag turns.\n- Clicking a painting walks up to it and turns to face it, framing the work and its placard beside a detail card with title, fictional artist, year, medium, size, a curatorial note and the seed. Prev/Next or ←/→ glide to neighbouring works, even across the corridor. Escape, Step back or ↓ return to the centre line.\n- Clicking the floor walks to that spot. Clicking a wall approaches the nearest work on it. Clicking the end wall walks to the end of the room.\n- Enter or the View button approaches the work you are facing. The top-right label always names the nearest work, and keys 1–8 jump straight to a work.\n- An on-screen pad has hold-to-walk and turn buttons for touch and mouse. Keyboard activation takes discrete steps.\n- The floor-plan minimap shows the corridor, colour-coded painting positions, and your position with a view cone. Its marks and the I–VIII index buttons are clickable.\n- On phones (≤720 px) the page switches to a swipeable, scroll-snapped wall of the same framed works under spotlights, with placards, dots and prev/next buttons. Tapping a work opens a bottom sheet with a larger image and the full card.\n- Reduced motion makes camera moves near-instant and removes the bob, sway and fades.","sourceHash":"5b66a344615c4003a28b5afb7470fb5e20b97b06157dfb484b5dd71cd4715439","name":"午夜画廊","category":"交互网页","description":"闭馆后的博物馆走廊由 CSS 三维空间构成。聚光灯、地砖与墙面绘画建立安静的观看距离。","interaction":"W/S 或上下键前进后退，A/D 或左右键转向；点击画作靠近，Esc 退回。"},{"id":"040","slug":"040-lemon-tart-recipe","title":"Nonna Lucia's Lemon Ricotta Tart","file":"040-lemon-tart-recipe.html","textFile":"040-lemon-tart-recipe.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/040-lemon-tart-recipe.jpg","prompt":"Design \"Nonna Lucia's Lemon Ricotta Tart\", a beautiful interactive recipe page. Mediterranean citrus palette: lemon #f7d44c, leaf green #5b8c51, terracotta #c65d3b, cream #fbf6ea, ink #2e2a24. Hero: a hand-drawn-style SVG illustration of the tart with lemons and leaves (wobbly line quality, flat colour fills, subtle paper texture), with lemon slices that rotate gently. Two columns on desktop (ingredients left, method right), one column on mobile. Features: a servings scaler (−/+) that recalculates every quantity with nice fractions (½, ¾) plus a metric/US toggle; an ingredient checklist with a satisfying strike-through animation; numbered steps that can be marked done, each with a built-in countdown timer where relevant (e.g. bake 35 min) showing ring progress and playing a gentle chime when finished (Web Audio); and a \"cook mode\" that enlarges the current step. Typography: warm serif headings (\"Iowan Old Style\", Palatino, \"P052\", Georgia, serif) with an italic, handwritten-flavoured style for notes such as \"Nonna's tip\". Include a short story intro and a print stylesheet (@media print).\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A warm, illustrated family recipe card for a fictional grandmother's lemon ricotta tart. A cream page with a faint dotted-linen print opens on a two-part hero. On the left is a \"Ricettario di famiglia\" kicker, a tilted italic terracotta \"Nonna Lucia's\" over a heavy serif \"Lemon Ricotta Tart\", a short story and an at-a-glance strip. On the right is a hand-drawn scene: a terracotta arch with a lemon-yellow sun, a golden fluted tart topped with candied lemon slices on a plate, a green gingham cloth fading into the page, a whole lemon, a cut half and leaves. Lemon slices slowly spin on the tart's surface (in perspective) and float around the scene. Below, a sticky ingredients card on the left and nine numbered method cards on the right carry the same palette of lemon #f7d44c, leaf #5b8c51, terracotta #c65d3b, cream #fbf6ea and ink #2e2a24. The current step is outlined with a lemon offset shadow, and Nonna's tips sit in tilted italic margin notes. A print stylesheet turns the page into a clean black-on-white card.","techniques":"- A hand-drawn illustration in inline SVG. One filter chain wobbles every line and fill (feTurbulence + feDisplacementMap), then composites a fine paper grain only inside the drawn shapes. There are flat colour fills, ink outlines, a gingham pattern, and nested linear-gradient masks that dissolve the tablecloth into the page on three sides.\n- Runtime-built SVG details: the tart's scalloped fluted rim and wall flutes are computed around an ellipse. Each lemon slice (rind, pith, flesh, segment membranes, juice sacs) has slightly irregular hand-drawn edges.\n- The animated layer is a separate overlaid SVG, so the heavy filter never repaints. Nine slices spin inside a scale(1, .36) perspective frame, so they turn on the tart's surface. Floating slices spin and bob, and a leaf sways (CSS transform-box: fill-box).\n- Typography: an Iowan Old Style / Palatino / P052 stack with a rotated italic byline, a small-caps first line, wavy-underlined \"Nonna's tip\" labels in italic, and tabular lining numerals for quantities.\n- Micro-interactions: circular checkboxes pop and draw their tick. A terracotta strike-through sweeps across the ingredient name via an animated background-size (it works across wrapped lines), and a lemon progress bar fills. Changed quantities bump. Timer rings deplete via stroke-dashoffset and pulse when finished.\n- A tactile \"pressed\" button style with an offset ink shadow, an offset lemon shadow on the current step, and a print stylesheet (@media print) with two-column ingredients and no controls.","originalInteraction":"- The servings scaler (− / +, 4 to 16) recalculates every quantity. Metric shows rounded grams and ml. US shows friendly cups, tbsp and tsp with ½ ¼ ⅓ ⅔ ¾ fractions and remainders (e.g. \"½ cup + 2 tbsp\"). Counts use halves, the tin size and in-step temperatures and thicknesses update, and a Metric / US toggle switches everything, including °C/°F in the method.\n- The ingredient checklist shows a gathered counter and progress bar. Checks, done steps, servings, units and the chime preference persist in localStorage (guarded with try/catch). \"Clear ticks\" resets them.\n- Each step can be marked done, which advances the highlighted current step. Steps with ingredient needs show chips with the scaled quantities.\n- Step timers (30, 15, 20, 35, 12 and 60 min) can be started, paused, resumed and reset, and several can run at once. Running timers appear in a floating dock that jumps to their step. On finish, a soft two-note synthesized bell chime plays (Web Audio, created on the first Start) and a toast appears. The header's \"Chime on/off\" toggle mutes it.\n- Cook mode is a full-screen, large-type view of one step. It shows a giant italic numeral, progress dots, ingredient chips, the tip and a big timer, with Previous / \"Done, next\" / Skip buttons. Arrow keys and Escape work, focus is trapped, and a screen wake lock is requested where supported.\n- \"Print card\" calls the browser's print dialog using the print stylesheet. Reduced-motion users get static slices and instant transitions.","sourceHash":"2cbf2de45f2e4eb36d84a0c5744848aca06446afd60cf688a3a8a9f1246f2e40","name":"柠檬挞食谱","category":"数据与工具","description":"一份虚构祖母的柠檬乳清奶酪挞食谱，以插画、亚麻纹理和温暖字体呈现，食材比例可以实际调整。","interaction":"调整份数查看用量变化，切换公制和美制单位，沿页面阅读制作步骤。"},{"id":"041","slug":"041-rain-on-glass","title":"Midnight Window","file":"041-rain-on-glass.html","textFile":"041-rain-on-glass.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/041-rain-on-glass.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Midnight Window\", rain on a window pane at night. Behind the glass, a canvas shows a blurred city bokeh scene (soft glowing circles in amber #ffb347, warm white, tail-light red #ff4b4b and teal signage #3fd0c9 on deep blue-black #0a0f1f), drifting slowly. On the glass: realistic raindrops — small static beads plus larger drops that grow, then slide down in wobbly paths leaving trails and merging with others; each drop refracts the background (render the background flipped and magnified inside the drop). Condensation fogs the glass; the user can wipe it clear with pointer or finger, and the cleared area slowly fogs back. Ambient layers: occasional distant lightning and rain sound synthesized with Web Audio (filtered noise plus droplet ticks) behind a \"sound\" toggle. A small caption in a light serif: \"It's 00:47. The city is still awake.\" using the live local time. Controls: rain intensity slider and a city/forest background toggle (forest = green bokeh). Performance-minded.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Midnight Window\" is a full-screen, nearly wordless nocturne: you are looking through a fogged window at night while rain runs down the glass. Behind it, an out-of-focus city glows in amber #ffb347 streetlights, warm-white windows, tail-light red #ff4b4b pairs that slowly drift away and teal #3fd0c9 signage on deep blue-black #0a0f1f. On the glass, condensation softens everything into milky halos, except where a hand has already wiped a band clear across the streetlights. Tiny beads speckle the pane, larger drops gather, merge, hesitate and slide in wobbling paths that cut thin clear trails through the fog, and every drop acts as a small lens showing the scene flipped and magnified. The only type is a small wordmark, the date and a light serif caption with the live local time, for example \"It's 00:47. The city is still awake.\" A compact frosted control bar holds rain intensity, a city / forest toggle, a wipe button and a sound toggle.","techniques":"- Layered canvas pipeline: a half-resolution bokeh layer, a quarter-resolution fog mask and fog composite, a full-resolution bead layer, and drops drawn last on the main canvas\n- Bokeh from pre-rendered disc sprites (flat body, brighter rim, soft edge) composited additively over a pre-blurred base (building or tree-trunk silhouettes and horizon glow painted small and upscaled); tail-lights and headlights drift, windows and signs twinkle and occasionally switch off and on\n- Condensation: the scene is downsampled to about 1/26 and upscaled for a heavy blur, boosted with a glow pass, a milky haze and a fine speckle texture, then masked by the fog-mask canvas (destination-in) and laid over the clear view\n- Refraction per drop: a CanvasPattern of the live background with a pattern transform that point-flips and magnifies it about the drop centre (s = c − k·(p − c)), filled into an ellipse that stretches while sliding, then shaded with a rim, top shadow, bottom caustic and specular sprite\n- Drop simulation with pooled objects: spawn-merging on landing, a size threshold for sliding, stick-slip friction, lateral wobble, trail droplets shed as they move (with area loss), merging collisions, and thin wobbling clear trails cut through fog and beads that slowly fog over again\n- Beads accumulate on their own layer and evaporate exponentially, keeping a steady fine texture\n- Distant lightning: multi-pulse flashes lit into the background layer, so the fog and every drop refract the flash\n- The first frame is pre-warmed (a two-pass hand-wipe band plus about a second of simulated rain), and the fog mask survives resizes by being rescaled","originalInteraction":"- Drag with mouse, pen or finger to wipe the glass: a soft brush clears the fog and beads and removes the drops it passes over; the cleared area slowly fogs back. A ring cursor shows the brush on desktop\n- Double-click to breathe on the glass and fog up a patch\n- \"Wipe\" button sweeps a clean arc across the pane (a keyboard and touch-friendly way in)\n- Rain slider (0–100) changes drop and bead spawn rates, and the loudness of the rain sound\n- City / Forest segmented toggle cross-fades the whole scene to green forest bokeh and changes the caption to \"The forest is still listening.\"\n- Sound toggle (off by default) creates a Web Audio graph on first use: looping filtered noise for the hiss and body of the rain, randomly scheduled droplet plinks and taps with stereo panning, and low rumbling thunder a few seconds after each flash\n- Caption and date update live from the local clock; rendering and audio pause while the tab is hidden; prefers-reduced-motion disables lightning, slows drops and drift, and removes UI transitions","sourceHash":"24fa62536dc1ff9a80c1c9efa42bfa21f88e1d1a6ccdd5db427ebe699fd77c9d","name":"午夜窗上的雨","category":"生成艺术","description":"城市灯光在雨玻璃后化成散景，水滴沿窗面滑落。画面几乎没有文字，雾气本身成为可触碰的介质。","interaction":"用鼠标、手指或笔擦拭玻璃；清晰区域会慢慢重新起雾。"},{"id":"042","slug":"042-fractal-explorer","title":"Infinite Coastline — Mandelbrot & Julia Explorer","file":"042-fractal-explorer.html","textFile":"042-fractal-explorer.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/042-fractal-explorer.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Infinite Coastline — Mandelbrot & Julia Explorer\". A full-screen canvas rendering the Mandelbrot set with smooth (continuous) colouring and a choice of cyclic palettes: \"Aurum\" (black → deep blue → gold → white), \"Abyss\" (navy → cyan → white), \"Magma\" and \"Pastel Dream\". Render progressively (low-resolution preview, then refinement in chunks scheduled with requestAnimationFrame, optionally in a Web Worker created from an inline Blob) so the UI never freezes. Interaction: click/tap to zoom in on a point (animated); right-click or a button to zoom out; drag to pan; mouse-wheel zoom; and a live mini Julia-set window that follows the cursor showing the Julia set for the hovered c — press J to make it the main view. HUD: coordinates, zoom level and iteration count (rising automatically with zoom); bookmarks of famous locations (Seahorse Valley, Elephant Valley, a triple spiral, a mini-brot). Palette-cycling animation toggle. Download PNG. A sleek dark UI with thin gold typography.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A full-screen, black-and-gold instrument for exploring the Mandelbrot set and its Julia sets. The fractal fills the window with smooth, continuous colouring, and the palette is mapped relative to the lowest escape value in view, so the far exterior falls to black and the coastline glows. Thin gold typography and hairline panels with corner ticks frame the image: a spaced-caps \"Infinite Coastline\" title, a position readout (real, imaginary, zoom in scientific notation, auto-rising iteration count, cursor or seed c, render progress and time), a bookmark panel whose live thumbnails are rendered by the page itself, and a toolbar with zoom, reset, four palette swatches (Aurum, Abyss, Magma, Pastel Dream), colour cycling, the Julia toggle and PNG export. A circular gold-ringed lens trails the cursor and shows, live, the Julia set for the point beneath it.","techniques":"- Escape-time kernel with smooth (continuous) iteration count, bailout radius 256, cardioid and period-2 bulb rejection and Brent-style periodicity checking\n- Multi-threaded progressive rendering: the kernel's own source is packed into an inline Blob and run in a pool of Web Workers (main-thread time-sliced fallback). An 8× preview pass is followed by full-resolution strips scheduled from the centre outwards, each painted as it arrives with dirty-rect putImageData\n- A float field of smooth iteration values is kept, so palette changes and palette cycling only recolour through a 2,048-entry cyclic LUT, with no recomputation\n- Palette mapping t = sqrt(mu − mu_min) × density, with mu_min taken from the preview pass, so every zoom depth keeps a dark exterior and a banded, glowing boundary\n- Animated zooms, pans, wheel zoom and pinch show a scaled and translated snapshot of the last frame, eased on a logarithmic scale path, until the new progressive render replaces it\n- Auto iterations rising with log zoom; precision guard at the limit of 64-bit floats, with a toast\n- Julia lens: a 160 × 160 live render on each pointer frame, eased to follow the cursor and flipping at screen edges; on touch screens it docks and shows the Julia set of the view centre\n- Hairline gold UI: thin sans in spaced caps, italic serif accents, monospaced tabular numerals, corner-tick panels, conic-gradient palette swatches and soft corner vignettes for legibility","originalInteraction":"- Click or tap to dive ×3 toward the point (animated); right-click to rise; drag to pan; mouse wheel zooms about the cursor; two-finger pinch zooms on touch screens\n- Hover to see the live Julia set for that c in the lens; press J (or the J button) to make that Julia set the main view, and press J or M to return to the saved Mandelbrot view\n- Bookmarks (buttons or keys 1–5): Seahorse Valley, Elephant Valley, Triple Spiral Valley, the period-3 mini-brot at about −1.7549, and the whole set; each flies there with an animated zoom\n- Palette swatches form a radio group (arrow keys supported); the cycle button or C animates the colours; [ and ] change detail (iteration multiplier); + and − zoom, arrow keys pan, H or 0 resets\n- Save exports the current render as a PNG through canvas.toBlob and a temporary download link, with a data-URL fallback\n- A hint strip explains the gestures and fades out; toasts and a polite live region announce palette, mode and precision changes; rendering pauses while the tab is hidden; reduced motion makes zoom jumps instant","sourceHash":"45c29a23d4d48e757a2361f9442f7970ccb8f7dcd2bdb5fc36e6320a888244e5","name":"分形深潜","category":"科学与模拟","description":"曼德勃罗集与 Julia 集在黑金色仪表中不断显现细节。连续着色让放大过程保持层次和深度。","interaction":"点击放大、右键缩小，拖动平移，滚轮或双指缩放；悬停查看对应 Julia 图形。"},{"id":"043","slug":"043-fashion-lookbook","title":"Atelier Noire — Autumn/Winter","file":"043-fashion-lookbook.html","textFile":"043-fashion-lookbook.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/043-fashion-lookbook.jpg","prompt":"Design \"ATELIER NOIRE — Autumn/Winter Collection\" (fictional), a high-fashion editorial lookbook. Palette: bone #efe9e1, blush #e8c4b8, oxblood #5a1a1d, black #0d0d0d. Typography is the star: an ultra-high-contrast Didone display stack (Didot, \"Bodoni 72\", \"Bodoni MT\", \"C059\", serif) set enormous and overlapping the imagery, mixed with tiny tracked sans labels. Since no photographs are allowed, each \"look\" is an abstract fashion illustration built from CSS/SVG — elongated silhouettes, draped fabric shapes with gradient folds, a hat brim, the geometry of a coat — elegant and minimal like fashion croquis. Layout: a horizontal-scroll gallery on desktop (the wheel drives horizontal movement) and vertical on mobile; asymmetric compositions with numbered looks (\"LOOK 07\"), material captions, and a giant word (\"RESTRAINT\") sliding in parallax behind. Interaction: hovering a look reveals a detail-zoom fabric swatch (procedural tweed, silk sheen, herringbone); a custom cursor becomes a thin ring labelled \"VIEW\"; a progress bar. Page-load intro: a curtain wipe.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"ATELIER NOIRE is an editorial lookbook for a fictional couture house's Autumn/Winter collection. It uses four colours: bone #efe9e1, blush #e8c4b8, oxblood #5a1a1d and black #0d0d0d. The page opens with a two-layer curtain wipe (oxblood, then black) that lifts off an \"AN\" monogram. Behind it is a cover where a giant Didone \"ATELIER\" sits behind a tall coated figure and an italic oxblood \"Noire\" overprints in front of it. On desktop the collection runs as one long horizontal strip driven by the ordinary vertical wheel. The strip holds eight numbered looks with asymmetric layouts, an italic manifesto (\"Take away until the silhouette holds its breath.\"), a Materials plate of eight woven swatches, and a \"Fin.\" colophon. A blush \"RESTRAINT\" set at 66vh slides behind everything at its own parallax rate. Each look is an original SVG fashion croquis drawn with elongated proportions from gradient-folded cloth, a hat brim, lapels, capes, drapes and trains. Giant Didone numerals (\"01\"–\"08\") sit behind the figures. Tiny tracked sans labels carry the look number and the material notes. On phones the same content becomes a vertical editorial scroll.","techniques":"- The display type is a Didone stack (Didot, Bodoni 72, Bodoni MT, C059) set at 25–66vh with tight negative tracking, contrasted with 10px sans labels tracked at 0.34em.\n- Horizontal scrolling uses a sticky stage. The scroller's height is set to the track width, so the native vertical scroll (wheel, keys, scrollbar) is translated into a lerped translate3d on the track. Sideways trackpad swipes are folded into the same position.\n- Parallax has three layers: the RESTRAINT word (0.36×), per-look numerals that drift against their panel, and the moving panels themselves.\n- Nine hand-drawn inline SVG figures use shared defs. Multi-stop linear gradients act as fabric folds (oxblood wool, bone silk, blush tulle, black velvet, bone gabardine, oxblood duchess satin), with a vertical shade gradient for weight. SVG patterns provide the herringbone and fantasy tweed.\n- Procedural cloth is drawn per pixel with ImageData on canvases: sheen bands for silk charmeuse, broken-twill chevrons for herringbone, flecked twill with slubs for tweed, looped yarn arcs for bouclé, crushed sine-noise pile for velvet, steep twill for gabardine, low-lustre highlight waves for satin, and felted fibres for cashmere.\n- Each look has a fabric \"loupe\", a circular magnified swatch with a bone mat ring, a leader line with a dot and a tracked caption. It scales in on hover or focus, pins on click, and appears automatically when the look scrolls into view on touch devices.\n- The header, progress bar and cursor use mix-blend-mode: difference so they stay legible over bone and oxblood panels.\n- The curtain is a clip-path inset wipe on two staggered layers, and the monogram lifts away before it.\n- The custom cursor is a small dot that eases after the pointer and becomes an 88px hairline ring labelled \"VIEW\" over a look.","originalInteraction":"- Desktop: the mouse wheel, trackpad (vertical or sideways), arrow keys (←/→ snap panel by panel), Page Up/Down and the scrollbar all move the horizontal gallery smoothly. The progress bar fills and the counter reads \"Look 03 / 08\", \"Interlude\", \"Cover\" or \"Fin\".\n- Hovering a look scales the figure slightly and reveals its fabric loupe. Clicking, or pressing Enter/Space on the focused figure, pins the loupe open.\n- The header nav (Looks, Materials, Atelier), the wordmark and \"Return to the cover\" jump through the collection.\n- Materials swatches tilt and grow on hover.\n- Phones and portrait tablets get a vertical layout. Touch devices get in-view loupes and no custom cursor. Reduced motion shortens the curtain and removes lerped easing and transitions.","sourceHash":"216f5c9675a5efdf1a1ce62b6922dafbd4a62777e17fab3bdf4684740e5d79b5","name":"高定时装画册","category":"交互网页","description":"虚构时装屋的秋冬系列用大幅衬线文字、骨白、淡粉和酒红展开。服装插画、布料细节与留白组成横向画册。","interaction":"桌面使用滚轮、触控板或左右键翻阅；继续浏览服装与面料细节。"},{"id":"044","slug":"044-tide-moon-clock","title":"Selene — Tide & Moon Clock","file":"044-tide-moon-clock.html","textFile":"044-tide-moon-clock.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/044-tide-moon-clock.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Selene — Tide & Moon Clock\", a poetic instrument showing today's moon phase and a stylised tidal cycle. Compute the current moon phase from the date (synodic month ≈ 29.53059 days from the reference new moon of 2000-01-06 18:14 UTC) and render a large, photoreal-leaning moon on canvas with a correct terminator, craters and maria from procedural noise shading, and earthshine on the dark side. Below it, a flowing sea of layered animated SVG waves (navy #0b1d3a, #13315c, #1d4e89 with silver highlights #c9d6e3) whose level rises and falls with a simplified semi-diurnal tide model (two highs and two lows per ~24h50m, clearly labelled illustrative, not for navigation). A circular 24-hour dial around the moon marks tide highs/lows and the current time. Text: phase name, illumination %, days until the next full/new moon, and the next (illustrative) high tide. Interaction: a date scrubber travels ±30 days so you can watch the moon wax and wane; hovering the sea makes it sparkle; a calm night mode dims the UI. Typography: a light, elegant serif with small caps.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Selene\" is a nocturne: a navy sky with faint stars, a large moon at its true phase for today, and a still, moonlit sea below. The sea rises and falls with an illustrative semi-diurnal tide. The moon sits inside a 24-hour dial with midnight at the top. Its tide ring swells outward at high water and draws in at low water, with times labelled and a pointer at the current hour. On either side, two quiet panels in light Garamond-style italics and small caps give the lunar state (phase name, illumination, age, time to the next full and new moon) and the tidal state (rising or falling, height within the range, spring or neap, next high and low). The tide is plainly marked as illustrative and not for navigation. A glass time scrubber floats on the water. Its track is lined with phase icons and it travels thirty days either side of today, so the moon waxes and wanes under your thumb.","techniques":"- The moon is rendered on canvas per pixel. The phase comes from the mean synodic month (29.53059 d from the 2000-01-06 18:14 UTC new moon) and sets the sun direction L = (sin φ, 0, −cos φ), which gives a correct terminator for a northern-hemisphere viewer\n- The surface is built once into buffers, then only relit when the date changes. 3D value-noise fBm is sampled on the sphere. Noise-warped blobs are placed where the real near-side maria sit (Procellarum, Imbrium, Serenitatis, Tranquillitatis, Crisium and others). Tycho, Copernicus, Kepler and Aristarchus have ray systems, and dark-floored Plato and Grimaldi are included alongside a seeded field of craters. A height field is turned into perturbed normals, so crater relief catches light along the terminator\n- Lighting uses a Lommel-Seeliger law (the flat look of a full moon) with a smooth terminator ramp. Blue-tinted earthshine on the night side grows toward new moon, and a soft CSS radial glow scales with illumination\n- The 24-hour SVG dial carries 96 ticks, hour numerals, italic \"midnight\" and \"noon\", and a closed tide curve r(θ) = R + A·h(t) filled with a translucent band. High and low marks carry two-line labels\n- The tide model: h(t) = A·cos(2π(t − T0)/12.42 h), which gives highs at the moon's upper and lower passage (about 24 h 50 m per pair). A = 0.86 + 0.14·cos 2φ, so springs fall near new and full moon and neaps near the quarters\n- The sea is four SVG wave layers in #1d4e89, #13315c and #0b1d3a gradients. Each is a sum of three travelling sines drawn with smooth quadratic segments, and silver crest strokes brighten under the moon. The water level follows the tide, with an eased lift that grows toward the front layers\n- A silver tide staff stands between the wave layers and is marked hw, mw and lw, so the water visibly rises and falls against it\n- A canvas overlay with additive blending draws a flickering moonlight path whose strength follows illumination, a horizon glow and pointer sparkles\n- The starfield is a seeded canvas with a handful of CSS-twinkling stars. The \"SELENE\" title is drawn as hairline outlined SVG text","originalInteraction":"- The date scrubber (a range input, ±30 days, keyboard accessible, with a descriptive aria-valuetext) eases the displayed date toward its target. The moon relights, the tide ring redraws and all readouts update\n- \"Play\" sweeps through the lunation at about 2 days per second. \"Today\" returns to the present\n- Moving the pointer over the sea spawns four-point sparkles on the water\n- \"Calm\" night mode fades all UI to a whisper, dims the moon slightly and brings the UI back on hover or focus. The choice is remembered in localStorage (wrapped in try/catch)\n- The clock stays live: the current-time pointer, tide state and next high and low update every minute\n- Everything re-lays out on resize. Canvases use devicePixelRatio (capped at 2). prefers-reduced-motion freezes the waves, the twinkling and the date easing, and steadies the glitter","sourceHash":"03ae239f67c500d98933366c20ae58d752fc60624e3f4d564deafdfa1b823b0e","name":"潮汐与月相","category":"数据与工具","description":"真实日期对应的月相悬在夜海之上，二十四小时刻度与示意潮汐一起变化，形成一枚安静的天文时钟。","interaction":"拖动日期滑杆查看前后日期的月相变化；潮汐为示意模型。"},{"id":"045","slug":"045-stained-glass","title":"Vitrail — Stained Glass Generator","file":"045-stained-glass.html","textFile":"045-stained-glass.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/045-stained-glass.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Vitrail — Stained Glass Generator\". A tall Gothic lancet window (pointed-arch SVG clipPath) filled with a Voronoi tessellation computed in JavaScript (half-plane clipping is fine), where each cell is a pane of jewel-toned glass — ruby #9b111e, sapphire #0f52ba, emerald #046307, amber #ffbf00, amethyst #9966cc — with subtle internal texture (noise, bubbles, streaks) and thick dark lead came lines (#1a1a1a with a slight highlight). Light pours through: the glass glows and coloured light patches are cast, blurred, onto a stone floor below, shifting as a \"sun angle\" slider moves. Interaction: click inside the window to add a cell (it splits smoothly); drag cell sites to watch the leading reflow; a \"Rose window\" mode switches to a circular window with radially symmetric cells; palettes (Chartres blues, Sunset, Modern pastel). The surrounding stone tracery is drawn in SVG with carved shading. Typography: a classical inscriptional serif with an illuminated initial letter drawn in SVG.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Vitrail is a stained-glass workshop set in a dim stone chapel. A tall Gothic lancet, an equilateral pointed arch clipped with an SVG clipPath, glows in an ashlar wall. Its glass is a Voronoi tessellation computed live in JavaScript, with each cell a pane of ruby, sapphire, emerald, amber or amethyst glass. Every pane has its own cut of a procedural sheet texture (mottled density, drawn streaks and trapped seed bubbles) and a gentle thickness gradient. Dark lead came with a highlight, solder joints at every junction and a heavy outer frame hold it together. Sunlight comes through the glass: the sun is a hotspot behind the panes, coloured light spills onto the carved stone, faint coloured shafts full of drifting dust hang in the air, and the window's image falls on the flagstone floor, sheared and stretched by the sun angle, with the lead lines casting thin shadows through the pool. The window frame is SVG stonework: a splayed reveal, concentric carved rolls lit from the upper left, a hood mould with label stops, voussoir joints, colonnettes with capitals and bases, and a sloping sill. A rose-window mode swaps in a circular window with radially symmetric glass, stone spokes, a carved hub and a cusped rim. The side panel is set in inscriptional capitals under an illuminated initial V, drawn in SVG with gold leaf, a diapered blue field and vine tendrils.","techniques":"- Voronoi by half-plane clipping against the convex window outline, with nearest-neighbour ordering and an early exit; recomputed every frame while seeds move\n- SVG clipPaths (objectBoundingBox) for the pointed arch and the circle, applied to the glass canvas with CSS clip-path\n- Per-pane rendering: a radial thickness gradient toward the sun, then a shared 256px procedural glass texture (value-noise density, wavy streaks, bubbles), composited with overlay at a unique rotation, scale and offset per pane\n- Lead came as layered strokes (dark channel, flat face, offset highlight), deduplicated solder joints, and a heavy perimeter came\n- Light transport faked with affine maps: the window's colours are projected onto the floor with a sun-dependent shear and foreshortening, interpolated through 14 translucent slices to make volumetric shafts, blurred in a low-resolution buffer and composited with screen blending\n- Colour spill on the stone tracery (blurred, masked outside the glass with an even-odd clip), a wall halo, a floor bloom, and a slowly breathing light level suggesting passing clouds\n- Dust motes that ride individual sun rays from the glass to the floor pool\n- Procedural ashlar wall with tooling pits and a flagstone floor in one-point perspective, plus a vignette\n- SVG tracery built at runtime from offset arch geometry (same arc centres, larger radii); gradient-stroked rolls imitate carved mouldings\n- Palettes: Jewel box, Chartres blues, Sunset and Modern pastel; colour families are spatially coherent through value noise, and in rose mode they follow the orbits so the symmetry holds","originalInteraction":"- Click the glass to split a pane: the new seed is born inside the pane under the pointer and slides out to the click point while its parent eases away, so the leading reflows smoothly\n- Drag any seed (handles appear on hover) and the leading and the floor light follow live; in rose mode, dragging one seed moves its whole symmetric orbit\n- Right-click, or long-press on touch, lifts a pane out; \"Relax leading\" runs animated Lloyd iterations; \"New design\" draws a fresh cartoon; \"Save PNG\" exports the window with a transparent surround\n- Lancet/Rose toggle, four palette buttons, and a sun-angle slider that sweeps from a low eastern dawn through noon to a western dusk, moving the hotspot, the floor pool, the shafts and the motes\n- Keyboard on the focused window: N new design, R relax, M switch window, P cycle palettes, arrow keys move the sun\n- Heavy light layers refresh at a throttled rate while dragging; animation pauses when the tab is hidden; reduced motion turns off the motes, the breathing light and the split animations","sourceHash":"316bdd4e427b252b1dfdf5327915d2e6328cf4c2d56c6692bfb748301af2b52f","name":"彩绘玻璃工坊","category":"生成艺术","description":"哥特式尖拱窗中，Voronoi 多边形像被铅条拼接的彩色玻璃。光线穿过不断变化的几何镶嵌。","interaction":"点击玻璃分裂一块窗格，拖动控制点改变分区形状。"},{"id":"046","slug":"046-habit-garden","title":"Tend — A Habit Garden","file":"046-habit-garden.html","textFile":"046-habit-garden.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/046-habit-garden.jpg","prompt":"Design \"Tend — A Habit Garden\", an elegant habit tracker in which every habit is a plant that grows with its streak. Soft sage palette: #f4f6f0 background, sage #9cb59a, deep green #2f4f3a, clay #d98e73, sun yellow #f2c14e. A header with today's date and a greeting based on the time of day. Each habit card holds a small SVG plant whose growth stage (seed → sprout → leaves → bud → bloom) reflects the current streak, a \"done today\" button with a satisfying bloom/particle animation, the streak count, and a 12-week contribution-style heatmap in greens (hover shows the date). Add, edit and delete habits in a modal (name, an icon from a set of SVG glyphs, colour). All data persists in localStorage; on first run, prefill three example habits with generated history so the page feels alive. A stats view shows a completion-rate ring chart and best streaks. Typography: a friendly humanist sans with rounded numerals. Micro-interactions: plants sway slightly; a watering-can cursor over the done button. Fully keyboard accessible and responsive.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Tend is a calm, greenhouse-flavoured habit tracker in which every habit is a potted plant. The page sits on soft #f4f6f0 with sage #9cb59a, deep green #2f4f3a, clay #d98e73 and sun yellow #f2c14e; the header pairs today's date and a time-of-day greeting (\"Good evening, gardener.\") with a small landscape of sage hills where the sun (or a crescent moon at night) sits on an arc that follows the actual hour. Each habit card holds an arched glasshouse window with a hand-built SVG plant in a terracotta pot whose growth stage — seed, sprout, leaves, bud, bloom — is driven by the current streak, a big rounded streak number, a \"Water today\" button, and a 12-week heatmap in greens. A Stats tab turns the same data into concentric completion rings, best-streak bars with miniature plants, summary tiles and a weekday rhythm chart. Type uses a rounded/humanist system stack (ui-rounded, SF Pro Rounded, Seravek, Segoe UI and friends) with tabular numerals.","techniques":"- Procedural SVG plants assembled from reusable leaf/stem/petal primitives: five distinct growth stages, flower and bud tinted by the habit's colour, a side bud and floating pollen at bloom, each stem swaying on its own CSS rhythm around the pot's rim\n- Arched \"glasshouse\" plant windows (asymmetric border-radius) with colour-mix() tints of the habit colour and a soft light bloom behind the plant\n- Particle celebration from a pooled fixed layer: water droplets fall onto the soil and squash, then petals in the habit colour, sage leaves and star-shaped sun sparkles burst outward (Web Animations API); the flower head or plant bounces, and a stage-up regrows the plant from 55% scale\n- 12-week contribution heatmap on an explicit 13-column CSS grid (weekday labels + 12 weeks), shaded by the length of the streak on each day rather than a flat done/not-done, with today ringed in clay\n- Header landscape: gradient-faded SVG hills, a time-of-day sun/moon position and CSS masks that dissolve the illustration into the page\n- Stats: SVG ring chart with animated stroke-dashoffset per habit, scaleX-animated streak bars, scaleY weekday bars with the best day highlighted in sun-to-clay\n- A watering-can cursor drawn at runtime on a canvas and applied to every water button\n- Sliding tab knob, pop-in dialog with blurred backdrop, staggered card rise-in, reduced-motion fallbacks for every animation","originalInteraction":"- \"Water today\" toggles today's completion (aria-pressed), updates streak, stage, heatmap and the header's \"n of m tended today\" bar; a stage change triggers a toast (\"Morning stretch grew into full bloom!\")\n- Heatmap: hover shows a tooltip with the date and streak position; click any past day to toggle it; the grid is one focusable element — arrow keys move a day cursor (with tooltip), Enter/Space toggles, Home/End jump\n- Add / edit / delete in a native dialog: name (validated), a radio group of ten SVG icon glyphs, six colour swatches, live plant preview; deleting requires a second confirming click (\"Really uproot?\"); N opens the dialog, Esc closes it\n- Garden/Stats tabs with arrow-key navigation; stats re-render live when data changes\n- All data persists in localStorage (guarded; a toast explains if storage is blocked); first run seeds three example habits with generated history (a 16-day bloom, a 5-day leafy plant, and a 13-day bud that blooms when watered today); \"Replant the demo garden\" resets after confirmation","sourceHash":"7798e072a66a1af82ce4032f47b6322d97945b69ebf7c63402944973123de58c","name":"习惯温室","category":"数据与工具","description":"每个习惯是一盆植物，完成记录决定它的生长阶段。连续天数、热力图与今日浇水进度共同组成温室。","interaction":"点击 Water today 标记或取消当天完成，观察植物和记录变化。"},{"id":"047","slug":"047-solar-system-scale","title":"Vast — A Scroll Across the Solar System","file":"047-solar-system-scale.html","textFile":"047-solar-system-scale.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/047-solar-system-scale.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Vast — A Scroll Across the Solar System\", a horizontally scrolling journey through the solar system at true relative scale: once the Sun's width is chosen (e.g. ~600px), every distance and diameter follows from real mean values, so Earth is a few pixels across and Neptune lies an enormous scroll away. Vertical wheel/scroll maps to horizontal travel; touch swipes work; a fast-travel control jumps between planets; and a \"speed of light\" button auto-travels at light speed relative to the scale (Earth arrives after ~8 min 20 s — show a real-time counter). Pure black background (#000) with very minimal white type; planets rendered as small canvas spheres with subtle banding and atmospheric shading. While crossing the emptiness, poetic and factual messages appear in the void (\"Most of space is this.\") and an odometer shows kilometres travelled and light-time. Typography: thin sans with generous letter-spacing. Keep all facts well established (mean distances in km, diameters).\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Vast\" is a horizontal journey from the Sun to Pluto at true relative scale, on a pure black sky with minimal thin, widely tracked white type. The Sun is drawn 600 pixels wide on desktop (240 on phones), and every diameter and mean distance follows from that single choice (1 pixel = 2,319 km on desktop). Earth is a 5.5-pixel dot 64,500 pixels from the Sun, Jupiter is a 60-pixel banded sphere, and Neptune lies almost two million pixels away. The emptiness is the subject. Short factual and poetic lines drift through the void (\"Most of space is this.\"). Planets too small to see get a thin locator ring and a magnifying loupe that states its magnification. A mechanical rolling-digit odometer reports kilometres from the Sun, AU, light-time and current speed, and a light-speed mode carries you at exactly c relative to the scale, so Earth arrives after 8 min 19 s of real time.","techniques":"- Virtual camera measured in kilometres (float64), with world-to-screen mapping at the chosen km-per-pixel; one full-viewport canvas at devicePixelRatio (max 2)\n- Procedural Sun built once per scale: limb-darkened radial gradient, thousands of granulation cells, faint supergranulation, faculae near the limb, two small irregular sunspot groups, and an additive corona glow\n- Procedural planet sprites lit from the Sun's side with a soft day/night terminator and atmospheric rims: banded Jupiter with the Great Red Spot, Saturn with split front/back ring sprites (Cassini division and ring shadow), Uranus with near-vertical rings, blue Neptune with a dark spot, cloud-flecked Earth, cratered Mercury, Mars with a polar cap, Pluto with its pale heart\n- True-scale drawing, plus a loupe for small bodies (a magnified sprite in a circular window, with a leader line and \"×N\" label)\n- Parallax dust that streaks with speed (the only cue of motion in empty space), million-kilometre ticks along a hairline ecliptic, and sparse asteroid and Kuiper belts whose brightness (never size) is exaggerated and labelled as such\n- World-anchored DOM messages and body tags that fade by distance from the centre and pin at the screen edge so they never clip\n- Rolling-digit odometer: each wheel rolls over as the one below passes 9 while moving, and settles on whole digits at rest\n- A linear map strip under the list of bodies shows how the inner planets huddle near the Sun","originalInteraction":"- Vertical wheel/trackpad (or horizontal) travels along the line; sustained scrolling accelerates smoothly, with inertia and friction\n- Drag or swipe in either axis with a fling (touch and mouse); arrow keys push, PageDown/PageUp jump to the next or previous body, Home returns to the Sun, End goes to the edge, number keys 0-9 fast-travel\n- Fast travel via the body list (full names, abbreviations on narrow screens) or by clicking or scrubbing the map strip; smooth, distance-scaled flights\n- \"Travel at light speed\" moves at exactly 299,792.458 km/s relative to the scale, timed by the real clock; the button shows elapsed real time, and a countdown shows when the next body arrives at c\n- The ending names Proxima Centauri's distance at the current scale and offers \"return to the Sun\"; an aria-live region announces the body you are passing\n- Resizing near the Sun keeps its framing; elsewhere you keep your exact kilometre. Reduced motion jumps instead of flying and stills the dust streaks","sourceHash":"d6a2ac9f5f0f6b66a1ab0b53a06edea5254fcae0db5528ac93fc7165c3dd4776","name":"太阳系真实比例","category":"科学与模拟","description":"以真实相对尺度从太阳穿行至冥王星。极简白字与黑色空间让天体之间巨大的距离变得可感知。","interaction":"滚动或拖动沿太阳系移动；持续滚动会加速，松手后惯性减弱。"},{"id":"048","slug":"048-claymorphism-kit","title":"Squishy — Claymorphism UI Kit","file":"048-claymorphism-kit.html","textFile":"048-claymorphism-kit.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/048-claymorphism-kit.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Squishy — Claymorphism UI Kit\" (a fictional product), a playful landing page in claymorphism: puffy, soft 3D shapes made with layered inner/outer shadows and radial gradients so elements look like modelling clay. Candy-pastel palette: bubblegum #ffb3c6, butter #ffe5a5, mint #b8f2e6, periwinkle #aab7ff, peach #ffcfb0 on a cream-lilac background (#f7f1ff). Hero: a cluster of 3D clay icons (heart, cloud, star, chat bubble, toggle switch, rocket) built purely with CSS/SVG that bob and wiggle; clicking one makes it squash and stretch with elastic physics and burst into little clay confetti. Sections: a component showcase (clay buttons with pressed states, toggles, sliders, a progress bar that fills with a jelly wobble), feature cards, a pricing section with a squishy toggle, and an FAQ accordion whose panels open with bouncy height animations. Typography: a chunky rounded sans (Nunito, \"Varela Round\", \"SF Pro Rounded\", \"Segoe UI\", system-ui, sans-serif) at heavy weights. Every interactive element should feel tactile.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Squishy — Claymorphism UI Kit\" is a playful landing page for a fictional product, built so almost everything looks like modelling clay and moves like it too. On a cream-lilac (#f7f1ff) ground in bubblegum, butter, mint, periwinkle and peach, a floating clay navigation pill leads to an asymmetric hero. On the left is a puffy multi-colour \"Squishy\" wordmark with extruded letters and a separate clay i-dot. On the right six clay icons (a smiling star, a sleepy cloud, a heart, a typing chat bubble, a toggle switch and a rocket) bob and wiggle over a soft clay cushion. Clicking an icon squashes it, stretches it into a hop, lands it with a jiggle and bursts it into clay confetti. Below come a component tray, tilted feature cards, a palette of copyable clay swatches, pricing with a squishy billing switch, a bouncy FAQ and a butter-to-peach call to action. Copy is original, and the page states that the product is fictional.","techniques":"- The clay recipe as CSS tokens: one lilac-tinted outer shadow plus a dark inner shadow bottom-right and a white inner shadow top-left, with radial-gradient highlights. A global --soft multiplier scales every shadow and is driven live by the \"Softness\" slider. Pressed states flip to deeper inset shadows with a squash scale.\n- One reusable SVG filter (feGaussianBlur and feOffset inner shade and highlight composited \"out\" against SourceAlpha, plus a feDropShadow) gives the SVG icons, wordmark, feature illustrations and FAQ markers their moulded volume. Each palette colour has a matching radial gradient.\n- The wordmark is measured at runtime (getStartPositionOfChar and getSubStringLength) so it fits whatever rounded font is installed. Each letter is SVG text with a 15-unit round-join stroke painted under its fill for puffy letterforms, a darker offset copy for extrusion, and the clay filter. Letters bob in a wave and wobble on a per-letter spring on hover or click.\n- The hero icons run a small physics model in rAF: a damped spring for squash/stretch (volume-preserving scale around a bottom origin), a rotation spring, and gravity for hops with bounce-on-landing. The \"Bounce\" slider changes spring stiffness and damping.\n- Clay confetti uses a pooled particle system on a fixed canvas (DPR-capped). It draws pre-rendered sprites (15 gradient-shaded balls, pills and stars across the five colours) with velocity-based squash. It sleeps when idle and is thinned for reduced motion.\n- The jelly progress bar is spring-driven: the fill overshoots, and its velocity is mapped to scaleY squish and skew wobble, with rising bubbles and a confetti pop at 100%.\n- Motion details: switch knobs spring with a squash keyframe, the segmented-control knob stretches as it slides, the pricing switch knob squashes mid-travel, prices squash-bounce as they change, checkmarks draw in, swatch blobs morph their border-radius, and FAQ panels open on a spring-simulated height that overshoots and settles.\n- Sections pop in using the individual translate and scale properties, so the reveal never clobbers each card's own hover transforms or tilt.","originalInteraction":"- Hero icons are buttons (mouse, touch, Enter/Space). Pressing squashes, releasing hops and bursts confetti. The toggle icon really flips between peach and mint, and the rocket jumps higher. Wordmark letters wobble on hover and burst on click.\n- Component tray: buttons with pressed states and a sticky aria-pressed toggle; three role=\"switch\" toggles; \"Softness\" and \"Bounce\" range sliders that retune the whole page; a jelly progress bar with +25% and Reset (it auto-demos to 75% when scrolled into view); a segmented control (radiogroup, arrow keys); clay checkboxes; a stepper; and a name field that \"moulds\" your blob with a greeting and confetti.\n- Palette swatches copy their hex to the clipboard (with a guarded fallback) and confirm with a toast.\n- Pricing: a switch changes monthly to yearly billing and the prices animate. FAQ: accessible accordion buttons (aria-expanded and aria-controls) with springy panels.\n- Sound: synthesized Web Audio squishes and boops, off by default, with a visible Sound switch in the nav. The AudioContext is created only after a gesture.\n- Keyboard focus rings throughout. prefers-reduced-motion disables idle animation, hops and springs, and turns the reveals into plain fades.","sourceHash":"7daa549ba295b7a95b8f17abfe2e61134b41feecb4d422f0f8a3bbdf17da1867","name":"软陶界面套件","category":"交互网页","description":"按钮、开关、图标和卡片像由彩色软陶捏成。按压、回弹与轻微变形使界面带有触觉联想。","interaction":"点击图标体验挤压与弹跳；切换开关，观察不同控件的反馈。"},{"id":"049","slug":"049-riso-halftone-lab","title":"Riso Lab — Halftone Print Studio","file":"049-riso-halftone-lab.html","textFile":"049-riso-halftone-lab.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/049-riso-halftone-lab.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Riso Lab — Halftone Print Studio\", a tool that turns procedural artwork into a two- or three-colour risograph print simulation. The source image is generated in-page, chosen from: a gradient sphere, a sunset landscape, a big typographic word the user types, a geometric face made of shapes, or a doodle drawn on a small canvas. The output renders colour separations as halftone dot screens at classic angles (e.g. 15°, 45°, 75°) in fluorescent pink #ff48b0, medium blue #0078bf, yellow #ffe800 and black, printed on off-white paper (#f4efe2) with multiply blending, slight misregistration, ink-density variation and paper grain. Controls: dot size/LPI, per-ink screen angle, misregistration amount, ink colours, dot shape (circle, line, square) and PNG download. Each ink has a small \"drum\" control that toggles its layer with a satisfying roller animation. Layout: a clean studio UI in white and black with tiny monospace labels, the print displayed like a poster pinned up with tape.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Riso Lab is a clean black-and-white print-studio tool whose output is pinned to a warm plaster wall. The left panel is Swiss-plain: hairline rules, tiny uppercase monospace labels, square slider thumbs, and a \"RISO LAB\" wordmark printed as three overlapping multiply layers. One of five procedural artworks (a lit sphere over a dusk floor, a layered-ridge sunset, a big tilted word you type, a flat geometric face, or your own doodle) is separated into three spot inks and screened as halftone at each drum's angle. The proof overprints fluorescent pink #ff48b0, medium blue #0078bf and yellow #ffe800 (plus black, teal and orange) on #f4efe2 paper with real multiply blending. The poster hangs from two torn washi-tape strips with a slight tilt and soft shadow, and carries print furniture: four registration targets and crop ticks printed by every drum (so misregistration shows as colour-fringed marks), a colour bar with solid, tint and overprint patches, and a colophon set in the darkest ink. A museum-style caption under the poster records the proof number, inks, angles, ruling and misregistration.","techniques":"- Colour separation by bounded least squares in optical-density space: each pixel's density relative to the paper is solved for up to three ink coverages (projected coordinate descent, clamped 0–1, light regularisation), so any ink combination gets a best-effort separation\n- Halftone screening per drum on its own canvas: a rotated cell grid at the drum's angle, with coverage sampled bilinearly from the separation map; circle dots (area-true radius, closing at high coverage), square dots, or line screens drawn as variable-thickness row polygons; dot gain and a clip to the image area\n- Each ink canvas is stacked with CSS mix-blend-mode: multiply, so layers overprint like translucent riso ink; misregistration is a per-drum CSS translate and micro-rotation that can change without re-rendering\n- Ink texture knocked out of each layer: a low-frequency density-drift field, starvation toward one edge, fine speckle and faint drum drag streaks, all seeded so \"Print again\" gives a new but repeatable pull\n- Paper: mottled formation, fibres and speckle, plus a multiply grain layer and a screen-blended \"tooth\" layer so paper texture shows through the ink\n- Live separation-plate previews per drum; a source thumbnail; ink drum controls built from CSS cylinders with shading, ridges, end caps and an LED\n- Wipe-in print pass animation (clip-path) when a drum is loaded or a new proof is pulled; a plaster wall with dotted texture, a ruler and a proof note","originalInteraction":"- Source picker (Sphere, Sunset, Word, Face, Doodle); Word shows a text field (up to 10 letters) that re-screens as you type; Doodle shows a drawing pad with four brush colours and Clear, and re-screens live while you draw (pointer events: mouse, pen, touch)\n- Ink drums A/B/C: click a drum to load or remove its layer, with a spinning-roller animation and a sliding cylinder; choose each drum's ink from six swatches (radio group); set each screen angle from 0 to 90° with a slider\n- Screen: dot shape (circle, line, square), dot size / ruling (lines across the image, shown in px), misregistration (0–10 px, instant) and ink grain / density drift\n- Print again re-rolls misregistration, ink texture and paper for a new numbered proof, which wipes in drum by drum; Download PNG flattens paper, inks (with the same offsets) and paper texture at 2× into a PNG\n- Rendering is batched through requestAnimationFrame; only the affected drum re-renders when its angle changes\n- On phones the poster comes first, with its caption and actions, and the full control panel follows below; reduced motion disables the wipe and roller animations","sourceHash":"aeb90f72c02e21aa5f02e81bbca63422a6fd3383e8eb30bf851203ca8234bfde","name":"孔版印刷实验室","category":"数据与工具","description":"程序生成的图像被拆成网点和叠印色，像一张钉在工作室墙面的 Riso 印刷样张。","interaction":"切换图像来源，输入文字或自由涂画，再调整印刷参数。"},{"id":"050","slug":"050-loader-atelier","title":"Loading, Beautifully","file":"050-loader-atelier.html","textFile":"050-loader-atelier.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/050-loader-atelier.jpg","prompt":"Design \"Loading, Beautifully — a Gallery of 16 Loaders & Micro-interactions\". A refined grid gallery (4×4 on desktop, two columns on mobile) on warm charcoal (#141414) where each card is a small stage demonstrating an original loading animation or micro-interaction made with CSS/SVG (JS only for toggling): e.g. a morphing blob, orbiting dots with trails, a pen stroke drawing a checkmark, a liquid-fill progress, folding squares, a breathing ring, a DNA ladder, a typewriter ellipsis, a pendulum, a gradient spinner with a comet tail, a pixel wave, a page flip, an elastic heart-like burst, a day/night toggle switch morph, a download button that morphs into a progress ring and then a success tick, and a skeleton-shimmer card. Each card has a title, a \"replay\" button and a \"copy CSS\" button that copies that card's CSS to the clipboard with a toast. Harmonised muted accents (#e07a5f, #f2cc8f, #81b29a, #3d405b, #f4f1de). Typography: a crisp sans with monospace labels.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Loading, Beautifully\" is a refined specimen gallery of sixteen original loaders and micro-interactions, laid out as a 4×4 grid of small stages on warm charcoal (#141414); it drops to three columns on tablets and two on phones. Each card pairs a stage, a faint dotted cutting-mat texture and a warm accent glow on hover, with a caption strip: a monospace index, the loader's name, the technique it demonstrates, a replay button and a \"copy CSS\" button. Every piece is drawn with CSS and inline SVG in the harmonised palette #e07a5f, #f2cc8f, #81b29a, #3d405b and #f4f1de. The masthead sets a large, tightly tracked sans title against monospace labels, with three hopping palette dots as its ellipsis, clickable palette swatches, and Pause all / Replay all controls. The sixteen: Tidal Blob, Orbit Trails, Ink Check, Liquid Fill, Origami Tiles, Breathing Ring, Helix, Typewriter, Pendulum, Comet, Pixel Wave, Page Flip, Elastic Heart, Day / Night switch, Download → Done, and Skeleton Shimmer.","techniques":"- One scoped <style id=\"css-…\"> block per loader, so \"copy CSS\" copies exactly the code that runs, dedented, with a comment header holding the loader's markup.\n- Tidal Blob: an eight-value border-radius morph with an inset-shadow body, a moving gradient sheen, a specular spot and a floor shadow that breathes with the float.\n- Orbit Trails: three counter-rotating orbits, each dot followed by four ghost copies offset by small negative animation delays on an eased rotation, so the trail stretches as it accelerates.\n- Ink Check: stroke-dashoffset draws a ring, then a tick, with a scale pop and a soft green bloom.\n- Liquid Fill: a registered @property integer drives both the liquid level and a CSS counter showing the percentage; two rotating rounded squares form the wave and foam.\n- Origami Tiles: nine two-faced tiles flip on alternating X and Y axes in spiral order using preserve-3d and backface-visibility.\n- Breathing Ring: scale and border-width inhale/exhale with echo rings and alternating \"in\" / \"out\" labels.\n- Helix: twelve phase-shifted columns with per-keyframe easing that approximates sine motion, depth scaling and collapsing rungs.\n- Typewriter: steps() width animation in ch units types, cycles the ellipsis and erases, with a blinking caret.\n- Pendulum: an eased swing about the pivot, a dashed arc drawn exactly on the bob's path, and a floor shadow using the individual translate and scale properties.\n- Comet: a conic-gradient ring cut with a radial mask, a glowing head and a slow counter-rotating dashed inner orbit.\n- Pixel Wave: a diagonal stagger computed with calc() from per-cell --x and --y custom properties, moving through slate, sage, sand and terracotta.\n- Page Flip: three pages turning on rotateY with perspective, shading at mid-turn and a terracotta cover.\n- Elastic Heart: a keyframed elastic overshoot with an eight-spark burst and a shock ring; a one-shot variant plays when you like it.\n- Day / Night: the sun knob slides with a springy cubic-bezier while a sky-coloured disc carves it into a crescent moon; clouds sink away and stars fade in and twinkle.\n- Download → Done: the pill collapses to a circle, a progress ring fills with a realistic stall, then the button turns green and a tick draws in.\n- Skeleton Shimmer: a highlight sweeps across each placeholder block, with delays staggered by position so it reads as one pass.","originalInteraction":"- Replay on any card swaps in a fresh clone of its stage, restarting every animation from frame zero and resetting any interactive state. Replay all cascades through the grid.\n- Copy CSS writes that loader's CSS (plus a markup comment) to the clipboard via the async Clipboard API, falling back to execCommand and finally to a modal with the code pre-selected; a toast confirms with a line count, and the button briefly turns into a tick.\n- Elastic Heart toggles liked/unliked (aria-pressed) with a burst. Day / Night is a real role=\"switch\" that demos itself every 2.6 s until you touch it. Download → Done runs its state machine on click and demos itself on load.\n- Pause all freezes every animation (animation-play-state); stages scrolled off-screen are paused automatically with an IntersectionObserver.\n- Fully keyboard-operable native buttons with visible focus rings; with prefers-reduced-motion each loader plays a single cycle, demos stop and hover lifts are removed.","sourceHash":"bcad5105cb2c718528f25ada30f312c69ea2939309ca2e6791a39d130fe64131","name":"等待的十六种方式","category":"排版实验","description":"十六个原创加载动效排列在暖炭色小舞台上，分别研究循环、节奏、变形与界面反馈。","interaction":"单独重播或依次重播全部动效；可复制对应 CSS。"},{"id":"051","slug":"051-winter-cabin","title":"Hytte — A Winter Cabin","file":"051-winter-cabin.html","textFile":"051-winter-cabin.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/051-winter-cabin.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Hytte — A Winter Cabin\", a cosy interactive scene: the interior of a log cabin at night, illustrated in SVG/CSS with warm wood tones (#7a4a2a, #a0673c). A stone fireplace with an animated fire (layered flame shapes with flicker plus canvas embers rising); a large window onto a snowy spruce forest with falling snow at several depths and a green-violet aurora (#4dffb4 → #8a5cff) rippling in the sky; a steaming mug on the sill; a reading lamp; and a sleeping cat that breathes. Warm vs cold light: the firelight flicker subtly modulates the room's lighting. Interaction: click the lamp to toggle it (room lighting changes); click the fireplace to add a log (the fire flares, more embers); click the window to open it (cold wind — snow blows inside briefly and the fire dims); click the cat to make its ear twitch; an optional ambient sound toggle (synthesized fire crackle and wind). A title placard in a Scandinavian-style serif. The composition adapts to portrait screens (window above, fireplace below).\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Hytte is a whole-screen illustrated interior: a Scandinavian log cabin at night, drawn in SVG with warm wood tones (#7a4a2a, #a0673c) and lit like a painting. A carved wooden placard reading HYTTE, \"a winter cabin\", hangs from a nail above a big mullioned window. Through the window, a snowy spruce forest at three depths sits under stars and a rippling green-to-violet aurora (#4dffb4 → #8a5cff) while snow falls outside. A blue enamel mug steams on the sill. A tripod reading lamp with a linen shade stands beside a pendulum wall clock that keeps the real local time. On the right is a rough stone chimney breast with an arched firebox, a timber mantel carrying candles, a red Dala horse and a spruce sprig, a basket of birch logs, and a grey tabby asleep on a braided rag rug in front of the hearth. The fire is animated, and its flicker drives the lighting of the whole room: a warm glow modulates over a cool night tone. On portrait screens the scene is recomposed, with the window above and the fireplace below.","techniques":"- Two hand-planned compositions (landscape 1600×1000, portrait 1000×1700) rendered into one SVG with \"meet\" scaling; the log wall and plank floor bleed far beyond the frame so any aspect ratio stays filled\n- Procedural SVG construction: horizontal logs with gradient rounding, chinking, grain, knots and butt joints; a perspective plank floor converging on a vanishing point; irregular rounded stones from jittered quadratic outlines with a shared highlight/shadow gradient; voussoirs laid along the quadratic arch of the firebox, with a keystone and jamb blocks\n- The wall is cut by an SVG mask so the window opening shows a canvas behind it: a cached sky gradient, twinkling stars, aurora rays built from one gradient sprite drawn as hundreds of thin strips (additive blending, rippling with sine and value noise) over soft radial glows, pre-rendered spruce rows with snow ledges, a drift lit warm by the cabin, and snow at three depths\n- A canvas fire clipped to the firebox arch: a coal bed, and three nested layers of Bézier flame tongues (red, orange, pale yellow) whose heights and sway come from value noise, with blue at the roots and additive blending; rising embers are clipped to the opening, and flare sparks leap out when a log lands\n- Room lighting through blend-mode overlays: a multiply night layer whose strength depends on the lamp and the fire, a screen-blended firelight gradient whose opacity follows the flame noise every frame, a lamp pool, and a cold aurora-tinted window glow\n- Real 3D casement sashes: HTML panes with frost gradients and muntins that rotate with CSS perspective about their hinges when the window opens\n- Life in the details: candle flames with halos, mug steam that the draught bends sideways, the cat breathing (body scaled from its base), spring-driven ear twitches and tail flicks, drifting \"z\"s, a swinging pendulum, and glowing seams on the charred logs that pulse with the fire\n- An added birch log flies from the basket in an arc, drops into the firebox and slowly chars from white bark to black","originalInteraction":"- Click the lamp (or L) to toggle it: the shade and bulb glow, and the room darkens or brightens\n- Click the fire (or F) to add a log: it arcs from the basket into the grate, the fire flares, sparks burst and the flames stay boosted for several seconds\n- Click the window (or W) to open it: the sashes swing in, snow blows into the room, the steam and flames lean, the fire gutters and the cold glow strengthens. It closes by itself after about five seconds, or on another click\n- Click the cat (or C) to make its ear twitch and tail flick, with a purr when sound is on\n- The Sound toggle synthesizes everything with Web Audio after the first click: a low fire roar, randomly scheduled filtered-noise crackles that speed up with the fire, wind whose band-pass is swept by an LFO and swells when the window opens, log thuds and a 26 Hz amplitude-modulated purr\n- Every object is also a real button with an aria-label, a tooltip on hover or focus and a visible focus ring. A live region narrates each change. Rendering pauses while the tab is hidden, and reduced motion slows the flicker, snow and pendulum","sourceHash":"a544e8041db412f5ed244e82314e204538b34a8c4af8017e1d80e466e3226ed6","name":"冬夜木屋","category":"生成艺术","description":"一间北欧木屋以木纹、炉火和雪夜窗景构成可互动插画。光源的开关会改变整个房间的气氛。","interaction":"点击台灯或按 L 开关灯光；点击炉火或按 F 添一根木柴。"},{"id":"052","slug":"052-dna-helix","title":"Helix — The Double Helix Explained","file":"052-dna-helix.html","textFile":"052-dna-helix.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/052-dna-helix.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Helix — The Double Helix Explained\", an educational, beautiful interactive visualization. Centre: a rotating B-DNA double helix drawn on canvas with 3D projection — two sugar-phosphate backbones as glossy ribbons (#2ec4b6 and #e71d36 with depth shading) and base pairs as rungs coloured by nucleotide (A #ff9f1c, T #ffbf69, G #6a4c93, C #8ac926), with correct pairing (A–T, G–C), ~10.5 base pairs per turn, and depth fading for the back strands. A clean biotech palette on off-white (#f7f9fb) with a dark-mode toggle. A side panel with annotations and leader lines (major/minor groove; hydrogen bonds: 2 for A–T, 3 for G–C; 5'→3' direction), a sequence input where typing A/T/G/C rebuilds the helix live (auto-generating the complementary strand), and an \"Unzip\" button that animates replication — the strands separate and new complementary nucleotides float in. Interaction: drag to rotate, scroll to zoom, hover a base pair to highlight and label it. Typography: a modern sans with a monospace sequence display.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Helix is a clean, editorial biotech explainer. On the left, a large canvas on an off-white dotted-grid \"lab bench\" (#f7f9fb) shows an idealised B-DNA double helix, rendered in true 3D with perspective. It is right-handed, has 10.5 base pairs per turn and a 0.34 nm rise, and its two backbones are offset about 135° around the axis, which produces a visibly wide major groove and a narrow minor groove. The helix slowly rotates on a slightly diagonal axis, beside a bold \"The Double Helix\" headline and a one-turn scale bracket. On the right, a white annotation panel lists five numbered features (5′→3′ direction, sugar–phosphate backbone, major groove, minor groove, hydrogen bonds). Live leader lines run from each note to numbered pins that track the matching feature as the molecule turns. Below the notes are the key measurements, a sequence builder with a colour-coded complementary strand, and a legend. An \"Unzip & replicate\" sequence animates a simplified semi-conservative replication. A dark mode moves the whole piece to deep navy.","techniques":"- Hand-written 3D pipeline on canvas 2D: model coordinates in ångström, spin about the helix axis, tilt, perspective divide, then an in-plane roll (diagonal on wide screens, near-vertical on phones)\n- Correct B-DNA geometry: right-handed twist of 360°/10.5 per bp, 3.4 Å rise, 10 Å radius, 135° backbone offset giving roughly 13 Å and 22 Å grooves; antiparallel strands labelled 5′/3′ at their ends\n- Glossy backbone ribbons (#2ec4b6, #e71d36) built from per-segment quads, lit by a Lambert and specular term from the ribbon normal, with phosphate beads and white 5′→3′ chevrons\n- Base-pair rungs split at a purine/pyrimidine-weighted point and coloured by nucleotide (A #ff9f1c, T #ffbf69, G #6a4c93, C #8ac926), with specular highlight lines and dashed hydrogen bonds (two for A=T, three for G≡C)\n- Depth handled by pooled draw items sorted back to front (painter's algorithm), plus depth fading of back elements toward the page colour (works in both themes)\n- Live leader lines: an SVG overlay connects panel anchors to the front-most matching feature each frame (a groove centre, a backbone point, an A–T and a G–C pair); canvas pins carry the numbers\n- Replication: a moving fork separates the strands onto two offset axes, free nucleotides fly in along eased wobbling paths, then new lighter-tinted backbones join up into two daughter helices\n- Pop-in growth for bases that change when the sequence is edited; a dotted-grid CSS background; a theme-aware palette","originalInteraction":"- Drag to rotate: movement across the axis spins the helix and movement along it tilts it; spin keeps its inertia and eases back into the auto-rotation. Scroll wheel or pinch to zoom; buttons for pause/play, zoom and reset; keyboard (arrows rotate and tilt, +/− zoom, Space pauses)\n- Hover a rung (or tap on touch) to highlight that base pair: a tooltip names both bases, purine or pyrimidine, and the number of hydrogen bonds, and the matching column lights up in the sequence display. Hovering letters in the sequence display highlights the pair in 3D\n- Hover an annotation to emphasise its leader line and pin\n- Sequence input accepts A/T/G/C only (other characters are stripped with a gentle warning), 2 to 40 bases; the helix rebuilds live with the complementary strand, and GC %, hydrogen-bond count, turns, length and a basic Tm estimate update. A Random button generates a new sequence\n- \"Unzip & replicate\" runs the fork and synthesis with stage captions, then becomes \"Rejoin strands\" to reverse it\n- The dark-mode toggle is saved in localStorage (guarded with try/catch); rendering pauses when the tab is hidden; reduced motion slows rotation and speeds up the replication steps","sourceHash":"887f52211ffc2824e5ddf8592138557837fd411cc2f83a2cb9c385bac0ce3bde","name":"双螺旋实验台","category":"科学与模拟","description":"理想化 B 型 DNA 双螺旋在浅色实验台上旋转，三维结构与右侧讲解共同呈现分子的空间秩序。","interaction":"拖动旋转，滚轮或双指缩放，查看配对和结构信息。"},{"id":"053","slug":"053-tarot-reading","title":"Arcana Nocturne — A Three-Card Reading","file":"053-tarot-reading.html","textFile":"053-tarot-reading.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/053-tarot-reading.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Arcana Nocturne — A Three-Card Reading\". A midnight-purple ground (#140a24) with drifting star dust and a velvet tablecloth texture; gold-foil (#d4af37 → #f7e7a1) and moonlight-silver accents. A deck of the 22 Major Arcana, each face designed procedurally in SVG with an original geometric/esoteric motif (e.g. The Moon: crescents and a path between two towers; The Star: eight-pointed stars above water; The Tower: lightning-struck tower geometry), framed with an ornate gold border, a Roman numeral and the title in engraved serif capitals. The back: an intricate symmetric pattern around a central sun/eye. Interaction: \"Shuffle\" animates the deck riffling; the user fans the deck and picks three cards (Past, Present, Future), which fly to their positions and flip in 3D with a glint; each revealed card shows an evocative, original short interpretation (framed as entertainment). Cards tilt with a shimmer on hover. Optional reversed cards. Typography: serif small caps with generous letter-spacing; body text in an italic serif.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Arcana Nocturne\" is a three-card tarot reading staged on a midnight-purple velvet table under drifting gold and silver star dust. A faint astrological wheel turns slowly behind three dashed slots (Past, Present, Future) marked with a crescent, a sun and a star. On load the reader spreads the deck: 22 card backs, each an intricate symmetric design of a sunburst eye inside a gilded medallion with crescents, a lattice ground and fleuron corners, fan out in a gentle arc. Drawing a card lifts it from the fan and flies it to its slot, where it turns over in 3D with a glint of light to reveal one of 22 original, procedurally drawn Major Arcana faces in gold foil and moonlight silver (a lightning-struck tower, a moon between two towers, an eight-pointed star over water, and so on). Each face has an arched night window, a numeral cartouche and a title plate. Under each card appears an engraved small-caps name, its orientation and an original italic interpretation. After the third card the deck gathers away and a single line ties the reading together. The whole page is framed as entertainment.","techniques":"- 22 card faces built at runtime as inline SVG from hand-written motif functions (sunbursts, eight-pointed stars, crescents, lemniscates, waves, flowers, towers, scales, wreaths) sharing one set of userSpaceOnUse gold-foil and silver gradients, so the foil sheen sweeps consistently across every card\n- Ornate frame per face: triple gold border with a dotted rail, fleuron corner symbols, an arched window clipped per card with star specks, spandrel stars, numeral cartouche and title plate; letters inside motifs (B and J, T A R O) are drawn as stroked glyph paths\n- Numerals and titles are HTML overlays scaled from the card-width custom property with gold gradient text, so they stay crisp and correctly placed through the 3D flip\n- Card back as a reusable SVG symbol: a 32-point sunburst computed at runtime, an eye with a silver iris, mirrored crescents, a diamond lattice pattern and corner fleurons\n- Each card is a nested 3D stack (position, hover lift, pointer tilt, flip) with backface-visibility faces, a CSS glint sweep after the flip, and a pointer-following shimmer blended with screen\n- Web Animations API choreography: gather, split into two bent piles, a staggered riffle into a Fisher–Yates order, squaring taps, an arced fan layout, arced card flights with a scale \"pop\", and the fan re-closing around each drawn card\n- Canvas star dust with twinkle, slow drift, glowing larger motes and pointer parallax; paused while the tab is hidden\n- Velvet tablecloth from layered radial gradients and fine crossed pile lines, with a dashed gold embroidery inset; a slowly rotating astrological wheel drawn with dash-array tricks (ticks, house lines and dots from single circles)\n- Foil title lettering with an animated gradient, small caps with generous tracking, italic serif body text, and notched gold-bordered buttons made with clip-path and a masked gradient border","originalInteraction":"- Click or tap any card in the fan to draw it; cards are laid in order as Past, Present and Future, each flipping on arrival and revealing its interpretation\n- Keyboard: the fan is a single tab stop with a roving focus; Left, Right, Home and End move between cards, Enter or Space draws\n- \"Shuffle the deck\" returns any laid cards face down, gathers everything, and riffles the deck with a visible split and interleave; \"Fan the cards\" (or tapping the deck) spreads it again\n- \"Reversals\" toggle (remembered in localStorage, guarded) lets roughly a third of draws land reversed, which rotates the face and switches to the reversed reading\n- Hovering a laid card tilts it in 3D toward the pointer with a moving shimmer; fan cards lift and glow on hover or focus\n- After three cards a summary sentence combines the three keywords; on phones the readings stack below the table\n- Optional Web Audio sound (off by default, visible toggle): paper flicks while shuffling and a soft bell as each card turns\n- Status line and aria-live readings announce each step; prefers-reduced-motion removes the flights, riffle, glint, drifting dust and wheel rotation","sourceHash":"c1dca416929c58797f46c1fcdd7f79a502528e7c62d5a2e09ff5eb543fe1309c","name":"星夜塔罗","category":"音乐与游戏","description":"三张塔罗牌在午夜紫色绒布上展开，星尘、占星圆环与翻牌动效共同构成虚构占卜体验。","interaction":"从扇形牌堆中依次抽取三张牌，查看过去、现在和未来的解读。"},{"id":"054","slug":"054-elastic-letters","title":"ELASTIC — Letterforms on Springs","file":"054-elastic-letters.html","textFile":"054-elastic-letters.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/054-elastic-letters.jpg","prompt":"Build \"ELASTIC — Letterforms on Springs\", an experimental typography playground. A giant word (default \"BOUNCE\", editable via an input) is converted into points sampled from its glyphs (draw the text on an offscreen canvas and sample filled pixels); each point is tied to its home position by a spring. Render the points as connected dots/filaments forming the letters. The pointer repels (or attracts — toggle) points with a force field; releasing lets them spring back with overshoot and jiggle. Click to explode the word into particles that reassemble. Palette: hot magenta #ff2e88 on deep ink #111018, with cyan #29e7cd on fast-moving points (velocity-coloured). Modes: dots; lines (connecting nearby neighbours); threads (points tied to home with visible springs). Sliders: stiffness, damping, force radius. UI typography in small monospace; the display lettering is generated from the heavy system sans. Responsive: sampling density adapts to screen size for performance.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"ELASTIC is a dark typographic playground in deep ink (#111018), hot magenta (#ff2e88) and cyan (#29e7cd). A giant word, BOUNCE by default and editable in the dock, is drawn in the heaviest system sans on an offscreen canvas and sampled into a hexagonal lattice of roughly 2,500 points, each tied to its home by a damped spring. At rest the letters are magenta dot-matrix glyphs sitting between dashed cap-height and baseline guides, with sidebearing ticks and a per-glyph point count (\"B · 477\") underneath like a type specimen. As soon as anything moves, velocity turns points cyan, short comet tails form, and a soft CSS-blurred bloom makes the whole word glow. On load the points fly in from a scattered ring, then a ghost pointer combs once through the word to show the spring jiggle before handing control to you. The UI is a restrained monospace dock: word field, render mode, repel/attract, three physics sliders and a magenta Explode button.","techniques":"- Glyph sampling: each character is drawn separately with custom tracking on an offscreen canvas, its alpha is read with getImageData, and a hexagonal grid is sampled; the pitch is solved from the filled area so the point budget adapts to screen size (about 700 to 3,400 points)\n- Portrait screens split the word into two rows so the letters stay large on phones\n- Spring physics in typed arrays (position, velocity and home as Float32Array), two semi-implicit substeps per frame, with stiffness, damping, a quadratic-falloff pointer field and a drag term from the pointer's own velocity\n- A slow travelling \"breathing\" wave on the home positions keeps the word alive while idle\n- Velocity colouring by counting-sorting points into seven magenta-to-cyan bins, one batched path per bin\n- Lines mode: precomputed hex-lattice neighbour links, binned by stretch and speed, that tear (disappear) past 3.2× their rest length\n- Threads mode: faint home anchors plus zig-zag coil springs drawn from home to point, which turn cyan when strongly stretched\n- Explosion: radial impulses with angular jitter while spring strength drops to zero and then ramps back, so the word reassembles with overshoot\n- A translucent clear gives motion trails; a quarter-resolution copy under CSS blur plus a screen blend gives the bloom; a separate HUD canvas draws the animated force-field ring, direction arrows and label\n- Typographic guides (cap height, baseline, sidebearings, glyph point counts) drawn from real text metrics","originalInteraction":"- Hover (mouse) or drag (touch) to push the points; switch the pointer to Attract to pull them instead (key A)\n- Click or tap without dragging to explode the word from that spot; the Explode button (or E / Space) bursts it from the centre\n- Type a new word (up to 10 characters): the points morph from the old word into the new one\n- Render modes Dots, Lines and Threads (keys 1–3); sliders for stiffness, damping and force radius, with live readouts\n- Synthesized Web Audio \"boing\" on explode and a soft pluck on mode or word changes, created only after a gesture, with a visible Sound/Muted toggle\n- Word, mode, sliders and sound preference are remembered with guarded localStorage; the loop pauses while the tab is hidden; reduced motion skips the intro scatter, ghost pass, trails and breathing","sourceHash":"b6ec9f617cb7aa2cfa7ff9e9a7b74acafe3e4e56fca1bbe7d1c128759d939026","name":"弹性字母","category":"排版实验","description":"巨大的文字由弹簧粒子组成，字形在指针推拉中散开又重新聚拢。文字内容与物理参数都可调整。","interaction":"移动指针推开粒子，点击引爆文字；切换吸引模式或编辑底部文字。"},{"id":"055","slug":"055-falling-sand","title":"Dune Engine — Falling Sand Sandbox","file":"055-falling-sand.html","textFile":"055-falling-sand.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/055-falling-sand.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Dune Engine — Falling Sand Sandbox\", a cellular-automaton physics toy. A pixel-art canvas (each cell 3–4px) with elements: Sand (warm variations #e6c07b, #d8a95b), Water (#3d7ee0 with shimmer, flows sideways), Stone (static greys), Fire (flickers, rises, burns wood, turns water to steam), Wood (#8b5a2b), Steam (rises and condenses back into water), Oil (floats on water, highly flammable), Lava (turns sand into glass), and Plant (grows into water). Correct density-based interactions (sand sinks in water, oil floats). Desert-dusk UI palette: sand #f3e3c3, terracotta #c1440e, deep dusk blue #1f2a44. An element picker as a row of textured swatches with icons, brush-size slider, pause, clear, and \"Scenes\" (hourglass, volcano, oasis). Pointer/touch painting interpolated along the stroke. A tiny monospace readout of particle count and fps. Efficient: typed arrays, bottom-up updates with alternating direction, ImageData rendering.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Dune Engine is a falling-sand physics toy styled as a desert-dusk instrument. A large pixel-art canvas, framed in terracotta, sits under a dusk-blue sky with faint stars and a banded retro sun low on the horizon; a sand-coloured tool panel holds textured element swatches, the brush, the controls and three scenes. The title \"DUNE ENGINE\" is drawn in a 5×7 bitmap font whose grains fall into place like sand. The simulation is a real cellular automaton on 3–4 px cells: sand piles and sinks through water, water shimmers and levels out sideways, oil floats and catches fire, fire flickers upward and burns wood and plants, steam rises and rains back down, lava crawls, melts sand into glass and crusts into stone where it meets the sea, and plants creep into any water they touch. It opens on an erupting volcano scene with a glowing lava conduit, a forested flank and an oily sea.","techniques":"- Typed-array grid (element, shade, life and update-stamp arrays) swept bottom-up with alternating row direction, so falls and flows stay unbiased and each particle moves at most once per frame\n- Density-based displacement: sand (20) sinks through water (10) and oil (8); gases rise through liquids; lava (30) stays under everything; element-specific dispersion distances for water, oil and lava\n- Reactions: fire ignites wood, plants and oil at different rates and boils water into steam; anchored flames on burning wood spawn free flames; lava turns sand to glass, ignites fuel and hardens to stone in water; steam and smoke have lifetimes, and steam condenses into rain\n- Rendering through one ImageData with a Uint32 view (one pixel per cell) drawn scaled up with smoothing off; per-element colour variations, animated water shimmer with bright surface cells, fire coloured by age with random flicker, lava with travelling heat bands\n- A cheap bloom: fire and lava heat is accumulated into a quarter-resolution buffer and drawn twice, smoothed and additively blended, so flames light up the rock around them\n- A static sky buffer with a vertical dusk gradient, one-pixel dithering, hashed stars and a banded retro sun, computed once per resize\n- Element swatches textured procedurally in tiny canvases (dispersed sand, shimmering water, brick stone, flame gradients, wood grain), each with a hand-drawn SVG icon and key badge\n- Procedural scenes: an hourglass with continuous glass walls and a wooden frame, a volcano with a lava crater, conduit and chamber, trees, a beach and an oil slick, and an oasis of sine dunes, a pool, reeds, curved palms and boulders\n- Bitmap-font title rendered to canvas, with grains that fall and bounce into their places","originalInteraction":"- Paint with mouse, pen or touch; strokes are interpolated along the path so fast drags leave unbroken lines, and holding still keeps pouring a stream\n- Right-click (or the Eraser swatch) erases; painting fire onto wood, oil or plants lights them\n- Element picker: ten textured swatches, also on keys 1–9 and 0; the selection is remembered in guarded localStorage\n- Brush size slider (1–14 cells) with a live preview dot and [ / ] shortcuts; a ring cursor on the canvas shows the brush footprint\n- Controls: Pause/Play (Space), Step one frame while paused (the \".\" key too), a speed toggle cycling ½×, 1× and 2×, and Clear, which sweeps the grid away with a moving terracotta line\n- Scenes: Hourglass, Volcano and Oasis rebuild the world instantly\n- A tiny monospace readout shows live particle count, frames per second, grid size and speed\n- The canvas re-fits on resize, preserving existing particles anchored to the floor; the simulation pauses while the tab is hidden, and prefers-reduced-motion starts it at half speed","sourceHash":"fd8b9dce00e63ba6b94336fe0e273a048bd8d1d16cea2b966a8b05538fcfda33","name":"落沙引擎","category":"科学与模拟","description":"像素画布中的沙、水与其他材料遵循各自的运动规则。沙漠暮色框架把物理玩具变成一台小型仪器。","interaction":"选择材料后绘制，按住持续倾倒；右键或选择橡皮擦清除区域。"},{"id":"056","slug":"056-architectural-blueprint","title":"Casa Lumen — Architectural Blueprint","file":"056-architectural-blueprint.html","textFile":"056-architectural-blueprint.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/056-architectural-blueprint.jpg","prompt":"Design \"Casa Lumen — Architectural Blueprint\", an animated technical drawing of a fictional modernist house. Blueprint blue (#0b3d91 to #134a9e with a fine grid of lighter lines) with white/cyan line work (#e8f1ff, #9fd3ff). The plan draws itself on load: walls first (thick lines with hatched cuts), then doors with swing arcs, windows, stairs, furniture outlines, dimension lines with arrowheads and measurements, room labels, a north arrow, and a title block in the corner (project name, scale 1:100, sheet A-101, drawn by). Use staggered SVG stroke-dashoffset animations, like a hand drafting. Interaction: hovering a room fills it with translucent cyan and shows its area (m²) in a floating label; switch between \"Floor plan\", \"Elevation\" (south façade) and \"Section\" views with a cross-drawing transition; a \"Pencil mode\" switch re-renders the drawing as graphite on paper (#f5f3ee paper, #333 pencil). Typography: technical lettering (monospace/architectural sans stack, uppercase).\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Casa Lumen is a drawing sheet for a fictional modernist house on blueprint blue (#0b3d91 to #134a9e), with a fine and a coarse grid, film grain, a double sheet border and zone letters and numbers in the margin. Its left side is the live drawing, in white (#e8f1ff) and cyan (#9fd3ff) line work. Its right side is a drafting column: a room schedule, general notes, a 1:100 scale bar and a full title block (project, drawing name, scale 1:100, sheet A-101, drawn by, checked, date, revision history). The house is designed to be internally consistent across three views. It is an 18 × 11 m single-storey pavilion wrapped around a glass patio open to the sky, with a 16-riser stair up to an upper studio and a roof slab that cantilevers 1.20 m over a south terrace. The South Elevation shows the same openings, and Section A–A (cut through the stair and patio) shows the noon sun at 75° and 28° for latitude 38.5° N passing under the overhang. Every view draws itself like a hand drafting. A Pencil mode re-draws the sheet in graphite on warm paper.","techniques":"- All geometry is authored in metres and turned into SVG at 1 m = 40 units by small drawing helpers (lines, rectangles, rounded rectangles, arcs, dimensions, arrowheads, scalloped tree canopies)\n- Staggered stroke-dashoffset drafting (pathLength = 1) in a real drafting order: walls and cuts, hatched poché, doors with swing arcs and windows, stair treads with a break line, furniture, dimension chains with arrowheads, lettering, then symbols; a progress rule in the toolbar names the layer being drawn\n- Plan conventions: 45° hatched wall cuts, window symbols in the wall thickness, sliding glazing on two tracks, dashed treads above the cut plane, a dashed roof overhang, a section line with A tags and view arrows, a north arrow, room numbers and areas with paper-coloured halos, and a gravel-stippled patio\n- Elevation: timber cladding strokes, glass reflection hatching, roof-terrace guard rails, the patio tree rising above the roof, a scale figure, level markers (±0.00, +3.60, +6.60) and dimension chains\n- Section: solid poché for cut concrete (slabs, walls, footings, parapets, a sawtooth stair), earth hatching, glass cut detail, dashed sun rays with arrowheads for the solstice noon angles, and the tree seen through the open patio\n- \"Cross-drawing\" view change: the old view erases (dashoffset to −1, reverse-staggered) while the new view starts drafting over it\n- Pencil mode swaps CSS custom properties to graphite (#333) on paper (#f5f3ee), crossfades the background layers, turns on faint construction lines, and runs the ink through an SVG feTurbulence + feDisplacementMap filter for a hand-drawn wobble\n- Technical lettering: condensed DIN/ISO-style sans stack in wide-tracked uppercase, with monospace for dimensions and title-block data","originalInteraction":"- Floor plan / Elevation / Section tabs (arrow-key navigable) switch views with the erase-and-redraw transition; the title block's drawing name and sheet number follow the view\n- Hover, focus or tap a room (plan), a space (section) or a glazing element (elevation): it fills with translucent cyan and a floating label gives its name, area in m² and dimensions; the matching row in the room schedule lights up, and hovering a schedule row highlights its room\n- The Pencil mode switch (role=\"switch\") erases the drawing and re-drafts it in graphite on paper; Redraw replays the drafting animation\n- On narrow screens the sheet stacks: the drawing sits in a horizontally swipeable frame (scrolled to the centre) above the schedule, notes and title block\n- Reduced motion shows every view complete, without the drawing or erasing animations","sourceHash":"bb0cb045b471fec3d5d6c99fc4991e6105c4956dd1940a41ed69dd2ee5faf623","name":"建筑蓝图","category":"数据与工具","description":"一座虚构现代住宅被绘成带坐标、图签和双层网格的蓝图。线条在平面、立面与剖面切换时擦除再重绘。","interaction":"切换图纸视图，悬停或聚焦建筑部位查看对应信息。"},{"id":"057","slug":"057-vinyl-listening-room","title":"Hi-Fi Walnut — Vinyl Listening Room","file":"057-vinyl-listening-room.html","textFile":"057-vinyl-listening-room.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/057-vinyl-listening-room.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Hi-Fi Walnut — Vinyl Listening Room\", a luxury music player. A photoreal-leaning turntable in CSS/SVG: a walnut plinth (#5c3d2e, wood grain from layered gradients), a brushed-aluminium platter, a black record with realistic grooves (repeating radial gradients) and specular light arcs that stay fixed while the record spins, a centre label designed per album, and a tonearm that swings onto the record when playing. Music: three original \"albums\" synthesized with Web Audio — ambient generative pieces (e.g. Rhodes-like FM chords under a slow melody, with soft vinyl-crackle noise), each with a distinct mood. The UI panel: generative CSS album art, track title, animated progress, play/pause, next, a volume knob, and a pair of VU meters whose needles respond to the audio (AnalyserNode). Palette: walnut, brass #c9a227, cream #efe6d8 and a deep green felt background #1f3b2d. Typography: elegant serif album titles with small caps. Changing albums triggers a record-swap animation.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Hi-Fi Walnut is a private listening room rendered as a luxury object. On deep green felt (#1f3b2d) lit by a warm lamp sits a top-down walnut turntable: a figured veneer plinth (#5c3d2e) with a brass inlay line, hinges and nameplate, a brushed-aluminium platter with strobe markings and an amber strobe lamp, a black record with fine grooves, dark silent bands exactly where the tracks change, and fixed specular light arcs that stay put while the record and its label turn underneath. An S-shaped aluminium tonearm with a brass counterweight lifts, swings onto the lead-in, lowers with a needle-drop thump and then tracks inward through the side. Below the turntable an integrated amplifier holds two cream analogue VU meters that glow amber and swing with the music, and a knurled brass volume knob. On the right a cream liner-notes card (#efe6d8) carries generative CSS cover art, album titles in display-serif small caps, the track list, a brass progress rail, the transport and a crate of three records. All three fictional albums are ambient pieces generated live with Web Audio: the candlelit Rhodes, flute and brushes of \"Moss & Lantern Light\", the rainy organ and electric piano of \"Tidewater Hours\", and the music-box bell arpeggios of \"Brass Constellations\".","techniques":"- Walnut veneer from inline SVG: an x-stretched feTurbulence grain mapped through feColorMatrix into walnut tones, a second low-frequency turbulence for darker figure, then lamp-light, bevel and brass inlay layers\n- Record built from stacked CSS gradients: px-pitch repeating-radial grooves, smooth lead-in, run-out and rim bands, faint asymmetric pressing sheen, dust specks, and two dark inter-track bands whose radii (CSS custom properties) are computed from each album's real track timing\n- Fixed specular \"bowtie\" highlights: a masked conic-gradient in screen blend mode that never rotates, so the grooves appear to turn beneath the light\n- Rotating per-album SVG labels with text on circular paths (title above, imprint below), a static spindle, and a platter with repeating-conic strobe dots lit by an amber lamp\n- Tonearm geometry solved from the pivot, arm length and spindle distance (θ = acos(k/R) − φ), so the stylus sits on the exact groove radius for the current album time; a blurred shadow twin and the cue lever respond to the lift height\n- Platter inertia: angular velocity eases up to 33⅓ rpm (200°/s) and coasts down slowly\n- Record swap with the Web Animations API: the disc lifts, spins away off the deck, the label and bands change, and the next record slides in and settles\n- VU meters in SVG with a true VU scale (needle position linear in voltage, −20 to +3 VU with a red zone), spring ballistics tuned to roughly 300 ms, peak lamps and a warm backlight\n- Generative album art in pure CSS: lantern glow with moss hills, tide bands with moon and rain, and brass orbits with a star field and SVG constellations\n- A felt texture painted once on a fixed canvas from thousands of short fibres, plus lamp light and vignette","originalInteraction":"- Play/Pause from the big brass button, a click on the record, the deck's START·STOP button or Space: the motor spins up, the arm cues and drops, and the music starts; pause lifts the arm in place and the platter coasts to a stop\n- Previous/Next (buttons or P/N) lift the arm, move it to the next track band and drop it again; tracks can also be chosen from the list, and the progress rail can be dragged or clicked to seek (←/→ jump 10 s)\n- Change the record from the crate (or keys 1 2 3), with the full lift-away and settle animation; if music was playing it resumes on the new record\n- Brass volume knob: drag vertically or horizontally, use the mouse wheel, or arrow and Page keys (it is an ARIA slider); ↑/↓ also adjust volume globally, and a MUTE toggle switch (or M) silences output\n- Audio: an AudioContext created on the first gesture; FM electric piano, flute with breath noise and delayed vibrato, inharmonic FM bells, a detuned-saw organ pad, a sine/triangle bass and brushed noise, all through gentle saturation, a glue compressor and a generated convolution reverb; wow at the platter's own 0.556 Hz, looping surface crackle with a scratch that ticks once per revolution, and rain for the grey record; bars are seeded, so seeking replays the same music\n- End of side: the stylus drifts into the run-out groove, thumps softly each revolution, then the arm returns automatically\n- prefers-reduced-motion shortens arm and swap animations; the frame loop pauses while the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"9fa1ed326f0f18b0bc016dd74102b649d4ededefdbe5a1da21507965083494ca","name":"黑胶聆听室","category":"音乐与游戏","description":"胡桃木唱机放在深绿毛毡上，唱臂、唱片与黄铜按钮构成完整的播放仪式。","interaction":"点击播放按钮、唱片或按空格；观察电机启动、唱臂落下与暂停抬臂。"},{"id":"058","slug":"058-memphis-fest","title":"Radical Shapes — Milano Design Fest '86","file":"058-memphis-fest.html","textFile":"058-memphis-fest.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/058-memphis-fest.jpg","prompt":"Design \"RADICAL SHAPES — Milano Design Fest '86\" (fictional), a Memphis-Group-inspired event landing page. Palette: black #111, pink #ff90b3, turquoise #2ec4b6, yellow #ffd23f, lilac #b388eb, red #ef476f on white, with terrazzo/confetti patterns and squiggle backgrounds (SVG patterns). The composition is bold, playful and asymmetric: geometric objects (cylinders, zigzags, dotted triangles, half-moons) float and rotate slowly around the hero title. Typography: chunky geometric sans headlines, bold outlines and two-colour offset text shadows. Sections: a countdown to the fictional opening with flip digits; a lineup of fictional designers on cards with patterned headers; a schedule timeline; a venue map made of playful illustrated blocks; and ticket tiers. Interaction: shapes bounce away from the cursor; clicking the hero spawns new random Memphis shapes with a pop; cards wobble on hover; a pattern-shuffler button changes every background pattern. Keep chaos from overwhelming readability: a strong grid underneath.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"RADICAL SHAPES is a landing page for a fictional festival, the Milano Design Fest '86, designed as if the Memphis movement had made its own website. Every block has a thick black outline and a hard offset shadow, and the page sits on a 12-column grid, so the loud patterns stay organised. The hero stacks the chunky geometric title (black-stroked letters with two-colour offset shadows) over a tilted terrazzo slab on graph paper, with a dotted turquoise disc, a squiggle band and a dozen hand-drawn Memphis objects floating around it: a striped cylinder, a zigzag, a dotted triangle, a half-moon, a banded sphere, a totem column, a lightning bolt and a rainbow arch. Below the hero come a black countdown band with yellow flip-card digits and saw-tooth edges; six designer cards with patterned headers and geometric portraits; a lilac graph-paper schedule with day tabs and a shaped-dot timeline; an illustrated map of a made-up district drawn from chunky SVG blocks; and three perforated tickets on a terrazzo pink field.","techniques":"- Nine SVG <pattern> tiles (terrazzo, confetti, squiggle, zigzag, polka dots, stripes, graph grid, dotted triangles, rainbow waves) inked through CSS custom properties, so a single variable swap recolours every pattern at once\n- Offset-shadow Memphis styling everywhere: 3px ink borders, hard box-shadows, black-stroked text with paint-order and two-colour text-shadows, tilted blocks and taped cards\n- A spring-physics layer for the hero shapes: each shape has a home position, velocity, damping, idle bobbing and rotation, and is pushed away by the cursor with a squash-and-stretch wobble\n- The Memphis shapes are drawn at runtime as small parameterised inline SVGs; new ones pop in with an overshooting scale-and-rotate plus a CSS confetti burst\n- 3D flip-clock digits (split halves plus a rotating leaf with backface-visibility) driven by a real countdown to the next 14 April, 18:00 Milan time, computed with Intl time zones\n- Illustrated SVG map with offset-shadow blocks, a dashed-road grid, a canal, lollipop trees and venues that lift on hover or focus and show a dashed selection ring\n- Ticket stubs with perforation lines and half-circle notches, a rotated \"Most radical\" badge, IntersectionObserver reveal-on-scroll, and a toast that slides up with an overshoot","originalInteraction":"- Move the cursor (or drag a finger) through the hero and the floating shapes bounce away, then spring back home\n- Click or tap anywhere in the hero to drop a new random Memphis shape with a pop and confetti (the oldest disappear after 18)\n- Cards wobble on hover or keyboard focus; the letters of the title hop when hovered\n- The Shuffle button (or the S key) reassigns every background pattern and rotates the pattern ink palette across the whole page\n- Schedule tabs (arrow keys supported, ARIA tablist) switch between five days; each day changes the theme card, its illustration and a staggered timeline\n- Venue blocks on the map are keyboard-focusable buttons; choosing one updates the info panel beside the map\n- Ticket \"Reserve\" buttons answer with a toast explaining that the festival is fictional; sticky nav links jump to each section\n- prefers-reduced-motion stops the bobbing, rotation, wobble, reveal and flip animations; the animation loop pauses when the tab is hidden or the hero is off-screen","sourceHash":"4e31b4c8fae581bcec533f1a021f8e69d09c17ec5ab13dd39c9a201117c490f4","name":"孟菲斯设计节","category":"交互网页","description":"虚构的 1986 年米兰设计节以几何图形、鲜明色块、粗边框和硬阴影组成活泼首页。","interaction":"移动指针推开几何形，点击主视觉增加新形状，体验弹性回位。"},{"id":"059","slug":"059-zen-sand-garden","title":"Karesansui — A Zen Sand Garden","file":"059-zen-sand-garden.html","textFile":"059-zen-sand-garden.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/059-zen-sand-garden.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Karesansui — A Zen Sand Garden\": a top-down view of a raked gravel garden on canvas with fine sand texture (#e8e0cf with grain noise and subtle lighting), a few natural stones (procedurally shaped and shaded, with moss patches #6b7d4b) and a wooden border frame (#5b3f2a). Dragging with the rake carves parallel grooves (4–5 tines) that follow your path, with realistic light and shadow on the ridges; the ripple tool draws concentric rings around a stone on click. Tools in a minimal vertical bar: rake, ripple, place stone, smooth (softly erases grooves), and \"return to calm\" (animated smoothing). Ambient touches: a maple leaf occasionally drifts across and settles; soft shadows move slowly as if the sun were passing; an optional distant wind chime (Web Audio, after a gesture). Typography: small, elegant serif captions and a vertically set title. Touch on mobile is first-class.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Karesansui is a small dry garden to tend by hand, seen from directly above. A mitred frame of dark wood (#5b3f2a) holds a bed of fine pale gravel (#e8e0cf). The gravel is raked into gently wavering straight furrows that break into concentric rings around two moss islands (#6b7d4b) and a lone stone. Stones and moss are generated procedurally and lit by a sun that slowly swings from morning to evening, so the furrows, stones and every cast shadow change as it moves. Red and amber maple leaves occasionally tumble across on the wind and settle, each trailed by its own drifting shadow. The page around the garden is dark and quiet. A vertically set title (枯山水 / KARESANSUI, with a small vermilion 静 seal) sits on the left, a slim washi-paper tool bar on the right, and small italic serif captions below.","techniques":"- The gravel is a real heightmap (Float32Array) lit per cell. Normals come from finite differences, with Lambert shading normalised so flat sand stays exactly #e8e0cf, a warm tint on lit faces, cool shadows, cavity darkening and baked grain and speckle.\n- Only changed regions are re-lit, via dirty rectangles and putImageData, then upscaled with smoothing into a device-pixel-ratio canvas. A multiply-blended grain tile keeps the texture crisp on high-DPI screens.\n- The rake stamps a five-tine cosine profile across the swept path, with outer spill ridges and soft entry. The rake head follows the pointer with physical lag, so strokes become smooth curves.\n- Ripples are computed as offset contours of each feature's irregular outline (a polar radius lookup table) and revealed outward with easing. Rings follow the shape of each moss island or stone, not a plain circle.\n- Stones are shaded per pixel from a procedural dome height field with Perlin weathering. They get Blinn specular, contact ambient occlusion, moss speckling and a stable hash grain. Blurred silhouettes are offset along the sun vector to cast long or short shadows.\n- Moss islands have fuzzy noise edges, cushion-like tufts, highlight tips and their own shading and shadows.\n- The frame is four clip-path mitred planks with procedurally generated grain (horizontal on the rails, vertical on the stiles) and bevel gradients. It casts an inner shadow onto the sand on the side away from the sun.\n- Maple leaves are seven-lobed Path2D sprites with radial gradients and veins. They tumble with a scaleX flip, sway and descend while their shadow separates and softens with height.\n- \"Return to calm\" first decays the whole heightmap to still sand, then sweeps a raking front across the garden to restore the classic composition.","originalInteraction":"- Rake (1 or R): drag with a mouse, pen or finger to draw five parallel furrows. Raking over a fallen leaf sweeps it away.\n- Ripple (2 or P): tap near a stone or moss island to ring it with animated concentric circles, or tap open sand for a lone ripple. Enter or Space on the focused garden rings a random feature.\n- Place stone (3 or O): tap the sand to set a new procedurally shaped stone, which rings itself. Tap an existing stone to lift it, leaving its impression in the gravel.\n- Smooth (4 or M): brush softly to erase furrows.\n- Return to calm (5 or C): an animated smoothing of the whole bed, then a slow re-raking.\n- Wind chime (B): optional distant Web Audio chimes with inharmonic tube partials and generated reverb, occasional gusts, a soft note when a leaf lands, and a quiet gravel-scrape noise that follows rake speed. It is off by default and starts only on a gesture.\n- Save (S): downloads the garden as a PNG.\n- Hover or focus shows tool tips with shortcuts. The caption below explains the current tool and the time of day.\n- Touch is first-class: the garden sets touch-action: none, and on phones the tool bar becomes a horizontal pill at the bottom while the vertical title shrinks to a slim column.\n- Reduced motion freezes the sun, removes falling leaves and makes \"return to calm\" instant.","sourceHash":"25bf3d389f9870bf1bb2d99640431aede977e69e6c772490144e268803c433ff","name":"枯山水庭院","category":"生成艺术","description":"俯视木框中的细砾、岩石、苔藓和落叶，纹理随着耙动被重新整理，形成可亲手照料的小庭院。","interaction":"拖动耙出平行纹路，切换涟漪工具在岩石周围绘制圆纹。"},{"id":"060","slug":"060-trading-terminal","title":"QUANTA Terminal","file":"060-trading-terminal.html","textFile":"060-trading-terminal.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/060-trading-terminal.jpg","prompt":"Build \"QUANTA Terminal\", a dark, premium trading dashboard with clearly fictional tickers and simulated data (labelled \"Simulated market\"). Near-black ground (#0b0e11), panel surfaces #12161c, gains emerald #16c784, losses crimson #ea3943, gold accent #f0b90b, hairline borders. Layout: a scrolling ticker tape at the top; a main candlestick chart (canvas) with volume bars, moving averages, a crosshair with OHLC tooltip, time-range tabs and smooth panning/zooming; a live order book with depth bars that flicker as orders update; a recent-trades feed; a watchlist with sparklines; a portfolio allocation donut; and a buy/sell ticket with validation and a confirmation toast. Data evolves in real time via a random walk with volatility clustering. Typography: tabular numerals, a clean sans, monospace for prices; prices flash green/red on change. Responsive: panels reflow into tabs on mobile. Smooth and crisp at high DPI.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A dark, dense, professional trading terminal for a clearly fictional market. Near-black ground (#0b0e11), hairline-bordered panel surfaces (#12161c), emerald gains, crimson losses and a gold accent (#f0b90b). A slim header carries the QUANTA hex mark, a pulsing \"Simulated market\" badge, live equity and a UTC clock. Beneath it, an edge-faded ticker tape scrolls all ten instruments. The workspace is a six-panel grid: a live order book with depth bars (left), the candlestick chart (centre), a watchlist with sparklines (right), a portfolio donut, a split buy/sell ticket and a recent-trades feed (bottom row). All instruments (Axolotl Mining, Zephyrine Air, Nimbus Nine…) and the \"Quanta Dollar\" quote currency are invented, and a footer states that everything is simulated in the browser. On tablets the grid reflows into two columns. On phones it becomes a sticky tab bar (Chart / Book / Trades / Trade / Markets / Wallet) showing one panel at a time.","techniques":"- A DPR-aware canvas candlestick chart (max 2×): pixel-snapped wicks and bodies, a volume pane, MA7/25/99 overlays, a nice-number price grid, adaptive time labels, a faint instrument watermark and a soft gold glow. A dashed last-price line with an axis tag and a pulsing dot marks the live candle. The y-range eases between views.\n- Crosshair with dashed guides, price and time axis labels, and a floating OHLC / change / volume tooltip card. A TradingView-style OHLC legend and live MA values sit above the plot.\n- Market data: a GARCH(1,1)-style random walk (volatility clustering plus occasional shocks) drives every instrument. Candle history is generated per timeframe at about 3% daily volatility and bent so the candle 24 h ago opens at the instrument's 24 h open, keeping the stats consistent. Sparklines are Brownian bridges from the 24 h open to the live price.\n- The order book uses pre-built rows updated in place, cumulative depth bars with eased widths, and a flicker animation on changed sizes. A spread row shows the last price with a direction arrow.\n- Price-change flashes (green/red background fades) on the header price, watchlist, tape and book. Alternating animation classes retrigger them without layout thrash.\n- An SVG donut built from stroke-dasharray arcs with gaps, hover emphasis and a live total-equity centre. Custom diamond-thumb range sliders with 25% tick marks and a filled track. Gradient buy/sell buttons, and toasts with a countdown bar.\n- Typography: a system sans for UI and a monospace stack with tabular numerals for every price, amount and time.","originalInteraction":"- Chart: drag to pan (with inertia), scroll wheel or pinch to zoom around the cursor, and double-click or the reset button to recentre. The zoom +/- buttons, arrow keys, + / − and 0 also work when the chart has focus. Time-range tabs (1m, 5m, 15m, 1H, 4H, 1D) regenerate the history, and MA legend chips toggle each average.\n- Order book: view toggle (both / bids / asks). Clicking any price level loads it into both ticket price fields (switching to Limit if needed).\n- Watchlist: a live filter search box. Clicking an instrument switches the whole terminal (chart, book, trades, ticket symbols and balances).\n- Ticket: Limit / Market tabs, price steppers, and amount, total and percentage slider fields that stay in sync. Validation covers price, a ±10% limit band, amount > 0, a 10 QD minimum, insufficient QD and insufficient holdings, with inline errors and field highlights. A valid order fills instantly (simulated), updates cash, holdings and the donut, adds a gold ◆ row to the trades feed and shows a confirmation toast.\n- Portfolio: hovering a donut segment or legend row highlights it. The header shows today's simulated P/L.\n- Phones: the tab bar switches panels, and choosing a watchlist item or book level jumps to the relevant tab. The tape pauses on hover. Reduced motion slows the tape and removes flashes. The market loop runs on requestAnimationFrame, so it pauses in hidden tabs.","sourceHash":"53698b353c2592aad8d3e90887dc6f025ed0e19277cc32d080569d1931ebf5ac","name":"行情终端","category":"数据与工具","description":"虚构市场的密集交易面板包含蜡烛图、行情和多种读数。暗色层级与涨跌色帮助快速扫视数据。","interaction":"拖动平移图表，滚轮缩放，双击重置；全部行情仅为演示。"},{"id":"061","slug":"061-firefly-meadow","title":"Dusk Meadow — Fireflies","file":"061-firefly-meadow.html","textFile":"061-firefly-meadow.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/061-firefly-meadow.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Dusk Meadow — Fireflies\", a tranquil canvas scene. A meadow at dusk: a sky gradient from deep indigo (#141b41) to a very soft dusty rose at the horizon (#e8a0a0), layered silhouettes of rolling hills and a treeline in near-black greens (#0b1a12), and a foreground of hundreds of grass blades swaying in a noise-driven wind field and bending away from the pointer. About 120 fireflies wander with boids-like motion, each blinking on its own rhythm in warm yellow-green (#e8ff8a) with bloom, and slowly synchronising their flashes over time (Kuramoto-style coupling) — a quiet emergent delight. The pointer is a gentle lantern: fireflies drift toward it when it is held still and scatter when it moves fast. A crescent moon rises slowly and stars appear as the sky darkens. Minimal controls at the bottom-left that fade when idle: sync strength, wind, and sound (synthesized crickets). Title in a delicate italic serif.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Dusk Meadow — Fireflies\" is a quiet, full-screen canvas landscape: a sky that slowly deepens from indigo #141b41 to a dusty-rose horizon #e8a0a0, stars that appear one by one as it darkens, a thin crescent moon with faint earthshine rising over layered hills, and a treeline of rounded canopies and spires in near-black greens (#0b1a12). A pale mist drifts along the meadow floor, and the foreground is a thicket of tall grass silhouettes swaying in a travelling wind. About 120 fireflies drift through three depth bands, blinking warm yellow-green #e8ff8a with bloom, and over roughly a minute they find each other and begin to flash together, lighting the whole meadow in shared pulses. The title is set in a delicate italic serif, with one accurate line about synchronous fireflies. A small, translucent control card in the bottom-left fades away when you leave it alone.","techniques":"- Depth-ordered compositing: sky gradient → stars → moon → pre-rendered land layer → drifting mist (screen) → far fireflies → back grass → mid fireflies → ambient light → front grass with rim stroke → lantern core → near fireflies → vignette\n- Time of day: the sky gradient is interpolated each frame between dusk and night palettes, a warm afterglow fades, and stars fade in by per-star brightness thresholds with twinkle; occasional meteors once it is dark enough\n- Crescent moon painted offscreen: a textured disc carved with destination-out, a faint earthshine disc, shadow-blur halo and a large soft glow; it rises along a gentle arc over several minutes\n- Land painted once per resize: sine-summed ridges with atmospheric colour steps, procedurally built treeline (clustered canopy circles, stepped conifer spires), and a rolling meadow whose distant grass catches the last light\n- Grass: several hundred blades as tapered quadratic-curve paths, batched into one path per layer and filled with vertical gradients; each blade is a damped spring driven by a value-noise wind field with travelling waves and gusts, plus a radial push away from the lantern\n- Fireflies: boids-style separation, cohesion and alignment plus wander, soft vertical bounds and depth drift; asymmetric flash pulses (quick rise, slower decay) drawn additively from a bloom sprite with a bright core\n- Emergent synchrony: locally coupled Kuramoto oscillators (neighbours within a sight radius) plus a small global mean-field term, with coupling that ramps up over the first minute, so unison grows slowly instead of snapping; a live \"in unison\" meter shows the order parameter\n- Collective light: the meadow gets a soft additive wash proportional to how many fireflies are lit at once, so synchronized flashes light the whole field","originalInteraction":"- The pointer (or a finger) is a lantern: a warm glow that parts the grass around it\n- Hold the lantern still and nearby fireflies drift in and circle it; move it quickly and they scatter\n- Sync slider sets the coupling strength (0 lets every firefly keep its own rhythm); Wind slider sets the strength of the wind field and the wind sound\n- \"Crickets\" toggle (off by default) starts a Web Audio scene: five synthesized crickets, each a sine voice gated into pulse trains with its own pitch, rhythm and stereo position, over a soft filtered-noise breeze\n- Controls fade to near-invisible after a few idle seconds and return on pointer movement, key presses or focus; all controls are keyboard-accessible with visible focus rings\n- The loop and audio pause while the tab is hidden; prefers-reduced-motion halves the grass sway, slows the fireflies, softens flashes and the shared meadow glow, stops twinkling and meteors, and slows the passage of time","sourceHash":"7ea4a55c2d06272eae39904bf05589b70ccce99dafaf42d1e4fd7addc0cc38fc","name":"萤火草甸","category":"生成艺术","description":"暮色、月牙与层叠草地之间漂浮着萤火虫。指针化成一盏灯，改变虫群和草叶的局部运动。","interaction":"缓慢移动灯光；停住吸引萤火虫，快速移动使它们散开。"},{"id":"062","slug":"062-lava-lamp","title":"GROOVY — Lava Lamp","file":"062-lava-lamp.html","textFile":"062-lava-lamp.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/062-lava-lamp.jpg","prompt":"Build \"GROOVY — Lava Lamp\", a 1970s lava lamp rendered with real metaball physics. A classic lamp silhouette (chrome/gold base and cap drawn in SVG with metallic gradients) centred on warm retro wallpaper (repeating SVG pattern of orange/brown circles, #f4a259, #bc4b51, #5b8e7d on #f4e1c1). Inside the glass, wax blobs heated at the bottom rise, cool and sink, merging and splitting (metaball field computed on a low-resolution grid with smooth thresholded edges, glossy highlights and inner glow). Colour combinations: \"Classic\" (red wax in yellow liquid), \"Purple Haze\" (magenta in blue), \"Lime\" (green in violet) and \"Ocean\". Interaction: click the lamp's switch to turn it on/off (the light fades, the wax slows and settles); a temperature knob; a colour-combo switch; the pointer heats the glass locally. The whole room's glow is tinted by the lamp colour. Typography: a groovy rounded bold display with an outline shadow.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A warm 1970s evening scene: a brass-capped \"Model 71\" lava lamp (a fictional model) stands on a walnut sideboard in front of concentric-circle wallpaper in #f4a259, #bc4b51 and #5b8e7d on #f4e1c1. Inside the tapered glass, wax blobs driven by a small heat simulation warm over the bulb, bud off the molten pool, stretch as they rise, cool under the cap, drift back down, merge and split. They are rendered as glossy, backlit metaballs with an inner glow and a halo of scattered light in the liquid. The lamp colours the room: a screen-blended glow on the wall and a pool of light on the tabletop follow the chosen combination, and the whole room dims when the lamp is switched off. \"GROOVY\" is hand-built from fat, round-capped SVG strokes with a chocolate outline, an offset rust shadow and a cream inline, and each letter wobbles slowly like warm wax. Chunky cream cards with offset shadows hold the story copy and a retro control panel.","techniques":"- Metaball field (compact (1 − d²/R²)² kernel, velocity-dependent vertical stretch, flattened pool blobs at the heater) evaluated on a 3 px grid, bilinearly upsampled per device pixel for crisp, smooth thresholded edges and written straight to an ImageData buffer\n- Per-pixel wax shading from the interpolated field gradient: a pseudo-3D dome normal, diffuse and specular highlight, a bright translucent core (\"inner glow\"), a backlit rim on each blob's lower edge, and a halo tinting the nearby liquid\n- Liquid lit by a column-by-row bulb glow near the base, darkened by lamp brightness; the pointer adds a warm hotspot\n- Physics: per-blob temperature with heating near the bulb, cooling toward the cap, ambient loss, buoyancy, viscous drag, tapered-glass wall collisions, soft separation, probabilistic merging (area-conserving) and splitting or budding from the pool; 40 simulated seconds run before the first frame\n- SVG lamp: brass cone base, collar and cap drawn with multi-stop metallic gradients, engraved bands, glowing vent holes, a cord and a glass overlay (edge shading, streak highlights, a bulb glow) above a clip-path-shaped canvas\n- Room lighting with CSS variables: --lf (bulb level) and --glow (combo colour) drive a screen-blended radial glow, a darkening vignette, the tabletop light pool and the vents\n- A repeating SVG pattern wallpaper, \"GROOVY\" built from rounded stroke skeletons (<use> layers for shadow, outline, gradient fill and inline highlight) with per-letter CSS wobble, and a CSS knob with conic tick marks and a gradient arc\n- Smooth 1.2 s colour cross-fades between combinations; automatic 30 fps fallback if the per-pixel pass runs slow","originalInteraction":"- Click the rocker switch on the lamp base (or press Space) to switch it on or off: the bulb fades up or down, the room darkens, and the wax slows, cools and settles into a pool, then reheats and rises again when switched back on\n- Turn the Heat knob by dragging around it, scrolling over it, or with arrow, Page and Home/End keys (it is an ARIA slider); more heat means faster, livelier convection and the named heat level updates\n- Choose Classic (red in yellow), Purple Haze (magenta in blue), Lime (green in violet) or Ocean (sea-foam in deep blue) from a radio group (arrow keys supported, or C to cycle); the wax, liquid, room glow and table light all cross-fade\n- Move the pointer or a finger over the glass to heat the wax locally: nearby blobs warm up and lift, and a warm glow follows the pointer\n- Live readouts show the average wax temperature, the number of blobs afloat and the bulb wattage; the loop pauses while the tab is hidden, and reduced motion slows the simulation and stops the lettering wobble","sourceHash":"d501910a95fe0246683e9d7b4ac30c5195766faaeb9cef61510dce7fdd2c74b4","name":"七十年代熔岩灯","category":"科学与模拟","description":"黄铜灯帽和玻璃瓶立在复古木柜上，蜡团在受热后升降、聚合，壁纸与房间光线共同营造年代感。","interaction":"点击底座开关或按空格，观察加热、冷却和蜡团沉降。"},{"id":"063","slug":"063-history-of-flight","title":"Skyward — A Brief History of Flight","file":"063-history-of-flight.html","textFile":"063-history-of-flight.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/063-history-of-flight.jpg","prompt":"Design \"Skyward — A Brief History of Flight\", an interactive horizontal timeline. Sky palette: dawn blue #bde0fe, cerulean #3a86ff, cloud white, sunset accent #ff8fab, ink #1d3557. A long drag-scrollable (and wheel/keyboard) timeline from 1783 to 1969 with well-established milestones: 1783 first crewed hot-air balloon flight (Montgolfier brothers' balloon); 1853 Cayley's crewed glider; 1891 Lilienthal's gliders; 1903 the Wright Flyer at Kitty Hawk (12 seconds, 120 feet); 1909 Blériot crosses the English Channel; 1919 Alcock and Brown make the first non-stop transatlantic flight; 1927 Lindbergh's solo transatlantic flight; 1932 Amelia Earhart's solo transatlantic flight; 1939 first jet-powered flight (Heinkel He 178); 1947 Chuck Yeager breaks the sound barrier in the Bell X-1; 1957 Sputnik 1; 1961 Yuri Gagarin orbits Earth; 1969 Apollo 11 lands on the Moon. Each milestone has an elegant SVG line illustration of the craft, its date and a short caption. Parallax clouds at several depths; a small paper plane travels along the timeline. Clicking a milestone expands its card. A progress altimeter shows the year. Typography: an aviation-inspired sans with serif captions.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Skyward is a horizontal, drag-scrollable timeline of flight from 1783 to 1969, told as a climb. The sky is painted live on a canvas and changes with the year at the centre of the screen. It starts as a pink dawn over the Montgolfier balloon, turns cerulean for the age of the Wrights, Blériot and the Atlantic crossings, deepens through 1947, and becomes star-filled space with the Earth's limb glowing below for Sputnik, Gagarin and Apollo 11. Thirteen well-established milestones stand on white \"flight strip\" cards: 1783 Montgolfier balloon, 1853 Cayley's glider, 1891 Lilienthal, 1903 Wright Flyer, 1909 Blériot, 1919 Alcock and Brown, 1927 Lindbergh, 1932 Earhart, 1939 He 178, 1947 Bell X-1, 1957 Sputnik 1, 1961 Gagarin and 1969 Apollo 11. The cards rise higher above the time axis as the years go on. Each card has an original SVG line illustration of the craft, its date and place, and a short serif caption. Opening a card gives the full story and key figures. A paper plane flies below the axis trailing a dashed contrail, and an instrument-style altimeter reads out the year. Type pairs a condensed aviation sans (DIN, Bahnschrift, Nimbus Sans Narrow) for numerals and labels with an old-style serif for captions.","techniques":"- The sky gradient is keyed to the year through six colour keyframes (dawn #bde0fe with a sunset #ff8fab glow, cerulean #3a86ff, high-altitude blue, ink #1d3557 night) with smoothstep blending. In the space age, twinkling stars and a radial-gradient \"Earth limb\" fade in.\n- Four parallax cloud layers use pre-rendered soft cloud sprites (stacked radial-gradient puffs with a shaded underside). They move at 0.06×, 0.18×, 0.42× and 1.25× of the scroll and drift slowly with time. The fastest layer passes in front of the cards on a separate canvas, and the clouds thin out as the page leaves the atmosphere.\n- Thirteen hand-drawn SVG illustrations use ink strokes with white, dawn and sunset fills. Each draws itself on the first time it flies into view (pathLength = 1 with an animated stroke-dashoffset, then the fills fade in). The cards visible at load are shown already drawn.\n- A piecewise-linear year↔position map spaces the milestones evenly while the decade ticks show time compressing. Italic gap labels (\"+70 yrs\", \"+4 yrs\") sit between stops.\n- A canvas paper plane pitches with scroll velocity, turns to face the direction of travel, bobs gently, and leaves a dashed contrail stored in world coordinates.\n- The altimeter is an SVG gauge with a bezel gradient, a 50-tick dial, a tens needle and a units needle driven by the fractional year, and a pink counter window.\n- A top route bar shows progress from 1783 to 1969 with a small plane glyph. The nearest card lifts, its gradient top rule extends, and its axis dot pings.\n- The detail card opens with a FLIP animation from the clicked card's rectangle and holds an enlarged illustration, the full text and a stats grid.","originalInteraction":"- Drag the sky with mouse or touch, with inertia on release. The mouse wheel or trackpad in either direction also moves the timeline. ←/→ and PageUp/PageDown jump between milestones, Home/End go to either end, and round prev/next buttons do the same with a \"05 / 13\" counter.\n- Click, or press Enter on a focused card, to open the expanded card. Inside it, Earlier/Later and ←/→ step through milestones, and Esc, the × button or the scrim closes it. Focus is trapped while it is open and returned to the card on close.\n- Tabbing to a card scrolls it into view, and a drag never triggers a click.\n- On resize the current year is kept and the view snaps to the nearest milestone, so the phone layout lands cleanly.\n- Reduced motion stops cloud drift, plane bobbing and the draw-on and ping animations. Colour changes toward night flip the page chrome to light text.","sourceHash":"691f4567ea14a127ed9d4ad4fe0d70c8ca4be153313a7e4ed01f2272583e5553","name":"飞行的历史","category":"数据与工具","description":"从 1783 年到 1969 年的飞行故事被画成横向上升的天空，年代推进时背景、飞行器与里程碑共同改变。","interaction":"拖动或滚动时间线，用左右键在里程碑间移动。"},{"id":"064","slug":"064-magnetic-poetry","title":"Fridge Verse — Magnetic Poetry","file":"064-magnetic-poetry.html","textFile":"064-magnetic-poetry.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/064-magnetic-poetry.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Fridge Verse — Magnetic Poetry\", a tactile word-magnet toy. A large enamel fridge door (subtle vertical gradient #eef0f2 with faint metallic noise and a chrome handle at the side) holding about 120 word magnets: small white tiles (#fffdf7) with a slight drop shadow and a serif word, each rotated slightly at random, a few coloured (pale pink, mint, butter) for flair. Choose a vocabulary (nouns, verbs, adjectives, suffixes -s, -ing, -ed) that makes poetic combinations likely. Dragging a magnet gives a pleasing lift (shadow grows, slight rotation) and a snap back onto the door; magnets may overlap. Features: a \"Shake\" button that scatters magnets with physics; theme packs (Love, Ocean, Space, Kitchen); a poem detector that reads tiles arranged in lines left to right and shows the current poem in a small card you can copy; a few decorative fridge items (a drawn polaroid picture, a fruit magnet). Persist positions in localStorage. Typography: classic serif tiles with a rounded sans for the UI.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Fridge Verse\" puts you nose to nose with a kitchen fridge. A tall enamel door (a #eef0f2 vertical gradient with faint metallic noise and a sweeping sheen) stands against a sage subway-tile wall, with a freezer compartment and a chrome seam across the top. A chunky chrome handle stands off the right side and casts its shadow on the door. About 125 word magnets cling to the door: small #fffdf7 tiles set in a classic Times-style serif, each tilted slightly, with a few pale pink, mint and butter tiles for flair. The rest of the fridge carries its own clutter: a drawn polaroid of a seaside sunset pinned by a red dome magnet, a glossy strawberry and a lemon-slice magnet. A ruled index card held by a blue magnet always shows the poem currently arranged on the door, ready to copy. The toolbar in the freezer strip is set in a rounded sans. It holds the theme packs (Love, Ocean, Space, Kitchen), a chunky red Shake button, Tidy and a sound toggle.","techniques":"- The enamel door is built from layered gradients, with a diagonal specular sheen and a canvas-generated speckle-and-brushing noise layer multiplied on top. The chrome seam, handle bar and feet are multi-stop metallic gradients with a hot highlight streak and a cast shadow\n- The subway-tile wall is drawn entirely from offset repeating CSS gradients\n- Word tiles use a paper-white gradient, a 1 px \"magnet thickness\" edge and a soft two-layer shadow. When lifted, the shadow deepens into a tall stacked drop shadow and the tile scales up. When dropped, it snaps back with an overshooting cubic-bezier\n- Colour variants and suffix or punctuation tiles are styled distinctly: suffixes are italic, and pastel tiles are chosen by a stable hash so the door looks the same on every visit\n- The inline SVG decorations include a polaroid scene (gradient sky, sun path on water, sailboat, gulls, walker, film vignette) with a handwritten-style caption, a strawberry with gradient body, seeds, leaves and gloss, and a lemon slice with procedurally drawn segments\n- Scatter layout: staggered, jittered rows with gaps wider than the poem-reader's threshold, so the random clutter never reads as a line. Reserved zones keep the handle, the decorations, the card and a hand-set starter poem clear\n- The index card has ruled lines and a red margin drawn with gradients, and its poem text sits on the rules. On phones it becomes a fixed bottom sheet that folds away","originalInteraction":"- Drag any magnet (mouse or touch, via pointer capture) to lift it. It tilts with your horizontal velocity, rises above everything and clacks back onto the door. Magnets may overlap. The polaroid and fruit magnets can be dragged too\n- Keyboard: every magnet is focusable. Arrow keys nudge it (Shift for bigger steps), and the poem updates after you stop\n- The poem detector links each magnet to its nearest right-hand neighbour on the same baseline, builds lines from those links, then chains lines that sit just below one another into a stanza. It keeps the richest stanza and joins suffixes with light English rules (dance + -ing gives \"dancing\", spin + -ing gives \"spinning\", salt + -ed gives \"salted\", holds, kisses). The result goes on the card\n- \"Copy poem\" uses the Clipboard API, falls back to execCommand, and finally selects the text with a prompt to press Ctrl+C. Everything is in try/catch\n- Shake rattles the door (a CSS keyframe shake) and throws every magnet with random velocity, spin and a little gravity. They bounce off the edges and around the card, then settle, and the poem is re-read\n- The theme packs swap 40 themed words over 85 shared ones: the old tiles fall away and the new ones drop in with a staggered spring. Each pack starts with its own starter poem\n- Tidy re-lays the current pack. Positions, the theme, the decorations and the sound preference persist in localStorage, stored separately for phone and desktop door sizes\n- Soft magnet \"clacks\" are synthesized with Web Audio (band-passed noise plus a falling sine thump) and start only on a gesture, with a visible speaker toggle. prefers-reduced-motion disables the shake animation and the spring transitions","sourceHash":"d32f13e6f949c0899c18d1dec17c1114c32778ad476d65f2cba18c90939eb7be","name":"冰箱上的诗","category":"排版实验","description":"一块有金属光泽的冰箱门上散落着单词磁贴，诗句通过真实的拖动、叠放与重新排列形成。","interaction":"拖动单词磁贴组合句子；松手时磁贴重新贴回冰箱表面。"},{"id":"065","slug":"065-generative-quilt","title":"Patchwork — A Generative Quilt","file":"065-generative-quilt.html","textFile":"065-generative-quilt.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/065-generative-quilt.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Patchwork — A Generative Quilt\". Every visit (and every click on \"Stitch a new quilt\") generates a unique quilt made from traditional blocks — Log Cabin, Flying Geese, Half-Square Triangles, Nine Patch, Pinwheel, Churn Dash, Ohio Star — laid out on a grid with sashing and borders. Fabrics are procedural SVG patterns: gingham, calico florals (tiny flower motifs), polka dots, stripes and chambray, in warm cottage palettes (e.g. barn red #9e2a2b, mustard #e09f3e, denim #335c67, cream #fff3b0, sage #9fb8ad). Visible dashed stitching, slight puffy shading on each patch (quilted look via inner shadows) and a wavy binding edge. Interaction: hovering a block lifts it slightly and shows its pattern name; clicking cycles its pattern; a palette chooser (Farmhouse, Indigo, Sunset, Scandinavian); a \"drape\" toggle shows the quilt hanging on a line, waving gently in the wind. Download PNG. Typography: a warm serif with an embroidery-like dashed underline.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Patchwork is a cottage-craft page that pieces a new quilt on every visit. It sits on warm linen and is set in a chunky warm serif with an embroidered, dashed underline and a spool-and-needle mark. The quilt is built entirely in SVG from seven traditional blocks: Log Cabin, Flying Geese, Half-Square Triangles, Nine Patch, Pinwheel, Churn Dash and Ohio Star. Each block is drawn from real piecing geometry in a unit square and turned a random quarter-turn. The blocks sit in sashing with cornerstones, a printed border and a wavy binding. Every patch is cut from procedural SVG fabric patterns: gingham, calico florals with five-petal flowers and leaves, polka dots, straight or bias stripes, chambray weave, flecked muslin and near-solids. The palettes are cottage-warm (Farmhouse's barn red, mustard, denim, cream and sage, plus Indigo, Sunset and Scandinavian). Patches get puffy quilted shading and dashed hand-stitching inset along every seam. A sewn-on label card gives the quilt a whimsical fictional name, a number, block counts with mini icons and its finished size, and holds the controls. A \"Drape on line\" mode hangs the quilt from wooden pegs on a sagging washing line under an open sky, where it billows in the wind.","techniques":"- Seeded procedural generation: each quilt favours two or three block types, with the occasional surprise, and a role system (light background, dark, accent, medium) maps fabrics to patches so contrast reads the way it does in real piecing\n- Fabrics as SVG <pattern> tiles generated per palette from its light, mid and dark tones (gingham by overlapping translucent bands, calico from petal circles, chambray from crossing hairlines, bias stripes via patternTransform)\n- Quilted puff: an objectBoundingBox radial gradient laid over every patch, plus seam lines and dashed running stitches on a mathematically inset copy of each convex patch; a fine weave overlay covers the whole quilt\n- Wavy binding built from alternating quadratic curves, border and binding stitch lines, cornerstones, and a soft blurred shadow under the quilt\n- Hover lift with a CSS transform and an SVG feDropShadow filter; a pop animation when a block is re-pieced; a staggered \"stitched in\" reveal for new quilts\n- Drape mode: the SVG is serialized into an image and texture-mapped onto a 22×16 cloth mesh with affine triangle warping on canvas; travelling sine waves and gusts, a sway that grows toward the hem, per-quad light and shadow in the folds, a catenary washing line and clothes pegs\n- Mini block icons in the legend are rendered from the same block geometry","originalInteraction":"- \"Stitch a new quilt\" generates a new design with a new name and number, and the blocks stitch in one by one\n- Hovering a block lifts it and shows a fabric-tag tooltip with the pattern name and a one-line note; clicking cycles the block through all seven patterns\n- Blocks are focusable buttons: Enter or Space re-pieces a block, and the arrow keys move between blocks\n- Palette chooser (Farmhouse, Indigo, Sunset, Scandinavian) re-dyes every fabric in place\n- \"Drape on line\" / \"Lay it flat\" toggles the waving washing-line view, which updates live if you change the palette or stitch a new quilt\n- \"Save PNG\" rasterizes the quilt at 2× and downloads it; the page is responsive: a 4×5 quilt beside the label on desktop, 3×4 stacked above it on phones\n- Reduced motion slows the wind and turns off the pop and reveal animations; the drape loop stops while the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"1907d353b27dec4f8b367477910e5a62eee06a03c1b9c6dc21afb34c46de370a","name":"生成式拼布","category":"生成艺术","description":"不同纹样的布块以 SVG 拼成被子，暖色衬线字、缝线和亚麻背景共同营造手工制作感。","interaction":"点击 Stitch a new quilt 生成新图案；悬停布块查看纹样信息。"},{"id":"066","slug":"066-planet-forge","title":"Planet Forge — Procedural World Generator","file":"066-planet-forge.html","textFile":"066-planet-forge.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/066-planet-forge.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Planet Forge — Procedural World Generator\". A canvas renders a rotating sphere with per-pixel shading (ray–sphere intersection or a pre-baked equirectangular texture sampled by rotation): terrain from fractal noise mapped to biomes (deep ocean, shallows, beaches, forests, deserts, mountains, ice caps by latitude), an animated cloud layer, an atmospheric rim glow, a day/night terminator with city lights on the night side, and optional rings casting a shadow. A cosmic black background with a faint nebula and stars. A sci-fi control panel (thin lines, cyan #4cc9f0 accents, glass panels) with sliders for sea level, temperature, cloud cover, terrain roughness, ring toggle, moon count, rotation speed and seed, plus presets (Earthlike, Desert world, Ocean world, Lava world, Ice world, banded Gas giant). Each planet receives a catalogue designation (e.g. \"KX-4471 b\") and playful stats. Interaction: drag to rotate, \"Randomize\", PNG download. Keep rendering efficient (render at lower resolution and upscale smoothly).\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Planet Forge is a procedural world generator styled as a deep-space survey console. The left stage is a cosmic black field with a painted nebula and seeded starfield, where a slowly rotating planet is shaded per pixel: fractal terrain mapped to biomes, drifting clouds, a soft day/night terminator with sparkling city lights on the night side, an atmospheric rim and halo, and optional rings that cast a real shadow band across the globe while the planet shadows the rings in turn. Moons orbit on faint traced ellipses and pass in front of and behind the planet. A survey record in the top-left gives each world a catalogue designation (\"JH-4471 e\"), a class, playful but plausible stats and a fictional field note; the right-hand console of thin cyan (#4cc9f0) lines and clipped-corner glass panels holds presets, sliders, toggles, seed and export.","techniques":"- Seeded 3D Perlin noise sampled on the unit sphere (no seams or pole pinching) into 512x256 equirectangular fields: domain-warped fBm heights with a ridged mountain term, moisture, cloud and settlement fields; sea level is set by a latitude-weighted height quantile so the slider maps to real ocean coverage\n- Biome colouring per world type (terrestrial, ocean, desert, magma, cryogenic, banded gas giant) from temperature (latitude + altitude lapse) and moisture, with hill-shade relief, smooth sea-ice/snow transitions, magma seas with floating crust, and a gas giant with warped bands, latitude-dependent wind shear and a great storm\n- Ray-sphere rendering in a low-resolution buffer: per-pixel normals, texture coordinates, diffuse, night factor, Fresnel rim, Blinn-Phong ocean glint and ring-shadow factors are precomputed for the current tilt, so each frame is only texture sampling (bilinear) plus lighting, then smoothly upscaled\n- Coastlines are cut along the bilinearly interpolated height contour inside mixed land/sea texels, giving antialiased sub-texel shorelines; clouds are bilinear and drift faster than the ground; city lights get a sub-texel hash sparkle\n- Rings rendered in the same pass by ray-plane intersection with a seeded band profile (including a Cassini-style gap), correct depth against the sphere, lit/unlit face shading, the planet's shadow on the rings and the rings' shadow on the planet\n- Moons are pre-rendered cratered sprites lit from the same sun, depth-sorted around the planet; a rotating ticked HUD arc, corner brackets and a canvas nebula with additive wisps complete the frame\n- Sci-fi console: clip-path panels with diagonal corner accents, diamond slider thumbs with glow, tick-marked tracks, square toggles and steppers","originalInteraction":"- Drag to spin the planet (with inertia); with a mouse, vertical drag tilts the view (re-projecting rings and shadows); arrow keys rotate/tilt when the canvas is focused; wheel or +/- zooms\n- Hovering the globe reads out latitude, longitude, biome and elevation or depth under the cursor; hovering the rings reports the particle band\n- Presets: Earthlike, Desert, Ocean, Lava, Ice, Gas giant; sliders for sea level, temperature, terrain roughness, cloud cover and rotation speed; toggles for rings and a survey grid (15-degree graticule drawn in the shader); moon stepper 0-4\n- Seed field + Forge regenerates the world and its designation; Randomize (or R) rolls a new type, seed and parameters; PNG (or D) exports the current view with a caption; G toggles the grid\n- Expensive regeneration is batched through a dirty-flag pump so dragging sliders stays responsive; reduced-motion slows rotation and removes HUD motion and inertia","sourceHash":"d600726567a1ff0a534c60add0c4f5ba13123481401112c33dc18ee2f90c20fc","name":"行星锻造厂","category":"科学与模拟","description":"地形、海洋、云层与环带由程序生成，在深空测绘仪表中组成一颗持续旋转的行星。","interaction":"拖动旋转、上下拖动调整倾角，滚轮缩放；尝试参数生成不同世界。"},{"id":"067","slug":"067-ascii-tides","title":"ASCII TIDES","file":"067-ascii-tides.html","textFile":"067-ascii-tides.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/067-ascii-tides.jpg","prompt":"Create \"ASCII TIDES\", an animated ocean scene made entirely of monospace characters rendered into a <pre> or a canvas character grid. Characters map to wave height and foam (e.g. \" .,:;~=≈*#@\"). Layers: a sky with slowly drifting ASCII clouds and a sun or moon made of characters, a horizon, rolling swells (sum of sines plus noise), foam crests, and a small character-built boat bobbing on the surface. Palette: amber phosphor #ffb000 on #140c00 by default, switchable to \"Seafoam\" (#7fffd4 on #001414) and \"Paper\" (ink #222 on #f2ecdc). Pointer/touch disturbances create ripples that propagate across the water (2D wave equation on the grid). A time-of-day slider changes the sky (stars at night). Typography: a monospace stack with tight line-height; the grid adapts to the viewport to keep characters crisp. A tiny caption in the corner inside an ASCII box-drawing frame. Smooth at 30–60fps with efficient string building.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"ASCII TIDES is a living seascape drawn only with monospace characters, glowing like amber phosphor (#ffb000 on #140c00) by default, with Seafoam (#7fffd4 on #001414) and Paper (ink #222 on #f2ecdc) palettes. Rows of tildes, dashes, colons and dots form real rolling swells that rise, occlude the water behind them and break into frothy '*', '#' and '@' foam along their crests. A small character-built sailboat bobs and leans on the waves, and a glitter path of '+' and '*' sparkles beneath a character sun, or at night a gibbous moon. Clouds drift across the sky, gulls flap by during the day, and stars twinkle (with the occasional shooting star) after dark. A time-of-day dial moves the sun across the sky from dawn to dusk and night, pointer or touch drags ripples through the water, and a box-drawn caption in the corner reports the time, the light and the swell.","techniques":"- Canvas character grid rendered from a pre-built glyph atlas: every character is drawn once per palette with its phosphor glow baked in, then blitted per cell with per-cell brightness, so a 180 x 65 grid stays smooth; cell size snaps to device pixels and the grid re-derives from the viewport on resize\n- The sea is ray-marched per column, voxel-landscape style: each column marches outward through a perspective heightfield (5 Stokes-shaped swells with deep-water dispersion, wave-group modulation, value-noise wind chop), so near crests genuinely hide the troughs behind them\n- Character choice comes from lighting, not just height: the sun is ahead, so the backs of swells shine as '~' and '≈' crest lines, the faces turned toward you fade through ';', ':', ',', '.' into empty troughs, far water thins to '-' and '.', and breaking crests froth into '*', '#', '@', '°'\n- A damped 2D wave equation on a world-space grid in front of the viewer adds ripples to the heightfield, so pointer disturbances spread as rings of characters that respect perspective\n- The sky from noise: two-octave drifting clouds using the \" .,:;~=≈*#@\" ramp, dusk and dawn horizon glow, a sun disc of '@#*' with a flickering ':.' halo, a gibbous moon with maria, and twinkling stars\n- The glitter path narrows with distance under the sun or moon; the boat is stamped in depth order inside the march (nearer water can hide its hull, while the gaps in its sail show the sea behind) and switches between leaning sprites with the local slope; a lamp lights on its masthead at night\n- Box-drawing lines are drawn by hand into the atlas so the corner caption frame joins perfectly; CSS scanlines and vignette for the phosphor palettes, a warm paper vignette for Paper","originalInteraction":"- Move the pointer over the water to trail ripples; click or tap to splash (bigger rings); Space drops a random splash\n- Time slider (0–24 h) sets the sun or moon position, light level, stars and glitter; \"let time pass\" runs the day (T key), and Left/Right arrows nudge the clock\n- Palette radio group: Amber, Seafoam, Paper (keys 1, 2, 3; arrow keys inside the group); the choice persists in localStorage (guarded)\n- Rain toggle (R key) adds falling '/' streaks that pepper the water with tiny impacts and raises the swell\n- The controls box can be collapsed; the animation pauses while the tab is hidden, and prefers-reduced-motion slows the sea to a calm drift with no shooting stars","sourceHash":"1b9743b0d09ee86d289838ef0b8935617d6efed3d968351cce88f43dd533b3d1","name":"字符潮汐","category":"生成艺术","description":"天空和海面仅由等宽字符绘成，琥珀色余辉像旧终端中的海景。浪、光线和日夜都在字符间变化。","interaction":"移动指针划出波纹，点击溅起水花；调整时间与配色。"},{"id":"068","slug":"068-bento-portfolio","title":"Mira Okafor — Bento Portfolio","file":"068-bento-portfolio.html","textFile":"068-bento-portfolio.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/068-bento-portfolio.jpg","prompt":"Design a bento-grid portfolio for \"Mira Okafor — Product Designer\" (fictional). Neutral palette: #f5f5f7 page, #ffffff tiles, #1d1d1f text, one vivid lime accent #c3f53c and a deep indigo feature tile #1b1f3b. A responsive bento grid of 10–12 tiles of varied sizes with 24px radii and delicate shadows: an intro tile with name and role; a local-time tile with a live analog clock; a \"Now playing\" tile with an animated equalizer; a project tile containing a mini animated UI mockup (a CSS-drawn phone screen scrolling); a stats tile with counting numbers; a location tile with a stylised abstract SVG map and a pulsing pin; a tools tile with floating chips; a testimonial tile; a \"Currently reading\" tile with a CSS book; a contact tile with a magnetic button; and a dark tile with a generative gradient mesh. Interaction: tiles lift and reveal extra detail on hover; a cursor spotlight glides across tile surfaces; clicking a project tile expands it into a full-screen case-study overlay with a smooth shared-element transition. Typography: a system sans with tight tracking.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A bento-grid portfolio for Mira Okafor, a fictional product designer. It is a calm, gallery-like grid on #f5f5f7 with white 24px-radius tiles, hairline-soft shadows, tight system-sans typography, one vivid lime accent (#c3f53c) and a deep indigo (#1b1f3b) feature tile. Twelve tiles of varied sizes sit on a 4-column named-area grid:\n- an intro with an illustrated avatar, availability ping and oversized tight-tracked name\n- a live analog and digital Lisbon clock with day/night glyph\n- a \"Now playing\" card with an equalizer\n- the indigo Tandem case-study tile, with a CSS-drawn phone rising from its bottom edge and its app feed scrolling\n- counting stats\n- an abstract SVG street map of Lisbon with a pulsing pin\n- floating tool chips\n- a generative gradient-mesh poster tile\n- rotating testimonials\n- a 3D CSS book for \"Currently reading\"\n- a wide Fieldnote case-study tile with an animated mini dashboard\n- a dark contact tile with a magnetic lime button\nProject tiles expand into full-screen case studies through a shared-element transition. All people, projects, quotes and numbers are fictional and labelled as such.","techniques":"- CSS grid with grid-template-areas that reflows from 4 to 2 to 1 column. Tiles enter with a staggered fade-rise that uses the independent translate property, so it never fights the hover lift.\n- Cursor spotlight: pointer position is written to per-tile --mx/--my custom properties (rAF-throttled), driving a radial surface glow (::before) and a lime rim light (::after) built with a padding-box mask-composite border.\n- Clock: 60 SVG ticks generated in JS, hands rotated each frame with a smooth-sweep lime second hand, and the Europe/Lisbon time derived from Intl.DateTimeFormat.formatToParts as a UTC offset that is refreshed every minute (so DST is handled).\n- Scalable mockups: the phone and dashboard are built entirely in em units, so the same DOM can be cloned and rendered larger in the case study. The phone feed scrolls with a stepped keyframe loop, and the dashboard bars grow while a line chart draws in.\n- Shared-element case-study transition: the overlay opens with a clip-path inset() that grows from the tile's exact rectangle and radius to full screen, while a FLIP transform morphs the cloned mockup from its tile position to its final spot. Content fades in behind it, and closing reverses all of this.\n- Generative mesh: a DPR-aware canvas that composites pre-rendered radial-gradient sprites with additive (\"lighter\") blending on Lissajous paths. One blob drifts toward the pointer. It adds a darkening gradient and a pre-built grain pattern, and there are four palettes to shuffle through.\n- Details: a CSS 3D book (cover, spine and page edge in preserve-3d, opening wider on hover), a double-pulse map pin, floating chips with individual drift periods, a pinging availability dot, a highlighter-style lime mark and a book progress bar.","originalInteraction":"- Hover or focus lifts tiles and reveals extra detail lines (always shown on touch devices). The spotlight follows a mouse pointer across every tile surface.\n- Project tiles use a stretched button over the whole tile, so mouse, touch and keyboard can all open them. The case study is a modal dialog with a focus trap, Esc or × to close, scroll lock and focus return.\n- \"Now playing\": the play button is a visible sound toggle. It starts a small live Web Audio synth sketch (pentatonic arpeggio, bass and delay), and the equalizer switches from CSS animation to real AnalyserNode levels. Audio is only created after that click.\n- Testimonials rotate every 6 s (pausing on hover or focus), with pill-dot controls. \"Shuffle\" reseeds the gradient mesh.\n- The magnetic \"Say hello\" button eases toward the pointer inside the contact tile. It and the Copy button copy the obfuscated address with a guarded clipboard fallback and a toast.\n- Stats count up once visible. Canvas, clock and song timers pause when offscreen or when the tab is hidden. prefers-reduced-motion removes the transitions and loops.","sourceHash":"8cf87dc3b60099a3ae6f661f8ca9f8cd84159a6554250d93b9df9494f0d40cff","name":"便当格作品集","category":"交互网页","description":"一位虚构产品设计师的作品、身份和碎片信息被装入不同尺寸的方格中，形成层次清楚的个人主页。","interaction":"悬停或聚焦卡片查看附加内容，点击项目打开详情。"},{"id":"069","slug":"069-honeycomb-apiary","title":"Apiary — The Honeycomb","file":"069-honeycomb-apiary.html","textFile":"069-honeycomb-apiary.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/069-honeycomb-apiary.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Apiary — The Honeycomb\", an interactive hexagonal-grid artwork. The screen fills with hexagonal cells (canvas or SVG) in honey tones — amber #f6a821, gold #ffc93c, deep honey #c77d0a, wax #fff1c1 — with dark comb gaps (#2a1a05). Cells have a subtle inner bevel and translucency: some are filled with glowing honey (animated fill level with a meniscus shine), others are empty wax, a few are capped. Hovering sends a spreading ripple of brightness through neighbouring cells (delayed by hex distance). A handful of bees (small creatures with blurred beating wings) fly figure-eight \"waggle dance\" paths and visit cells, filling them over time. Interaction: click a cell to fill it with honey (drip animation); drag across cells to paint; a \"Harvest\" button drains the cells with a flowing animation into a jar counter; the grid regenerates on resize. A side panel explains the waggle dance in two short, accurate sentences. Typography: a warm serif title with small-caps labels.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Apiary is a living honeycomb that fills the screen: hundreds of pointy-top hexagonal cells in amber #f6a821, gold #ffc93c, deep honey #c77d0a and wax #fff1c1, separated by dark comb walls (#2a1a05). Empty cells read as faceted wax tubes lit from the upper left. Honey cells are translucent and glow from within, each filled to its own level with a curved meniscus and a small specular glint. Capped cells, clustered toward the top of the frame as in a real comb, are pale domed wax lids. Seven bees with banded abdomens, fuzzy thoraxes and ghosted, motion-blurred wings trace figure-eight waggle dances whose dotted trails fade on the comb, then fly off to visit cells and top them up. A warm glass panel with a serif title and small-caps labels holds an illustrated honey jar, the Harvest button, live counts of honey, capped and empty cells, and a two-sentence account of the waggle dance with a diagram and a live readout of what the current dancer is signalling.","techniques":"- Hex grid in axial/offset coordinates with exact pixel-to-cell cube rounding and hex distance; organic honey, capped and empty zones from a smooth sine-noise field plus a top-to-bottom gradient\n- Pre-rendered sprites rebuilt on resize: a faceted wax tube (six walls shaded by a light vector, beveled rim), a translucent radially glowing honey body, a domed textured cap, and a hex-shaped glow\n- The static comb is baked into one base canvas; each frame layers honey (cropped from the sprite bottom-up, so the fill level is exact inside the hexagon), meniscus curves, caps and an additive 'lighter' pass for the honey's inner glow and ripples\n- Brightness ripples spread by hex distance with a delay per ring, from hover, keyboard moves and an intro wave across the comb\n- Drip animation: a stretching droplet falls under gravity to the honey surface, then the level eases up with a sloshing surface\n- Bees drawn with canvas paths (legs, clipped abdomen bands, radial-gradient thorax with fuzz, antennae, three ghosted wing positions per side for blur) plus soft shadows; the waggle run wiggles along its axis, then alternating left and right Bézier return loops form the figure-eight\n- Harvest stream: pooled particles fly along quadratic curves from each draining cell into the jar on a top overlay canvas, with fading tails; the SVG jar's honey level rises with an animated wave and the lid bumps when a jar seals\n- A baked vignette and warm light falloff; the glass panel carries a faint hexagonal pattern","originalInteraction":"- Hover: each new cell under the pointer sends a ripple of light through its neighbours, delayed by hex distance\n- Click or tap a cell to drip honey into it; drag to paint many cells; clicking a full cell asks the bees to cap it\n- Harvest (panel or mobile dock, or H) uncaps and drains every filled cell in a wave that starts nearest the jar; honey flows into the jar, which counts poured cells, seals jars and shows progress toward the next\n- Keyboard: focus the comb and use the arrow keys to move a cursor, Enter or Space to fill, H to harvest\n- New frame regenerates the comb with a fresh pattern; Bees flying / resting toggles the bees; the grid also regenerates on resize\n- The live dance readout translates the current dancer's waggle angle and run length into the direction of the flowers relative to the sun\n- On phones: a compact title bar with a jar counter, a bottom dock with Harvest and \"The dance\", which slides up the explanation sheet; reduced motion stops wing blur, instant-fills cells and slows the bees; animation pauses when the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"c742f0d05e9839954a5887c400be68020e354336c4bf622e929c19edf43de1ae","name":"蜂巢与蜂蜜","category":"生成艺术","description":"数百个六角巢室在琥珀色与蜡白之间交错，蜂蜜和光线沿蜂巢网格扩散，蜜蜂穿行其间。","interaction":"移动指针触发光波，点击或拖动向巢室注入蜂蜜。"},{"id":"070","slug":"070-chess-study","title":"Grandmaster's Study","file":"070-chess-study.html","textFile":"070-chess-study.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/070-chess-study.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Grandmaster's Study\", an elegant, playable two-player chess board (local hot-seat). A luxurious board of maple (#e8d0aa) and walnut (#8a5a3b) squares with subtle wood-grain gradients, a lacquered frame with brass corner fittings, and file/rank coordinates engraved in serif. Pieces drawn in SVG as original Staunton-inspired silhouettes — ivory (#f7f0e1, warm shading) and ebony (#1e1b18, soft highlights). Full legal chess rules: legal move generation with check detection, castling, en passant, promotion (with a chooser), and checkmate/stalemate detection. UX: click or drag pieces; legal moves shown as subtle dots/rings; last move highlighted; check shown as a red glow on the king; a move list in algebraic notation in a side panel styled like a score sheet; a captured-pieces tray; undo; flip board; new game. Smooth gliding piece animations and a soft synthesized \"clack\" on each move (toggle). Responsive: the board fits the width on phones with the panel below. Typography: a classic serif.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Grandmaster's Study is a complete two-player (hot-seat) chess game staged like a fine board on a green leather desk under a warm lamp. The board alternates maple (#e8d0aa) and walnut (#8a5a3b) squares with real-looking grain: maple runs horizontally and walnut vertically, as on a hand-made board. It sits in a deep red-brown lacquered frame with a gloss highlight, a thin brass inlay, gold-filled engraved serif coordinates on all four sides and four screwed brass corner fittings. The pieces are original Staunton-inspired SVG silhouettes in warm ivory (#f7f0e1) and ebony (#1e1b18), lit from the upper left with highlights, detail lines and soft cast shadows. Beside the board, a classic serif panel carries the title, a status plate, brass control buttons, a green-baize tray of captured pieces with the material balance, and a cream score sheet with a red margin, blue ruled lines, editable player names and moves in algebraic notation. On phones the board fills the width and the panel stacks below.","techniques":"- Wood grain from two inline-SVG feTurbulence filters (horizontal and vertical fractal noise) blended with multiply and split between light and dark squares by a conic-gradient checkerboard mask; per-square tonal variation from seeded gradient angles; an overall board sheen.\n- A lacquer frame built from layered gradients and inset shadows, with an inner brass fillet (stacked box-shadows), gold-filled engraved coordinates (text-shadow bevel) and reusable brass corner symbols with bevel gradients and slotted screws.\n- Piece symbols (<symbol> + <use>) filled by per-side linear gradients; highlight and detail strokes read CSS custom properties through the use shadow tree, so one drawing serves both ivory and ebony.\n- Pieces glide on transform transitions; the selected piece lifts; dragged pieces scale up with a deeper shadow; captured pieces shrink and fade; pieces drop in with a staggered entrance at the start of each game.\n- Legal targets shown as soft dots, and capture targets as rings; the last move is tinted amber; check is a red radial glow under the king.\n- Brass buttons with bevels and a pressed state; a paper score sheet built from gradients (margin rule, ruled lines, vignette) with a highlighter mark on the current move; a baize tray with an inset shadow; a fixed double gold tooling line around the viewport and a feTurbulence leather texture.","originalInteraction":"- Full legal chess: move generation with check detection, castling (with through-check rules), en passant, promotion with an on-board chooser (queen, rook, bishop, knight; Esc cancels), checkmate and stalemate, plus draws by the fifty-move rule, insufficient material and threefold repetition. Move generation was verified with perft against standard test positions.\n- Click a piece then a target, or drag and drop it; an illegal drop glides back. Clicking a selected piece deselects it.\n- Keyboard: every square is a focusable button with a descriptive label; arrow keys move between squares, Enter or Space selects and moves, U undoes and F flips. Moves are announced in a live region in spoken form (\"knight takes e5, check\").\n- Undo takes back one move at a time (pieces glide back and captured pieces reappear); Flip turns the board with a quick crossfade and re-engraves the coordinates; New game asks for a second click to confirm mid-game, then pieces drop back in.\n- Synthesized wooden \"clack\" for each move (filtered noise plus a body thump), a heavier double knock for captures, two knocks for castling, a soft bell on check and a lower bell at checkmate or a draw. It starts on the first interaction and has a Sound toggle.\n- Player names typed on the score sheet are used in the status line; with prefers-reduced-motion, glides and entrance animations are turned off.","sourceHash":"5598b33e78a9e02ed5c2b26b96d96622a1b60764fb41afd63afd198b79ec62b3","name":"棋手的书房","category":"音乐与游戏","description":"枫木与胡桃木棋盘置于绿皮桌面，灯光照亮完整的双人国际象棋游戏，支持标准合法走法。","interaction":"选择棋子再选择目标格；支持王车易位、吃过路兵和升变。"},{"id":"071","slug":"071-palette-studio","title":"Chroma — Colour Palette Studio","file":"071-palette-studio.html","textFile":"071-palette-studio.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/071-palette-studio.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Chroma — Color Palette Studio\", an elegant tool for generating harmonious palettes. A clean white UI (#ffffff, #f4f4f5 panels, #18181b text). The main view is five tall colour columns filling the screen (horizontal bands on mobile); each shows hex, RGB, HSL and a name (nearest match from a curated list of ~150 named colours) and has lock, copy and adjust controls. Space (or a button) generates a new palette that respects locked colours using harmony rules — analogous, complementary, split-complementary, triadic, tetradic, monochromatic — computed in OKLCH for perceptual evenness (implement the conversion). Extra panels: a WCAG AA/AAA contrast checker for text on each colour; a live preview card (a mini UI mockup) using the palette; a gradient preview; and export (CSS variables, JSON) copied to the clipboard. Interaction: drag columns to reorder, click a hex to edit it, and a colour-wheel diagram showing the harmony geometry with draggable points. Smooth colour transitions. Typography: a crisp sans plus monospace.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Chroma is a calm, precise palette studio in a white interface (#ffffff, #f4f4f5 panels, #18181b ink) set in a crisp system sans with monospace values. Five full-height colour columns fill the screen, and they become horizontal bands on phones. Each column shows the nearest of the 148 CSS named colours, a large editable hex, RGB, HSL and OKLCH values, and a contrast chip with its WCAG grade. Each also has drag, lock, copy and adjust controls in quiet translucent circles. A sidebar holds an OKLCH harmony wheel that draws the rule's geometry (triangle, rectangle, arc or line) with a draggable point per colour, plus the rule's name and description and a lightness ramp. Below the fold, \"Check it, try it, take it\" gathers a 7×5 WCAG contrast matrix, a live mock interface for a fictional trip-planning app whose roles are assigned automatically, an OKLab gradient with a side-by-side comparison against sRGB interpolation, and CSS or JSON export.","techniques":"- A hand-written colour pipeline: sRGB ↔ linear RGB ↔ OKLab ↔ OKLCH (Ottosson's matrices), with gamut mapping by binary search on chroma at constant lightness and hue\n- Six harmony rules (analogous, complementary, split-complementary, triadic, tetradic, monochromatic) defined as hue offsets for five slots, with a deliberate OKLCH lightness plan (one deep, one pale, three between) and chroma shaped to peak at mid lightness; locked colours anchor the base hue at their own slot\n- The harmony wheel is a per-pixel OKLCH disc (hue by angle, chroma by radius) rendered once to an ImageData cache, with the rule's geometry drawn on the rim, dashed spokes, and colour-filled handles that carry white rings, shadows and dashed rings when locked\n- Nearest-name lookup uses Euclidean distance in OKLab across all 148 CSS named colours; a ≈ marks near matches\n- Exact WCAG relative luminance and contrast ratio in the column chips and the matrix, with AAA, AA, AA-large and fail badges and hatched \"same\" cells\n- A mock UI coloured entirely through CSS custom properties: roles are assigned by lightness and chroma (ink, surface, primary, secondary, accent), accessible \"on\" colours are chosen automatically, and tints are mixed in OKLab\n- Gradient stops are pre-interpolated in OKLab and emitted as ordinary CSS stops, so they work in every browser; linear, radial and a seamless conic variant are offered\n- Smooth transitions of background and text colour on every column, matrix cell, ramp bar and preview element; slider tracks are live OKLCH gradients for L, C and H\n- A live logo mark whose five dots show the current palette, syntax-coloured export code with inline swatches, and toasts that carry a colour chip","originalInteraction":"- Space or the Generate button makes a new palette with the chosen rule, keeping locked colours. The segmented control switches rules\n- Per column: drag the grip to reorder (the other columns slide aside; the arrow keys work on a focused grip), lock or unlock, copy the hex, click the hex to edit it inline (#rgb or #rrggbb; invalid input shakes), or open the adjust popover with OKLCH lightness, chroma and hue sliders\n- On the wheel, dragging a point rotates the whole harmony while that point's radius sets its chroma. Shift-drag, or dragging a locked colour, moves just that one colour\n- Undo and redo with buttons or Ctrl/⌘+Z and Shift+Ctrl/⌘+Z (60 steps). The palette and rule are saved to localStorage and mirrored into the address hash (both guarded with try/catch), so a reload or a shared hash restores them\n- Export copies CSS custom properties (hex plus oklch()) or JSON to the clipboard, with an execCommand fallback and a toast. The gradient's CSS copies the same way, with a Linear, Radial or Conic choice and an angle slider\n- Visible focus rings, labelled icon buttons, aria-pressed on toggles, Escape closes the popover, and reduced motion removes the colour transitions","sourceHash":"91b3e356355e296d442973dc036333385c91bdafd3d502f937aca79e699b75ac","name":"配色工作室","category":"数据与工具","description":"五列颜色构成整张工作台，配色规则、锁定和重排围绕同一组色样展开，数值始终清晰可见。","interaction":"空格生成新配色，锁住需要保留的颜色，拖动手柄重排色列。"},{"id":"072","slug":"072-double-exposure","title":"Somewhere Inside","file":"072-double-exposure.html","textFile":"072-double-exposure.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/072-double-exposure.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Somewhere Inside\", a double-exposure art piece: a large human profile silhouette (a smooth SVG path of a face in profile with shoulders) acts as a mask revealing a whole landscape inside it — layered mountain ranges, a pine forest, a lake reflecting the moon, drifting fog and birds — while outside the silhouette is a pale misty gradient. Palette: a dusk gradient inside (#1d2b53 → #7e2553 → #ff9e7d), mist outside (#f2efe9 → #d9d4cc), ink details. The inner landscape moves in subtle parallax with the pointer (and slowly on its own), fog layers drift, and the moon's reflection shimmers. Original poetic text fragments fade in and out beside the profile in an elegant italic serif. Interaction: pointer parallax; clicking cycles the inner landscape between \"Mountains\", \"Ocean\" (waves and a lighthouse) and \"City\" (skyline with lights), each with its own palette and a smooth cross-fade. A film-grain overlay. The composition reflows for mobile, with the silhouette scaled and centred.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Somewhere Inside\" is a quiet double-exposure plate. A large human profile with a low bun faces right on pale, grainy mist (#f2efe9 to #d9d4cc) and works as a window onto a whole landscape at dusk. Inside, a sky of #1d2b53 to #7e2553 to #ff9e7d rises behind layered mountain ranges, drifting fog, a pine treeline, a still lake that reflects everything, a glittering moon path and a tiny rowing boat. The lake sits in the neck and the forest along the shoulders. Ink-dark birds cross the edge of the silhouette, so they read as dark shapes against the sky inside and as ink marks on the mist outside. Beside the face, an italic serif column holds the title and original four-line poem fragments that fade in and out, with a scene index below. Clicking the portrait travels further in: the landscape cross-fades to an Ocean (rising moon, lighthouse on a headland whose flaring lamp sits where the eye would be, rolling swells) and then to a City (skyline of lit windows, blinking radio mast, reflections on a river, a night train crossing a bridge). Each scene has its own palette and accent colour.","techniques":"- The silhouette is one hand-authored SVG path, used as a Canvas Path2D: it clips the landscape, draws an offset \"print shadow\" and a fine ink contour, and its stroke is layered four times inside the clip for a soft inner edge vignette\n- Every scene is procedural and drawn in a 1000-unit portrait box: mountain ridges from summed sharp-crested sine fields, tiered pine Path2Ds, a skyline of seeded rectangles with window grids, and animated multi-sine wave layers with crest highlights\n- Pointer parallax with per-layer depth factors (sky, moon, far range, mid range, fog, treeline, water, foreground), plus a slow idle drift\n- Water reflections are pre-rendered flipped layers that shimmer by being redrawn in thin sine-displaced slices into an opaque scratch canvas, which is then composited once (so no seam lines)\n- Moon glints and moon paths are flickering ellipses; lighthouse beam built from a lantern flare with additive blending and a sweeping cone that foreshortens as it turns; blinking aviation light\n- Feathered procedural fog sprites (radial blobs masked with destination-in gradients) drift inside and outside the portrait\n- Scene changes cross-fade two full scenes over 1.7 s with eased alpha, and the accent colour of the page shifts with them\n- Film grain: a generated noise tile in an oversized overlay-blended layer, jittered with a steps() CSS animation; soft radial vignette\n- Typography: an italic old-style serif (Iowan / Palatino / P052) for the title and fragments, a humanist small-caps face for labels; fragments fade in with a blur-to-sharp transition","originalInteraction":"- Move the pointer to drift the layers inside the head in parallax (touch taps work too)\n- Click or tap the portrait to travel to the next landscape (Mountains, Ocean, City); the three labelled buttons jump directly, and the arrow keys or 1/2/3 do the same from the keyboard\n- Poem fragments rotate every few seconds, restart when the scene changes, and are announced through an aria-live region\n- The composition reflows under 820 px wide: the title and fragment move above, and the portrait is scaled, centred on the head and anchored to the bottom edge\n- The canvas pauses while the tab is hidden; reduced motion stops the drift, grain jitter, water shimmer, train and beam sweep, and softens the text transitions","sourceHash":"9e9f44ffb997cd7d09a07bc84d33cd8f7f78e1977e71bcd3c4259743976b44ed","name":"内心的远方","category":"生成艺术","description":"人物侧影成为山、海与城市的窗口，双重曝光中的景色随指针产生层间视差。","interaction":"移动指针观察视差，点击人物或场景标签切换风景。"},{"id":"073","slug":"073-circuit-board","title":"Silicon — Living Circuit Board","file":"073-circuit-board.html","textFile":"073-circuit-board.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/073-circuit-board.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Silicon — Living Circuit Board\". An SVG printed circuit board in rich green solder mask (#0a4d2e with subtle texture) with gold traces and pads (#d4a537 with highlights), white silkscreen labels (component references such as U1, R12, C4, \"REV 2.1\"), chips (black epoxy with pin legs and printed markings), capacitors, resistors with colour bands, LEDs and a crystal oscillator. Signals — glowing pulses of cyan (#3cf2ff) and warm white light — travel along traces from chip to chip, routed along a graph of trace segments. Interaction: hovering a component highlights all of its connected traces and shows a spec tooltip; a \"power\" switch boots the board (LEDs light in sequence, a small OLED component shows boot text); clicking a chip \"overclocks\" it (faster pulses, a slight heat shimmer); a \"logic probe\" cursor mode shows voltage readings on traces. A title block in silkscreen monospace in the corner. Scales responsively via viewBox.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Silicon\" is a fictional development board rendered as one large responsive SVG and laid on a dark teal anti-static mat beside a datasheet-style control panel. The board is green solder mask with noise texture, a soft sheen and hatched copper pours. Its gold traces, routed at 45 degrees with mask clearance halos, connect a 64-pin QFP processor (U1), a TSOP memory (U2), a SOIC logic chip (U3), a DPAK regulator (U4) and a QFN USB bridge (U5). Around them sit a 25 MHz crystal, aluminium electrolytic capacitors with vent crosses and polarity stripes, carbon-film resistors with correct four-band colour codes, SMD parts, five status LEDs and a power LED, a 2x10 header, USB-C and barrel-jack connectors, a slide switch and a small OLED module. White silkscreen marks every reference (U1, R12-style labels, C4, \"REV 2.1\"), and a monospace title block sits in the corner. The page boots itself: the switch slides on, the power LED lights, warm-white power flows down the 3V3 trunk, the LEDs light in sequence, the OLED types a boot log, and cyan data pulses start racing from chip to chip.","techniques":"- Board built from data at runtime: each net is a list of points produced by a small 45-degree router (horizontal-first, vertical-first and centred dogleg), drawn as a wide mask-coloured clearance halo, then three copper strokes (dark edge, gold face, offset highlight)\n- Parts drawn from reusable SVG builders: QFP, TSOP and SOIC bodies with tinned legs on gold pads, epoxy gradients, pin-1 dimples and laser-etched markings; plated through-hole rings with specular dots; axial resistors with real colour bands; electrolytic cans with a sleeve stripe and a scored vent\n- Solder mask texture from an feTurbulence noise filter over a diagonal mask gradient, a radial sheen, a glossy diagonal highlight, a blurred board shadow, hatched copper pours with stitching-via grids, fiducials and mounting holes\n- Canvas overlay aligned with the viewBox for all light: pulses with tapered tails sampled along each polyline and pre-rendered glow sprites composited with \"lighter\", pad flashes on arrival, LED blooms, an OLED glow, and orange heat bloom with rising haze on overclocked chips\n- Pulses are routed across the net graph: when one reaches a part it continues along another net of that part, so signals hop from chip to chip; power pulses stay on the power nets\n- Overclocked chips get an animated feTurbulence and feDisplacementMap heat-shimmer filter and an orange outline\n- The OLED is live SVG text: a typed boot log, then a dashboard with packets, core clock, temperature and current, plus an animated square-wave scope trace\n- Datasheet panel with gold corner brackets, a physical-looking rocker switch, tabular meters and a condensed technical typeface paired with monospace","originalInteraction":"- Power: the panel rocker, clicking SW1 on the board, or the P key boots or shuts down the board (switch slide, LED sequence, OLED collapse on power-off)\n- Hover or keyboard-focus any part to highlight all of its connected traces (others dim, a warm glow runs along them) and show a spec tooltip with live status\n- Click a chip (U1 to U5), or press Enter on a focused chip, to overclock it: faster and denser pulses on its nets, heat shimmer, temperature and current rise, and the red HOT LED blinks; click again or use \"Cool all chips\" to return to nominal\n- Logic probe mode (button or L): the cursor becomes a drawn probe that snaps to the nearest trace and reads its net, endpoints and voltage (12 V, 5 V, 3.3 V rails, ground, 25 MHz clock, LED forward voltage, or logic HIGH/LOW with pulse detection); works with touch drag\n- Touch: first tap selects a part and shows its tooltip, a second tap on a chip overclocks it; Esc clears the selection and puts the probe away\n- Status LEDs react to traffic (bus, I/O), a heartbeat and the clock; the meters show supply, rail, current, core clock, temperature and packet count\n- Optional Web Audio sound (off by default): a power chirp and an overclock whine\n- prefers-reduced-motion: the board boots instantly, pulses move at half speed without haze, and switch transitions are removed","sourceHash":"4e4f1369339af4c243c6870ff6681d075364052ff4500cedeb78a7db3046a21c","name":"电路板解剖","category":"数据与工具","description":"一块虚构开发板被细致绘成 SVG，芯片、铜线、焊点、指示灯和 OLED 屏幕与数据手册式面板对应。","interaction":"点击电源或按 P 启动；悬停、聚焦元件查看说明。"},{"id":"074","slug":"074-soap-bubbles","title":"Iridescence — Soap Bubbles","file":"074-soap-bubbles.html","textFile":"074-soap-bubbles.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/074-soap-bubbles.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Iridescence — Soap Bubbles\". A soft, bright sky (#dbeafe → #fef3f2 gradient with faint drifting clouds). Bubbles of varied sizes float upward and drift on a gentle wind, wobbling as their shape oscillates; each is rendered with thin-film interference colours — swirling bands of magenta, gold, cyan and green on a mostly transparent sphere — plus a bright window-shaped specular highlight and a smaller secondary one, with the swirl animating over time (canvas gradients and noise-driven hue shifts). Bubbles collide softly. Interaction: clicking/tapping a bubble pops it — it bursts into a ring of tiny droplets and a quick shimmering film fragment, with a soft \"pop\" (Web Audio, after the first gesture); dragging blows a new bubble from a wand (it grows with drag distance and detaches on release); wind direction follows pointer movement. A tiny, elegant \"bubbles popped\" counter. Typography: an airy light sans title that is itself slightly iridescent.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Iridescence is a light, airy scene: a pale sky that fades from powder blue (#dbeafe) to blush (#fef3f2) with a warm sun bloom in one corner and faint clouds drifting across. Soap bubbles of many sizes rise slowly and drift on a gentle wind, wobbling as they go; one large \"hero\" bubble anchors the opening frame. Each bubble is mostly transparent, with swirling bands of magenta, gold, cyan and green that strengthen toward the rim, a bright four-pane window reflection in the upper left and a smaller glint lower right. The bands keep turning slowly over time. The title \"iridescence\" is set in an ultralight, widely spaced sans filled with a slowly sliding pastel-iridescent gradient. A tiny counter of bubbles popped, with a spinning rainbow ring, sits in the top corner, and soft glassy pills hold the hints, a wind vane, Blow and Sound.","techniques":"- Physically motivated thin-film textures: reflectance sin²(2π·n·d·cosθt/λ) with n = 1.33, sampled at paired red, green and blue wavelengths, computed per pixel at startup into five 256×256 sprites (two at boot, the rest over later frames)\n- Film thickness from a gravity gradient (thin crown, thick base) plus a domain-warped, radially twisted simplex swirl; a \"black film\" fade where the film is thinnest, Fresnel-weighted alpha so the centre stays clear and the rim glows, and a faint sky reflection in the upper half\n- Two film layers per bubble, counter-rotating and cross-fading at their own rates, so the bands appear to swirl\n- Quadrupole shape wobble (squash and stretch along a turning axis) driven by a damped oscillator that collisions and air kick; smaller bubbles wobble faster\n- Pre-rendered highlight sprites: a softly blurred four-pane window reflection and radial glints, placed with skew transforms\n- Procedural cloud sprites built from soft radial puffs with a cool shaded underside, drifting with parallax on a separate sky canvas\n- Soft pairwise collisions with mass-weighted separation and impulse, buoyant lift, and noise-driven turbulence\n- Pop effect: a ring of tinted droplets with gravity and highlights, curling iridescent film fragments stroked with gradient arcs, and a fading flash ring\n- The wand (lavender ring and handle, with film inside the ring) is drawn on canvas, and the new bubble inflates out of the ring along the drag direction\n- DPR-aware canvases; the pastel gradient title is animated with background-clip text","originalInteraction":"- Click or tap a bubble to pop it, with a synthesized \"pop\" (a band-passed noise burst plus a falling sine blip, lower for bigger bubbles) and a bump of the popped counter (kept in guarded localStorage)\n- Press on empty sky and drag to blow a bubble from a wand: it grows with the drag distance and detaches on release, carrying the flick velocity; a quick tap releases a small bubble\n- Moving the pointer steers the wind (direction and strength shown by the wind vane), which then relaxes back to a light breeze\n- \"Blow\" button and keys B, Space or Enter blow a bubble; P pops the largest; the arrow keys push the wind\n- Visible Sound/Muted toggle; audio starts only after a gesture; animation pauses while the tab is hidden; reduced motion slows drift, swirl and wobble and shows fewer bubbles","sourceHash":"1579499c091d243c19fbb4bdcb28d37408a8431514d6aebd0dc246be9b49c82e","name":"虹彩肥皂泡","category":"生成艺术","description":"半透明气泡在淡蓝和浅粉天空中升起，薄膜虹彩、反射与轻柔运动共同表现空气感。","interaction":"点击气泡使其破裂；声音仅在主动交互后产生。"},{"id":"075","slug":"075-calligraphy-pad","title":"Brush & Ink — Calligraphy Pad","file":"075-calligraphy-pad.html","textFile":"075-calligraphy-pad.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/075-calligraphy-pad.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Brush & Ink — Calligraphy Pad\". A sheet of rice paper (#f5f0e1 with visible fibres and an optional faint grid of practice squares) on a dark wood desk (#2b1d14), with an inkstone and a brush illustrated at the side. Drawing produces convincing brush strokes: width responds to speed (slow = thick, fast = thin) and to pointer pressure when available; ink runs out over a long stroke, producing dry-brush \"flying white\" texture; stroke ends taper; ink bleeds slightly into the paper (a subtle blurred halo). Ink colours: sumi black, diluted grey and vermillion. Tools: brush size, ink wetness, undo, clear (the paper slides away and a fresh sheet slides in), a red seal stamp you can place (SVG seal with generated square characters), and PNG download. Include a \"trace\" mode that shows faint model strokes of a simple character (e.g. 永) to practise over. Typography: small, elegant serif labels with vertical-text accents.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Brush & Ink is a quiet, lamp-lit calligraphy desk. A sheet of rice paper with a deckled edge, drifting fibres and faint laid lines lies on a dark procedurally grained wooden desk; to its left a lacquer-dark tool rail with vertical 筆墨 lettering, to its right an illustrated inkstone with an ink stick, a bamboo brush on a porcelain rest and a carved red seal. On load the brush writes 永 (eternity), the character used to teach the \"eight principles of yong\", stroke by stroke, followed by a small vertical inscription giving the current season and year in the sexagenary cycle, and the seal. From then on the paper is yours: strokes swell when you move slowly and thin when you move fast, respond to pen pressure, taper at the ends, bleed a soft halo into the paper and run dry over long strokes into streaky \"flying white\".","techniques":"- A custom brush engine that stamps along each stroke at sub-pixel spacing: width follows smoothed speed (slow = thick) and pen pressure, eases in at the entry, and tapers into a tail whose length depends on exit speed\n- Dry brush: 26 simulated bristles with their own dryness bias and low-frequency noise; as the stroke's ink falls, the solid core shrinks and only bristles with enough ink leave marks, producing parallel streaks of flying white; bristle normals are smoothed so turns do not fan out\n- Ink bleed: every wet stamp also paints a larger, low-alpha disc on a separate canvas shown through a CSS blur, giving a subtle feathered halo that is stronger for wetter ink\n- Layered canvases (paper, grid, trace, bleed, live stroke, ink); the live stroke is drawn opaque and composited at the ink's alpha on release, so diluted grey stays even within a stroke but darkens where strokes overlap\n- Procedural rice paper: soft mottling, thousands of curved light and dark fibres, specks, laid lines, edge tint and a randomly jittered clip-path polygon for the deckled edge\n- Procedural wooden desk: planks of wavy grain lines that swerve around knots, seams, and a lamp-glow vignette\n- The model 永 is defined as five stroke skeletons (dot, horizontal-turn-hook, horizontal-sweep, sweep, press) with per-point pacing, interpolated with Catmull-Rom splines and written by the same engine, so the model has true brush modulation\n- Seal carving: seeded, rectilinear pseudo-characters in a 2×2 layout cut out of a vermilion square, with edge wear and speckle, rendered as SVG for the preview and to canvas for stamping\n- Vertical CJK accents set with writing-mode: vertical-rl in a Kai-style font stack, set against small italic Garamond-style labels","originalInteraction":"- Draw with mouse, finger or pen (coalesced pointer events for smooth curves); a ring cursor shows the brush footprint on desktop\n- Ink wells choose sumi black, diluted grey or vermilion (radio group with arrow-key support); the brush illustration's tip darkens with the chosen ink and fades as the stroke's ink runs out\n- Sliders set brush size and ink wetness; wetter ink lasts longer and bleeds more\n- Undo (button or Ctrl/Cmd+Z) removes the last stroke, seal or inscription by replaying the recorded strokes exactly, with the same seeds\n- Clear slides the sheet off the desk and slides a fresh sheet, with new fibres, in from the other side\n- Trace shows the faint model 永 in a large practice square with numbered stroke order; Grid toggles faint vermilion practice squares (twelve small squares, or the large one while tracing)\n- Seal: pick it up from the rail or the desk, see a ghost follow the pointer, click to press it onto the paper; \"carve another\" generates a new seal\n- Save downloads a PNG composed of paper, grid, trace, blurred bleed and ink\n- Clicking the inkstone grinds fresh ink with a ripple; settings persist in guarded localStorage; the drawing re-renders at the new size when the window changes; reduced motion writes the opening character instantly and skips the sliding animations","sourceHash":"2a8df5b510a307537ea5225191193d40c82e723be9ebadd97cd1cba874463e92","name":"笔墨书写台","category":"生成艺术","description":"宣纸铺在深色木桌上，墨色、笔锋与纸纹共同模拟书写触感。笔画随落笔速度和输入方式变化。","interaction":"使用鼠标、手指或压感笔书写，切换墨色并调整笔刷。"},{"id":"076","slug":"076-mission-control","title":"ARES VII Mission Control","file":"076-mission-control.html","textFile":"076-mission-control.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/076-mission-control.jpg","prompt":"Create \"ARES VII Mission Control\", a dashboard for a fictional crewed Mars mission with clearly simulated telemetry. Aesthetic: \"NASA-punk\" — dark slate panels (#0e1116), safety orange #ff6b1a, off-white #e8e6e3, amber warnings #ffb000, cyan data #4ee1ff, thin grid lines, rounded-rectangle panels with corner brackets and tiny monospace labels. Panels: a mission elapsed time (MET) counter and a countdown to orbit insertion; a trajectory diagram (SVG Hohmann transfer between Earth and Mars with the spacecraft moving along it); live telemetry charts (cabin O₂, CO₂ ppm, power, velocity) as smoothly scrolling line charts; crew status cards with heart-rate waveforms; a comms log whose messages arrive after a one-way light-time delay; a subsystem status grid with nominal/caution/warning lights; and an animated spacecraft schematic. Interaction: click subsystems for detail; an \"acknowledge\" button when a random caution appears (flashing, with a Web Audio beep after a gesture); a toggle between Earth time and Mars sol time. A responsive grid that collapses gracefully.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"ARES VII Mission Control is a \"NASA-punk\" flight-control dashboard for an invented crewed Mars transfer, and it is labelled throughout as simulated. Slate panels (#0e1116) sit on a faint cyan grid, each with safety-orange corner brackets, a numbered monospace header and small status chips. Off-white numerals, cyan data traces and amber caution states complete the palette. The header carries the mission patch, a live mission elapsed time, a countdown to Mars orbit insertion and a clock that switches between Earth UTC and Mars time, plus a sound toggle and a physical-looking MASTER CAUTION lamp. Six panels fill the grid: a heliocentric Hohmann-transfer diagram, four scrolling strip-chart telemetry channels, an animated vehicle schematic, a 12-light subsystem matrix, four crew biomedical cards with live ECG sweeps, and a comms log whose traffic is governed by the one-way light time. The fiction rests on real mechanics. The transfer uses a = 1.262 AU and e = 0.208 (258.9 days). Position comes from solving Kepler's equation, speed from vis-viva, and light time from the Earth–ship range. Array power falls as 1/r², crew dose accumulates at the ~1.84 mSv/day the Curiosity rover's RAD instrument measured in cruise, and the Mars clock uses the Mars24 Mars Sol Date formula.","techniques":"- Corner-bracket panel chrome built from eight gradient strokes on a pseudo-element, over a 24 px blueprint grid on the page background\n- Trajectory SVG: Earth and Mars orbits, the full transfer ellipse drawn from its focus, a flown arc (solid orange) and remaining arc (dashed) re-traced from the solved true anomaly, a pulsing spacecraft marker, TMI/MOI markers, and an Earth line of sight labelled with the live light time\n- Four canvas strip charts sized to devicePixelRatio: a 10 Hz ring buffer with sub-sample interpolation for smooth scrolling, a moving time grid, dashed amber limit lines, gradient area fills, a glowing head dot, auto-scaling on the nav channel, and amber recolouring when a limit is crossed\n- ECG sweeps drawn incrementally like a bedside monitor (erase bar plus sweep head) from a synthetic PQRST waveform at each crew member's heart rate; heart icons pulse on each beat\n- Vehicle schematic in SVG: solar arrays foreshorten as they track, coolant flow runs as animated dashes, nav lights blink, RCS puffs fire occasionally, the high-gain antenna turns toward Earth's computed direction and radiates beam arcs when a downlink arrives, and parts glow when their subsystem is hovered or in caution\n- Caution system: a full-width annunciator strip below the header, part of the layout so it never covers a panel. It reads \"All systems nominal\" with a cyan lamp, and on a fault switches to amber with a blinking warning triangle and an Acknowledge button. The master caution lamp flashes, the tile light blinks and the schematic part flashes, then all go steady amber after acknowledgement and back to nominal once resolved\n- Condensed display type for titles and tabular monospace for every number","originalInteraction":"- Click MASTER CAUTION or Acknowledge to silence and acknowledge the active caution. A random fault (CO₂ scrubber, array string, coolant pump, HGA pointing, RCS heater, distiller) is raised 12–20 s after load (so the first frame is calm and nominal) and again roughly every 30–50 s; some faults change the telemetry (CO₂ climbs, array power drops)\n- The caution tone is a two-note Web Audio chime repeating every 1.8 s until acknowledged, and uplinks and downlinks play authentic Quindar tones (2525 Hz / 2475 Hz). Audio starts only after your first click or key press, and a Sound toggle mutes everything\n- Click any subsystem tile to open a detail drawer (an Esc-closable dialog) with live parameters, limits and a description; hovering a tile highlights its part on the schematic\n- Earth UTC / Mars sol toggle switches the clock between UTC with day-of-year and Mars Coordinated Time with Mars Sol Date and mission sol\n- The SIM ×1 / ×60 / ×3600 rate control speeds up mission time, so the spacecraft visibly advances and light-time delays play out quickly; Replay transfer animates the whole 259-day cruise\n- The comms uplink field sends your message to the crew: it shows in-transit progress for one light time, the crew replies after a short delay, and the reply arrives one light time later. Crew activities (sleep, exercise, lab, galley…) change over time and shift heart rates\n- Responsive grid: three columns on desktop, two on tablets, a single scrolling column on phones (where the annunciator strip stacks its text and gives Acknowledge a full-width button); reduced motion stops the flashing, flows and blinking","sourceHash":"db9b44c507d5e31cb94f1bd8fede511574450a3c4a2fe1e9cb20344ccc349a9c","name":"火星任务控制室","category":"数据与工具","description":"虚构载人火星任务的仪表板包含多种遥测、状态和告警。深色面板与醒目的功能色组织密集信息。","interaction":"确认告警，切换并查看各项模拟遥测；所有任务数据为演示。"},{"id":"077","slug":"077-noir-story","title":"The Glass Alibi","file":"077-noir-story.html","textFile":"077-noir-story.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/077-noir-story.jpg","prompt":"Build \"The Glass Alibi\", a short interactive film-noir detective story (original, about six scenes with branching choices and two or three endings). Black-and-white visuals with dramatic chiaroscuro: venetian-blind shadow bands sweeping across the scene, rain streaking the window (canvas), a flickering desk lamp, a steaming coffee cup, film grain and vignette overlays, and occasional old-film frame jitter. Scenes are illustrated with SVG silhouettes (office, rainy alley, jazz club, pier) in greys, with a single accent colour reserved for the key clue (a red lipstick mark #b3001b). Narration and dialogue appear with a typewriter effect in a period serif; choices are presented as typed index cards. Include an ambient sound toggle (synthesized rain and a low, jazzy bass line). Features: a case-file notebook that collects clues, iris-wipe scene transitions, and restart. Typography: a Courier-like monospace for notes and a condensed serif for titles.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"The Glass Alibi is a short, original film-noir mystery played in black and white on a letterboxed silver screen. Port Calloway, 1947: private eye E. Crane takes a late-night visit from torch singer Nora Vale, whose alibi for the killing under Pier 9 is a lipstick-stained glass at the Blue Lantern. Five scenes (office, rain-soaked alley, jazz club, dressing room, fog-bound pier) branch into three endings (Red on the Glass, The Last Ferry, Wrong Song), and whether you solve it depends on noticing one thing. Everything is rendered in greys, with chiaroscuro light cones, rain, grain, vignette and projector flicker, except the single key clue: a crimson lipstick mark in #b3001b that appears on the tube in the gutter, the rim of the glass on table six, the cold-cream rag, and the clue entries in the case file. Narration types out in a period serif, choices arrive as typed index cards, and each scene opens and closes with an iris wipe and a silent-film intertitle.","techniques":"- Five hand-built SVG scenes (1600×900, preserveAspectRatio slice) made of silhouettes and grey gradients, sharing one defs sheet: a reusable woman silhouette and hat, light-cone and halo gradients, a brick pattern, and blur filters for frosted glass and fog\n- Chiaroscuro details: a desk lamp that flickers at random, a coffee cup with CSS-animated steam, a bulb swinging on its wire over the stage door, a spotlit microphone, mirror bulbs that stutter, drifting fog banks, lamp halos over black water\n- Venetian-blind shadows cast across the office, plus periodic headlight sweeps of blind-shaped light bands (masked repeating gradients in screen blend) through the office, club and dressing room\n- Canvas weather: slanted rain streaks from a pooled particle set with splashes on the wet ground, rain confined to the office window with beads that slide down the glass, and slow cigarette-smoke haze in the club\n- Film treatment: pre-baked noise tiles jumped around at half frame rate for grain, occasional vertical scratches, a random projector flicker layer, a heavy vignette, and brief frame jitter every few seconds\n- Iris-wipe transitions (clip-path circle via the Web Animations API) with a bordered intertitle card between scenes, and a rubber-stamp verdict (Case Closed / Off the Record / Case Cold) on the endings\n- Title lettering drawn in SVG with textLength and lengthAdjust to condense a serif face into tall film-poster capitals\n- Index-card choices: ruled paper from layered gradients, a typed Courier face, random tilt, staggered drop-in, and locked cards that are struck through with a hint when you lack the proof\n- A manila-grey case-file drawer that clips inside a fixed viewport wrapper, numbering clues with typed entries and red kiss marks only on the key evidence","originalInteraction":"- Play cards with a click or the 1–4 keys; investigative cards can be played one after another within a scene before you move on, and a Continue card follows each reply\n- The typewriter reveals narration with punctuation pauses; click the text or press Space/Enter to finish it at once; a screen-reader live region receives each full line\n- Clues are filed automatically (a toast and a stamp animation on the Case file button); open the file with the button or C, close it with Esc\n- Branching: pocketing the lipstick unlocks new readings of the glass and a confession backstage; the accusation on the pier is only playable with the crimson tube plus either the glass or Vera's confession, which then offers the final choice between the two good endings\n- Sound toggle (or S), off by default: synthesized rain (filtered noise and a low rumble), distant thunder, a swung walking upright-bass line with brushes, soft typewriter key clicks, and a carriage bell at the end of each line\n- Restart from the top bar, the case file, or the ending cards\n- prefers-reduced-motion shows text instantly, stops grain, jitter, sweeps and scene animations, and replaces the iris wipe with a cut; rendering pauses while the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"7dfcf4f2563e88532dbbb18b1256f2252a4f4bd82bca758fbbfbbe202c09ea19","name":"黑色电影谜案","category":"音乐与游戏","description":"一段原创侦探故事被放进黑白宽银幕，人物对话、调查卡与打字式字幕推动剧情。","interaction":"点击调查卡或使用数字键选择行动，逐步推进情节。"},{"id":"078","slug":"078-fractal-tree-seasons","title":"Four Seasons — A Fractal Tree","file":"078-fractal-tree-seasons.html","textFile":"078-fractal-tree-seasons.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/078-fractal-tree-seasons.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Four Seasons — A Fractal Tree\", a generative tree swaying in the wind whose appearance follows a seasons slider. Trunk and branches are grown recursively (randomised angles, tapering widths, gentle curvature) and drawn on canvas with bark colouring; noise-driven wind bends branches in proportion to their depth. Seasons: Spring (pale blossoms #ffc8dd and fresh green buds), Summer (a lush green canopy #52b788 with dappled light), Autumn (leaves turning #e76f51, #f4a261, #e9c46a and falling with tumbling physics to pile on the ground) and Winter (bare branches, snow accumulating on limbs, snowfall). Sky and ground transition smoothly with the season. Interaction: drag the slider (or press \"Play the year\" for a 60-second cycle); click the tree to shake it (leaves or snow fall); \"New seed\" grows a new tree with a growth animation. Typography: a gentle serif title with month names around a small circular season dial.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Four Seasons is a quiet, painterly landscape with one generative tree standing on a gentle rise, rolling hills dotted with small trees behind it, and a sky, a sun and drifting clouds that all follow the time of year. The tree is grown recursively from a seed and sways in a gusty, noise-driven wind, with the thinnest twigs bending the most. Scrub through the year and it changes: fresh green buds and pale pink blossom in spring, a dense summer canopy with dappled light on the grass, leaves turning red, orange and gold in autumn before tumbling down into a pile around the trunk, and bare winter limbs gathering snow while flakes fall across a white meadow. A gentle italic serif title sits top-left; top-right, a small circular dial with the twelve months set around its rim, coloured season arcs, a leaf-tipped hand, a dot for today's date and a season glyph at its centre. The page opens on today's date in the northern hemisphere.","techniques":"- Breadth-first recursive branch generation (seeded PRNG) with random 2- or 3-way forks, tapering widths, per-branch curvature, phototropism for the young limbs and outward droop for the outer ones; the crown is measured and scaled to fit landscape or portrait screens\n- Per-frame hierarchical layout, so wind sway accumulates down the tree in proportion to depth, plus a fast damped oscillation when the tree is shaken; grows in on load and on \"New seed\" with a depth-staggered growth animation\n- Trunk and major limbs drawn as filled, tapered quadratic ribbons with a flared root base and a sun-side bark highlight; finer branches batched into one stroked path per depth\n- Thousands of leaves drawn as rotated ellipses, batched by quantised colour (spring tint, summer greens, autumn blend) so each colour is a single fill; a soft foliage mass gives the crown volume, and sun-lit leaves flicker to suggest dappled light\n- Deterministic calendar model: each leaf has its own bud, turn and fall dates and each blossom its own petal-drop date, so scrubbing backwards or forwards is always consistent; forward motion releases real falling particles\n- Tumbling leaf, petal and snow-clump physics (drag, terminal velocity, wind drift and flip), a seeded leaf pile that grows through autumn and browns under snow, snow drawn along the upper side of limbs in proportion to how horizontal they are, and snowfall\n- Seasonal palettes interpolated cyclically for sky, sun, clouds, hills, meadow and bark; distant trees change with the season; grass strokes, a canopy shadow and sunflecks; feTurbulence paper grain and a vignette overlay","originalInteraction":"- Drag the gradient \"Time of year\" slider (keyboard accessible, with a readable value such as \"Late September · Autumn\") or drag around the season dial to scrub the calendar\n- \"Play the year\" runs a full 60-second cycle and becomes Pause (P key); the dial hand, month highlight, season glyph and label all follow along\n- Click or tap the tree (or focus it and press Enter or Space) to shake it: petals drift in spring, a few leaves fall in summer, leaves shower down in autumn, and snow tumbles off the branches in winter before slowly building up again\n- \"New seed\" (N key) grows a completely new tree with a growth animation and a new leaf pile\n- A live region announces season changes and actions; prefers-reduced-motion calms the wind, growth and particle speeds; the loop pauses while the tab is hidden and everything re-lays out on resize","sourceHash":"254cbe48d047119e0e83eb3195da866cbaef393186e09843db8128fb269ca14b","name":"一棵树的四季","category":"生成艺术","description":"递归生成的树木站在起伏山丘上，枝叶、天空和光线随一年中的时间变化，形成安静的季节画面。","interaction":"拖动时间滑杆或季节圆盘；播放全年变化观察树木生长和落叶。"},{"id":"079","slug":"079-pulsar-ridges","title":"Pulsar Ridges","file":"079-pulsar-ridges.html","textFile":"079-pulsar-ridges.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/079-pulsar-ridges.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Pulsar Ridges\", an animated, original piece in the spirit of classic stacked ridge-line plots. Black background (#000) with ~60 stacked white lines (#f5f5f5) drawn on canvas with occlusion (each line filled black beneath so nearer lines hide farther ones); each profile comes from layered noise plus a central peak region and animates continuously, so the ridges roll like a landscape of signals. Interaction: the pointer position becomes the source of the peaks (ridges swell toward the cursor); clicking switches data modes: \"Pulsar\" (sharp central peaks), \"Terrain\" (mountain noise), \"Audio\" (a built-in synthesized drone analysed with an AnalyserNode drives the lines, after a gesture) and \"Heartbeat\" (ECG-like spikes). Settings: line count, amplitude, speed, colour mode (white, amber, spectrum gradient). Minimal typography: a small, uppercase, widely tracked monospace caption at the bottom like an album credit. PNG download. Perfectly crisp on high-DPI screens.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Pulsar Ridges is an original, animated homage to the stacked pulse plots of radio astronomy: sixty crisp #f5f5f5 lines on pure black (#000). Each line is a signal profile that occludes the lines behind it, so the stack reads as a landscape seen from a low angle. The profiles never stop moving. Pulses roll from the back of the stack toward the viewer, a noisy hiss shivers along every line, and the centre swells with clusters of sharp peaks. The pointer becomes the source of the signal: the peak region follows it horizontally, and lines near it swell into a raised mound. Clicking cycles through four signals, Pulsar, Terrain, Audio and Heartbeat. The only typography is a small, uppercase, widely tracked monospace credit line at the bottom, like the text on an album sleeve.","techniques":"- Painter's-algorithm occlusion on canvas: lines are drawn back to front. Each profile is first filled black down past its baseline, then stroked, so nearer ridges hide farther ones exactly.\n- Device-pixel-ratio canvas (capped at 2) with round joins and line width derived from line spacing, which keeps it crisp on high-DPI screens. Headroom and spacing are solved from the line count and amplitude so the top peaks never clip.\n- Pulsar signal: six Gaussian pulse components per line. Their position, height and width come from seeded Perlin noise over a continuous pulse index that shifts with time, so pulses roll smoothly toward the viewer, over a central envelope and fine hiss.\n- Terrain signal: ridged multi-octave noise (1-|n|)^3 with a wide envelope, rolling like a mountain range seen line by line.\n- Heartbeat signal: an ECG waveform (P, Q, R, S and T waves as signed Gaussians) with per-beat amplitude variation and a per-line phase offset, forming diagonal waves of spikes.\n- Audio signal: a real-time spectral waterfall. A Web Audio drone (detuned saws, sub square, fifths, a resonant low-pass swept by an LFO and chord glides) plus a noise-and-blip pulse every 1.337 s is analysed with an AnalyserNode (FFT 2048). Frequencies are mapped on a log scale and mirrored around the centre, and each new spectrum enters as the front line with a smooth sub-line scroll.\n- Mode changes cross-fade two generators over 0.7 s. The pointer adds a gaussian mound and gain field, eased in and out.\n- Colour modes: white, amber (#ffb000), or a spectrum running from violet at the back to gold at the front. The credit line tints to match.\n- PNG export renders the plot again at 2400×3000 with 520 samples per line and draws a letter-spaced credit, giving a poster-ready image.","originalInteraction":"- Move the pointer (mouse, pen or touch drag) over the plot and the peaks gather and swell toward it. In Audio mode the pointer's height also sweeps the drone's filter cutoff.\n- Click or tap (or press Space or Enter on the focused canvas) to cycle Pulsar → Terrain → Audio → Heartbeat; keys 1–4 jump straight to a mode. Entering Audio mode starts the synthesised drone. That click is the required user gesture, and a Sound toggle appears at bottom left (M key).\n- The Settings panel (bottom right, or the comma key) has mode chips, sliders for line count (20–100), amplitude and speed, colour chips, Save PNG and Freeze. It also carries a short accurate note on pulsar PSR B1919+21 and a list of keyboard shortcuts.\n- Keyboard: [ and ] change line count, ↑/↓ amplitude, ←/→ speed, C cycles colour, F freezes, S saves a PNG, Esc closes the panel.\n- Settings persist in localStorage (guarded). Animation pauses while the tab is hidden, and reduced motion slows the roll to a gentle drift.","sourceHash":"a01828861133f082375beb4ac853660f96bced87dba1a6baaf8604f90e676eef","name":"脉冲山脊","category":"生成艺术","description":"六十条白色信号曲线层层遮挡，像黑色空间中不断起伏的山脊，重现脉冲图的视觉力量。","interaction":"移动指针改变峰值区域；点击切换 Pulsar、Terrain、Audio 和 Heartbeat 模式。"},{"id":"080","slug":"080-falling-notes-piano","title":"Luminote — Piano with Falling Lights","file":"080-falling-notes-piano.html","textFile":"080-falling-notes-piano.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/080-falling-notes-piano.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Luminote — Piano with Falling Lights\". A full-width three-octave piano keyboard along the bottom (glossy black and ivory keys with realistic depth and pressed states), playable by mouse/touch (multi-touch) and computer keyboard (two rows mapped). Above it, a dark stage (#0a0a12) where notes fall toward the keys like a rhythm-game visualizer: glowing rounded bars coloured by pitch (a soft chromatic rainbow), with sparks and a light burst when they land. Sound: a pleasant piano-like tone synthesized with Web Audio (several detuned harmonics, fast attack, exponential decay, and reverb from a convolver with a generated noise impulse). Modes: \"Free play\" — played notes rise as light trails; \"Listen\" — auto-plays public-domain melodies (Ode to Joy, the opening of Für Elise, Twinkle Twinkle Little Star, the opening of Gymnopédie No. 1) with falling notes; \"Learn\" — the song waits for you to press the right key. Tempo slider and a note-name toggle. Typography: a refined sans with small caps.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A nocturnal, immersive piano visualizer. A deep #0a0a12 stage fills the screen above a realistic three-octave keyboard (C3–C6, 37 keys) with a lacquered fallboard and a thin red felt line. Glowing rounded bars fall toward the keys, each coloured by pitch class on a soft chromatic rainbow (rose C through orange, lime, cyan and indigo to magenta B). When a bar lands, its key sinks and glows, a light beam rises from it, and a burst of sparks and an elliptical bloom flare up. Faint key lanes, octave marks, drifting dust and a violet horizon glow give the stage depth. A floating glass control bar carries the rainbow small-caps \"Luminote\" wordmark, the three modes, a melody picker, play/restart, a tempo slider, a label toggle and mute. The page opens in Listen mode, already raining the opening of Für Elise silently, with a breathing \"Tap anywhere to wake the piano\" pill.","techniques":"- A DPR-aware canvas stage over a pre-rendered backdrop (gradient, lane shading, octave labels). Additive (\"lighter\") compositing for bars, beams, blooms and sparks. Bars are rounded rects with a vertical gradient, a pitch-coloured shadowBlur halo and a bright inner core.\n- A pooled spark system (420 particles, gravity, drag, streak rendering), key light beams that fade on release, radial bloom bursts, twinkling dust motes and a rainbow horizon line at the keys.\n- CSS-built keys. Ivory keys use layered gradients with a front lip (inset shadows); black keys have a glossy highlight strip and a raised lip. Pressed states translate the key, shrink the lip and cast a glow in the note's colour (a CSS custom property per key). Learn-mode hints pulse, and wrong notes shake.\n- Web Audio piano synthesis: eight sine partials with slight detune and inharmonic stretch, per-partial decays, a 5 ms attack, pitch-dependent exponential decay, a lowpass whose cutoff falls over time, a filtered-noise hammer tick, a convolver reverb from a generated stereo noise impulse (with sparse early reflections and a darkening tail), and a gentle compressor.\n- A glass UI with backdrop blur, a gradient-text wordmark, small-caps labels and a rainbow progress bar.","originalInteraction":"- Play with mouse or touch: multi-touch chords work, and sliding across keys plays a glissando. On a computer keyboard, the bottom row Z…/ (with S D G H J L ;) starts at C3 and the top row Q…] (with 2 3 5 6 7 9 0 =) starts at C4. Pressing near the front of a key plays slightly louder.\n- Free play: every note you play rises from its key as a ribbon of light that grows while held and floats away on release.\n- Listen: auto-plays Ode to Joy, the opening of Für Elise, Twinkle Twinkle Little Star, or the opening of Satie's Gymnopédie No. 1 (with its alternating G/D major-seventh accompaniment). Notes fall in time and loop \"da capo\". Play/pause and restart are available.\n- Learn: the melody falls and stops when the next note reaches the keyboard. That key glows, and the coach shows its name and computer key until you play it. Wrong notes shake, a first-try accuracy score is kept and a result card appears at the end.\n- The tempo slider (40–160%) keeps the fall speed constant in pixels while changing the pace. Labels switch between note names, computer-key letters and off, and are remembered with tempo and mute in localStorage (guarded).\n- Audio starts on the first gesture, and the mute button fades the master output. On phones, a compact bar hides the extra settings behind a toggle. The stage and keyboard scroll sideways together, auto-following the upcoming notes unless you scroll yourself. Reduced motion trims sparks and dust.","sourceHash":"5c30a16ec9909537e38ca0d931e8e9dac5e56a7353a9e4e3dc8c9dd5b98c2bef","name":"落光钢琴","category":"音乐与游戏","description":"三组八度的琴键上方落下彩色音符，触键时光束、火花和琴键下沉共同响应演奏。","interaction":"鼠标、触屏或电脑键盘演奏，支持和弦与滑奏；体验下落音符的视觉反馈。"},{"id":"081","slug":"081-glitch-portfolio","title":"SIGNAL/NOISE","file":"081-glitch-portfolio.html","textFile":"081-glitch-portfolio.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/081-glitch-portfolio.jpg","prompt":"Design \"SIGNAL/NOISE\", the portfolio of a fictional glitch artist, \"k.oshiro\". Stark black #000 and white #fff with RGB channel accents (#ff0040, #00ff9f, #0070ff). The page is intentionally corrupted but controlled: a headline with RGB split and horizontal slice displacement that re-randomises periodically; project thumbnails that are generative canvases (pixel-sorted gradients, datamosh-like block smears, scanline interference) and glitch harder on hover; CRT scanlines and occasional full-screen tear events; a scrolling \"corrupted\" hex-dump sidebar. Sections: a works grid (eight generative pieces with titles and years), an about text in monospace that types itself with random character corruption resolving into readable text, an exhibitions list and contact. Interaction: hovering intensifies the glitch; clicking a work opens it full-screen with a \"corruption\" slider; a \"stabilize\" toggle calms every effect (and prefers-reduced-motion defaults to stable). Typography: monospace and a heavy grotesk. Despite the chaos, it stays navigable and legible.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"SIGNAL/NOISE\" is the portfolio of k.oshiro, a clearly fictional glitch artist, designed as a controlled corruption: pure black #000 and white #fff with RGB channel accents (#ff0040, #00ff9f, #0070ff), a heavy grotesk for display and monospace for everything else. A hex-dump sidebar scrolls endlessly down the left edge (it collapses into a three-line strip on phones). A two-line \"SIGNAL/ NOISE\" headline fills the hero, with its channels split and horizontal slices displaced in periodic bursts, and a short manifesto sits in the space beside \"NOISE\". Below are a stat strip, a 4×2 grid of eight live generative works, an About section whose monospace text decodes itself out of glyph noise, an exhibitions list and a large lowercase contact line. CRT scanlines sit over everything and occasional full-screen tears rip through the page, but hierarchy, spacing and contrast keep it navigable, and one \"Stabilize\" switch calms every effect.","techniques":"- Headline on canvas: the text is rendered three times in the red, green and blue accents and composited with \"lighter\", so aligned layers sum to white and misaligned ones fringe in colour; random horizontal bands get independent offsets and occasional single-channel slices, re-randomised in bursts every few seconds and continuously on hover\n- Eight distinct generative works, each built procedurally at 192×144 and upscaled pixelated: column pixel-sorting of a sunrise gradient (Dawn Buffer), three drifting scanline fields producing moiré colour (Vertical Hold), shapes with exponential red/blue channel bleed (Chroma Bleed), live static with a rolling bar and a ghosted \"NO SIGNAL\" (Dead Channel), a portrait smeared by repeated macroblock copies along motion vectors (Macroblock Elegy), animated XOR and multiply bit patterns (Bit Rot Season), 8×8 DCT-basis blocks with posterised quantisation and chroma errors (Q-Table Ghost), and an orthographic globe with misparsed points piled on 0°N 0°E (Null Island)\n- A shared per-frame corruption pass on raw ImageData: seeded row-band displacement, sine wobble, per-channel horizontal offsets, alternating scanline darkening, copied, inverted or channel-swapped blocks, and flash lines, with intensity easing between idle and hover\n- Full-screen tear events: an inline SVG filter (turbulence quantised into bands → feDisplacementMap on x only → red-channel offset → screen blend) applied to the page for a split second, plus an overlay canvas of coloured and black noise bars\n- CRT overlay of repeating scanlines, vignette and a stepped flicker; hex dump built from a real message stream with randomly corrupted bytes highlighted in RGB and the occasional inverted line, recycled as lines scroll out\n- Self-decoding biography: a scramble window of random glyphs travels ahead of the resolved text, with sporadic single-character corruptions that heal as it completes\n- Stepped (steps()) transitions, RGB text-shadow jitter on hover, and hard-edged buttons with red and blue shadow offsets","originalInteraction":"- Hover or focus the headline to lose the signal (continuous slicing); hover or focus a work to push its corruption from idle to heavy and speed up its frame rate\n- Click (or Enter/Space) a work to open a full-screen viewer at double resolution with a \"Corruption\" slider (0–100) driving the effect live, prev/next buttons, ←/→ keys, Escape to close, a focus trap, and focus returned to the card\n- \"Stabilize\" switch (role=switch) calms everything: static headline, near-clean thumbnails, no flicker, no tears, a slow hex dump, and instant readable text. It is remembered in localStorage (guarded) and turns on by default under prefers-reduced-motion\n- Sticky header with section links and live telemetry (signal percentage, frame counter); the About text starts decoding when scrolled into view\n- \"Copy address\" copies the non-URL contact phrase with the Clipboard API, falling back to a hidden field and execCommand, with a status message either way\n- Thumbnails only animate while on screen (IntersectionObserver); the loop pauses when the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"c8a0d28b5bf8030c613bb5a88cc17addceb511dac8b2c9c1dbac0c197c4b445e","name":"信号与噪声","category":"交互网页","description":"虚构故障艺术家的作品集将 RGB 分离、水平切片和乱码变成可控制的视觉语言。","interaction":"悬停标题或作品增强失真；点开作品，用 Corruption 滑杆调整破坏程度。"},{"id":"082","slug":"082-perfume-rosee","title":"Rosée — Eau de Parfum","file":"082-perfume-rosee.html","textFile":"082-perfume-rosee.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/082-perfume-rosee.jpg","prompt":"Create a product page for \"Rosée — Eau de Parfum\" by a fictional maison. Palette: blush #f4d6d0, rose gold #b76e79 → #e8b4a0, cream #fff8f3, deep plum text #3b1f2b. Hero: a tall faceted glass bottle drawn in SVG with realistic refraction highlights and a gold cap, holding pink liquid whose surface sloshes in a gentle wave and responds to pointer tilt (the liquid level tilts against the pointer). Rose petals (gradient SVG shapes) drift down the page with a 3D flutter, staying clear of the text column. Sections: the fragrance pyramid (top/heart/base notes) as an elegant layered triangle where each note appears on hover with a small botanical line illustration; \"The Story\" editorial paragraph; a size selector (30/50/100 ml) that resizes the bottle smoothly and updates the price; and a luxurious \"Add to bag\" button with a shimmering sweep. Typography: a refined high-contrast serif with wide-tracked sans captions.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A quiet-luxury product page for Rosée, an eau de parfum from the fictional Maison Élorine, in blush #f4d6d0, cream #fff8f3, rose gold #b76e79 to #e8b4a0 and deep plum #3b1f2b. The hero pairs a tall, octagonal faceted glass bottle with a jewel-cut gold cap, standing on a cream plinth inside a soft halo, with a rose-gold Didone wordmark \"ROSÉE\" drawn in SVG (hairline serifs, heavy stems, a light sweep of shine). The pink liquid inside is alive: its surface ripples gently, and as the pointer moves the bottle leans slightly toward it while the liquid lags, sloshes and settles, tilting against the pointer. Rose petals flutter down in 3D and steer around every text block. Below the hero, a layered pyramid presents the top, heart and base notes, each revealed with hand-drawn botanical line illustrations that draw themselves. An editorial \"Story\" follows, with a drop cap, a pull quote and a fictional perfumer's signature. Wide-tracked sans captions set off the high-contrast serif throughout.","techniques":"- Custom SVG Didone wordmark built from rectangles, evenodd bowls and a lens-shaped S spine, filled with a rose-gold gradient; a masked highlight bar sweeps across it\n- Faceted bottle: octagonal outline with front face, side facets, shoulder and base bevels. One live liquid path is reused through <use> in three clip regions, and the side-facet copies are shifted and re-tinted to fake refraction, so the surface line breaks at each facet edge. Streak highlights, facet edge lines, a thick pink base, suspended glints and a meniscus band complete the glass\n- Liquid physics: the bottle tilt follows the pointer on a damped spring, the surface angle chases the opposite tilt on a softer spring (visible slosh), and two travelling sine waves ripple the surface; the path is rebuilt every frame\n- Size changes (30/50/100 ml) scale the bottle on a critically damped spring about its base, and resize the plinth shadow and pink caustic; the price counts to the new value with an ease-out\n- Petals: pooled DOM elements holding a gradient SVG petal symbol, moved with translate3d plus rotateX/Y/Z under perspective for a true 3D flutter; each frame they read the rectangles of all [data-clear] text blocks and steer sideways around them, and respawn in free columns\n- Pyramid tiers are real buttons shaped with clip-path trapezoids under a shared drop-shadow; the active tier turns plum\n- Nine botanical line drawings (bergamot, pink pepper, pear, damask rose, peony, lily of the valley, sandalwood, vanilla, ambrette) drawn with pathLength-normalised stroke-dashoffset transitions, with a delayed blush fill\n- The \"Add to bag\" button is a layered rose-gold gradient with a skewed light sweep (faster on hover), a success state, a petal burst from the button and a springy bag-badge pop","originalInteraction":"- Move the pointer or a finger anywhere: the bottle leans toward it and the liquid sloshes and tilts against it; when idle, the bottle sways gently\n- Choose 30, 50 or 100 ml (a radio group with arrow keys): the bottle grows or shrinks smoothly, the label and price update, and the change is announced to screen readers\n- \"Add to bag\" increments a bag badge (saved in localStorage inside try/catch), shows a confirmation, bursts petals and announces the new total; the bag button reads back its contents\n- Hover, focus or tap a pyramid tier to reveal its notes as cards whose line illustrations draw themselves in\n- Header links jump to the product, notes and story sections; all controls have visible focus styles; the loop pauses while the tab is hidden, and reduced motion keeps the bottle still, slows and simplifies the petals and disables shimmer and line-draw animation","sourceHash":"0209a01493bea764402588ce343d36e651f43970bdf5cc9d7ae63394b3d91b24","name":"晨露香水","category":"交互网页","description":"虚构香水瓶以切面玻璃、玫瑰金瓶盖与轻微晃动的液面构成产品主视觉，整体呈现安静的奢华感。","interaction":"移动指针观察瓶身倾斜和液体晃动；选择容量改变瓶体与价格。"},{"id":"083","slug":"083-dot-globe","title":"Connected — A Dot-Matrix Globe","file":"083-dot-globe.html","textFile":"083-dot-globe.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/083-dot-globe.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Connected — A Dot-Matrix Globe\". A rotating 3D globe rendered on canvas as a sphere of evenly spaced dots (Fibonacci sphere), with bright land dots and faint ocean dots — land determined from an embedded low-resolution land mask (a compact hand-encoded bitmap, e.g. a 72×36 character grid of the continents). Palette: midnight background #050a18, ocean dots #1b2a4a, land dots #5ad1e6 → #a6fff2, arcs in coral #ff6f61 and gold #ffd166. Animated great-circle arcs travel between about fifteen major world cities placed at their real latitude/longitude (London, New York, Tokyo, São Paulo, Lagos, Sydney, Mumbai, Cairo…), each with a glowing head, a fading tail and a pulsing ring on arrival. An atmospheric glow halos the globe. Interaction: drag to rotate with inertia; hover a city dot for its name; click a city to fly the globe round to face it; auto-rotation resumes after idle. A side panel with clearly simulated stats (\"connections per second\"). Typography: a clean geometric sans.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Connected is a rotating dot-matrix Earth floating in a midnight #050a18 starfield. 12,000 points (7,000 on phones) sit evenly on a Fibonacci sphere. Each one is classified as land or ocean by a hand-encoded 72 × 36 bitmap of the continents (5° cells, stored as compact run lengths) that is sampled bilinearly, so the 5° steps soften into rounded coastlines. Land dots glow from #5ad1e6 to #a6fff2 under a light from the upper left, ocean dots are a quiet #1b2a4a, and the continents on the far side show faintly through, like glass. A cyan atmosphere haloes the sphere and a rim light catches its edge. Great-circle arcs fly between fifteen real cities: London, New York, Tokyo, São Paulo, Lagos, Sydney, Mumbai, Cairo, Los Angeles, Singapore, Moscow, Johannesburg, Mexico City, Dubai and Beijing. Arcs are coral when eastbound and gold when westbound, and each has a white-hot head, a tapering tail and a pulsing ring when it lands. A glass side panel shows clearly simulated traffic statistics in a clean geometric sans (Futura, Century Gothic, URW Gothic).","techniques":"- Fibonacci-sphere point distribution (golden-angle spiral). Land and ocean are classified once from the run-length-decoded 72 × 36 mask using bilinear smoothing at a 0.47 threshold.\n- Rotation uses yaw and pitch with precomputed trig per frame, then orthographic projection. Dot size and alpha scale with depth, and land dots are bucketed into 8 brightness levels by Lambert lighting. All dots are drawn as batched arc paths, one fill per colour bucket, for speed.\n- Back-hemisphere land is drawn at 7% opacity for a see-through glass globe, over a radial-gradient sphere body, atmosphere halo, rim light and a pre-rendered DPR-aware starfield.\n- Arcs are slerped great circles lifted by a sine altitude profile proportional to their angular distance. They have per-point limb occlusion (hidden only when behind the sphere and inside its disc), a faint full-route trace, an 18-segment tail whose width and alpha grow toward the head, and a radial-gradient head. Arrival rings are ellipses foreshortened by their depth.\n- City markers have glow sprites, a pulsing selection ring and uppercase canvas labels near the centre of view.\n- The panel shows connections per second with a live 60-sample sparkline (a gradient-filled canvas), routes in flight, latency random walk, handshakes, data moved and busiest route. City activity bars grow and flash on each arrival. Everything is marked \"Simulated\".","originalInteraction":"- Drag the globe with mouse or touch (pointer capture). Release with speed and it keeps spinning on inertia. Auto-rotation eases back in after 3.5 s of idleness, and the \"Auto-rotate\" toggle turns it off.\n- Hovering a city shows a tooltip with its name and real coordinates. Clicking it, or choosing it in the side list or the phone chips, flies the globe round to face that city with an eased shortest-path rotation and launches new arcs from it.\n- With the canvas focused, the arrow keys rotate the globe (Shift for bigger steps). The \"Traffic\" toggle pauses new arcs.\n- The layout is responsive. Desktop has the globe beside a 360 px glass panel. Phones get the globe above a compact stats card with horizontally scrolling city chips and a lighter 7,000-dot sphere. Canvases match their container at DPR up to 2, animation stops while the tab is hidden, and reduced motion disables auto-spin and makes fly-tos instant.","sourceHash":"f73bd47829638e559e775990859312208e9c1918e4e1b7b3ba688ba3579641d3","name":"点阵地球","category":"数据与工具","description":"上万颗发光点组成旋转的地球，陆地与海洋用不同亮度区分，城市位置成为探索入口。","interaction":"拖动旋转，松手保留惯性；悬停或点选城市查看位置。"},{"id":"084","slug":"084-snowflake-generator","title":"Crystalline — No Two Alike","file":"084-snowflake-generator.html","textFile":"084-snowflake-generator.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/084-snowflake-generator.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Crystalline — No Two Alike\", a procedural snowflake generator. Each snowflake grows with 6-fold (dihedral) symmetry using a branching algorithm — main arms with side dendrites, plates and needles that vary with \"temperature\" and \"humidity\" parameters, loosely echoing the Nakaya diagram. Rendered in SVG/canvas as translucent ice with inner facets, edge highlights and a faint glow on a deep winter-blue background (#0c1b33 → #1c3a5e). The hero shows one large flake slowly rotating; below it, a gallery of smaller generated flakes. Background: gentle snowfall of micro-flakes with depth blur. Interaction: \"Grow a snowflake\" animates growth outward from a hexagonal seed; temperature and humidity sliders show how the form changes (plates at some settings, dendrites at others — keep the explanation light and approximate); clicking a gallery flake enlarges it; each flake gets a catalogue number and date; download as SVG. Typography: a thin, wide-tracked sans with a delicate serif italic.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Crystalline — No Two Alike\" is a quiet laboratory of ice on a deep winter-blue field (#0c1b33 → #1c3a5e), where soft snow falls through three depths of focus. On the left, a slim panel in thin, wide-tracked sans with delicate serif italics holds the conditions in the cloud: temperature and humidity sliders and a small, simplified Nakaya diagram whose glowing cursor can be dragged directly. A one-line, deliberately approximate explanation of the resulting habit sits below. On the right, one large, slowly rotating snowflake glows like translucent ice, with a catalogue plaque beneath it (number, date and time, habit name). \"Grow a snowflake\" grows a new crystal outward from a tiny hexagonal seed, numbers and dates it, and files it in the catalogue below. The catalogue is a grid of smaller flakes. Each card opens an enlarged view with its conditions and seed, a \"grow alike\" action and an SVG download.","techniques":"- Procedural crystal generator with true six-fold dihedral symmetry. Primitives are built for one arm plus one half-arm, then rendered as 6 rotations plus a mirrored half, so every flake is exactly D6-symmetric\n- Morphology is driven by continuous scores loosely after Nakaya: dendrites peak near −15 °C in moist air, sectors at moderate vapour, plates near 0 °C and in dry air around −10 to −20 °C, needles near −5 °C, and columns near −7 °C or below −22 °C. The scores feed core-plate size, arm length and width, branch count, secondary-branch density, tip plates, ribbed sector wedges and needle spikes\n- Arms and branches are drawn as tapered hexagonal-profile strips with pointed tips, split into lit and unlit facet halves with a central ridge and small transverse ribs. Plates carry vertex ridges and inner rings, and end-on columns get hollow concentric hexes. Fern-tree envelopes, a seeded PRNG and outward secondary dendrites make no two alike\n- The translucent ice look comes from a radial ice gradient body with non-scaling bright edge strokes, facet overlays and ridge lines, plus a double CSS drop-shadow glow. Sparkle points mark the arm tips. The rotation is a composited CSS transform inside a circular clip\n- Growth is animated by clipping every primitive against an expanding radius. Strips lengthen with a live pointed tip and hexes swell from their centres, eased so growth is fast and then settles, all starting from a visible hex seed\n- The SVG export is a hand-assembled standalone document with background, gradients, a glow filter, a title and description, and the catalogue label. It downloads via a data: link\n- Snowfall is a canvas of pre-rendered soft sprites in four depth classes. Farther flakes are smaller, dimmer and slower. The nearest are large, blurred bokeh\n- The mini Nakaya chart is SVG, with blurred region blobs, rotated and small-caps labels, axes and a glowing draggable cursor","originalInteraction":"- The temperature (−30 to −1 °C) and humidity (0 to 100%) sliders, or dragging on the Nakaya chart, set the conditions. The explanation text updates, and the current flake is re-shaped live as a \"preview\" with the same seed\n- \"Grow a snowflake\" grows a new seeded crystal from its hexagonal seed over about 3.6 s. It is given the next catalogue number and the current date and time, and it arrives at the head of the catalogue grid with an entrance animation\n- The \"SVG\" button downloads whatever flake is shown. Catalogue cards rotate slightly on hover and open a modal dialog (with native Esc, a close button and click-outside) offering \"Download SVG\" and \"Grow alike\", which copies the conditions back to the grower and grows a sibling\n- The catalogue (up to 36 entries) and the numbering counter persist in localStorage, guarded by try/catch. The header shows the running catalogue count\n- Responsive: on phones the flake leads, with the Grow and SVG buttons directly beneath it and the catalogue in two columns. prefers-reduced-motion stops the rotation and the snowfall and makes growth instant","sourceHash":"f508393a169928566133ac915bde6c468207c097f73a7af0842a3e1cc108390c","name":"雪花生成器","category":"科学与模拟","description":"温度和湿度决定冰晶的生长形态，一片雪花从六角种子逐步展开，背后是轻缓飘雪的深蓝天空。","interaction":"调整温湿度或拖动形态图，点击 Grow a snowflake 生成新的雪晶。"},{"id":"085","slug":"085-art-nouveau-botanicals","title":"Maison Lierre — Paris 1899","file":"085-art-nouveau-botanicals.html","textFile":"085-art-nouveau-botanicals.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/085-art-nouveau-botanicals.jpg","prompt":"Design \"Maison Lierre — Botanical Teas, Paris 1899\" (a fictional tea house) as an Art Nouveau poster page in the spirit of the era's great poster artists (original artwork, nothing copied). Palette: muted sage #a3b18a, olive #588157, antique gold #c9a227, terracotta #bc6c25, cream #fefae0, deep teal #264653. The page is framed by an ornate SVG border of whiplash curves, stylised lilies and ivy that draw themselves on load and sway gently; a circular halo motif with radiating mosaic sits behind the title; flowing, organic panels hold the content (the house's story, three signature blends, an afternoon-tea menu, a contact card). Decorative initials and dividers. Typography: a serif stack plus custom SVG-drawn title lettering with curved terminals. Interaction: hovering a blend grows vines around its card; a subtle gold sparkle on the halo; a toggle between \"Day\" (cream) and \"Salon\" (deep teal with glowing gold lines). Graceful responsiveness: the border simplifies on phones.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Maison Lierre is an Art Nouveau poster page for a fictional botanical tea house in Paris, 1899. It is an original homage to the era's great poster artists, not a copy of any of them. A fixed ornamental frame wraps the viewport: a teal outer rule, a gilt inner rule with notched corners, whiplash corner flourishes ending in spirals and lily buds, ivy climbing both sides, and small cartouches at top and bottom. All of it draws itself on load and sways gently. At the centre of the hero, \"MAISON LIERRE\" is set in custom SVG lettering: monoline capitals with curled terminals, drawn on in teal and gold. It sits inside a circular halo of hand-set mosaic tesserae ringed by sunburst rays, with gold glints sparkling across the tiles. Two tall stylised lilies with whiplash petals, stamens and blade leaves flank the halo, and ivy garlands wreathe it above a curved ribbon reading \"Botanical Teas · Paris 1899\". Below, organic panels with gilt-and-olive double borders hold the house's story, which opens on an illuminated drop cap. Three arch-topped cards present the signature blends with botanical vignettes (chamomile and lime blossom, a scalloped rose, jasmine sprays), and vines grow around a card when you hover it. There is an afternoon-tea menu with dotted leaders and period franc prices, and a calling-card contact panel with an \"ML\" monogram. A toggle switches between \"Day\" (cream paper) and \"Salon\" (deep teal with glowing gold lines). On phones the frame simplifies to the rules and small corner flourishes.","techniques":"- Custom SVG title lettering: nine hand-built glyphs (M, A, I, S, O, N, L, E, R) set by a tiny layout routine, rendered in three stroke layers (teal outline, gold core, offset highlight) and drawn on with pathLength-normalised stroke-dashoffset\n- Mosaic halo generated at runtime: four rows of slightly askew tesserae in sage, olive, gold and terracotta, rows of tiny dots, 96 alternating sunburst rays, a slow counter-rotation of the ring and the rays, and randomly timed star glints\n- Stylised lilies built procedurally (whiplash petal paths with curled tips, stamens with terracotta anthers, blade leaves and buds), mirrored on both sides and swaying from their roots\n- A fixed viewport frame rebuilt on resize: double rules with notched corners, mirrored whiplash corners, climbing ivy with trilobed leaves, cartouches; draw-on and bloom animations are staggered with CSS custom-property delays\n- Day/Salon theming through CSS custom properties; in Salon mode the gold linework glows through stacked drop-shadow filters, and the halo disc swaps gradients\n- Arch-topped cards and organic panels made with elliptical border-radius and layered box-shadow rings (gilt / paper / olive)\n- Hover vines on each blend: pathLength-normalised vine strokes grow around the card, then ivy leaves and berries spring in with staggered transitions\n- Text on a curved path for the ribbon, dotted-leader menu rows, an illuminated drop cap floated into the first paragraph, and ornamental SVG dividers","originalInteraction":"- Day / Salon toggle (aria-pressed); the choice is remembered in guarded localStorage and the theme-color meta updates with it\n- Hovering or keyboard-focusing a blend card lifts it and grows ivy around its arch; a tap on touch screens focuses the card to do the same\n- \"Reserve a table\" answers with a gentle, clearly pretend confirmation announced through a live region\n- Everything is semantic HTML (header, sections, articles, aside, address, footer) with a visually hidden H1 and a hidden letter behind the drop cap for screen readers\n- Motion: draw-on and bloom on load, swaying lilies and ivy, halo rotation and sparkles; prefers-reduced-motion shows the finished artwork with all motion off","sourceHash":"d54fe78cd772df973e449feea906b4ec2b65b27ab9e5ebbf545f4e6e754d3459","name":"新艺术植物茶室","category":"交互网页","description":"虚构巴黎茶室用藤蔓、百合、金边和鞭状曲线组成新艺术海报，边框与植物缓缓生长。","interaction":"切换 Day / Salon 场景，悬停茶品卡片观察藤蔓生长。"},{"id":"086","slug":"086-cellular-tapestry","title":"Rule 30 — Tapestries of Elementary Cellular Automata","file":"086-cellular-tapestry.html","textFile":"086-cellular-tapestry.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/086-cellular-tapestry.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Rule 30 — Tapestries of Elementary Cellular Automata\". Explore all 256 Wolfram elementary cellular-automaton rules rendered as woven textiles: each cell is a small woven block with warp/weft shading, so Rule 30's chaos, Rule 90's Sierpiński triangles and Rule 110's structures read as jacquard fabric. Palette sets: \"Kilim\" (madder red #a4161a, indigo #1b263b, saffron #f4a300, cream #f1e9da), \"Monochrome linen\" and \"Neon loom\". The rule is displayed as its eight neighbourhood patterns → output, each clickable to toggle a bit, building a custom rule number live. The tapestry scrolls continuously upward as new rows are generated (like a loom producing cloth), with a shuttle illustration moving across. Controls: rule number input/slider, random or single-cell start, speed, palette, and \"famous rules\" chips (30, 90, 110, 184, 150, 45, 73). Hovering the tapestry shows the rule applied at that cell. Typography: a textile-label serif with monospace numbers.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Rule 30\" is presented as a museum installation: a cream, linen-textured gallery label on the left (a textile-label serif with monospace numbers), a working loom hung on a dark gallery wall in the centre, and a column of loom controls on the right. The loom weaves an elementary cellular automaton live: every pick of the shuttle computes one new generation from the row above, and the cloth scrolls upward out of the loom as it grows, so Rule 30's chaos, Rule 90's Sierpiński triangles and Rule 110's gliders appear as jacquard fabric. In the default Kilim palette the ground is indigo #1b263b warp, the pattern floats in madder #a4161a weft with a saffron #f4a300 band every 36 picks, and cream #f1e9da selvedges and marker stripes frame it; Monochrome linen and Neon loom re-dye the whole cloth.","techniques":"- Woven-cell sprites pre-rendered per palette: \"on\" cells are weft-faced (horizontal rounded threads with gradient shading), \"off\" cells warp-faced (vertical threads), each with the crossing thread peeking through in a twill offset, four jittered variants and fibre flecks, so the automaton reads as jacquard\n- Ring-buffer cloth canvas: each new generation is drawn once as a row of sprites (plus striped selvedges); every frame the visible cloth is blitted in at most two drawImage calls with a sub-row offset, giving smooth continuous upward motion at any speed\n- The pick in progress is revealed only behind the travelling shuttle, which carries a wound bobbin of the current weft colour and trails a curved weft thread from the selvedge; the reed/beater snaps toward the fell at each completed pick\n- Canvas-drawn loom: wooden cloth beam, side posts, reed dents, beater and bottom beam with grain lines; loose warp threads fan out below the fell (pre-rendered once); cloth edges are shaded like fabric under light\n- Rule changes weave a cream checkered marker stripe into the cloth, so a single tapestry can record several rules in sequence\n- Rule table of eight neighbourhood tiles (CSS-drawn cells in the live palette colours) with the rule's binary digits aligned beneath; wall colour, tiles and swatches follow the palette through CSS custom properties","originalInteraction":"- Rule controls: number field, −/+ steppers, a 0–255 slider with a wooden shuttle-shaped thumb, Arrow Left/Right keys, and the eight clickable tiles that flip individual output bits to build any rule live\n- Famous-rule chips (30, 90, 110, 184, 150, 45, 73) with an accurate one-line note for each; the label lists the rule's mirror, complement and mirror-complement equivalents as clickable links (e.g. 86, 135, 149 for Rule 30)\n- First row: single centred cell or random (re-threads the loom); loom speed 1–40 picks per second; Pause/Weave (Space); Rethread (R); palettes Kilim, Monochrome linen, Neon loom (1/2/3, radio group with arrow keys); Save swatch exports the current loom as PNG\n- Hovering the tapestry outlines the three parent threads and the chosen cell, shows a tooltip with the pick number, the neighbourhood → result in thread colours and the rule bit used, and highlights the matching tile in the rule table\n- Rule and palette persist in guarded localStorage; the cloth is pre-woven on load so the first frame is already a full tapestry; reduced motion halves the weaving speed","sourceHash":"5ffabb0feb580b4f4837a6322ddae6ed72be3a95d45669ce7b75b598c2dabb87","name":"细胞织锦","category":"科学与模拟","description":"初等细胞自动机被表现为一台正在工作的织机，每次梭子穿行都会织出新一代规则图案。","interaction":"切换规则编号，或点击八个规则格修改结果；观察不同规则织出的结构。"},{"id":"087","slug":"087-sketchbook-portfolio","title":"Pages from Juno's Sketchbook","file":"087-sketchbook-portfolio.html","textFile":"087-sketchbook-portfolio.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/087-sketchbook-portfolio.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Pages from Juno's Sketchbook\" (a fictional illustrator), a portfolio designed as a physical sketchbook. Dot-grid paper (#fbf8f1 with faint blue dots), graphite lines (#3d3d3d), highlighter yellow (#fff176) and a single red-pencil accent (#d64545). All illustrations are SVG line drawings with a hand-drawn wobble (SVG turbulence displacement plus slightly irregular paths) that draw themselves as they enter the viewport. Elements: washi-tape strips holding polaroid-style frames of CSS/SVG doodle artworks; sticky notes in a casual handwriting stack (\"Segoe Print\", \"Bradley Hand\", \"Comic Neue\", Chalkboard, cursive); margin doodles (coffee rings, arrows, stars); a spiral binding along the left edge. Sections: a hello note, selected work (six sketched projects), process scribbles, and contact written on an envelope. Interaction: pages flip horizontally with a page-curl animation on desktop and scroll on mobile; doodles wiggle on hover; a pencil cursor leaves a faint fading trail.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Pages from Juno's Sketchbook\" is the portfolio of Juno, a clearly fictional illustrator, built as a physical spiral-bound sketchbook lying on a desk beside a pencil and an eraser. A kraft cover with an elastic band, a lined label, a red rubber stamp, a star, a \"hello!\" dot and a die-cut cat sticker opens onto dot-grid pages (#fbf8f1 with faint blue dots) held by a metal wire coil threaded through punched holes. Inside: a hello note with a self-portrait, two pages of selected work (six projects shown as polaroids stuck down with washi tape), a page of process scribbles (thumbnails, a joke \"ideas vs. cups of coffee\" chart, a tool checklist), and a contact page written on an airmail envelope that opens to reveal a letter. Everything is drawn in graphite #3d3d3d with highlighter-yellow #fff176 washes and a single red-pencil accent #d64545, and every illustration draws itself line by line as its page appears. On desktop the pages peel over like real paper; on phones the book lies open as one long scroll.","techniques":"- A true 2D page curl: the dragged corner defines a fold line (the perpendicular bisector of corner and pointer); the page is clipped on one side, a paper-back flap is mirrored across the fold with a CSS reflection matrix and clipped to the peeled region, and a sheen band on the flap, a drop shadow and a cast-shadow band on the revealed page follow the fold\n- The corner is constrained so paper never stretches (it stays within reach of both ends of the spine); the flap swings past the spine and fades as the page goes over\n- Hand-drawn wobble: all line art passes through SVG feTurbulence + feDisplacementMap filters over slightly irregular hand-plotted paths; hovering switches between three turbulence seeds for animation-style \"line boil\" plus a small wiggle\n- Self-drawing illustrations: every stroke uses pathLength=1 with a dash/gap pattern offset into the gap (so not even round caps show), then transitions to 0 with staggered delays; the highlighter sweeps in and the red underlines draw on\n- Spiral binding from an inline SVG pattern of metallic-gradient coils aligned to CSS radial-gradient punched holes\n- Paper objects in CSS: torn-edge washi tape (zigzag clip-path over striped, dotted, grid and yellow patterns), polaroids, sticky notes with adhesive strips, index tabs on the fore-edge, airmail stripes, a perforated stamp (radial-gradient mask) and a postmark, turbulence-distorted coffee rings\n- An envelope with correct paper layering: its flap rotates open in 3D and drops behind the letter halfway through via a delayed z-index transition, while the letter slides up out of an opaque pocket\n- A pencil cursor (inline SVG data-URI) that leaves a faint graphite trail on a full-screen canvas, each segment fading out over about a second","originalInteraction":"- Desktop: turn pages with the hand-drawn arrow buttons, Left/Right or PageUp/PageDown, Home/End, the corner button (Enter/Space), or the coloured index tabs (which riffle quickly through the pages in between); or grab the page corner and peel it by hand, and on release it completes the turn or falls back\n- Phones (at or below 860px): no flipping; the pages stack as a scroll with the coil on each page, and each page's drawings draw themselves as it scrolls into view (IntersectionObserver)\n- Hover a doodle or polaroid to make it wiggle and boil; hover, focus or click the envelope to open it and read the letter\n- \"copy\" copies the (fictional, deliberately non-address-shaped) contact line through the Clipboard API, falling back to selecting the text with a hint\n- The graphite trail follows mouse pointers only; prefers-reduced-motion shows every drawing already finished, turns pages instantly, and disables the wiggle, boil and trail","sourceHash":"d099aee509e6cbd47d2b074404729ac0f35a2231e94297a8a65bf4d1c31ac431","name":"插画师的速写本","category":"交互网页","description":"虚构插画师的作品藏在一本带线圈、贴纸、松紧带和点阵纸的速写本里，翻页成为主要浏览方式。","interaction":"拖动书角或点击箭头翻页，也可使用左右键与彩色索引。"},{"id":"088","slug":"088-hourglass-timer","title":"Tempus — Hourglass Timer","file":"088-hourglass-timer.html","textFile":"088-hourglass-timer.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/088-hourglass-timer.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Tempus — Hourglass Timer\", a beautiful focus timer with a physically simulated hourglass. Two glass bulbs drawn in SVG with realistic reflections inside a turned-wood and brass frame (#6b4226, #c9a227); sand simulated as particles (or a cellular sand grid) flowing through the neck: the top pile drains with a conical dip, a thin stream falls, and a mound builds in the lower bulb with grains tumbling down its slopes. Warm desert-glass palette: sand #e3b778, glass tint #e8f4f8, dusk background #2d1b3d → #d98d62. Set the duration with elegant preset chips (1, 5, 15, 25 min) or a custom input; the flow rate adapts so the sand runs out exactly on time. Click the hourglass (or a flip button) to flip it with a smooth 180° rotation — the sand keeps flowing with gravity reversed. Remaining time appears as a large, thin numeral with a progress arc; a gentle chime plays at the end (Web Audio). Pause/resume. Typography: classic serif numerals with old-style figures.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Tempus is a focus timer built around a physically simulated hourglass standing in a desert at dusk. The sky runs from violet #2d1b3d to apricot #d98d62, with a low sun glowing through the glass, layered dunes, faint stars and drifting dust motes. The hourglass has a turned-wood (#6b4226) and brass (#c9a227) frame: two carved caps with grain lines and brass knobs, three turned posts with brass rings (the back one seen faintly through the glass), and brass collars where the bulbs meet the caps. Its SVG glass bulbs carry soft tint, inner shading and long specular reflections. Inside, every grain of sand (#e3b778 family) is simulated. The top pile drains into a conical crater, a thin continuous stream falls through the neck, and a mound builds below with grains tumbling down its slopes. Beside it is an elegant panel: a letter-spaced TEMPUS mark; a large, light classic-serif numeral (old-style figures where the font provides them) inside a ticked dial with a sand-gold progress arc; brass-rimmed preset chips (1, 5, 15, 25 min); a custom-minutes field; and Pause/Start, Flip, Reset and chime controls.","techniques":"- One bulb-profile function (a straight 0.82-slope funnel near the neck, clipped by a sine-power dome) drives both the SVG glass outline and the sand grid mask, so the grains always sit exactly inside the drawn glass.\n- The sand is a cellular automaton on an 85×201 grid, with per-grain colour, free-fall velocity (up to 5 cells per step, so the stream accelerates and thins), and two passes per row. Straight falls are resolved first, so voids climb vertically and the top pile forms a proper central crater. Then blocked grains tumble diagonally, with random friction for natural 45° slopes.\n- A neck \"gate\" makes the timing exact. Each frame the target number of grains still above the neck is total × remaining / duration, and only the surplus may cross, so the top bulb empties precisely when the clock reaches zero at any preset. Pausing closes the gate and the stream stops.\n- Time is anchored to a wall-clock end timestamp, with a 500 ms background watchdog, so the countdown and chime stay correct in hidden tabs. The sand catches up at full flow when you return.\n- Flip: the whole assembly (back SVG, sand canvas and front SVG) rotates 180° with an eased CSS transform. Gravity reverses at the half-way point, and the remaining time becomes the elapsed time, exactly as in a real hourglass. Sand in the new top bulb then drains again.\n- Rendering: each frame is written to a 32-bit ImageData at grid resolution and scaled up crisply. Per-column depth shading lightens the surface grains and darkens the buried ones for volume, and a continuous highlighted thread is drawn from the neck to the pile top while sand runs.\n- Craft details: turned posts generated from a radius profile via Catmull-Rom to Bézier curves, brass gradients, wood-grain strokes, clip-pathed inner rim shading, per-bulb gradient reflections, and a ground shadow.\n- The dial has 60 generated ticks with quarter marks, a gradient arc whose stroke-dashoffset tracks remaining time, and a glowing end-dot. It pulses when time is up, and the browser tab title mirrors the countdown.","originalInteraction":"- Preset chips (aria-pressed) and a custom-minutes form (1–180) set the duration and refill the top bulb. Start/Pause toggles the flow. Reset refills. \"Turn again\" appears when all the sand is below.\n- Flip with the Flip button, a click or tap on the hourglass itself, or the keyboard (the hourglass is a focusable role=\"button\" responding to Enter and Space).\n- Keyboard shortcuts: Space starts or pauses, F flips, R resets, 1–4 choose presets. Status changes are announced through a polite live region.\n- At zero a gentle synthesized two-note bell chime plays: inharmonic partials with exponential decay and a soft delay tail, via Web Audio. The AudioContext is only created after a user gesture, and a bell toggle button (aria-pressed) mutes it.\n- Responsive: side-by-side on desktop, and stacked on phones with the brand on top, the hourglass, then the dial and controls. Reduced motion makes the flip instant and removes the decorative drift.","sourceHash":"9b0b6d2295845f7e81606e16dd74951c812816ece6613e30ec79abb4c029731b","name":"沙漏计时器","category":"数据与工具","description":"暮色沙丘前的木质黄铜沙漏用沙粒模拟表达时间流逝，玻璃和沙堆形态持续变化。","interaction":"选择时长后开始，暂停或重置；点击沙漏或 Flip 翻转。"},{"id":"089","slug":"089-radar-console","title":"SENTINEL — Radar Array","file":"089-radar-console.html","textFile":"089-radar-console.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/089-radar-console.jpg","prompt":"Create \"SENTINEL — Radar Array\", a radar/sonar console as polished UI art, set in a clearly fictional scenario. Phosphor green (#39ff88) on dark olive-black (#020a05), with an amber mode. The centrepiece: a circular PPI radar scope on canvas with range rings, bearing ticks (0–359°), a rotating sweep beam with a fading afterglow wedge, and contacts (blips) that brighten when swept and then decay; contacts move along plausible tracks (aircraft, ships, a fast unknown), each with a small data tag (ID, heading, speed, altitude) and a trail of past positions. Surrounding console panels: a contact table, a signal-strength waterfall/spectrogram, system status indicators, a compass heading readout and a scrolling event log. Interaction: click a contact to lock on (target reticle, detailed readout); a range selector (10/25/50 nm) that rescales smoothly; a radar/sonar toggle — sonar switches to a blue-green water palette with pings and a Web Audio ping sound after a gesture. Typography: monospace with stencilled headings.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"SENTINEL is a radar and sonar console built as UI art for a clearly fictional training exercise (\"Exercise Grey Heron\", picket station K-7, all contacts fictional). Phosphor green #39ff88 glows on olive-black #020a05 under CRT scanlines and a vignette, with stencil-cut headings and monospace readouts everywhere. The centrepiece is a large plan-position-indicator scope: a bezel with 1°, 5° and 10° bearing ticks and 000–350 labels, range rings that rescale smoothly between 10, 25 and 50 nm, a dotted graticule, and a rotating sweep with a conic afterglow wedge. A true phosphor-persistence layer holds island coastlines, sea clutter, a drifting rain squall and speckle, and the returns fade after each pass. Aircraft, ships, a helicopter, a fishing boat, a ferry and a fast unknown move on plausible tracks. Each shows blips that flare when swept and then decay, trails of past plots, velocity leaders, domain symbols and data tags (ID, heading, speed, altitude). Around the scope sit a sortable track table with a symbol legend, system status lamps with live values and level bars, a target-lock readout with CPA/TCPA and an A-scope trace, a Doppler waterfall, an own-ship heading tape and a timestamped event log. Sonar mode turns everything blue-green: expanding ping rings replace the sweep, subsurface and biologic contacts appear, and the waterfall becomes a LOFAR-style tonal display. An amber P3 phosphor mode is also available.","techniques":"- PPI scope on canvas with a pre-rendered bezel layer (face gradient, graticule, dashed cross and diagonals, 360 ticks, bearing labels), rebuilt on resize or palette change\n- Phosphor persistence: an offscreen layer that fades exponentially each frame (destination-out), painted only inside the angular slice the beam swept this frame (radar) or the range band the ping crossed (sonar), with returns sampled from a rasterised island mask (bright coasts facing the antenna, grainy interiors), sea clutter or reverberation, a rain cell, a fish school and noise\n- Sweep beam as a conic-gradient afterglow wedge plus a glowing beam line (with a sliced fallback); in sonar, a glowing expanding ping ring\n- Contacts as vector overlays in world coordinates (nautical miles), so range changes rescale smoothly; blip brightness decays exponentially from the last hit; trails of past plots; the lock reticle tracks the predicted position with rotating brackets, corner marks, a bearing line and a pulse\n- Tags flip sides so they stay on the canvas; unknown contacts use a warning yellow\n- Doppler waterfall built from ImageData rows scrolled by self-drawImage; each track's radial velocity sets its line position, and the locked track burns brighter; sonar shows paired tonal lines; history is pre-filled so it never opens empty\n- An A-scope trace (amplitude against range) for the locked target, and a canvas heading tape with cardinal points\n- CSS theme variables swap the whole console between green, amber and water palettes; stencil headings use a mask cut; CRT scanlines, a vignette and bracketed panel corners","originalInteraction":"- Click a blip on the scope (or a row in the track table, with Enter/Space from the keyboard) to lock on: lock beeps, the reticle appears, and the readout shows bearing, range, heading, speed, altitude or depth, IFF, closest point of approach with time to it, track quality and an A-scope\n- RANGE 10 / 25 / 50 NM (or keys 1 2 3) rescales rings, blips and trails smoothly while old phosphor fades quickly\n- MODE RADAR / SONAR (or M): sonar recolours the console, fires a synthesized Web Audio ping with an echo loop every few seconds (a softer echo when a ping reaches the locked target), and changes which contacts are held, the status values and the waterfall type\n- PHOS P1 GRN / P3 AMB switches the radar phosphor colour; SND toggles all sound (off by default; audio starts only after a gesture)\n- RELEASE or Esc drops the lock; hovering a blip brightens its tag\n- A live simulation at ×8 time (labelled): tracks turn, orbit, zigzag, shuttle and meander; contacts leaving 60 nm respawn; the event log records new tracks, IFF failures, locks, mode and range changes\n- On phones the scope comes first at full width, with the lock readout, waterfall, heading, log, track table and status stacked below; reduced motion slows the sweep and stops the blinking; the loop pauses when the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"00414838ee331f852023e771311e5d94ec08a81df71eb7a81064af9a391f80b1","name":"雷达控制台","category":"数据与工具","description":"虚构训练场景中的雷达屏带有磷光、扫描线、方位刻度与目标列表，追踪数据与扫描回波联动。","interaction":"点击雷达光点或目标列表锁定对象，切换量程查看模拟轨迹。"},{"id":"090","slug":"090-word-clock","title":"Horologe — The Word Clock","file":"090-word-clock.html","textFile":"090-word-clock.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/090-word-clock.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Horologe — The Word Clock\", a typographic clock that spells the current time in words on a grid of letters (an original English layout in the letter-grid clock tradition: \"IT IS HALF PAST TEN\", five-minute precision plus four corner dots for single minutes). An 11×10 letter grid in a clean, wide-tracked sans; inactive letters are faint (#2a2a2a on #111) and active ones glow warm white (#fff6e5) with soft bloom, fading on and off in a gentle stagger when the time changes. Faces: \"Obsidian\" (dark), \"Paper\" (ink on #f3efe6), \"Copper\" (letters etched into a brushed-copper gradient, lit ones glowing) and \"Neon\" (magenta tube glow). A small subtitle gives the date in words. Interaction: a face switcher; clicking the grid toggles between the English layout and a second-language layout (e.g. Spanish); a fullscreen button; and a \"demo\" button that runs through a whole day in 20 seconds. The clock scales to fill any screen while keeping its square grid.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Horologe is a typographic word clock that spells the current time on an original 11×10 letter grid: \"IT IS TWENTY FIVE TO TEN\", to five-minute precision, with four corner dots adding the single minutes. The English layout is my own arrangement. It adds NOON and MIDNIGHT for 12:00, and its seven filler letters secretly spell MOMENTS. The second-language layout is an original Spanish grid (\"SON LAS NUEVE Y MEDIA\", \"ES LA UNA MENOS CUARTO\", \"… EN PUNTO\") whose fillers spell MOMENTO. Letters are set in a clean geometric sans on a grid that spaces them widely. Inactive letters are faint (#2a2a2a on #111) and active ones glow warm white (#fff6e5) with a soft bloom; they fade off and on in a gentle, reading-order stagger when the time changes. Four faces restyle everything, including the page, controls and dots: Obsidian (black glass with a diagonal reflection), Paper (letterpress ink on #f3efe6), Copper (letters etched into a brushed-copper plate, lit ones backlit and glowing) and Neon (magenta tubes on a dark brick wall, flickering as they strike). Beneath the face, a serif subtitle gives the date in words (\"Tuesday, the twenty-second of September\") with the year spelled out. The face always stays square and scales to fill the screen.","techniques":"- 110 letter cells in a CSS grid sized from a single --S variable (min of viewport width and height), so the square face and its type scale together on any screen.\n- Every face is a set of CSS custom properties on <body> (page background, panel gradient, sheen, off/on colours, layered text-shadow bloom, radius), so a face switch is one attribute change with smooth colour transitions.\n- Stagger choreography: letters switching off fade first, then new letters light in reading order via per-cell transition-delay; minute dots follow in clockwise order.\n- Language change as a diagonal flip wave: each cell folds away on rotateX with a delay from row plus column, the letters are rewritten while hidden, then they fold back in.\n- A brushed-copper plate from stacked repeating-linear-gradients over a multi-stop copper gradient, with an inset bevel and etched off-letters (paired light/dark text-shadows).\n- Neon tube ignition: a keyframed flicker on each newly lit letter (fill-mode backwards, so it never blocks the flip), a magenta ambient glow and a brick wall made of repeating gradients.\n- A glass sheen for Obsidian, letterpress deboss for Paper, and a demo progress hairline with a monospace readout.","originalInteraction":"- Click, tap or press Enter on the clock (it is a real button) to switch between the English and Spanish layouts; the EN/ES pill and the L key do the same. The date and year switch language too.\n- Face switcher with four swatches (keys 1–4); the choice and language are remembered in localStorage (guarded with try/catch).\n- Demo (D) runs a whole day, 00:00 to 23:59, in 20 seconds, with fast transitions, a progress hairline and a digital readout, then returns to the real time. Pressing it again stops early.\n- Fullscreen (F) with auto-hiding chrome and cursor after 2.2 s of stillness.\n- M reveals the hidden word in the filler letters, one letter at a time.\n- The face's accessible label and a polite live region give the time in words (including the extra minutes); updates pause while the tab is hidden; prefers-reduced-motion shortens the fades and removes the flicker and flip.","sourceHash":"134d6d4a34e26788837cb3366894764c18a739230af4886c23a7622516d00786","name":"文字时钟","category":"排版实验","description":"当前时间藏在字母方阵中，以亮起的词语拼成句子；四角的小点补充分钟精度。","interaction":"点击时钟切换英语和西班牙语，使用色样或数字键切换表面材质。"},{"id":"091","slug":"091-alpenglow-day-cycle","title":"Alpenglow — A Day in the Mountains","file":"091-alpenglow-day-cycle.html","textFile":"091-alpenglow-day-cycle.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/091-alpenglow-day-cycle.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Alpenglow — A Day in the Mountains\", a parallax landscape that cycles through a full day. Six procedurally generated mountain ridge layers (midpoint displacement) with atmospheric perspective (farther = lighter and bluer), a foreground lake reflecting sky and mountains with gentle ripples, pine silhouettes on the nearest ridge, and birds that occasionally cross. Time of day animates (or is scrubbed with a slider): pre-dawn indigo, sunrise with pink alpenglow on the peaks (#ff9eaa, #ffd6a5), bright noon blues, golden hour, sunset reds, dusk violets, and a starry night with a moon and the occasional meteor. The sun and moon follow arcs; sky gradients interpolate smoothly between keyframes; ridge colours are recomputed for each moment. Interaction: pointer parallax; a time slider with icons; \"Play day\" (60 seconds); clicking the sky regenerates the mountains from a new seed. Typography: a small, elegant sans showing the current time and a poetic name for each phase (\"Blue hour\").\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Alpenglow is a full-screen canvas landscape that lives through a whole day, in a quiet, graphic style. Six mountain ridges are generated by midpoint displacement and recede from dark forested hills to tall, snow-capped peaks, each layer lighter and bluer with distance. Beneath them, a still lake mirrors the scene with gentle ripples. Pines line the nearest shores, a reed-fringed bank frames the lower left, and birds occasionally cross by day. The first frame opens at sunrise, as pink alpenglow (#ff9eaa, #ffd6a5) catches the summits over a lavender valley. From there the day plays through in 60 seconds: bright noon blues, golden hour, sunset reds, violet dusk, and a starry night with a moon and the occasional meteor, before blue hour returns. Small letter-spaced sans type shows the time, a poetic name for the phase (\"Blue hour\", \"Afterglow\") and a line of description. A glass dock holds the time slider, whose track is painted with the day's own sky colours and marked with moon, sunrise, sun and sunset icons.","techniques":"- Midpoint-displacement ridges (513 samples each) with per-layer roughness and peak sharpening, a gaussian \"signature summit\" lift and renormalisation, redrawn every frame from a seed\n- Atmospheric perspective: each ridge colour is mixed from near and far tones and then toward the horizon haze according to depth, with a vertical gradient that pales toward the valley floor\n- Low-poly light and shadow: a smoothed, wide-window slope finds whole faces turned away from the sun (which side depends on whether the sun is east or west), and each face becomes a triangular shadow falling from its high end; on snow the same faces turn cool blue\n- Ragged snowlines from a second noise field, snow tinted by the light of the moment, and an alpenglow gradient laid over the upper faces at sunrise, sunset and afterglow\n- Twelve colour keyframes (sky top, middle and horizon, haze, near and far rock, glow, snow, water, cloud, star visibility) smoothstep-interpolated for any time of day\n- The sun and moon travel on arcs between rise and set. The sun's disc and halo warm as it sinks; the moon is drawn gibbous with craters and a halo; a low sun warms the horizon band\n- Twinkling tinted stars, meteors with gradient tails, cloud wisps made from a tinted soft sprite, morning and evening mist on the water, and small flocks of flapping birds\n- The lake is built from 2-pixel strips of the world above, flipped and shifted by two travelling sine waves whose amplitude grows toward the viewer, then tinted and darkened, with faint shimmer bands, a glitter path under the sun (or the moon at night) and a shoreline highlight\n- Pointer parallax with eased offsets per layer (from 8% for the far range to 80% for the nearest, with the bank strongest), mirrored correctly in the reflection\n- New ranges rise out of the lake layer by layer with a staggered smoothstep animation","originalInteraction":"- Move the pointer (or drag on touch) for parallax depth\n- Click open sky to generate a new range from a new seed; the \"New range\" button and the N key do the same, and the range number is shown bottom-left\n- The time slider scrubs through all 24 hours (and pauses playback). \"Play day\" / \"Pause\" (or Space) runs the full cycle in 60 seconds, and \"Now\" jumps to the real local time\n- The clock, phase name, colour-matched phase label and descriptive line update continuously. A polite live region announces each new phase, and the slider exposes aria-valuetext\n- Drawing pauses with the tab (requestAnimationFrame) and the clock resumes cleanly. Reduced motion starts paused, damps the ripples and reeds, and skips meteors and birds","sourceHash":"a75d4218b39d4debaa9c377691e14405a07dd8e287a7a08865b5b0db867b2e82","name":"山间的一天","category":"生成艺术","description":"六层山脊、湖面倒影、松林和飞鸟穿过完整昼夜，日出时的粉色山光逐步让位于白昼与星空。","interaction":"拖动时间滑杆，点击天空生成新山形，播放完整的一天。"},{"id":"092","slug":"092-soundscape-mixer","title":"Ambience — Soundscape Mixer","file":"092-soundscape-mixer.html","textFile":"092-soundscape-mixer.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/092-soundscape-mixer.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Ambience — Soundscape Mixer\", a calm app for mixing natural ambient sounds, all synthesized with Web Audio (no audio files): rain (filtered noise plus random droplet clicks), thunder (low noise bursts), wind (band-pass-swept noise), ocean waves (noise with slow amplitude swells), campfire (crackle impulses and a low rumble), forest birds (short FM chirps at random intervals), crickets (pulsed high sine tones), café murmur (band-limited noise through formant-like filters) and a soft drone pad. Each sound is a card with a hand-crafted SVG illustration that animates subtly while that sound is active (raindrops fall, flames flicker, waves roll), a volume slider and an on/off toggle. Earthy, calm palette: moss #606c38, deep forest #283618, cream #fefae0, sand #dda15e, clay #bc6c25. Presets (\"Stormy cabin\", \"Beach at dusk\", \"Deep focus\"), a master volume, and a sleep timer that fades everything out. The background gradient shifts subtly with the active mix. Typography: a soft humanist sans. Audio starts only after a gesture.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Ambience is a calm, tactile mixer for natural sound, set in the requested earthy palette: cream #fefae0 paper, moss #606c38, deep forest #283618, sand #dda15e and clay #bc6c25. A sticky side panel holds the brand, a large clay play button inside a breathing, audio-reactive orb, a live status line, master volume, four presets (Stormy cabin, Beach at dusk, Deep focus, Summer night) and a sleep timer with a progress ring. The main area is a 3×3 grid of cream cards, one per sound, each topped by a hand-drawn SVG vignette: rain from a cloud into rippling puddles, a thundercloud with lightning, gusting wind with tumbling leaves, rolling sea swells, a campfire, two birds on a branch, crickets under a crescent moon with fireflies, a café cup with steam and chatter bubbles, and a singing bowl for the drone pad. Every sound is synthesized in real time with the Web Audio API, and the page background slowly re-tints (wet, green, warm, night) to match whatever is in the mix.","techniques":"- Nine hand-authored inline SVG illustrations, desaturated and still when off, with scoped CSS keyframe motion when on: falling drops and ripple rings, drifting clouds, flowing dash-offset gust lines, tumbling leaves, swaying grass, looping wave layers with a slow swell, three-layer flame flicker with rising sparks and a pulsing glow, hopping birds with song marks, pulsing cricket arcs, blinking fireflies, rising steam, chatter bubbles, a swaying pendant lamp, expanding singing-bowl rings and a travelling sine line\n- Audio-to-visual sync: each thunder burst triggers a lightning strike on its card just before the rumble, bird phrases pop song marks, and café clinks spark on the cup\n- Audio-reactive orb: an AnalyserNode drives 48 log-spaced bands, smoothed into two layered organic blobs (quadratic midpoint curves) around the play button, which breathe gently when paused\n- Mood background: stacked radial/linear gradient layers (wet, green, warm, night) whose opacities are weighted by which sounds are on and how loud they are, cross-fading over 2.4 s, over a procedural paper-grain multiply layer\n- Custom range sliders with a filled track driven by a CSS variable, springy toggle switches, pulsing \"live\" dots, embossed cream cards with soft forest shadows, humanist sans typography (Seravek / Candara / Cantarell)","originalInteraction":"- Nothing sounds until the visitor acts: Play, a card switch, a card illustration or a preset creates the AudioContext inside that gesture. Play toggles to Pause (with a fade, then the context is suspended); Space also toggles when focus is not on a control\n- Each card has a role=\"switch\" on/off toggle (the illustration is also clickable) and its own volume slider; the master volume sets the overall level through a gentle compressor\n- The synthesis: rain = pink and brown noise beds plus scheduled band-passed droplet clicks and rising plinks; thunder = random low-passed brown-noise rumbles with rolling sub-swells and occasional cracks; wind = band-pass noise swept by two slow LFOs plus a whistling resonance; ocean = filtered noise with scheduled amplitude and brightness swells and a foam wash; campfire = clustered crackle impulses and pops over a modulated low rumble; birds = FM chirp phrases from four \"species\" through a soft echo; crickets = three panned sine pulses at around 4.4 to 5.1 kHz in chirp trains; café = formant-filtered noise voices with syllabic modulation, a room bed and cup clinks; drone = a detuned D-based chord through a slowly moving low-pass\n- Events are timed on the audio clock by a lookahead scheduler, which uses a longer lookahead in background tabs so the sound keeps running there\n- Presets switch and set levels for several sounds at once. The sleep timer (15, 30 or 60 minutes) counts down and fades everything out over the last stretch, then pauses\n- The mix, levels, master volume and active preset are saved in localStorage (guarded with try/catch); the visualizer animation pauses while the tab is hidden; reduced-motion mode freezes the illustration animations","sourceHash":"917708214cf6cb39a74c30d684495ee5e7db94162a06756a3f866f5e3a9501cf","name":"自然音景混音台","category":"音乐与游戏","description":"雨、海浪、森林等自然声音被装入一组带插画的控制卡，音量、预设与睡眠计时器共同构成混音工具。","interaction":"点击播放后选择声源、调整音量，或直接切换预设组合。"},{"id":"093","slug":"093-strandbeest-walker","title":"Windwalker — A Kinetic Sculpture Simulation","file":"093-strandbeest-walker.html","textFile":"093-strandbeest-walker.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/093-strandbeest-walker.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Windwalker — A Kinetic Sculpture Simulation\", inspired by Theo Jansen's wind-walking beach creatures (an original rendering). Simulate the Jansen linkage with its canonical link lengths (a=38, b=41.5, c=39.3, d=40.1, e=55.8, f=39.4, g=36.7, h=65.7, i=49, j=50, k=61.9, l=7.8, m=15), solving every joint position each frame from the crank angle with circle–circle intersections. Draw a multi-legged creature (e.g. 6–12 legs with phase offsets) walking across a beach (sand #e9d8b4, sea #6fb3c7, sky #f0f4f5) in its pale-yellow tube aesthetic (#f4e3a1 tubes with darker joints), with each foot's path traced as the characteristic curve and a wind sail driving it. Interaction: a wind slider (walking speed); a \"single leg diagram\" toggle showing one linkage with labelled lengths and its foot trajectory; a trace-paths toggle; buttons to add/remove leg pairs; drag to pan along the beach. Parallax clouds and footprints fading in the sand behind it. Typography: an engineering-notebook sans with monospace labels.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"\"Windwalker\" is a full-screen beach scene in which an original multi-legged wind sculpture, built from pale-yellow tubes with darker joints and topped by translucent billowing sails and a red pennant, strides along the sand. Behind it are a pale sky with a soft sun, drifting clouds, distant birds and a teal sea with rolling wave lines and a sliding swash. Every leg is a true Jansen linkage using the canonical lengths (a=38, b=41.5, c=39.3, d=40.1, e=55.8, f=39.4, g=36.7, h=65.7, i=49, j=50, k=61.9, l=7.8, m=15). Each frame, all joints are solved from circle–circle intersections of the crank angle, and the body is moved so the planted foot never slips. Red-pencil dashed curves trace each foot's characteristic path, footprints fade in the sand behind, and small sand puffs rise at each touchdown. The interface reads like an engineer's field notebook: a taped, ruled title label, a monospace readout strip, a graph-paper \"fig. 1\" diagram of a single leg with every length labelled, a control dock, and below the scene a notes page with the full table of link lengths and how the solver works.","techniques":"- Linkage solver: crank tip from θ, then five circle–circle intersections (upper joint, knee, the outer corner of the upper triangle, the rear corner of the foot triangle, the foot), each on a fixed branch that was verified numerically to stay valid through the whole turn and to give the classic flat-bottomed foot path\n- Mirrored leg pairs share a crank (solved at π − θ and reflected); each pair has a golden-ratio phase offset, and alternate stations sit on the far side of the spine, drawn lighter, smaller and offset for depth\n- Slip-free walking: the lowest foot is the contact foot, the body advances by the opposite of that foot's motion in body space, and the body height settles so that foot rests on the sand, which produces the natural bob\n- Tubes drawn as three strokes (outline, pale-yellow body, specular highlight) with glazed rigid triangles, darker joint caps, crank hubs, a braced spine and frame, and sails built from quadratic curves that billow and flap with the wind\n- Pre-rendered sky, sun glow, sea gradient and sun glitter; clouds as layered radial-gradient puffs at three parallax depths; animated wave lines and swash foam\n- Sand drawn with ground parallax tuned to exactly 1 on the walking line, so wind ripples, shells and pebbles (hashed per world tile) stay locked to the footprints\n- Pooled footprints that fade over 45 seconds, touchdown puffs, wind-blown sand grains and a soft elliptical shadow\n- Graph-paper diagram canvas: the leg to scale with labelled lengths on paper-backed tags, hatched fixed pivots, dimension lines for a and l, the crank circle, and the dashed foot trajectory with a live foot marker","originalInteraction":"- Wind slider (0 to 12 m/s) sets the crank speed, the sail billow, the pennant, the cloud drift and the blowing sand; at zero the beest stops\n- Plus and minus buttons (or + and - keys) add or remove leg pairs, from 1 to 8 pairs (2 to 16 legs)\n- \"Leg diagram\" (D) opens the single-leg figure with its labelled lengths and foot trajectory; on wide screens the camera leads a little so the beest stays visible\n- \"Trace paths\" (T) shows or hides every foot's characteristic curve and current foot position\n- Drag the beach (mouse or horizontal touch swipe) or use the arrow keys on the focused scene to pan; \"Follow\" (F or Home) re-centres the camera on the beest\n- Live readouts show crank angle, pace and distance walked (reading one linkage unit as one centimetre)\n- Optional Web Audio (off by default): filtered-noise wind that follows the slider and soft tube taps at each footfall\n- The page opens with a few seconds already walked, so there are footprints in the sand; prefers-reduced-motion lowers the wind, damps the sails and removes puffs and blowing grains","sourceHash":"55efbd112c06aeb2d9dca7fc4f3fd6a6654fec3d9e3204b4f819c7fc6b17149f","name":"风力仿生兽","category":"科学与模拟","description":"由真实 Jansen 连杆结构驱动的多足风力雕塑行走在海滩上，帆、旗与腿部运动共同响应风速。","interaction":"调整风速和腿的数量，打开 Leg diagram 查看单腿连杆结构。"},{"id":"094","slug":"094-watercolor-landscapes","title":"Aquarelle — Generative Watercolour Landscapes","file":"094-watercolor-landscapes.html","textFile":"094-watercolor-landscapes.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/094-watercolor-landscapes.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Aquarelle — Generative Watercolor Landscapes\". Each generation paints a new landscape in convincing watercolour: layered translucent washes built by recursively deforming polygons (many low-alpha layers with jittered edges) for sky gradients, distant hills, mid-ground mountains, a lake, trees as dabbed blobs, and a sun or moon — all on textured cold-press paper (procedural grain, with pigment darkening where it pools at edges). Palettes rotate among seasonal watercolour sets: \"Ultramarine & Burnt Sienna\", \"Sap Green Spring\", \"Quinacridone Sunset\" and \"Payne's Grey Winter\". The painting builds up layer by layer over about six seconds, as if being painted. Interaction: \"Paint another\"; palette choice; a \"wet-in-wet\" slider controlling bleed; clicking the paper drops water that creates a cauliflower bloom in the wet wash; \"Sign & download\" adds a small signature and date before saving a PNG. The painting sits on a wooden easel/table background. Typography: a delicate serif with a script-like italic.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Aquarelle is a painting table rather than a web page. A sheet of warm, cold-press watercolour paper with a slightly deckled edge is taped at its corners to a procedurally grained oak table, tilted a little and casting a soft shadow. Beside it sits a dark enamel paint box with four sets of pans, a wet-in-wet slider with a water-drop handle, and two buttons: a paper \"Paint another\" and a brass \"Sign & download\". Each generation paints a fresh landscape inside a crisp masked border, and you watch it build over about six seconds while an italic status line narrates the steps: laying in the sky, dropping in the sun or lifting out the moon, glazing the far hills, blocking in the mountains, pulling the lake across, mirroring the reflections, dabbing the far shore, washing in the foreground, then dabbing in conifers or round trees, birds, grass flicks and splatter. Four seasonal palettes change the mood: Ultramarine & Burnt Sienna, Sap Green Spring, Quinacridone Sunset (with a low, lifted sun) and Payne's Grey Winter (with a lifted moon and snow-capped peaks). The type pairs a calligraphic script title (\"Aquarelle\") with a delicate old-style serif (a Garamond, Baskerville and Palatino stack) set in italics and letterspaced capitals.","techniques":"- Recursive polygon deformation (midpoint displacement with Gaussian magnitudes and per-vertex variance): each wash is a deformed base shape, re-deformed for every one of dozens of low-alpha layers\n- Multiply compositing for transparent, subtractive pigment mixing, plus a darker low-alpha edge stroke on each layer so pigment pools at the drying edges\n- Granulating pigments (ultramarine, burnt sienna, Payne's grey) alternate flat fills with speckled noise patterns\n- \"Lifting\" passes that paint back toward the paper colour for clouds, sun halos, the moon, snow caps and horizontal glints on the water\n- Midpoint-displacement skylines: rolling far hills, peaky mountains, a mirrored and compressed reflection, and an undulating shoreline that overlaps the lake\n- Conifers as zig-zag silhouettes of drooping branch tips, with a darker glaze down the shadow side; deciduous trees as clusters of dabbed blobs over a trunk\n- A timeline scheduler that maps about six seconds onto the painting steps, with overlapping windows so wet sky bands bleed into one another and a per-frame time budget\n- The wet-in-wet slider scales deformation variance, layer count and alpha (dry brush through soaked)\n- Cauliflower blooms: a many-lobed outline that grows over about a second, re-frilled every frame, lifting pigment inside while ragged dark lines of the sampled local pigment pile up at the drying front\n- Procedural paper (value-noise tooth, speckle and fibres) blended with multiply; procedural oak planks with warped grain and seams; a CSS deckle clip-path, torn masking tape, a drop shadow and a fading wet sheen","originalInteraction":"- \"Paint another\" (key N) paints a new seeded composition; choosing a pan set (keys 1–4) repaints the same composition in the new palette\n- The wet-in-wet slider, from \"dry brush\" to \"soaked\", repaints with softer or crisper washes\n- Click or tap the painting to drop clean water: a cauliflower bloom spreads in the wash, and blooms are larger while the paper is wet (the status line reports wet, damp, drying, dry)\n- \"Sign & download\" (key S) finishes the painting instantly if needed, adds a script signature with the painting number and today's date in the bottom margin, and saves a PNG (painting plus paper grain) via toBlob\n- Palette, wetness and the running painting number are remembered with guarded localStorage; the same seed repaints on resize; the loop pauses while the tab is hidden; reduced motion paints the landscape at once instead of animating it","sourceHash":"f6303d909a388bcdf68d903b134a4995740e46d2b916e97f5dfa26c6182f42a9","name":"水彩山水画桌","category":"生成艺术","description":"水彩纸被胶带固定在木桌上，天空、远山和地景依次铺色。湿画参数改变晕染、边缘与水迹。","interaction":"生成新画面，切换调色盘和干湿程度；点击画面滴入清水，签名后可下载。"},{"id":"095","slug":"095-magnetic-field","title":"Lines of Force — Magnetic Field Explorer","file":"095-magnetic-field.html","textFile":"095-magnetic-field.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/095-magnetic-field.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Lines of Force — Magnetic Field Explorer\". A lab-styled canvas where you place and drag bar magnets (red N / blue S halves, #d62828 / #1d4e89) on a sheet of paper; the field is computed from magnetic dipoles (or magnetic charges at the poles) and rendered in three switchable styles: (1) iron filings — thousands of tiny dark filings aligned with the field, denser where it is stronger, like the classic school experiment on off-white paper; (2) field lines — smooth streamlines traced from the poles with arrowheads, in elegant ink; (3) a heat map of field magnitude in a refined sequential palette. A small compass follows the pointer and points along the field. Interaction: drag magnets; rotate with the wheel, R key or a handle; double-click to flip polarity; add/remove magnets; presets (single bar, attracting pair, repelling pair, quadrupole, horseshoe). Short explanatory captions in a sidebar. Palette: steel grey #6c757d, paper #f8f6f1, red/blue poles. Typography: a clean scientific sans.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Lines of Force is a physics bench in steel grey: a sheet of millimetre paper, pinned at the corners, lies on a brushed-metal table beside an off-white lab notebook of controls and notes. Red-and-blue bar magnets and a steel horseshoe sit on the paper, and their field is drawn live in one of three styles: tens of thousands of iron filings that swing into alignment like the classic classroom experiment, ink field lines with arrowheads traced from pole to pole, or a heat map of field strength in a paper-to-teal-to-navy sequential palette with contour lines. A brass-rimmed compass follows the pointer and settles along the field with a damped swing, a monospace readout reports |B| and its angle, and a figure caption beneath the sheet describes the arrangement and view, like a plate in a physics textbook.","techniques":"- Gilbert (magnetic-charge) model: each pole is a cluster of three softened inverse-square charges spread across the magnet's end, giving realistic fields for bars, a horseshoe and multi-magnet arrangements\n- Iron filings: a jittered grid of around 25,000 slivers with random resting angles; each aligns to the local field by an amount set by log |B|, grows longer and darker when aligned, becomes sparser in weak regions, and rotates toward its new angle over a few frames, drawn as four batched Path2D alpha buckets\n- Field lines: midpoint (RK2) streamline integration from seeds laid just outside each north pole face, stopping at magnet bodies or the paper edge; an occupancy grid keeps lines evenly spaced; backward traces from south poles add only the lines that arrive from beyond the sheet; arrowheads are placed by arc length\n- Heat map: |B| sampled on a coarse grid, mapped through a log scale and an 8-stop palette lookup table, with band edges darkened into contour lines, then smoothed as it is scaled up (coarser while dragging, finer at rest)\n- Magnets drawn on canvas with rounded bodies, bevel gradients, drop shadows, a light outline for contrast, counter-rotated N/S labels and a flip animation that squeezes the magnet end over end while its poles swap\n- Selection shown by a dashed outline and a ring rotation handle on a dotted stem\n- SVG compass with generated tick marks, a red/blue needle on a spring, and glass highlight; the heat-map legend and preset icons are small inline SVGs\n- Paper made from layered repeating gradients (5 mm and 1 mm grid), a vignette and metal pins; the bench is brushed steel from fine repeating gradients","originalInteraction":"- Drag any magnet to move it; the field, filings, lines or heat map update continuously (coarser while dragging, then refined on release)\n- Rotate with the mouse wheel over a magnet (5° steps, or 1° with Shift), by dragging the ring handle (Shift snaps to 15°), with the R key (Shift+R for the other direction) or the Rotate 15° button\n- Double-click a magnet, press F or use Flip poles to swap north and south, with an end-over-end flip animation\n- Add bar places a new magnet at the emptiest spot; Remove or Delete takes away the selected one; [ and ] cycle the selection; arrow keys nudge it (Shift for larger steps)\n- A strength slider scales the selected magnet's pole strength from 0.3× to 2×\n- View switcher (radio group, arrow keys supported) cross-fades between iron filings, field lines and the heat map, and updates the explanatory notes and legend\n- Presets: single bar, attracting pair, repelling pair, quadrupole, horseshoe and a closed circuit of four magnets, each with a short caption; editing a preset turns it into a new numbered figure\n- The compass follows the mouse or the finger (dragging on empty paper moves it on touch screens) and parks in a corner when the pointer leaves; the readout gives |B| in arbitrary units and the field angle\n- Rendering only runs while something is changing and pauses when the tab is hidden; prefers-reduced-motion snaps filings and the needle instead of animating them","sourceHash":"aa7507f97519a462ea52f58f6fe82467df8a1e16f729806f39e66697a479e4a7","name":"磁场实验台","category":"科学与模拟","description":"条形与马蹄磁铁摆在毫米方格纸上，铁屑、磁力线或热力图即时呈现叠加后的磁场。","interaction":"拖动磁铁，使用滚轮或旋转控制改变方向，再切换场的显示方式。"},{"id":"096","slug":"096-concrete-monolith","title":"MONOLITH — Architecture & Landscape","file":"096-concrete-monolith.html","textFile":"096-concrete-monolith.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/096-concrete-monolith.jpg","prompt":"Design \"MONOLITH — Architecture & Landscape\" (a fictional studio), a brutalist-minimal architecture portfolio: raw concrete surfaces (procedural texture from layered noise with formwork tie-hole dots and board-mark grain) in greys #8d8d8a, #b5b3ad, #d6d4ce and charcoal #262624, with a single accent of oxidised-copper green #4f7f73. Typography: huge, tight, uppercase grotesk headlines and precise small captions with project data (location, year, area in m²). Composition: a severe grid, vast negative space, heavy slabs. Five fictional buildings appear as axonometric SVG drawings of monolithic volumes whose light and shadow shift as a \"sun angle\" slider moves (shadows computed from the angle). Scroll storytelling: slabs slide in; a building assembles piece by piece as you scroll; a horizontal material-library strip shows concrete, corten, timber and glass as procedural textures. Interaction: the sun slider; hovering a project rotates its axonometric slightly; clicking opens a project sheet. Calm, heavy, precise motion.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"MONOLITH is the portfolio of a fictional brutalist architecture and landscape studio, built on a severe 12-column grid whose hairline guides stay visible over the whole page. The hero is a full-bleed wall of procedurally generated board-formed concrete (formwork panel joints, 150 mm board marks, tie holes with rust-free drip stains). Over it sit a tight uppercase grotesk \"MONOLITH\" across the full width, a short manifesto, precise uppercase captions, and a large axonometric of the chapel \"Vessel\" standing on a model plinth. A heavy charcoal manifesto slab slides in from the left. Five fictional buildings follow as rows of vast negative space, each an axonometric monolith beside tabulated data (location, latitude, year, area in m², height), with a huge outlined index number behind. Next, a pinned sequence assembles the Archive of Rain piece by piece as you scroll. A horizontal material library shows concrete, weathering steel, larch, low-iron glass and oxidised copper as live procedural textures, and a charcoal studio footer ends the page. Oxidised-copper green (#4f7f73) is the only accent. A fixed sun control drives every drawing: shadows, face shading and even the light across the hero concrete follow a real equinox sun path at 50° N.","techniques":"- Tileable procedural textures on canvas: periodic value-noise fBm for mottling, anisotropic noise for board-grain streaks, per-board tone shifts, joint lines, and radial-gradient tie holes with drip stains; separate generators for rusted weathering steel (blotches, pitting, runoff), silvered larch (warped grain, open joints, knots), low-iron glass (sky noise, reflection bands, mullions) and copper verdigris (standing seams, brown remnants)\n- A small axonometric engine in SVG: orthographic camera (30° elevation, adjustable azimuth), back-face culling, painter's ordering along the view vector, and Lambert shading of each face from the sun vector into a five-stop concrete ramp (a separate copper ramp for the lantern)\n- Real sun geometry: altitude and azimuth from the equinox hour angle at 50° N; every box's eight corners are projected along the sun ray onto the ground and convex-hulled into one shadow path, clipped to a physical model plinth so long evening shadows stay on the base\n- Facade detail derived from the geometry: board-mark lines every 0.6 m and tie holes on a 1.2 m grid on each visible face, cut openings, glazing, cast-glass pools on roofs, copper seams, and non-scaling hairline edges, like an architectural model drawing\n- Calm, heavy motion: 1.2–1.5 s ease-out rises, slabs sliding in from the sides, hover rotation eased toward a target azimuth, and pieces in the construction sequence dropping and settling with a cubic ease-out\n- Typography: huge tight uppercase grotesk (Helvetica Neue / Arial / Nimbus Sans) at −0.05 to −0.07 em tracking, outlined ghost numerals, wide-tracked micro-captions and monospace indices","originalInteraction":"- The fixed sun control (07:00–17:00) re-computes shadows and shading on every visible drawing, moves the sun on the mini dial, updates the header readout (time, azimuth, altitude) and shifts the light across the hero concrete\n- Hovering (or focusing) a project slowly rotates its axonometric by about 9°; clicking the drawing or \"Project sheet\" opens a modal sheet with a larger, gently turning axonometric, the description, a full data table and a north-up footprint plan (Esc, the close button or clicking the backdrop closes it, and focus returns to the trigger)\n- The Process section is pinned while you scroll: six steps (raft, walls, floors, screen, roof, copper lantern) assemble in order, with the active step highlighted and a progress meter\n- The material strip can be swiped, dragged with the mouse, stepped with the arrow buttons, or scrolled with the keyboard, and snaps card by card; hovering a swatch slowly zooms it\n- Anchor navigation in the header; responsive layout (on phones the giant wordmark leads the hero, the grid collapses to one column and the sun control becomes a bottom bar); reduced motion removes the slides and easing","sourceHash":"97235ae2f3fdfebb074b6132633a78d4cbfc781e781441bcbc5ffa3fe34c74d1","name":"混凝土建筑档案","category":"交互网页","description":"虚构建筑事务所的作品集以十二列网格、木模混凝土与轴测图呈现，光照时间直接影响建筑阴影。","interaction":"调整太阳时间，悬停或点选建筑图查看细节和视角变化。"},{"id":"097","slug":"097-harmonograph","title":"Harmonograph — A Drawing Machine","file":"097-harmonograph.html","textFile":"097-harmonograph.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/097-harmonograph.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Harmonograph — A Drawing Machine\", simulating the Victorian pendulum drawing device. Two or three damped pendulums (x, y and rotary) combine to trace delicate, decaying Lissajous-like curves: x(t) = A1·sin(f1·t + p1)·e^(−d1·t) + A2·sin(f2·t + p2)·e^(−d2·t), and similarly for y. Render a very fine pen line accumulating on cream paper (#f6f1e3) in sepia ink (#3e2c1c) or selectable inks (indigo, oxblood, or gold on black paper). The machine is illustrated beside the drawing (a wooden table with pendulums swinging in sync, SVG). Controls styled as brass knobs and engraved dials: frequencies (with harmonic-ratio presets such as 2:3, 3:4 and near-unison detuning), phases, damping, pen width and speed. \"Release pendulums\" starts a fresh drawing that animates stroke by stroke; \"Randomize\" picks tasteful parameters; \"Save drawing\" downloads a PNG. Show the current ratio as a musical interval name (fifth, fourth). Typography: Victorian scientific-instrument labels in serif small caps.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Harmonograph is laid out like a plate from a Victorian book of scientific recreations. On a dark mahogany desk sits a cream sheet (#f6f1e3) where a simulated three-pendulum machine draws in very fine sepia ink (#3e2c1c): two damped pendulums steer the pen in x and y and a third, rotary pendulum rocks the paper, so the figure spirals inward into the dense, decaying Lissajous knots these parlour machines were loved for. Beside the plate, an engraving of the pendulum table in hatched sepia line work swings in step with the drawing, and below it a walnut instrument panel with brass trim holds engraved ratio tabs, six knurled brass dials and inkwell swatches. A cartouche names the current frequency ratio as a musical interval (perfect fifth, fourth, octave and so on) with its size in cents and how far it is detuned, and every drawing is captioned as a numbered plate in serif small caps. Indigo and oxblood inks are also offered, as is gold ink on black paper.","techniques":"- The motion follows the brief's model exactly: x(t) = A1·sin(f1·t + p1)·e^(−d1·t) + A2·sin(f2·t + p2)·e^(−d2·t), with a second term on each axis from the rotary pendulum (sine on x, cosine on y), slightly different damping per pendulum, and a rotary frequency just below the x pendulum's so the figure slowly precesses\n- Stroke-by-stroke ink: the pen advances in 0.8 ms steps and is stroked in short runs whose opacity follows pen speed (a slow pen lays down more ink), so overlapping passes build up the dark decaying core\n- Layered canvases: a paper layer painted once with laid fibres, faint foxing and an age vignette (dark fibres for the black paper), the accumulating ink layer, and a pen layer with a small wet-ink bloom at the nib\n- A ghost preview: while a dial is being turned, the whole future figure is sketched faintly across the sheet\n- The engraved Fig. 1 in inline SVG uses hatch and cross-hatch patterns, turned table legs and labelled leader lines; its X and Y pendulums, rotary platform, pen arm and link rod move with the live equations\n- Brass knobs cloned from a template: an engraved dial ring whose tick marks come from dash patterns on a pathLength-normalised circle, a covered bottom wedge for the 270° sweep, a knurled edge from a fine dash ring, and a domed radial-gradient cap whose pointer rotates\n- Victorian typography: Didone and Baskerville-style stacks, small caps throughout, a double-rule masthead and an oval brass cartouche; the plate numbers are Roman numerals\n- A wood-grain page and panel built entirely from layered CSS gradients","originalInteraction":"- Frequency-ratio tabs (1:1 unison, 1:2 octave, 2:3 fifth, 3:4 fourth, 3:5 major sixth, 4:5 major third, 5:6 minor third) or keys 1–7 each start a new plate\n- Six brass dials, each an ARIA slider you can drag vertically or horizontally, scroll, or set with arrow, Page, Home and End keys (Shift for bigger steps): Detuning (±2 % for near-unison beating), Phase, Damping, Rotary amplitude, Pen width and Speed; dials that change the figure show a ghost preview and re-release when you let go, while pen width and speed change the running drawing\n- Release pendulums (R) starts a fresh plate from t = 0 and animates it stroke by stroke; Randomize (Z) picks tasteful musical settings; Space lifts and lowers the pen\n- Ink & paper: sepia, indigo and oxblood on cream, or gold on black (I cycles); switching re-inks the drawing so far instantly\n- Save drawing (S) exports a PNG of the sheet with its plate caption, through canvas.toBlob and a temporary download link\n- The cartouche and plate caption update live with the interval name, cents, detuning, phase and rotary settings; the first plate opens part-drawn so the page starts on ink\n- prefers-reduced-motion halves the drawing speed and throttles the engraving's animation; drawing pauses while the tab is hidden","sourceHash":"f8d6a6597237f71bc08f6ce4d0494f5b001e99e065b52429ef2f5c66195b9c90","name":"谐振绘图机","category":"科学与模拟","description":"三摆系统在纸面上留下逐渐收束的李萨如曲线，黄铜旋钮与木桌把数学模拟变成古典科学玩具。","interaction":"切换频率比，拖动旋钮改变失谐与阻尼，观察笔尖绘出的曲线。"},{"id":"098","slug":"098-paper-moon-storybook","title":"Pip and the Paper Moon","file":"098-paper-moon-storybook.html","textFile":"098-paper-moon-storybook.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/098-paper-moon-storybook.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Pip and the Paper Moon\", an interactive children's picture book (an original short story in eight spreads about a little fox who wants to give the moon a gift). Watercolour-pastel illustrations built from SVG shapes with soft gradients, paper texture and gentle outlines: a forest at dusk, a pond reflecting the moon, a hill, a lantern festival, and more. Palette: peach #ffcdb2, dusty pink #e5989b, mauve #b5838d, pale blue #a8dadc, midnight #1d3557, warm yellow #ffd166. Book UX: a two-page spread on desktop and a single page on mobile; a realistic 3D page turn (CSS 3D with shading on the curling page) triggered by clicking page corners, arrow keys or swiping. Each spread hides small delights: tap the fox to make it hop, tap stars to twinkle and chime, tap the pond for ripples, fireflies follow the finger. Large, readable story text in a friendly rounded serif; a \"Read along\" mode highlights each word in turn and turns the page automatically. A cover page with a sparkling title.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Pip and the Paper Moon is an original bedtime picture book that you read like a real hardback. A small fox called Pip notices that the moon gives light to everyone but never gets anything back, and sets out to find it a gift: a pebble dropped into the moon on the pond, a feather, an acorn and a blackberry that gets eaten, then a lantern festival where Pip learns to cut paper so it glows and sends a little paper moon up to keep the real one company. The book opens on a sparkling cover, runs through eight illustrated spreads and closes on \"The End\" and a \"Read it again\" page. Every picture is painted from SVG shapes in a peach, dusty-pink, mauve, pale-blue, midnight and warm-yellow watercolour palette, with wobbly hand-painted edges, soft glows, gentle outlines and paper grain. The book sits on a midnight cover board with a visible page stack, while fireflies drift over the whole scene. On desktop it is a two-page spread; on phones it becomes a single page, with the picture cropped around each spread's focal point and the text set large underneath.","techniques":"- A real 3D page turn: the turning leaf is built on the fly from three hinged CSS-3D segments (50/30/20 % of the page) with preserve-3d and backface-hidden front and back faces; the outer segments lead or lag the main rotation to make the paper curl, and per-segment shading and gloss overlays follow each segment's angle\n- Cast shadows sweep across the page underneath as the leaf lifts; the closed cover sits centred and the book slides open as the first page turns; the spine gutter is shaded on every page\n- Watercolour look: an feTurbulence + feDisplacementMap filter gives hills, trees and shores wobbly painted edges; layered semi-transparent gradients, soft moon and lantern glows, low-opacity outlines and a multiplied fractal-noise paper grain on every page\n- Each illustration is drawn once as a 2000 × 1400 spread and cropped with viewBox into its left and right pages (and re-cropped around a per-spread focus for phones), so the pictures run seamlessly across the gutter\n- Hand-built SVG characters and props: Pip in sitting, looking-up and sleeping poses, a beetle, Badger Bramble, a hedgehog, a rabbit, an owl, paper lanterns and the paper moon, plus pines, ferns, fiddleheads, clouds and a sparkle star field\n- Cover title in a warm rounded serif with a shimmering gradient sweep (background-clip text) and twinkling CSS sparkles; a canvas firefly layer with additive glow sprites and gold sparkle bursts","originalInteraction":"- Turn pages by clicking the curling page corners (they peel up on hover or focus), with the arrow buttons, the ArrowLeft/ArrowRight or PageUp/PageDown keys, or by swiping; dots show where you are in the book\n- Read along (button or R) highlights each word in turn with natural pauses at commas and full stops, then turns the page by itself and carries on to the end\n- Hidden delights: tap Pip to make the little fox hop (on every spread Pip appears in), tap the stars to make them twinkle and ring a pentatonic bell with a sparkle burst, tap the pond on the third spread for ripples and a wobbling moon reflection; fireflies follow your pointer or finger and drift around the book when you stop\n- Web Audio bells, a soft page swish and a hop sound start only after a gesture; the bell button (or M) mutes, and the choice is remembered in guarded localStorage\n- \"Read it again\" returns to the cover; each turn announces the spread and its text through a polite live region; the layout switches between spread and single page on resize; prefers-reduced-motion shortens the turns and stops the ambient animation","sourceHash":"aae378411e3a28621d4d9e7cd5efa6837bcfbffba1dd522c7e4e0f3712c0f3dd","name":"小狐狸与纸月亮","category":"交互网页","description":"原创睡前绘本讲述小狐狸为月亮寻找礼物的故事，插画、书页弯曲与逐字高亮共同完成阅读体验。","interaction":"点击书角、箭头或滑动翻页；开启 Read along 跟随逐字高亮阅读。"},{"id":"099","slug":"099-droste-infinite-zoom","title":"Droste — The Infinite Room","file":"099-droste-infinite-zoom.html","textFile":"099-droste-infinite-zoom.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/099-droste-infinite-zoom.jpg","prompt":"Build \"Droste — The Infinite Room\", an endless-zoom artwork: a self-similar scene that zooms forever — a room with a framed picture on the wall that contains the same room, which contains the picture… Render on canvas by drawing the scene recursively at successive scales (each level scaled by the frame ratio), animating the zoom continuously and wrapping seamlessly each time a full level is reached. The room is illustrated in a flat, elegant mid-century-modern style: warm walls (#f1dac4), a teal armchair (#2a9d8f), a mustard lamp (#e9c46a), a geometric rug, a window with a changing sky and a plant; the framed painting is the recursion portal. Each depth level shifts the palette subtly so the journey feels alive. Interaction: scroll/pinch controls zoom speed and direction (zooming out is infinite too); click to switch scenes (\"Room\", \"Spiral staircase\" with an Escher-like spiral, \"Ocean window\"); pause; and a \"Droste twist\" toggle that applies a logarithmic-spiral (conformal) transform for the famous Droste spiral effect. Typography: tiny modernist sans captions.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"Droste — The Infinite Room is a flat, mid-century-modern illustration that never ends. A room with warm striped walls (#f1dac4), a teal lounge chair (#2a9d8f), a mustard floor lamp (#e9c46a), an oval geometric rug, a potted rubber plant, a tulip side table, a sunburst clock that shows the real time, and a window onto hills under a moving sky has a framed painting on its wall. The painting contains the same room, which contains the painting, and so on. The camera drifts into the painting continuously and wraps seamlessly at each level, and scrolling reverses it into an endless zoom out. Every level carries its own gently shifted palette and its own time of day in the windows, from dawn through golden hour to a starry night with the lamp aglow. The journey therefore keeps changing while the room stays the same. Two more scenes share the conceit: an Escher-like spiral staircase whose portal lies at the bottom of the stairwell, and an ocean window with a lighthouse, sailboat and gulls, whose portal is a canvas on an easel. A \"Droste twist\" bends the whole infinite image into the famous logarithmic spiral. The only labels are tiny, tracked, geometric-sans captions on paper chips.","techniques":"- True recursive rendering on canvas. Each scene is drawn in 1600×1000 design units with a portal of identical aspect ratio. Levels from the first one that fully covers the screen down to the one where the portal falls below ~2 px are drawn with setTransform, each clipped to its own frame.\n- The zoom centre is the fixed point c = P0 / (1 − s) of the similarity map that sends the scene into its portal. Magnification r^f with f in [0,1) therefore wraps exactly onto the next level, forward or backward, with no seam.\n- Per-depth palettes: every base colour is converted to HSL and nudged by a smooth function of absolute depth (cached), so levels differ subtly and stay stable through each wrap.\n- The sky model blends five sky states (night, dawn, day, golden, dusk) with time and depth. It drives window gradients, sun and moon arcs, stars, drifting clouds, hill shading, sea colour, the lighthouse beam, and the brightness of the lamp's light cone.\n- Flat vector illustration built from paths, béziers, ellipses and gradients: the tufted chair, the sunburst clock (live time), the concentric diamond rug, and the swaying leaves. The spiral stair is a logarithmic spiral of wedge treads with risers, a handrail, balusters and tiny walkers descending forever. The ocean has animated wave bands and glitter.\n- Droste twist (WebGL): the self-similar image is rendered once into a 1–2k texture centred on c. A fragment shader maps each pixel through log-polar coordinates, multiplies by α = 1 − i·ln(r)/2π, reduces the real part modulo ln r, and samples the texture. The result is Escher's conformal spiral, zooming along the spiral as the level phase advances. A CPU per-pixel fallback runs at reduced resolution if WebGL is unavailable.\n- Scene switches cross-fade two full recursive renders. The twist view cross-fades with CSS opacity, and the 2D renderer idles once the twist fully covers it.","originalInteraction":"- The mouse wheel or trackpad changes zoom speed and direction continuously, from outward through stillness to inward. On touch devices, pinch outward to speed up the zoom in and inward to slow it or reverse it; a vertical drag also works. The − and + buttons and ↑/↓ nudge speed, and 0 resets it. A gauge shows direction and levels per second, and a depth counter (desktop) shows how deep you are.\n- Click or tap the picture, the Scene button, → or N (← goes back), Enter on the focused canvas, or keys 1–3 to switch between Room, Spiral staircase and Ocean window.\n- Pause or Space freezes zoom and ambient motion. Droste twist or T toggles the logarithmic-spiral view.\n- Captions sit on small paper chips with 9–12 px tracked uppercase sans, laid out for phones with a two-row bottom bar.\n- Reduced motion slows the default zoom, stops the stair rotation, waves and drift, and makes cross-fades instant. Animation pauses while the tab is hidden.","sourceHash":"84e4ccece84509e17e5b05e0fd38ead3b33e305ea8e48131aca97465f07ab58b","name":"无限房间","category":"生成艺术","description":"一间中世纪现代风格的房间里挂着同一间房间的画，镜头不断穿过画框，形成无缝的递归缩放。","interaction":"滚轮改变缩放速度和方向；触屏可用双指或竖向拖动，0 重置。"},{"id":"100","slug":"100-organic-wave-lab","title":"Organic Wave Lab — Ripple Tank","file":"100-organic-wave-lab.html","textFile":"100-organic-wave-lab.txt","thumbnail":"thumbs/100-organic-wave-lab.jpg","prompt":"Create \"Organic Wave Lab — Ripple Tank\", an interactive wave-interference laboratory. A full-screen 2D wave simulation (discrete wave equation on a grid with damping and absorbing boundaries) rendered with gorgeous shading: a water-like surface lit from above with normal-based highlights, coloured from aqua #3ee6d6 through deep teal #0b4f6c with coral #ff7b6b crests (plus palettes \"Lagoon\", \"Bioluminescent\" and \"Monochrome ink\"). Tools: click to drop a droplet; place continuous oscillating point sources (adjustable frequency/phase) to show interference patterns; draw walls/barriers with the pointer; and presets — double-slit experiment, single-slit diffraction, a lens (a region of slower wave speed that focuses waves), reflection in a parabolic dish, and rain. A lab-instrument UI panel (glassy, rounded, organic shapes) with sliders for wave speed, damping and source frequency, plus a probe that plots amplitude over time at a point as a live oscilloscope trace. Performance: a Float32Array grid at reduced resolution, drawn via ImageData and smoothed. Typography: a rounded scientific sans.\n\nSHARED REQUIREMENTS (apply to every page in this 100-page collection):\n1. One standalone HTML5 file. All CSS inline in <style>, all JavaScript inline in <script>. No external resources of any kind: no CDN, no libraries, no web fonts, no external images/SVG/audio files, no network requests. All visuals come from CSS, inline SVG, Canvas and procedural code.\n2. Valid HTML5: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html lang=\"en\">, <meta charset=\"utf-8\">, a viewport meta tag, a meaningful <title> and meta description, well-nested semantic markup, unique ids, labels/aria attributes for controls, <button type=\"button\"> for actions.\n3. Typography from local system font stacks only, always ending in a generic family; display lettering may be drawn with SVG or Canvas.\n4. Responsive from 360px phones to large desktops with no horizontal page scroll; canvases match their container and devicePixelRatio (capped at 2) and re-layout on resize.\n5. Works offline by opening the file directly (file://); no console errors; storage access guarded with try/catch.\n6. Smooth motion via requestAnimationFrame and transform/opacity; animation pauses when the tab is hidden where it matters; honours prefers-reduced-motion with a calmer fallback.\n7. Pointer events so mouse and touch both work; visible :focus-visible styles; keyboard support where it makes sense.\n8. Any sound is synthesized with the Web Audio API, starts only after a user gesture, and has a visible mute/toggle.\n9. Premium finish: a deliberate palette and spacing, polished micro-interactions, a beautiful first frame before any interaction, and original copy (no lorem ipsum). Factual content must be accurate; fictional brands, people and data are clearly fictional.\n10. Quality bar: visually impressive, bookmark-worthy, showcase-grade, premium, full of delightful details, and clearly distinct from the other 99 pages in concept, layout, palette, typography and motion.","originalDescription":"A full-screen ripple tank that feels like a luminous physics instrument. The whole viewport is a live water surface solved from the discrete 2D wave equation and lit from above: deep teal troughs (#0b4f6c), aqua slopes (#3ee6d6), coral-tinted crests (#ff7b6b), sharp specular glints and soft caustic brightening where waves focus. It opens mid-experiment. A glowing wave paddle sends plane waves into a pearl-white barrier, and two slits spread them into a fan of interference fringes with clear nodal lines. Two organic, asymmetric-cornered glass instruments float above the water. On the left is the lab console (tools, experiments, medium, oscillators, palette, actions). At the bottom right is a probe oscilloscope, joined by an animated dashed \"lead\" to a crosshair probe in the water. Three palettes restyle both the water and the glass: Lagoon, Bioluminescent (electric blue on midnight with cyan-green crests) and Monochrome ink (an ink-wash print on warm paper). On phones the tank runs in portrait (waves travel downward), the console becomes a bottom sheet and the scope docks top-right.","techniques":"- Simulation: Float32Array leapfrog integration of the wave equation on a reduced grid (about 62k cells, sized to the screen). A per-cell speed factor creates the lens region (n ≈ 1.54). Walls act as reflecting cells. Global damping acts on velocity, and a quadratic sponge layer forms the absorbing boundaries. Three substeps run per frame, and presets are pre-warmed so the first frame already shows developed interference.\n- Rendering: a per-pixel pass writes ImageData at grid resolution, which the browser upscales smoothly (bilinear) to the DPR-sized canvas. Normals from central differences give Lambert diffuse light and a Blinn specular term (^64). Negative-Laplacian \"caustic\" brightening marks focusing crests, and softsign tone mapping on height and slope keeps faint diffracted ripples visible without blowing out standing waves. Palette LUTs are 256-entry smoothstep ramps, lens cells get a subtle tint, and walls are bevel-shaded (lit and shadowed edges).\n- A vector overlay canvas draws pulsing source glows with a phase-breathing ring, the wave paddle, the selection ring with a frequency/phase label, the dashed lens outline with its index label, the dish focus marker, the probe crosshair, a flowing bezier lead to the scope, and tool cursors.\n- Oscilloscope: a DPR-aware canvas with an instrument grid, a glowing auto-gained trace from a 3-second ring buffer, and live PEAK / FREQ (from zero-crossings over the active window) / RMS readouts.\n- Glass UI with backdrop blur and saturation, uneven border-radii for an organic pebble shape, a top-left highlight sheen, rounded-sans labels, gradient-filled range tracks and palette-driven CSS custom properties.","originalInteraction":"- Tools (buttons or keys 1–5). Drop: click or drag to release droplets. Source: click to place an oscillator, click to select, drag to move, Delete to remove. Wall: drag to draw barriers. Erase: drag to open gaps. Probe: drag the oscilloscope's measuring point.\n- Experiments: Double slit, Single slit, Two sources (point-source interference), Lens (a slow-wave region that focuses plane waves), Parabolic dish (plane waves reflect to a marked focus) and Rain (random droplets). Each rebuilds the tank and adapts to landscape or portrait orientation.\n- Sliders: wave speed, damping, and source frequency (all sources, or only the selected one) plus phase for the selected source, with live readouts in tank-Hz. Waves visibly change wavelength and interference spacing as you drag.\n- Actions: Pause/Play (also Space), Calm (clear the water, also C), Empty (remove all walls and sources for free experiments), and Snapshot (downloads a PNG of the tank with its overlay).\n- Palette switching restyles the water, glass, scope and theme colour, and is remembered in localStorage (guarded). User walls and sources are stored in normalized coordinates and re-rasterized on resize. On phones the bottom sheet's handle expands the full console. Reduced motion lowers the simulation rate, and the loop runs on requestAnimationFrame so it pauses in background tabs.","sourceHash":"a42d6ab1ec3dd928332f544be4005b2667e0a5c08ce04695fde51bdd9a061aa1","name":"有机波纹实验室","category":"科学与模拟","description":"实时波动方程把整张屏幕变成发光水槽，波源、障碍物与双缝共同形成衍射和干涉图案。","interaction":"选择滴水、波源、墙壁、擦除或探针工具；切换实验，直接在水面上操作。"}];
